window.REVISED_CYCLES = {"c1":{"title":"Cycle 1: Understanding the Questions","html":"<div class=\"revised-cycle\"><header class=\"intro guide-heading\" id=\"top\"><h1>Cycle 1: Understanding the Questions</h1><p class=\"lead\">Current template questions, plain-language explanations, and a critique of the CTC example.</p><p>The template and handbook used here are Version 09. Each question is followed by its meaning, relevant rubric components, example response, and critique.</p></header><details class=\"contents-index\"><summary>Questions and reference</summary><nav aria-label=\"Cycle 1 questions\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/top\" aria-current=\"location\">Introduction</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/preparation\">Before you begin</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-data\">I.1 / Data and student-group selection</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-qualitative\">II / Qualitative evidence table</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-mission\">III.1 / School vision and mission</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A1\">IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A2\">IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A3\">IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A4\">IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A5\">IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B1\">I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B2\">I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B3\">I.3 / Using research to examine factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B4\">I.4 / Identifying the educational need</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B5\">II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C1\">1 / Describing proposed strategies</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C2\">2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C3\">3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C4\">4 / Applying targeted feedback</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C5\">5 / Building participation and anticipating implications</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/D1\">1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/D2\">2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/D3\">3 / Planning professional learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/version-alignment\">Template and handbook</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-sources\">Evidence sources</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-record\">Source notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/score-summary\">Score profile</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Rubric 1.1: Quantitative inquiry</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Rubric 1.2: Qualitative inquiry</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3: Equity gap analysis</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4: Contributing factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Rubric 1.5: Problem statement</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6: Strategies and alignment</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Rubric 1.7: Feedback and feasibility</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8: Leadership learning</a></nav></details><section id=\"preparation\" class=\"major-section\"><span id=\"foundation\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span>\n<h2>Before you begin</h2>\n<p>Read Parts A-D and their rubrics before collecting evidence. Part A establishes the equity issue; Part B develops contributing factors and a focused problem; Part C proposes strategies and uses feedback to refine them; Part D reflects on leadership learning. Arrange access to evidence, relevant perspectives, and strategy feedback early enough to inform the writing.</p>\n<p>Keep one student group and one state indicator connected across the cycle. The questions below follow the current template. Plain-language explanations and critiques are teaching guidance. Rubric estimates consider all relevant responses in the CTC example and are instructional judgments, not official scores. Interpolated text is labeled and excluded from the estimates.</p>\n</section>\n<article id=\"A-data\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Data and student-group selection</h2><section class=\"question\"><span id=\"A-group\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tIdentify the one specific student group you have chosen to further investigate.</p><p class=\"task-directions\">Directions: Complete the following table. While this template reflects the categories found on the California School Dashboard, it is neither an exclusive nor a mandatory list of categories, and you may add or delete rows as needed.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Choose one state indicator, compare the school and its student groups across the three most recent years, and identify one group for further investigation. Make the table understandable: the measure, years, groups, and reporting populations should be clear. The short identification answer names the group; Part A IV.1 develops the reason for choosing it.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Reading the data accurately</summary><p>A rate is a count divided by the population eligible for that measure. Keep the numerator, denominator, reporting year, and definition together; enrollment may not be the reporting denominator. Subtract two rates to describe a percentage-point difference.</p><p>Compare both the group's trend and its difference from the comparison group. Schoolwide results include the selected group; overlapping group rows cannot be added. Annual results may involve different students, so they are not automatically a matched-cohort comparison. Missing or suppressed results are not zero.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Analyze three years of quantitative data for one state indicator.</li><li>Identify relevant patterns and explain the selected student group.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how additional data clarify group differences within the indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"11\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"694\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>California state indicator (e.g., academic performance):</p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p> [Chronic Absenteeism Rate  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>LAUSD Whole Child Integrated Data </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year </p><p> 24-25 </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Second Most Recent Year 23-24 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent </p><p>Year </p><p>22-23 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>All Students </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>33.78% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>36.39% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>35.91% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>51.09%  of 167 (53% </p><p>NC) </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>51.71% of 170 (59% </p><p>NC) </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>47.27% of 162 (56% </p><p>NC) </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Socioeconomically Disadvantaged </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>36.53% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>38.23% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>40.03% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Students with Disabilities </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>40.61% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>45.56% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>48.08% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Asian </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>20% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>14.29% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>11.54% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>African American </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>42.86% (of 23 students) </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>58.62% (of 24 students) </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>53.85% (of 25 students) </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Filipino </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>20% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>24.14% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>25.71% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Hispanic </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>33.66% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>36.79% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>36.78% </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"13\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"236\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>SES, Academic Focus - F. My teachers make learning fun </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year </p><p>2024-2025 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Second Most </p><p>Recent Year </p><p>2023-2024 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent Year </p><p>2022-2023 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>All Students </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>46% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>41% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>37% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>50% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>53% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>45% </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"15\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"278\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>SES, Opp.For Part. - D. I participate in extra-curricular activities offered through my school.  </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year </p><p>2024-2025 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Second Most </p><p>Recent Year </p><p>2023-2024 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent Year </p><p>2022-2023 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>All Students </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>49% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>46% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>52% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>37% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>35% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>48% </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"17\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"509\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Student English </p><p>Language </p><p>Acquisition </p><p>Results </p><p>Summative ELPAC - ELPI scores (Source: CA Dashboard) </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year 2024 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Second Most </p><p>Recent Year </p><p>2023 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent </p><p>Year </p><p>2022 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>ALL English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>51% Progressed One </p><p>ELPI  Level </p><p>44.4% Maintained ELPI </p><p>Level </p><p>4.6% Decreased one </p><p>ELPI Level </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>46.7% Progressed </p><p>One ELPI Level </p><p>46.2% Maintained </p><p>ELPI Level </p><p>7.1% Decreased one </p><p>ELPI Level </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>45.6% Progressed </p><p>One ELPI Level </p><p>41.1% Maintained </p><p>ELPI Level </p><p>12.8% Decreased one ELPI Level </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><section class=\"interpolated-passage\"><h4>Student-group response</h4><p class=\"source-note\">Interpolated teaching response based on the CTC example.</p><blockquote class=\"interpolated-response\"><p>I have chosen English Learners (EL) at High School P as the one student group I will further investigate. My selected California state indicator is Chronic Absenteeism. The three years of attendance data show a consistently higher chronic absenteeism rate for EL students than for all students at the school.</p></blockquote></section></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The CTC example supplies a useful foundation: three annual attendance comparisons and a clearly selected EL population. The annual differences matter more than a rounded three-year average. The EL rate falls slightly in the final year, yet the gap widens because the schoolwide rate falls more.</p><table class=\"audit comparison-table\"><caption>Annual differences calculated from the CTC example</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>EL students</th><th>All students</th><th>Difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2022-2023</td><td>47.27%</td><td>35.91%</td><td>11.36 percentage points</td></tr><tr><td>2023-2024</td><td>51.71%</td><td>36.39%</td><td>15.32 percentage points</td></tr><tr><td>2024-2025</td><td>51.09%</td><td>33.78%</td><td>17.31 percentage points</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The table compares EL students with all students, a group that includes EL students. It is not an EL-versus-non-EL comparison. The enrollment counts also need to be checked against the population eligible for each reported measure; an apparent mismatch is a reason to investigate definitions, not proof that a rate is wrong.</p><p>The supplementary survey and language-progress tables can inform the inquiry only when their measures are interpreted correctly. A survey's remaining percentage may include neutral responses; maintaining an ELPI category does not mean a student learned nothing.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Report the annual differences in percentage points and explain both the persistent disparity and the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Identify the year, population, denominator, and source for each measure; reconcile apparent inconsistencies.</li><li>Keep the selected group consistent and explain the contribution of the additional measures in Part A IV.1.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.1: 3 / 5</h4><p>The tables and accompanying narrative establish a three-year pattern, relevant group, and rationale, supporting the overall 3 for Rubric 1.1. The additional measures are not connected clearly enough to the attendance disparity to support a confident 4. The interpolated identification sentence is excluded from this estimate.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A-qualitative\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"\"><span id=\"qualitative-analysis\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II / Qualitative evidence table</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">Directions: Identify each of the three qualitative sources you have selected, and record and analyze the qualitative data you have collected from each source.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record what each qualitative source revealed and explain what it contributes to understanding the selected group and indicator. A source is the person or material; an interview is a method; a finding is what was learned; analysis explains its significance and limits. Listing interview participants completes only the first part of this task.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Turning sources into findings</summary><p>Choose sources that can illuminate the selected population's experiences and the school conditions related to the indicator. Ask about concrete events: what happened, what support was available, whether it was usable, and what happened next. For documents, compare written expectations with available evidence of follow-through.</p><p>Record each source's contribution before comparing accounts. Look for recurring themes, differences, and counterexamples. A theme is an interpretation supported by evidence, not a count of interview labels. Explain whose experience is represented and what remains unknown; one account does not establish how common a condition is.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Collect and analyze at least three different, relevant qualitative sources.</li><li>Explain relationships among their findings, the quantitative data, and the equity issue.</li><li>For Level 4, deepen understanding with additional qualitative data as appropriate and provide a complete connected analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"21\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"168\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Data Sources </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Qualitative Data </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>ELD Teacher </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview  </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>EL Chronic Absentee/Student </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>PSA </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>ELAC Parent Member </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The four perspectives are relevant, but the findings cells are empty. This makes it difficult to see what each source contributed. The narrative later supplies findings, so the problem is incomplete presentation and analysis rather than evidence that no interviews occurred.</p><h4>How to complete the findings column</h4><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching reconstruction from findings already reported in the CTC example narrative.</p><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Qualitative findings and their contribution\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Reported finding</th><th>Contribution to the inquiry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELD teacher</th><td>Frequent enrollment interrupts instructional continuity and requires repeated review.</td><td>Investigate entry routines and instructional support; this is not evidence of teacher incompetence.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Student</th><td>The student reports a difficult placement experience and improved attendance after placement changed.</td><td>Examine the individual experience without treating it as a groupwide effect.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">PSA counselor</th><td>Unstable housing interferes with a student reaching school.</td><td>Examine attendance access and support; the account does not establish how common the barrier is.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELAC parent</th><td>Bus-route changes and less frequent service make travel to school more difficult.</td><td>Investigate transportation conditions and who is affected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The findings can be organized into two lines of inquiry: instructional continuity and placement, and access to school and attendance support. Comparing these accounts is analysis. Simply counting four interviews does not show how their findings relate or how widespread each experience is.</p><p>The attendance figures establish a group disparity. These accounts suggest conditions to investigate; they do not quantify how much of that disparity each condition produced. A strong response identifies both the contribution and the limits of each source.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Populate each findings cell with the actual evidence already described in the narrative.</li><li>Compare the accounts to identify themes, differences, and questions that remain unresolved.</li><li>Use a focused follow-up, where needed, to examine an unresolved condition rather than collecting another source merely to increase the count.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.2: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.2 reflects relevant collection but only partially supported analysis and connections. Completing this table would improve traceability; the related narrative must also explain the findings accurately to establish Level 3 and then the added depth of Level 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A-mission\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / III.1 / School vision and mission</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tInvestigate the vision, mission, and/or goals at your school and document your findings.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Investigate the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals and document the commitments relevant to your selected population. Explain why those commitments matter for that group. This question establishes the school's intentions; Part A IV.5 develops the comparison between those intentions and the equity-gap evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Document specific school commitments that can inform the equity analysis.</li><li>Connect those commitments to the selected group and indicator in the Part A analysis.</li><li>Rubric 1.3 evaluates the integrated analysis across the relevant responses, including the thorough synthesis required at Level 4.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"6\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"771\"><p>[High School P’s vision is to provide a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve college and/or career readiness upon graduation. The school’s mission is to carry out the following: 1) Maintain high expectations and provide rigorous, standards-based, and real world learning opportunities for all students. 2) Provide a safe, personalized, and small-school learning environment for all students. 3) Foster a culture, which builds the capacity of faculty and staff to serve all students. 4) Provide community-based resources to students and parents with the objective of mitigating obstacles to student learning. 5) Create a culture where stakeholders are empowered and actively engaged to affect change within the school and community. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The CTC example identifies substantive commitments: personalized education, staff capacity, community resources, and participation. The next instructional step is to explain their relevance to English learners experiencing chronic absenteeism, rather than leave that connection implicit.</p><table class=\"audit mission-connections\"><caption>Connections to investigate for the selected EL population</caption><thead><tr><th>School commitment</th><th>Relevance to this population</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Personalized education</th><td>Appropriate placement and instruction for EL students with differing language-development needs.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Staff capacity</th><td>Support for teachers managing language instruction and continuous enrollment.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Community resources</th><td>Usable assistance with reported transportation and housing barriers.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Educational partner participation</th><td>Meaningful opportunities for EL students and families to influence school decisions.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These commitments guide questions about actual access. A promise of community resources, for example, makes the transportation account relevant to examine. The promise alone cannot establish that assistance reached students or that the school failed to provide it. Identify the source and date of the adopted wording, then use actual findings when making the later comparison.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source and date of the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Select the commitments most relevant to EL students' instructional experience and attendance access.</li><li>Develop the evidence-based comparison in Part A IV.5, distinguishing intended provision from students' reported experiences.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>This passage supplies useful context. The overall 2 for Rubric 1.3 comes from weaknesses in the combined equity analysis, not from treating a mission quotation as a separately scored answer. The later responses must connect these commitments to a well-supported explanation of the disparity.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tReflecting upon the data collected and analyzed, what supports your decision to select this specific equity gap for your identified group of students? Share your analysis for the specific quantitative data you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template and your rationale for collecting it. How are these data relevant to understanding equity issues at the school for student groups?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why this student group and equity gap deserve investigation. Interpret the three-year quantitative pattern, explain why you collected the additional numerical data, and show what those data contribute to understanding equity at the school. A list of percentages is incomplete without the reasoning that connects them to your choice.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/version-alignment\">Template and handbook cross-reference note</a>.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Explain the three-year pattern and the rationale for selecting the group.</li><li>Show how additional quantitative findings clarify the indicator and group differences.</li><li>Distinguish observed patterns from explanations the available data do not establish.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"25\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1585\"><p>[The California indicator I chose to investigate is Chronic Absenteeism. The first data table presents chronic absenteeism rates over the course of 3 years for all students and various demographic groups.The average chronic absentee rate of all students at High School P is 35% which will serve as the baseline to compare the different student groups to determine the equity gap. To start, English Learners’ (EL) three year average rate is 50%, Socioeconomically disadvantaged students have 38%, Students with disabilities are at 45%, and Asian students have the lowest average at 15%. The African American student population has a rate of 51%, Filipino students have a 23% rate, and lastly the Hispanic group is at 36%. The pattern that stands out on this chart is that most of the subgroups improved their attendance from 2022-2023 to 2024-2025. There were small reductions in the numbers (improvement), with the exception of the EL, Asian, and African American subgroups which had an increase in the second year, but for the most part the data shows improvement when comparing the second year to last school year’s data. When comparing the average chronic absenteeism rate for all students (35%) to the average of the EL student (50%) there is a 15% equity gap. Furthermore I’m noting that there was no improvement over the course of the last three years, in fact it worsened. My rationale is that because the EL population is a larger group at High School P (~166 students) and because the equity gap has persisted, the focus group I will continue to analyze will be the EL group. </p></div><h4>Additional quantitative evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"25\" data-start=\"1585\" data-end=\"3359\"><p>To further understand this disparity over the past three years, I explored additional data points from the School Experience Survey (SES), and the California Dashboard English Learner Progress Indicator (ELPI) scores. I wanted to look at additional data to understand potential causes that have sustained the equity gap. The second chart presents data from the Academic Focus section of the SES, which asks students if their teachers make learning fun. I wanted to see if EL students find learning enjoyable, which is usually a major reason why students consistently attend school. The data shows that EL students at High School P are enjoying learning with their teachers at a much higher average rate of 49% when compared to the general population where only 41% think their teachers make learning fun. Although more of the EL population finds learning fun when compared to all students, it is possible that the remaining 51% are those struggling to be in school every day. The third chart is from the School Climate section of the SES and it presents the percentage of involvement students have in extracurricular activities. At the secondary level, students who are involved with sports and clubs often demonstrate proficient attendance to maintain competition eligibility or they are held accountable for certain club projects and responsibilities. I wanted to see if EL students participate in school groups that keep them accountable for their attendance. The data shows that ELs are less involved in these experiences at an average rate of 40% compared to the general population at 49%. This low number of involvement in the school culture may affect the connectedness ELs have with school in general which may limit the inherent interest of being in school daily.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"26\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1434\"><p> \tBecause chronic absenteeism has a negative impact on academic performance, I analyzed language acquisition progress. The fourth chart presents ELPI data from the state’s dashboard which indicates that on average only 48% of ELs are demonstrating improvement in their English language acquisition. This is significant because half of High School P’s EL population are Newcomers who are at the initial stages of English language acquisition and may still be adjusting to housing situations, culture, and possibly overcoming trauma thereby limiting their attendance and academic success. Newcomers may represent the other half of ELs that are showing limited language skill progress. It is possible that EL students get discouraged and lose interest in school when their language acquisition development is not properly supported and begin to fail academically which may cause the chronic absenteeism, but this could be interchangeable where EL students are not developing their language skills because of socioemotional or economic factors that limit their attendance. My findings align with current research that states, “The existing literature establishes a clear link between absenteeism and student performance (Liu et al., 2021).” My rationale is that it is most likely that half of chronic absentee EL students are the Newcomer subgroup who are struggling to acquire language and adjusting to a new school in a new country.  ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response names Chronic Absenteeism, selects EL students, and explains why supplementary measures were examined. Those are useful parts of a rationale. Its description of the trend needs greater precision: the annual differences widen from 11.36 to 15.32 to 17.31 percentage points, while the EL rate improves slightly in the final year.</p><p>The statement 'there is a 15% equity gap' confuses percent with percentage points. More consequentially, the response suggests that the remaining 51% of survey respondents are the absent students, and that half of chronically absent EL students are newcomers. Separate aggregate percentages do not establish either overlap.</p><p>The survey and language data may suggest questions about school experience. Explain that contribution without treating them as proof of who is absent or why. The research citation concerns a broad attendance-performance relationship; it does not resolve these local measurement and inference problems.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use annual percentage-point differences and acknowledge the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Remove unsupported claims that identify absent students from separate survey or newcomer percentages.</li><li>Explain how each additional finding changes the interpretation of the attendance disparity and identify what remains uncertain.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.1: 3 / 5</h4><p>An overall 3 for Rubric 1.1 is plausible because the indicator, three-year pattern, group selection, and rationale are evident. A 4 needs additional data that actually clarify the indicator differences; the unsupported connections in this response limit that case.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tDescribe the three different qualitative data sources you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template. How did these sources provide more information about this student group for the California state indicator?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>For each qualitative source, explain the perspective it provides, what you learned, and how that finding adds to your understanding of the selected group and indicator. Explain why the source was useful. A biography of each participant or a list of interviews does not show the contribution of the evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Describe at least three different qualitative sources and analyze their relevant findings.</li><li>Explain what each source adds to understanding the group and state indicator.</li><li>Use the findings together to deepen the inquiry, with further qualitative evidence as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1790\"><p>[I strategically chose to interview the following four qualitative sources to understand the equity gap for EL students. They are: ELD Teacher, EL chronic absentee student, PSA counselor, and an EL Parent who is a member of the EL Advisory Committee. The first interview was with an ELD teacher who is new to the profession and only teaches the Newcomer course which is the first course in the EL Master Plan to help ELs acquire the English language. All newcomer enrollees are placed in her class throughout the year quite frequently and she expressed how challenging it is to teach a course where there are sometimes more than 2 students being added to her classes almost every other week. Her goal is to help all students feel welcomed and included, but with new students being constantly added, she doesn’t feel there is continuity in her lessons because she has to pause or review objectives constantly. She also notices the inconsistent attendance in her class. Some of her Newcomer students are out due to doctor visits for vaccinations, their parents are ill so students must take on part-time jobs, and some come with traumatic pasts in their home country and don’t have stable housing. The teacher’s responses helped me understand that EL students struggle with developing a connection to school because many deal with significant outside factors that keep them away from school.Her experience also speaks to what Bryk, Gomez, et al highlight, “The task of managing heterogeneous classrooms is a key teaching problem, and classrooms have clearly become more complex environments (62). This systemic issue is affecting the quality of instruction and may have a detrimental effect on Newcomer’s language acquisition rate, which relates to their academic performance and attendance. </p></div><h4>Student perspective</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"1790\" data-end=\"2629\"><p>I also interviewed a recently reclassified EL student who was a chronic absentee. He shared that the reason he missed most of last year is because he didn’t like his teachers or classes. Although fluent in English, he remained classified as EL (identified in a public elementary school) due to not being reclassified at his private middle school. Upon entering High School P he was placed in ELD courses, which left him depressed and disengaged. With support from the EL Coordinator, he reclassified at the end of last year and was programmed into Mainstream English. He now attends regularly, enjoys school, and has a more positive view of his teachers and classes. What I learned is that timely reclassification is essential, preferably before high school, because course misplacement causes frustration, disengagement, and absenteeism. </p></div><h4>Counselor perspective</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"2629\" data-end=\"3276\"><p>The third interview with the PSA counselor helped me understand how issues of parent drug addiction and homelessness lead to chronic absenteeism among EL students. She shared a story of how a student and his parents were living in a car. There had been times when the student woke up in the back seat and found himself in another city, nowhere near the school. This went on for a while until the PSA found out and reported the situation to DCFS and the child went into the foster system. The PSA shared that EL students find it hard to maintain connectedness with school when they are experiencing traumatic events that cause chronic absenteeism. </p></div><h4>Parent perspective</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"3276\" data-end=\"4021\"><p>My final interview was with an EL parent on the EL Advisory Council (ELAC). This parent brought up a concern about recent bus route changes that had an impact on students. Previously, two routes serviced the school’s neighborhood with buses picking up every 15 minutes. Now there is only one route making stops further away for some and less frequent buses often leaving students stranded or forced to wait longer than expected to board a bus. Many families cannot afford private transportation so some students stay home rather than walking 3-5 miles to school. This parent’s community insight amplified my understanding about how transportation barriers can disproportionately affect EL students and directly contribute to their absenteeism.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher, student, counselor, and parent provide distinct perspectives. The teacher describes continuous enrollment; the student describes placement; the counselor and parent describe access barriers. These findings make the source choices understandable and provide more than a list of methods.</p><p>The response becomes less convincing when one student's experience becomes a general claim that course misplacement causes absenteeism. Attribute the experience to that student and explain what it makes worth investigating. Similarly, the teacher's account supports examining instructional continuity, not assuming inadequate competence.</p><p>The strongest explanation would show how the accounts complement or challenge one another. It should also describe relevant collection arrangements and limitations, while including only the personal details needed to understand the finding.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Organize each source around its perspective, finding, contribution to the attendance inquiry, and limitation.</li><li>Qualify the placement conclusion as an individual account and identify the further evidence needed for a broader claim.</li><li>Explain how sources were selected and how collection arrangements supported relevant, accessible participation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.2: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.2 recognizes substantive findings but incomplete analysis of their relationships and limits. A clearer explanation of each source's contribution and the connections among them would strengthen the Level 3 foundation.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tWhat patterns and/or trends related to the California state indicator and student group equity issues did you find in the qualitative data? How do they relate to the quantitative data patterns and/or trends you identified?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Move from individual accounts to patterns across the qualitative evidence. Identify which sources support each pattern, then explain how it relates to the numerical trend. Include evidence that complicates your explanation. The task is to connect different kinds of evidence, not repeat every interview or assume that all sources confirm the same story.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubrics 1.2 and 1.3</h3><ul><li>Rubric 1.2: analyze relationships among qualitative findings and quantitative patterns.</li><li>Rubric 1.3: integrate the findings into a clear understanding of the group's equity issue.</li><li>A stronger synthesis explains both supporting evidence and the limits of the proposed interpretation.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a> / <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"30\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"862\"><p>[The first major pattern and trend that emerged from the diverse viewpoints from the qualitative data is that English learners at the school are not making sufficient progress towards becoming proficient in acquiring English language skills. Even after three years of ELD instruction, many students at High School P still struggle with developing enough English communication skills to reclassify or to successfully pass other content courses. The teacher I interviewed shared her experience teaching the Newcomer course and described how students are not learning English at an appropriate pace. This is also reflected in the quantitative data from the ELPI scores which indicate that only 48% of EL students have advanced in their proficiency levels over the past three years. This means that 52% of EL students are not improving on their proficiency levels.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"31\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"598\"><p> \tA second pattern and trend that emerged from the qualitative data is that EL students don’t feel connected to the school. Many of the Newcomer students have expressed their inability to feel connected to their new school because they can’t communicate with most of their teachers and the majority of the school population. In addition, LTEL students who feel stuck because they have not reclassified before high school often develop low self-esteem and begin to disengage. This might explain why only 40% of EL students are engaged in extra curricular activities as indicated in my third chart.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"32\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"730\"><p> \tA third pattern and trend that emerged from the qualitative data is that many of our EL students are facing significant challenges stemming from their socioeconomic status. Both the PSA counselor and ELAC parent in their interviews mentioned that many of our EL students don’t have reliable transportation and experience homelessness, which in turn impacts their attendance. EL students who are dealing with these traumatic circumstances are more than likely focused on getting by rather than being in school every day, hence the disengagement and absenteeism. This is also reflected in the quantitative data from my first chart that indicated approximately 50% of EL students are chronic absentees for the last three years.  ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The accounts support investigating at least two themes: instructional continuity and placement, and barriers to reaching school or obtaining assistance. The numerical attendance pattern makes these themes relevant, but it does not establish their prevalence or causal contribution.</p><p>The claim that 52% of EL students are not improving treats language-progress reporting categories as a complete account of learning. Maintaining a category is not equivalent to learning nothing. Annual distributions also do not necessarily follow the same students.</p><p>A useful synthesis would identify the sources behind each theme and explain their relationship to attendance. The parent and counselor accounts, for example, complicate an explanation centered on students' motivation by describing circumstances that can prevent attendance.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name each theme and identify the specific accounts supporting or complicating it.</li><li>Correct the interpretation of ELPI categories before connecting them to instructional experience.</li><li>Explain the relationship to the numerical pattern without claiming that separate datasets identify the same students.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.2: 2 / 5, Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall estimates are 2 for Rubric 1.2 and 2 for Rubric 1.3. Relevant sources and themes are present, but inaccurate quantitative interpretation and overstated connections weaken the integrated analysis needed for higher levels.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"4\"><span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDefine the equity gap you identified for a student group through your quantitative and qualitative data analyses. Discuss related research and explain how this research informs and/or supports your equity gap analysis finding for the California state indicator and student group you identified.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>State precisely what the equity gap is for the selected group and indicator. Bring together the numerical disparity and the qualitative findings that help interpret it. Then explain how relevant research informs that interpretation. Research should contribute an idea or finding you can apply and examine, not serve as a quotation added after the conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Define the group's equity gap through integrated quantitative and qualitative analysis.</li><li>Explain patterns and the relationship between resources, access, and outcomes.</li><li>Discuss the research required by the prompt and explain its relevance to the selected group and indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"34\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"2477\"><p>[The equity gap I identified at High School P is related to chronic absenteeism and how it has persisted at a significantly high rate over the course of three years among the EL student population. When compared to the all students with an average rate of 35% over three years, the EL students have a much higher rate at 50% which is a 15% equity gap. The research emphasizes the need to “Examin[e] absenteeism rates among LTEL and newcomer EL subgroups is essential because in addition to the usual factors affecting absences across the k-12 span (i.e., illness, change of residence, lack of academic motivation, etc), absenteeism for these subgroups in secondary school could reflect a generalized disconnect between needs and services” (Finn et al., 2023). The qualitative findings support this idea by highlighting the importance of knowing the right resources for each subgroup. For example, Newcomers may be experiencing difficulty accessing content due to language acquisition barriers which in turn disheartens them and causes disengagement in school. These students are already having a hard time adjusting to a new country, surviving poverty, and homelessness. They would benefit from specialized social services to ensure their daily attendance in school. In addition, long term English learners fall into chronic absenteeism due to poor reclassification support and course programming that leads to lack of student interest in school. Having to take two periods of English every year limits their opportunity to take elective courses or engage in sports teams, which limits the joy of being in school. The research further posits that supporting ELs to reclassify before getting to high school can have positive outcomes and deter chronic absenteeism. For example, “We expect absenteeism to be lower among RFEP students than ELs because their educational experience is more mainstream, that is, they are less likely to face curricular tracking, labeling, and other exclusionary practices that are consistent with the EL designation…Although not all studies show positive effects of reclassification on student outcomes, a robust body of evidence indicates that reclassification has a positive impact on academic outcomes (Carlson et al., 2016).The research clearly indicates two very different approaches to addressing chronic absenteeism among EL students and it’s important to understand how to develop and sustain systems that ensure the appropriate supports. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies a persistent attendance concern and begins distinguishing different EL experiences. This is useful because a group-level rate does not imply that every member faces the same conditions.</p><p>The distinction between newcomer and other EL experiences is not supported by separate attendance analyses here. Treat it as a question to examine. Connect the actual teacher, student, parent, and counselor findings to the disparity without turning those accounts into subgroup rates or proven causes.</p><p>Research can help explain why a reported condition matters. State the relevant finding, compare its setting and population with this inquiry, and explain the limit of the application. A general citation cannot establish the local effect of a policy or service.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Define the disparity with the selected group, indicator, years, and correct numerical units.</li><li>Connect each interpretive claim to an actual quantitative or qualitative finding.</li><li>Explain the research connection and retain uncertainty where local evidence is incomplete.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.3 reflects an identifiable equity concern with an incomplete synthesis. A stronger response would make the numerical, qualitative, and resource-access relationships explicit before claiming the thorough understanding associated with Level 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"5\"><span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tHow is the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school related to specific components of the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Compare the equity-gap findings with specific school commitments documented in Part A III. Explain what the school promises for this population, what the evidence suggests about students' experiences, and where the two align or appear to differ. Repeating the mission or stating that the gap conflicts with it leaves the comparison unfinished.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Connect the selected group's equity gap to specific school commitments.</li><li>Use quantitative and qualitative findings to explain the relationship.</li><li>For Level 4, provide a thorough synthesis of the disparity and the conditions that help interpret it.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"36\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1697\"><p>[The school’s vision is to provide a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve college and/or career readiness upon graduation. Unfortunately, the EL students are not benefiting from a personalized educational experience as they are being placed in ELD courses based on their grade level and not their proficiency level due to the new programing policy from the district. The support EL students are being given to help them reclassify is also not curtailed to their individual needs, which may be the reason they feel disengaged. EL students who are fluent in English and have not reclassified usually feel stuck in their courses and begin to lose interest in attaining their diploma, which starts to affect absenteeism. On the other hand, Newcomer students who have been in high school over three years and are still not fluent in English or college ready will most likely start to consider their future options and pursue that as opposed to finishing their high school career. High School P values providing a “safe, personalized, and small-school learning environment for all students” as one of its mission goals, but it’s struggling to provide a personalized learning environment for EL students which supports their diverse academic and socioemotional needs. The systems the school had in place to address chronic absenteeism did not make a dent in the EL population and the equity gap persisted. The data reveals that our most vulnerable students are slipping through the cracks and a more streamlined personalized approach to supporting EL subgroups needs to be established or revisited to ensure the school is working towards meeting its mission goals. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response usefully returns to personalized learning and community support. The placement account is relevant to the first commitment; the transportation and housing accounts are relevant to the second. These are concrete points of comparison for EL students.</p><p>The claim that existing systems 'did not make a dent' goes beyond the evidence. Persistent disparity does not show whether support was implemented, who received it, or what would have occurred without it. Describe the apparent mismatch and identify what needs verification.</p><p>The comparison should retain the selected population throughout. A mission for all students applies to EL students, but neither the mission nor one participant account establishes what every EL student received.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each relevant commitment with the particular finding that helps assess it.</li><li>Replace claims of no effect with an evidence-based account of the unresolved access or outcome concern.</li><li>Identify the service or implementation evidence needed to distinguish planned support from support received.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.3 includes a real strength here: explicit mission alignment. Unsupported conclusions about service effects and weaknesses in the wider synthesis keep that alignment from establishing a higher level by itself.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tWhat potential contributing factors are suggested by the data you have collected and analyzed that may have created or added to the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible conditions that may have created or contributed to the single equity gap. Draw each factor from the Part A findings and explain the school practice, arrangement, or process involved. 'Potential' matters: the evidence should justify investigating the factor without pretending that the inquiry has proved its causal effect.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Use the equity analysis to identify relevant contributing factors, including institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Connect factors to supporting inquiry evidence and relevant research across Part B.</li><li>For Level 4, explain several factors in detail using supporting evidence from Step 1.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"64\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1709\"><p>[The equity gap I identified in Step 1 at High School P is within the EL student population who have a 50% chronic absentee rate for the past three years. When compared to the general population at 35%, the equity gap is 15%. Based on the quantitative and qualitative data, the following are three potential factors which may have contributed to the equity gap. They are as follows: Contributing Factor #1: (Structural) EL Newcomer students have inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning experiences. The EL teacher at High School P has not participated in the appropriate training to teach the Newcomer course. Students are not learning at an appropriate pace and eventuallty become disheartened and disillusioned when they start failing their classes due to their language barrier. This disengagement contributes to their absenteeism. The interview with the ELD teacher revealed that she struggled to master the course content because of the continuous enrollment of students throughout the year which made it difficult to pace her instructional goals. These circumstances and her inexperience affect the language acquisition progress of EL students as well as their academic achievement in other content courses due to limited English proficiency. The quantitative data of the ELPI scores demonstrate the slow language acquisition of EL students where only 48% of them are advancing on the English Language Proficiency Assessment of California (ELPAC) exam. The slow language acquisition and poor academic achievement (falling behind on credits) among Newcomer students causes the disconnect from school and as a result they begin to explore job opportunities and stop prioritizing education.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"65\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"77\"><p>Contributing Factor #2: (Institutional) A recent change in the district’s EL </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"66\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1116\"><p>Masterplan course placement policy is not serving the needs of Long Term English Learners (LTEL). The policy change eliminated the LTEL course, which was supplemental to secondary ELA courses and designed to support EL students who were fluent in English to meet the state mandated reclassification criteria. Now, all EL students are placed in ELD courses based on their grade and not their fluency levels. This creates mixed-proficiency classes where some EL students feel alienated because the content is not at their fluency level. They feel like they have been placed in remedial classes and this affects their self-esteem. The interview with the newly reclassified/past chronic absentee student revealed that being placed in an ELD course made him feel like he was being treated as unintelligent. This led to low self-esteem, disengagement, and absenteeism. The inappropriate student learning experience is corroborated by SES quantitative data that shows only 49% of EL students find learning fun which means that 51% may be our chronic absentee students who are disengaged and dismissing school altogether.   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"67\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1343\"><p>Contributing Factor #3:  (Structural) The socioeconomic barriers of unstable housing and unreliable public transportation contribute to the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students. In her interview, the PSA counselor reported that the EL students on her caseload who are new to the country usually experience homelessness due to unstable work opportunities, or dire family relations. These traumatic experiences prevent consistent attendance which hinder the EL students’ ability to develop meaningful connections with their peers and coursework. Similarly, in the interview with the ELAC Parent I learned that the issue with unreliable public transportation in the community makes coming to school significantly challenging for EL students, who often opt to stay home instead. The quantitative data found in the SES is indicative of EL students' inability to connect to their school’s culture by way of extracurricular activities because they are not in school or prioritize getting home as soon as school lets out. The data shows that ELs are less involved in extracurricular activities at an average rate of 40% compared to the general population at 49%. This low number of involvement in the school culture may affect the connectedness ELs have with school in general which may limit the inherent interest of being in school daily.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies relevant areas to examine: instructional continuity, placement, and assistance with attendance barriers. Each can be connected to a source in Part A and to a process the school can investigate.</p><p>The statement that the teacher has not received appropriate training is not established by the reported interview. A description of frequent enrollment becomes an unsupported personnel judgment. This is especially consequential because Rubric 1.4 explicitly identifies biased or superficial factors as a Level 1 concern.</p><p>Housing and transport conditions can be outside the school's direct control. Explain the related school responsibility, such as referral, coordination, or follow-through, instead of listing the external condition as though it identifies the school process to change.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each potential factor to a named finding from Part A.</li><li>Replace the unsupported training judgment with the documented question about continuity and instructional support.</li><li>Explain the institutional or structural process involved and identify the evidence needed to test the proposed explanation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.4: 2 / 5</h4><p>The working overall estimate for Rubric 1.4 is 2 because plausible factors are present but several conclusions exceed the evidence. The unsupported personnel judgment creates a material risk of a lower score; a clearer evidence-to-factor connection is needed for Level 3.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tHow do these specific contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, impact student learning or well-being for the student group?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how each identified factor could affect this group's learning or well-being. Describe the pathway from the condition to a student experience and its possible consequence. Keep the explanation tied to Part A evidence; naming a factor again or saying it causes the gap does not explain its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Explain how the identified institutional or structural factors relate to the group's equity issue.</li><li>Support proposed effects with the Step 1 findings and relevant research.</li><li>For Level 4, develop detailed explanations of several factors and their contribution to disparities.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"69\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"836\"><p>[The institutional and structural contributing factors have impacted student well-being and learning by creating the 15% equity gap of chronic absenteeism among EL students for the past three years. First, the inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning has disheartened many Newcomer students at High School P over the years because they are not learning English language skills at an adequate pace. Even after two years of ELD instructions, Newcomer students find it significantly challenging to keep up with graduation requirement courses or making up credits for past classes they have failed. The language barriers that limit their academic achievement not just in ELD courses, but in all other content courses, adds to the chronic absentee equity gap of EL students who may lack motivation to be in school consistently. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"70\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"852\"><p>Second, the disservice to EL students caused by the new course placement policies of the most recent EL Master Plan have negatively impacted EL student learning and well-being by lowering the self esteem of students. With the quick roll out of the policy, teachers were not adequately prepared to scaffold and differentiate course content that would serve the needs of a mixed-proficiency class and as a result many students did not receive the targeted language supports to acquire language skills and meet reclassification criteria. These circumstances lead to the alienation of some students while the teacher teaches at the level of others. This combination of poor instructional experiences and negative perceptions undermines students' academic progress, sense of belonging, and overall well-being contributing to disengagement and absenteeism.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"71\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"663\"><p>Lastly, the socioeconomic circumstances of unstable housing and unreliable public transportation negatively impact EL students’ learning and well-being because they create significant stress that can be overbearing. The trauma of not having a place to sleep or enough to eat outweighs any academic concern which causes student disengagement from school; their main concern is survival. EL students who rely on public transportation may encounter unpredictable challenges when commuting to school every day. These transportation related stressors can contribute to heightened anxiety thereby leading students to forgo attending school to avoid these difficulties. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"72\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"309\"><p>The combined effects of language barriers, academic struggles, and socioeconomic stress can negatively impact students’ sense of belonging, self-confidence, and overall emotional well-being, which makes it harder for ELs to stay engaged in school creating the significant equity gap of chronic absenteeism. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response attempts to discuss instructional access, placement, and socioeconomic barriers. Those conditions can affect students through different pathways, so they need separate explanations.</p><p>The opening claims that the factors created the entire numerical gap. The inquiry does not estimate that contribution. A transport problem may prevent reaching school, while an instructional concern may affect the learning experience; neither pathway has been quantified by these data.</p><p>Claims about motivation, self-esteem, and achievement should be limited to the actual accounts and findings. An interpretation becomes stronger when the writer explains its support and uncertainty instead of making it sound conclusive.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain one evidence-supported pathway for each factor.</li><li>Remove the assertion that the identified factors produced the whole numerical disparity.</li><li>Distinguish attendance-access barriers from assumptions about students' motivation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.4: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.4 reflects identifiable mechanisms with insufficiently supported local conclusions. Detailed explanations can strengthen the response only when they remain grounded in the inquiry.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.3 / Using research to examine factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tCite research related to your findings regarding contributing factors that may have created or added to the equity gap you have identified for the student group.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use research to examine the contributing factors identified from your findings. For each relevant study, explain what it found, how that finding relates to your factor, and why the connection is appropriate for this group and setting. Embed citations in the response as directed by the template and handbook.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Cite research relevant to the potential contributing factors.</li><li>Explain the connection between the research, local findings, and selected equity gap.</li><li>Support increasingly detailed understanding of the factors rather than adding citations without analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"74\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1174\"><p>[Research aligns with my findings regarding the contributing factors that add to the EL population’s chronic absentee equity gap.The first source shows that EL students do not have access to competent and rigorous learning. According to L. Santibanez and P. Gandara “Preparing teachers for an increasingly diverse classroom has been teacher education's \"central challenge\" for the past several decades (Rueda &amp; Stillman, 2012). This challenge is particularly acute in the nation’s secondary schools…ELs are a particularly vulnerable population at the secondary level as the academic and linguistic demands are greater at this level, and there is very limited time to acquire needed skills” (2018). This research highlights a significant issue that is affecting ELs across the state, and particularly Newcomers when they enroll at High School P. Their expectation is to quickly learn English to integrate into their school and culture, but their ELD teacher lacks the skills to help them learn the language. They become disheartened and discouraged as courses get more challenging and they can not keep up with the language skills demands. This has added to the equity gap.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"75\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1225\"><p>A second research source supports my findings about the inequitable district policy on course placement and how it adversely affects the chronic absenteeism equity gap particularly relating to LTEL students. According to Callahan R, et al “Under the auspices of ESL placement, when a student is identified as deficient in English, intentionally or not, [his] access to academic content is delayed until [he] is deemed sufficiently proficient in English (as measured by academic achievement in English as well as by her English language proficiency (Harklau, 1994b; Linquanti, 2001; MacSwan &amp; Rolstad, 2003) to engage with the academic content: the same academic content in which [he] is now behind as [he]has had limited exposure while learning English.” (2010) The student I interviewed experienced this type of set back due to not being reclassified while attending a private middle school. When he enrolled in High School P and was placed in an ELD course he was devastated because he felt stuck in the EL program. Although fluent in English, having to take an extra English course during the day felt burdensome and it affected his well-being, thereby creating low-self esteem that led to disengagement and absenteeism.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"76\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1803\"><p>The third research source confirms that “EL-classified students live in socioeconomic contexts that are associated with more absences: poverty, food insecurity, less parental supervision, frequent health issues, transportation issues, or familial or other obligations that necessitate students—especially older students—to stay at home and care for other children or family members, for example (Attendance Works and Everyone Graduates Center, 2017).” (Santibañez, L., Gottfried, M. A., &amp; Freeman, J. A., 2024) High School P is located in a low-income immigrant community, and 30% of its general student population is identified as living in poverty. The socioeconomic barriers of their communities are contributing factors that affect the attendance equity gap. Lastly, it is important to note research that presents the vulnerability of Newcomer EL students who are migrants and sometimes part of “The subgroup of students who are both homeless and chronically absent...For example, 9% of students who experienced homelessness have been identified as unaccompanied every year since SY 2015-16. Migratory students have accounted for 1% of students who experienced homelessness since at least SY 2014-15. While English learners steadily increased among 9 students experiencing homelessness between SYs 2014-15 and 2017-18, the annual increase was by one percentage point or less.” (National Center for Homeless Education 2022) The influx of Newcomer students to High School P is determined by external variable societal factors, which is why it is important to communicate a receptive and welcoming tone for new EL students as soon as they enroll in order to establish a sense of trust that they have a safe place they can count on when struggling with circumstances that limit their daily attendance.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response brings research into the discussion of instructional support and attendance barriers. That is necessary, but the presence of a citation does not establish that the study supports the precise local claim.</p><p>Broad findings about teacher preparation cannot prove that an individual teacher is unqualified. Similarly, research about placement or socioeconomic conditions needs a specific connection to the local evidence and an explanation of the limits of transfer.</p><p>The cited studies and the local poverty figure need verification. Check quotation accuracy, the populations studied, and the apparent copied page-number artifact before using them to support a conclusion.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify each cited study and identify its relevant finding, population, and setting.</li><li>Explain which factor the finding supports and which Part A evidence supplies the local connection.</li><li>Qualify the application where the research setting or evidence differs from this school.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.4: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.4 recognizes an attempt to use research, but several applications are broader than the local evidence permits. Clear, relevant research connections are part of the Level 3 foundation.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"4\"><span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.4 / Identifying the educational need</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tIdentify areas of educational need related to the single equity gap for a group of students (e.g., the school needs additional supports or materials, necessary specialists or counselors, after-school programs for students).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify what the selected group needs in order to experience more equitable learning or well-being. Describe the unmet condition indicated by the inquiry before naming a program. Supports, staffing, materials, or opportunities may be relevant, but explain the need they would address and its relationship to the single equity gap.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Draw the educational need from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Keep the need responsive to the identified student group.</li><li>Establish a focused basis for the feasible problem statement evaluated by Rubric 1.5.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"78\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"155\"><p>[In order for the High School P to address the institutional and structural causes of the 15% chronic absentee equity gap among EL students, it needs to:  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"79\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"706\"><p>Create an ELD teacher cadre with designated teachers who want to prioritize the educational needs of EL students and are vested in developing their teaching skills to help their students achieve academic success. This Professional Learning Community (PLC) can access targeted research based instructional strategies on differentiated instruction, scaffolding, and culturally and linguistically responsive pedagogy. The school should support this group with peer mentors, instructional coaches, or the Bilingual Program Coordinator to guide conscientious skill development. Better instructional quality will be evident for EL students who will value their learning experiences and continue going to school. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"80\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"516\"><p>Create a Tier 2 pull-out intervention program run by the EL Coordinator or Instructional Coach where they analyze EL student ELPAC scores to determine what domain/s is/are keeping students from meeting reclassification criteria. The small group intervention will target specific language skill development  once or twice a week for the entire semester before the new ELPAC administration in the spring. This will help LTEL students feel they matter and are cared for when it comes to their reclassification efforts. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"81\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"336\"><p>Offer culturally and linguistically relevant extended learning opportunities that expose EL students to various parts of their new city which will facilitate acquiring language in non-academic settings. These experiences will also contribute fun and new ways of learning which will inspire students to be more engaged in their school.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"82\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"695\"><p>Empower and support the Pupil Services and Attendance (PSA) counselors to create attendance intervention teams made up of teachers, academic counselors, school climate advocates, parent volunteers, and administrators who will be assigned chronic absentee students. This team will be responsible for making sure they call their student’s home when absent, welcome them back when they return to school, facilitate conversations with the student to establish trust and encourage them to continue coming to school, and/or learn about factors that limit their attendance. These concentrated efforts will ensure the most at risk students are given proper resources to address their individual needs.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response proposes a cadre, instructional arrangements, extended learning, and mentoring. These may be possible responses, but the underlying educational need becomes difficult to isolate.</p><p>For example, a teacher cadre is a delivery strategy. The need might concern dependable access to appropriate instruction during enrollment changes. Stating that condition first lets the school examine which response best fits the evidence.</p><p>The same distinction applies to mentoring: supportive relationships and timely access to assistance are different needs, even if one proposed program tries to address both. Select a defensible focus for the problem statement.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the unmet educational or access condition in plain language.</li><li>Explain the Part A evidence and contributing factors that support that need.</li><li>Reserve detailed program design for Part C after the problem has been defined.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.5: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.5 reflects a responsive concern whose need and proposed solutions are not clearly separated. A focused, evidence-based need would strengthen the foundation for a feasible Level 3 problem statement.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"5\"><span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tProblem Statement: Describe the equity gap that needs to be addressed at the chosen school for the California state indicator and the identified student group’s area of need (achievement and/or well-being).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the single equity gap the school needs to address, keeping the indicator, student group, and learning or well-being need explicit. Draw the statement from the inquiry and contributing factors. Explain why the problem is responsive to the group and feasible to address within the school's actual context.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Develop a feasible problem statement from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Make the statement clearly responsive to the selected group's learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain responsiveness and feasibility in the school's culture and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"85\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"660\"><p>[The equity gap analysis I completed revealed a clear equity gap for EL students on the California state chronic absentee indicator at High School P. This group has a chronic absentee rate of 50%. When compared to the general school population which has a 35% rate, there is a clear equity gap of 15% for the three years I have analyzed. The qualitative data collection revealed themes of insufficient progress of EL students towards becoming proficient in English language skills,  EL students feeling disconnected to the school, and significant socioeconomic challenges, which added to my understanding of the patterns and trends in my equity gap analysis.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"86\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"640\"><p>This equity gap analysis led to my determination of the following contributing factors: EL Newcomer students have inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning experiences, new district course placement policy fails to meet the needs of LTEL students, and several socioeconomic barriers contribute to the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students. High School P’s inability to address these issues has sustained the equity gap for the last three years. The quantitative and qualitative findings corroborate that these issues contribute to the equity gap. The identified group’s areas of need are associated with these factors.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"87\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1547\"><p>EL students need adults at the school who are committed to growing professionally to meet their educational needs and demonstrate a vested interest in being a part of their academic achievement. Quality instructors will not only produce improved language development and academic performance, but also instill in EL students a sense of pride and belonging to a school that looks after their achievement and well-being. EL students also need adults who are willing to develop and implement intervention programs that will target supporting their reclassification attempts early on in high school. Reclassification criteria at upper grade levels is significantly more challenging compared to ninth or tenth grade. Helping students reclassify sooner than later will help them lose the sense of being stuck in the EL program and motivate them to attend school consistently and continue their education in a traditional high school program. In addition, students need school personnel mentors who will develop a trusting relationship with them through well-being check-ins, frequent family communication, and goal setting. According to a study by R. Balfanz and V. Byrnes titled “Meeting the Challenge of Combating Chronic Absenteeism” there is proof that “Quality mentoring, especially as part of a school-wide effort, can be leveraged as a strategy to improve attendance and boost academic achievement…” (2013). The chronic absentee equity gap demonstrates how EL students are falling through the cracks without proper monitoring and intervention.   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"88\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1954\"><p>In order to close the equity gap, High School P needs to create an ELD teacher cadre that is committed to teaching EL students and developing the necessary skills to meet the diverse needs of those students. This will ensure EL students that their education matters to the school therefore it should matter to them by being in school every day.  The school also needs to create a Tier 2 intervention pull-out program that provides intense individualized reclassification supports for LTEL students. This will give LTEL students the confidence to continue their reclassification efforts and motivate them to improve their attendance. Lastly, the school needs to provide extended learning opportunities to EL students to support language acquisition in a social setting, and create a cadre of designated support staff to mentor chronic absentee students. This problem statement is responsive to the school culture and context because there is a Bilingual Coordinator who can organize professional development opportunities to the willing ELD teacher cadre. Additionally, the school has a part-time intervention coordinator who can develop and implement a Tier 2 program in collaboration with the Bilingual Coordinator. High School P also counts on three PSA counselors who usually take on five or more interns. They can develop a mentoring program and recruit various willing personnel to mentor chronic absentee students. Furthermore, this problem statement is feasible for the school’s culture and context as there is enough personnel who can align their time and resources to assist the EL population. By addressing the identified needs and contributing factors, High School P can live up to its mission and vision of “providing a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve” by implementing the proposed changes. This will assist in the reduction or eliminate the 15% equity gap of chronic absenteeism among EL students.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response keeps EL students and attendance visible and attempts to discuss local resources. Those are useful foundations for a problem statement.</p><p>It then expands into several programs and assumes sufficient staff capacity. Staff roles alone do not establish available time or agreement; the later PSA feedback about recruiting mentors makes this assumption especially important to revisit.</p><p>The promise to reduce or eliminate the gap turns an intended outcome into an assurance. A focused statement can justify improvement work while acknowledging that the effect of a proposed response remains to be examined.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Reduce the statement to one evidence-supported educational need and a feasible school-level scope.</li><li>Support feasibility with actual responsibilities, time, resources, authority, and constraints.</li><li>Use percentage-point units and describe attendance improvement as an intended outcome to assess.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.5: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.5 reflects a relevant but overly broad problem and unverified feasibility assumptions. A 4 requires a convincing explanation of responsiveness and feasibility on top of the clear Level 3 problem.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"strategy-map\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 1 / Describing proposed strategies</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tFor the student equity gap you identified in Step 1, describe the potential strategies you identified for equitable school improvement and how they are to be applied.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the strategies you propose and how each would operate at the school. Connect them to the equity gap and explain the activities, responsibilities, and application well enough for the reader to understand the proposal. This is a plan for improvement; distinguish proposed actions from actions already completed.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the proposal to the evidence</summary><p>Keep the reasoning visible without repeating the full investigation. A proposed strategy responds to a supported finding or a clearly identified condition to investigate; it is not evidence that improvement has already occurred.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Connection</th><th scope=\"col\">What to explain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Finding and potential factor</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify the evidence and the school-related condition it suggests. Keep an untested explanation provisional.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Proposed action and access</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Explain what would change for the selected student group and why that change addresses the need.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Feedback and refinement</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify who examined feasibility, what they contributed, and how the proposal changed.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Remaining uncertainty</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">State what would need to be examined before claiming that the response works.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Propose strategies informed by the analysis, factors, and problem statement.</li><li>Explain how the strategies are applied and aligned with school commitments.</li><li>For Level 4, show a strategic and contextually responsive approach to the equity issue.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"93\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"355\"><p>[The equity gap I identified at High School P is related to chronic absentee rates among EL students, who averaged 50% over a period of three years. When compared to the general population at 35%, there is a clear equity gap of 15%. To help address this inequity, I have selected two focus strategies that would fit the current context of High School P.  </p></div><h4>Instructional collaboration</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"93\" data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"2712\"><p>Strategy 1: Create an ELD Teacher cadre committed to developing their teaching skills to improve the quality of instruction in ELD courses. This strategy will address the structural Contributing Factor #1 which indicates that EL Newcomer students have inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning experiences. My reasoning for doing this is based on the quantitative data from my equity gap analysis which revealed that only 48% of EL students have progressed one level on the ELPAC, meaning most are not developing their English language skills or falling behind academically. When EL students fall behind in their courses and eventually on graduation credits they begin to disengage from school which leads to chronic absenteeism. The ELD teacher cadre will allow teachers to collaborate on research-based strategies for language acquisition. The school’s Bilingual Coordinator will lead, with meetings twice a month in the fall and monthly in the spring. The Bilingual Coordinator will also curate a resource bank with SDAIE strategies, Culturally and Linguistically responsive pedagogy, and Translanguaging philosophies to guide the meeting objectives. During the meetings cadre participants will work on setting instructional goals to implement in between meetings. The ELD teacher cadre will also analyze data (attendance and academic) to understand the importance of creating learning environments that are culturally and linguistically responsive and focus on differentiation to meet the needs of every student. The idea is for the ELD teachers to get into a PDSA cycle where they plan to use the strategies, try them out with their students, evaluate the effectiveness of student engagement, then share their findings with the cadre at the next meeting. The vision for the cadre will be presented to the principal to gain approval. Once the idea is approved, it will be presented to all ELD teachers at their department meeting to gain buy-in and recruit participants. This PLC addresses the problem statement by providing ELD teachers the opportunity to hone in on their methodology and provide more rigorous high quality instruction. When EL students see an improvement in the quality of their lessons they will begin to value being in school consistently, which will reduce frustration and disengagement, thereby increasing attendance. </p></div><h4>Mentoring and support</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"93\" data-start=\"2712\" data-end=\"4483\"><p>Strategy 2: Create a mentoring and well-being check-in program for chronic absentee students. This strategy will address the structural Contributing Factor #3 which indicates that EL students face socioeconomic barriers of unstable housing and unreliable transportation. My reasoning for doing this is because the qualitative data from my equity gap analysis revealed that EL students who experience chronic absenteeism face significant socioeconomic barriers like homelessness and unstable public transportation. A structured mentoring and well-being check-in program will provide collaborative educational partner support that strengthens family contact which will build trust and strengthen students’ sense of belonging resulting in improved attendance. This mentoring program will be led by the PSA who will recruit participants by presenting attendance data to the out of class support personnel at their monthly meetings and identify the students who are in most need of individualized intervention. After giving an overview of the mentor responsibilities and expected outcomes, the PSA will recruit volunteer mentors and assign one student to each. The PSA will provide a one-page conversation guide and remain accessible to coordinate resources for students and families. This group will meet once a month for the entire school year to report on each student’s progress or lack thereof, and to bring up any questions or concerns they may have as they continue to mentor the students. This program addresses the problem statement because mentors can identify socioeconomic barriers during check-ins and connect students to school or community resources which will reduce stressors and allow students to engage in school consistently thereby reducing absenteeism.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response provides concrete operational detail: a Bilingual Coordinator, a meeting rhythm, an instructional inquiry cycle, a PSA role, and a mentoring guide. This makes the proposed work understandable.</p><p>The instructional rationale is weakened by treating ELPI category maintenance as lack of learning and by unsupported claims about competence. Correct the evidence interpretation before using it to justify the strategy.</p><p>The mentoring proposal recognizes material barriers, but a check-in does not itself provide transport or resolve housing instability. Explain the referral and follow-through process, who can provide assistance, and how the team would know that support was usable.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct the quantitative interpretation supporting the instructional proposal.</li><li>Explain why the selected strategies fit the supported need and which concerns they leave unresolved.</li><li>Specify the route from identifying a barrier to obtaining assistance and the responsibilities requiring agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.6: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.6 reflects concrete, connected strategies and school-goal alignment. A more accurate rationale and a complete account of how support reaches students would strengthen the contextually responsive Level 4 case.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tExplain how your potential strategies address the equity gap described in your problem statement.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why each proposed strategy is expected to address the problem statement. Describe the mechanism: what would change in students' access or experience, and how that change relates to the identified need. Use relevant evidence to justify the expectation without presenting a hoped-for outcome as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Explain how strategies respond to the identified problem and supporting findings.</li><li>Keep the mechanism of improvement relevant to the selected group and school.</li><li>Distinguish the proposal's rationale from demonstrated implementation results.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"95\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1461\"><p>[My first potential strategy addresses the equity gap in the problem statement with the idea of a cadre of ELD teachers that provides them opportunity to learn collaboratively under the guidance of the Bilingual Coordinator. It is important to invest in the professional capacity of ELD teachers because they are the key educational partners who engage with EL students every day. The way they set up their classrooms, interact with students, and scaffold and differentiate lessons is what communicates to students that they are cared for and considered; that they are seen and they matter. Lacking these qualities in ELD classrooms would contribute to the disengagement and ultimate disconnect of EL students, resulting in chronic absenteeism. For EL students to value their education, ELD teachers must be better prepared - this is a challenge not unique to High School P. The research that supports investing in this type of ELD teacher professional development and how it improves EL student engagement was written by L.Santibanez and P.Gandara who studied the “scarcity of fully authorized teachers” to teach EL students. In their qualitative work they found that “the best way to “to improve their teaching of ELs [teachers] would benefit from coaching, observing and collaborating with other teachers (PLCs).” (2018) The benefits of the ELD teacher cadre will not just be improved instruction, but more engaged EL students with proficient attendance.   \t</p></div><h4>Mentoring rationale</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"95\" data-start=\"1461\" data-end=\"2943\"><p>The second potential strategy that addresses the equity gap in the problem statement is the creation of a mentoring and well-being check-in program. It will maximize on the large human capital that exists at High School P in the large out of class support personnel such as coordinators, counselors, itinerants, and assistant principals. The PSA counselor can develop a feasible and powerful Tier 3 intervention program that can provide EL students access to socioemotional support for those students dealing with traumatic life challenges and encourage them to continue to push through on the toughest days. Mentors have the potential to build trusting relationships with students and make an impact on their educational outlook. In addition, mentors can help identify the specific needs students and their families may have in a timely manner. This allows for resources and immediate supports to be provided to ensure students are safe and able to attend school consistently and reduce chronic attendance. The research that supports this approach was conducted by R. Balfanz and V. Byrnes in New York City where they found that “In the 25% of schools with the greatest impacts, chronically absent students supported by Success Mentors gained, on average, more than a month of school.” (2013) When students feel supported they are motivated and more willing to keep trying in the face of adversity. By building these connections, the attendance gap for EL students will improve. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response connects teacher collaboration to instructional experience and mentoring to relationships and assistance. These are plausible mechanisms, but their effectiveness depends on whether the proposed changes reach the students concerned.</p><p>The mentoring quotation describes results in the highest-impact quarter of schools. Even if the original study is verified, that result is not an expected effect for High School P. Explain the conditions that support transfer and the differences that limit it.</p><p>Several sentences promise attendance improvement. Replace that certainty with an explanation of the intended pathway and the evidence that would show whether it is working.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the proposed change in student experience for each strategy.</li><li>Verify and contextualize the research, including the restriction to the highest-impact schools.</li><li>State attendance improvement as an outcome to examine and distinguish relational encouragement from material assistance.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.6: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.6 recognizes a relevant rationale. The case for 4 remains limited by uncertain local fit and overstated outcomes; additional research quotations alone would not resolve those issues.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tExplain how your potential strategies for improvement address or take into account the potential contributing factors—including institutional and/or structural factors—and how they align with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Make two connections for each strategy: explain which contributing factor it addresses and how it aligns with a specific school commitment. Show the chain from finding to factor to action to school goal. Explain any important factor the chosen strategies do not address.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Connect strategies to the findings and institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Explain alignment with specific elements of the school's vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Show why the approach responds to this school's equity issue and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"97\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1712\"><p>[Based on my equity gap analysis, a potential strategy that addresses Contributing Factor #1 is to create an ELD Teacher cadre committed to developing their teaching skills to improve the quality of instruction in ELD classes. The goal would be to improve ELD instruction so that students can learn more effectively which will improve their interest in the value of their education, inspire them to reengage in school, and lessen chronic absenteeism. Better trained ELD teachers will ensure that students have access to rigorous and competent learning experiences. Based on the quantitative data from my equity gap analysis, there is a significant lack of improvement in ELPI scores, and only 49% of EL students find learning fun according to the school’s SES survey. This implies that ELs are not learning adequately or finding intrinsic value in learning when they come to school, so they opt to stay home to help their families or find jobs. This data was corroborated by the qualitative data from the ELD teacher interview who shared her struggles when teaching the Newcomer EL course and from the EL students who felt he was not treated well by his ELD teacher. These circumstances have contributed to the disproportionate chronic absentee rate because EL students feel disengaged and disconnected from school. The potential strategy aligns to the school’s Mission of fostering “a culture, which builds on the capacity of faculty and staff to serve all students.” The ELD teacher cadre demonstrates commitment to teacher growth and equitable, individualized learning. By improving the EL educational experience, more EL students will remain in school consistently and improve their attendance proficiency.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"98\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1807\"><p>Based on my findings in my equity gap analysis, my strategy addresses Contributing Factor #3 by creating a mentoring and well-being check-in program for chronic absentee students. The goal of the mentoring program would be to create a consistent relationship that builds a sense of belonging. By helping EL students feel seen and valued, they will be motivated to be in school more frequently or find necessary resources to alleviate their circumstances. The qualitative data in my equity gap analysis revealed that the chronic absentee rate is affected by a significant number of EL students who face socioeconomic barriers that make it challenging to be in school every day. The most prominent barriers being experiencing homelessness and unreliable public transportation. In addition, these stressors cause EL students to feel disconnected from their education. This was corroborated by the quantitative data from the school’s SES where an average of 40% of EL students participate in extracurricular activities offered at High School P. The majority of EL students are not a part of the greater school community and chronically absent EL students may not prioritize these opportunities when questioning having a place to sleep or hoping to get home safely. High School P can make an impact on EL attendance trends by investing in a mentor program. The recommended strategy aligns with the school’s vision which is to “provide a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve college and career readiness upon graduation.” Each mentor will be able to provide personalized attention to their assigned student and guide them through various academic and/or personal challenges. This individualized approach would illustrate High School P’s commitment to fulfilling its vision. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response explicitly links staff collaboration to capacity building and mentoring to personalized support. These are clearer alignments than a general assertion that a strategy promotes equity.</p><p>The survey does not establish that students find little value in learning and therefore choose work over school. That assumed chain weakens the factor-to-strategy connection. Use the actual reported instructional and access concerns.</p><p>The placement concern is not clearly resolved by either proposed strategy. Explain its status instead of implying that two programs address every factor in the inquiry.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>For each strategy, identify the finding, factor, proposed action, and specific school commitment.</li><li>Remove unsupported assumptions about students' motives.</li><li>Account for the placement concern and explain how mentors would respond to needs beyond their role or resources.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.6: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.6 includes a strength here: explicit school-goal alignment. More accurate factor connections and a clear account of the strategies' scope would strengthen the Level 4 explanation.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"4\"><span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 4 / Applying targeted feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDescribe the targeted feedback you received on each proposed strategy from your administrator, supervisor, or other key educational leader(s), and explain how you would adjust your proposed strategies to address the needs of the student group and inform equitable school improvement based on that feedback.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what feedback you received on each proposed strategy and what you changed, or would change, because of it. Explain the reason for each decision, including a recommendation you did not adopt. Keep the sequence visible so the reader can follow the proposal from feedback through revision.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Apply targeted feedback to strengthen the proposed strategies.</li><li>Explain appropriate adjustments and their implications for the group and school.</li><li>For Level 4, seek further feedback on revised strategies and develop a detailed communication plan across diverse educational partners.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"100\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"2022\"><p>[ \tRound 1:  I met with the school Principal at High School P and asked him for feedback on my strategies because he has been working with the PSAs for the last two years to improve chronic absenteeism at the school.  Strategy 1: The feedback was positive. The suggestions for improvement included making sure that the cadre uses ELPAC data to determine what language domains most EL students need support in. Also, to ensure that best practices for EL students are not just being developed in language courses, the principal recommended that the ELD cadre members collect evidence of the instructional strategies implementation and their results, so that at the end of the year they can put together a presentation for the entire faculty to create buy-in for a similar cadre of teachers that teach EL students in other content courses.  Strategy 2: The feedback was positive. The suggestion for improvement was to add a talking point in the check-in conversation guide about student interests in sports offered at school or possible clubs that may sound fun to them. The goal would be to connect the students with other trusting adults on campus to increase the sense of support and belonging.   \tAfter receiving feedback from the principal, I made the following adjustments: Strategy 1: Create an ELD Teacher cadre committed to developing their teaching skills to improve the quality of instruction in ELD courses. Based on the feedback I received, I will ensure that the Bilingual Coordinator has administrative access to the school’s Learning Management System (LMS) where she can create a Group for the cadre. In this digital space, the Coordinator can organize the instructional resources, lesson plans, and materials that are used and developed by the ELD cadre and invite the rest of the faculty to explore its contents. By promoting this at faculty meetings, hopefully other teachers can adopt EL strategies in their classrooms and facilitate access to improved instructional quality for EL students. Strategy 2: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"101\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"82\"><p>Create a mentoring and well-being check-in program for chronic absentee students. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"102\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"302\"><p>Based on the feedback I received, I will create a list of clubs at High School P with information on meeting times and locations. I will also create a list of sports teams, their season, and coach contact information so that mentors can easily reference the information when talking to their mentees.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"103\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1482\"><p> \tRound 2: I met with the school’s PSA counselor to share the adjusted strategies and received the following feedback: Strategy 1: This was well received and they mentioned that a lot of chronic absentee students who are facing socioeconomic challenges often think about finding a job. They recommended advising the ELD teacher cadre to frequently connect learning to work-based skills so that students find value in their learning and are more likely to be in school every day. Strategy 2: They mentioned that in the past, when these types of programs are in the initial stages of being implemented it may be a challenge to recruit the mentors because not all out of class personnel want to give up their work time. They suggested the possibility of expanding the opportunity to teachers. By giving teachers training and resources to mentor one of their students, there may be a possibility of having a larger reach. After receiving this feedback from the PSA I made additional adjustments to Strategy 1: I would enlist the support of the school’s Work Based Learning coordinator to support the cadre by offering her services of coordinating guest speakers and/or field trips that connect to any work related topics/skills that teachers may be covering. Strategy 2: I would coordinate the opportunity for the PSA and I to present the chronic EL absentee data to the entire faculty and the idea of the mentoring program. Together we would invite teachers to volunteer as mentors. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The principal-to-revision-to-PSA sequence is a substantial strength. It shows a second partner considering adjusted strategies, rather than merely two unrelated conversations.</p><p>The PSA's recruitment concern changes the assumed mentor pool and leads to a proposal to include teachers. Preserve this clear feedback-to-decision connection while recognizing that a wider recruitment pool does not establish participation.</p><p>Some other changes are less complete. The principal's recommendation about ELPAC domains and implementation evidence is not fully answered by creating an LMS group. Explain the actual analytical work the group would do and confirm the added coordinator's capacity.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each substantive recommendation to a decision, proposal change, and reason.</li><li>Explain how ELPAC domains and implementation evidence would guide the instructional work.</li><li>Distinguish completed proposal revisions from staffing, resources, and activities still awaiting agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.7: 4 / 5</h4><p>The overall 4 for Rubric 1.7 is plausible when this feedback sequence is read with C5's communication plan. It remains near the 3-to-4 boundary if the plan is insufficiently detailed or workable. A 5 would require the explicit bias-examination coaching described in the rubric.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"5\"><span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 5 / Building participation and anticipating implications</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tDescribe steps you would take to create school-level and community educational partner buy-in to the final adjusted set of proposed strategies. What potential implications for proposed strategies do you anticipate encountering as you address the equity gap at the school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how different educational partners would become involved in the adjusted proposal and what practical consequences or constraints you anticipate. Specify opportunities for people to influence the plan, not only receive an announcement. Connect likely implementation difficulties to the steps you would take to address them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Provide relevant steps for buy-in and communication.</li><li>Anticipate realistic implications of implementation at this school.</li><li>For Level 4, develop a detailed, workable communication plan involving diverse educational partners, alongside further feedback on revisions.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"841\"><p>[In order for the adjusted strategies to be implemented successfully and truly make a difference in the EL chronic absentee equity gap, I need to generate buy-in from educational partners across the school: Teachers:The Bilingual Coordinator and I would visit an English department meeting and present data on the EL chronic absentee equity gap to create a sense of urgency for intervention. The idea of the ELD cadre, how it addresses a contributing factor, and its goals will also be shared to enlist interested volunteers who will commit to the endeavor. The importance of achieving equitable outcomes for ELs will be emphasized. For the mentoring program, the PSA and I will present the same data at a faculty meeting to ensure all educational partners understand our efforts in recruiting their participation to reduce the equity gap.  </p></div><h4>Support personnel</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"841\" data-end=\"1748\"><p>Out of Class Support personnel: To enlist the participation of the Bilingual Coordinator and the PSA in leading the ELD cadre and the mentoring program I would offer my support as co-leader so that we can share in the planning responsibilities. I would emphasize a team approach so that together we make decisions about our collaboration in an effort to meet the school’s vision and extensive mission, which include providing a personalized educational experience that supports all students to achieve and building the capacity of our teachers to serve all students. These strategies are feasible because the Bilingual Coordinator and the PSAs may already be expected to implement these types of strategies as part of their job descriptions, but have not due to time constraints and other responsibilities. With coordinated support, they may be inclined to develop and implement the recommended strategies. </p></div><h4>Families</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"1748\" data-end=\"2291\"><p>Parents: In order to get parent involvement, the Bilingual Coordinator, PSA, and I would schedule a meeting after school for all ELD parents. We would mail out letters, send home flyers, program a mass phone call and email from the principal inviting them to attend and enjoy a light dinner. At this meeting we would also present the attendance data, explain the importance of being in school every day, present our proposals for the ELD cadre and mentoring program, and kindly ask them to ensure they send their students to school every day. </p></div><h4>Students</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"2291\" data-end=\"2919\"><p>Students: To create buy-in from students I would start by introducing myself to them during their ELD periods and let them know that I’ll be visiting them often to see what and how they are learning because their education matters to me. I would make sure to identify the chronic absentee students and make sure to acknowledge when they are present and encourage them to keep returning. For the mentees, I would ensure funding to provide tangible incentives that mentors can distribute when their students reach and maintain attendance goals. These can be things like donuts, pizza lunch, movie tickets, or school merchandise.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"106\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1417\"><p>In the culture and context of this school, in order to ensure buy-in, clear communication with all educational partners is essential so everyone understands the motives behind the strategies. Both strategies are designed as collaborative team efforts to ensure transparency, feasibility, and lasting student success.The following are possible implications of my proposed strategies. Challenging implications: There may be initial enthusiasm among educators participating in the ELD cadre and the mentoring program, but ongoing commitment may decline without strong accountability and incentives. There may also be inconsistent implementation of the work expected to be done. Some may engage deeply, while others not so much, which could leave uneven student experiences. In addition, educators tend to feel overburdened sometimes which may lead to dropping out of the programs. Positive implications: improved attendance for EL students as well as increased communication with families which will increase their access to valuable resources and build trust. The regular use of data fosters a culture of evidence-based decision making. In addition, the leaders of the programs can sponsor recognition events and incentives to foster a positive campus climate where EL students feel valued. Aligning school, home, and community efforts will improve attendance and ultimately help High School P close the equity gap.  ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response differentiates teachers, support personnel, parents, and students. It also recognizes workload and implementation concerns, giving the participation plan useful specificity.</p><p>Families and students are mainly asked to support a plan already selected. Asking families to ensure attendance sits uneasily beside the reported transport barriers. Include opportunities to identify what arrangements are usable and what the proposal has missed.</p><p>Time, confidentiality, and incentives need attention. A role description does not establish spare capacity, and attendance rewards can disadvantage students whose barriers remain unresolved.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify when each group can question or change the proposal and how its feedback returns to decisions.</li><li>Provide accessible participation arrangements suited to language and scheduling needs.</li><li>Explain how workload, confidentiality, recruitment, and unequal access would change the implementation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.7: 4 / 5</h4><p>Read with C4, the working estimate for Rubric 1.7 is 4. The differentiated plan and further feedback support that judgment, but the detail and feasibility of participation remain important weaknesses. The score is an estimate, not evidence that buy-in has already occurred.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tProvide your rationale for the administrator, supervisor, or educational leader feedback you chose to gather in Step 3 and how the feedback impacted your approach to building positive educational partner buy-in.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why you chose these feedback partners and how their feedback changed your approach to gaining participation. Connect the partner's relevant expertise to a specific recommendation, decision, and lesson for leadership. Praise for a leader's qualities does not by itself explain the effect of the feedback.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Explain the rationale for selecting feedback partners and its effect on the approach to buy-in.</li><li>Refer to specific evidence from the cycle.</li><li>For Level 4, show how school context affects the approach to equity leadership.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"115\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"2055\"><p>[The rationale for selecting the Principal of High School P was for this expert guidance on instructional development and effective attendance interventions. He has an extensive career in education and since he started at this school two years ago, his shared experience when making important structural changes have started to show significant improvements. It was important for me to share my proposed strategies with him because I trust his feedback. His input helped shape my approach to being an effective equity-driven leader because he models it. Through our conversations I was able to broaden my perspective and be more considerate of district and state expectations while remaining collaborative by utilizing a democratic and transparent approach when asking teachers to do more for our students. His feedback impacted my approach to building buy-in in the following way: he supported the idea of the ELD teacher cadre because this type of collaborative work allows for teacher autonomy and capacity building, which supports effective educational partner buy-in. Teachers buy-in to implementing change when they feel empowered, rather than directed. In addition, as the student-centered, data-driven leader he is, his recommendation to use ELPAC data to guide the objectives of the cadre and eventually determine a measurable outcome helped see how buy-in is gained when there is tangible evidence that justifies the work that is being proposed and it’s not just someone’s prerogative to create more work for teachers. The principal’s feedback on the mentor conversation guide to include talking points about clubs and sports impacted my approach to developing strategies by keeping student data in mind. Based on my use of the SES data on student connectedness to the school’s culture, he reminded me that quantitative data can be further understood when interacting with students, particularly during the well-being check-ins. Keeping students at the center of our work has proven to be an effective way of gaining educational partner buy-in. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"116\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1770\"><p>My rationale for selecting the PSA to provide me with feedback on the two potential strategies I identified in Step 3 was because of her expertise in supporting students with chronic absenteeism and her direct experience working with families experiencing socioeconomic challenges. Her feedback was important to me because I wanted to make sure my proposed ideas are feasible and realistic. Over the course of her career she has implemented many intervention strategies that were developed collaboratively among her colleagues at various schools and some were district directives. In that time she has learned what approaches yield higher educational partner buy-in. Her feedback shaped my approach to building buy-in in important ways. For the ELD cadre she recommended that teachers keep in mind the importance of highlighting work-based skills that students can develop in each lesson. This led me to make a connection with an important resource at High School P; the new Work-Based Learning Coordinator (WBLC). By connecting the WBLC and the ELD cadre more teachers will buy-in to the idea of participating in the cadre’s work because they will feel supported by an additional person who can enhance their efforts. For the mentoring program, she noted potential challenges when recruiting out of class support personnel and suggested giving teachers the opportunity to be mentors as well. This feedback impacted my approach to developing buy-in by presenting the attendance data and the well-being mentoring program to the entire faculty to recruit volunteers. I realized that by expanding this opportunity, buy-in is strengthened when participation is distributed more broadly, making this feel like a shared responsibility rather than the job of certain people. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The principal contributes instructional expertise and the PSA contributes attendance and feasibility expertise. These are relevant reasons for seeking their perspectives.</p><p>The most convincing reflective evidence is a changed assumption: the recruitment concern led to a broader proposed mentor pool. Explain what that taught the writer about testing capacity and involving people who understand the work.</p><p>The account sometimes shifts from a revised plan to an implication that buy-in is achieved. A decision to invite more people is not evidence of their agreement. Keep the reflection consistent with the revisions actually documented in Part C.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific recommendation and decision to explain the contribution of each partner.</li><li>Identify the assumption or leadership practice that changed.</li><li>Distinguish intended participation from actual agreement and reconcile the reflection with Part C.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.8: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.8 reflects a clear feedback rationale and evidence of learning. Stronger connections between school context, changed decisions, and future practice would support a more convincing 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tReflect on what you have learned, including strengths and areas for growth as an equity-driven leader. Then discuss and refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 as to how you addressed equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at your school.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you learned about equity leadership, identify strengths and areas for growth, and show how your work addressed this group's equity needs. Support the reflection with specific evidence from Steps 1, 2, or 3. Describe changes in your understanding and decisions, not only the activities you completed or traits you believe you possess.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Reflect on growth in addressing the selected group's equity needs and gaps.</li><li>Use specific cycle evidence to explain strengths and areas for growth.</li><li>For Level 4, demonstrate how social, economic, or cultural context affects the leadership approach.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"118\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"564\"><p>[During this cycle, I have learned the importance of approaching my work with a conscientious focus on equity-driven ideals. As I engaged in both quantitative and qualitative data collection, my initial instinct was to propose solutions and interpretations. However, through my course readings and reflections at each step, I learned to pause and take a step back. This allowed me to broaden my perspective to understand the social and cultural context of the school, learn from others, and develop more equitable approaches to bringing about school improvement.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"119\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1313\"><p>In Step 1 I learned the importance of analyzing various data sources to find connections that may help to deepen the understanding of a specific student group. I also learned that it’s important to find the “story” the quantitative data shows before engaging in gathering qualitative data because this research should serve to fill the gaps in the quantitative data. I found that I had to be strategic about the questions I wanted to ask each of my sources. I became mindful of being culturally sensitive with my inquiries based on the context of their realities. This helped me gain a clear understanding of the equity gap and its contributing factors. This equity gap analysis also helped me understand that this is a process an equity-driven school principal should implement regularly to truly understand student progress and the various systems within a school that sustain or prevent it. In addition, when I am in the role of school principal, I will be aware of the conversations I have with all educational partners as it is valuable qualitative data that can help me interpret quantitative data, and vice versa. The concerns, ideas, and proposals brought to me by teachers, students, and parents will inform my approach to building collaborative systems that bring about and sustain school improvement.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"120\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1116\"><p>In Step 2 I learned about the responsibility of an equity-driven leader to identify equity gaps and invest the time in analyzing the institutional and structural factors that produce them. Understanding the distinction between institutional and structural factors was also key in developing my proposed strategies. For example, at High School P there are many “commonly accepted and deeply ingrained norms, values, beliefs, systems, and practices” that have been in place for many years due to minimal staff and faculty turnover. The institutional factors have established a culture of long standing practices that I believe contributed to the limited attention given to the EL population over the years thereby sustaining the equity gap. With this in mind, I realized that effective improvement would require a paradigm shift, making it essential for me to develop buy-in strategies to enlist likeminded educational partners committed to change. On the other hand, understanding what structural factors are at High School P gave me a framework to utilize the potential resources available for a potential strategy. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"121\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"77\"><p>Something else I learned during Cycle 1 from my course readings, is the term </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"122\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"633\"><p>“solutionitis” which is used in the book Learning to Improve: How America’s Schools Can Get Better at Getting Better by A. Bryk et.al (2015). This term refers to the tendency of educators to jump quickly to implementing solutions without first deeply understanding a problem. I recognized that I have done this sometimes in the past, therefore, it became imperative for me to develop disciplined awareness of this and eliminate the habit. In my equity gap analysis I developed critical skills that will help me move away from this approach and focus on evidence driven inquiry as the framework for my approach to school improvement. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"123\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"935\"><p>In Step 3 I continued to implement what I learned from my course readings in Learning to Improve by A. Bryk et.al (2015) to narrow in on the contributing factors of the EL equity gap. Using a “Fishbone Diagram” I was able to list several key factors I thought were causing the problem which then created a more broader picture of the systemic issue. After this thorough analysis, I was able to identify two strategies that could address the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students. These strategies came from a deeper understanding of High School P’s culture and context, the feasibility of implementation, and research based evidence on their effectiveness. By incorporating feedback from my principal and PSA colleague, I learned that being an equity-driven leader also means being open to refining strategies, being transparent with educational partners, and ensuring there is buy-in for implementation of new interventions.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"124\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"545\"><p>Lastly, I will continue to use the skills I developed in this cycle in the future when I become a principal. I will refine these skills presently while engaging in PDSA cycles at my school because an equity-driven leader intentionally identifies and addresses the systems that sustain inequalities through data-informed, collaborative action. I will keep my actions student-centered and data-driven, remain neutral and open-minded when gathering qualitative data, and employ democratic processes to remain transparent when looking for buy-in. ] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"126\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"817\"><p>[Based on my experience in Cycle 1, what I learned about myself is that I rely on my background knowledge and experience to immediately attempt to solve a problem. I am now aware of my “solutionitis.” What really helped me come to terms with this was comparing my thought process with my principal’s. When I would speak to him about daily school issues throughout the period of this cycle, I paid attention to his behavior and choice of words when addressing the issues I brought to him. Being neutral, transparent, and always being mindful of employing a democratic process with decision making were traits I picked up on. Considering High School P is a Pilot School, these are essential to his success as our principal. I am now mindful about how I can emulate these traits as I grow into future leadership roles.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"127\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1876\"><p>What I also learned about my areas of strength is that I am an effective data analyzer, critical thinker, and collaborative team player. My ability to work with others shaped my proposed strategies to address the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students because I believe school can bring about effective improvements when faculty and all other educational partners work together rather than individually in classrooms or offices. Another strength is my ability to share my extensive experience in education to build capacity among my colleagues while at the same time remaining open-minded to learn from new experiences and perspectives. My collaboration with my principal and PSA colleague helped reshape my strategies and it was an essential reminder that a different perspective is what helps us learn fast, which is why collaboration should be the foundation of school improvement. These strengths can help me address the equity needs for EL students with a systemic, collaborative, and reflective approach. After learning the protocol of an equity gap analysis I plan to utilize those steps to continue to examine EL student data in other California state indicators. I’d like to see if EL students are experiencing equity gaps in graduation rates, college and career readiness, and SBAC proficiency scores at High School P. If I were to find more gaps, I would bring my findings to the school’s Governance Council so that the matters can be taken up by one of its committees. As the collaborative leader that I am at my school, I would attend the committee meetings and invite other educational partners so that together we create a shared ownership of the equity gap problem. I would ensure that strategic approaches to meeting the needs of the EL population arise from these committee meetings so that this subgroup doesn’t continue to fall through the cracks.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"128\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"443\"><p>This cycle also helped me recognize my areas of growth. I’ve already mentioned my tendency to jump to solutions, so I need to continue developing disciplined awareness of this habit and eliminate it. I also need to strengthen my ability to build a deeper understanding of my school’s existing systems and culture so that I validate the work that is already being done to bring about improvement, rather than blindly suggesting my strategies.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"129\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"551\"><p>In the process of addressing the equity gap among chronic absentee EL students, I have realized the need for me to be more involved in High School P’s governing systems so that I can work on developing my leadership style. I have begun attending the Governance Council meetings and the School Culture Committee as the first steps in learning more about how my school is being led and all the educational partners that are doing work to improve it. This cycle truly helped me understand the value in understanding the school’s culture and context.   ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The reflection recognizes a tendency to propose solutions too quickly and describes learning through feedback and collaboration. The two feedback rounds provide concrete support for collaboration as a strength.</p><p>The claim of effective data analysis needs qualification. Percentage-unit errors and unsupported subgroup inferences are consequential growth areas. A credible reflection can recognize a real strength while explaining an analytical limitation and how it would be addressed.</p><p>The qualitative evidence offers a deeper reflective opportunity: transport and housing accounts can challenge an explanation centered on motivation. Show how attending to that context changes a decision about support, rather than describing context without explaining its effect on leadership.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each claimed strength or growth area with an event, decision, or finding from the cycle.</li><li>Explain one initial interpretation that changed after considering another perspective.</li><li>Show how the school's context shaped an action and define the professional practice that needs improvement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.8: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.8 recognizes evidence-based learning and identified growth areas. Parts of the reflection approach Level 4, but the connection from context to changed leadership behavior needs greater precision.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 3 / Planning professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 1, identify specific professional learning goals for yourself and describe future steps for your professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Turn the learning from this cycle into specific professional goals and future steps. Identify the skill you need to develop, what you will do to develop it, and how you will judge your progress. A new committee role or another data project is a learning opportunity; explain the leadership growth it is intended to produce.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Identify future professional growth grounded in the cycle experience.</li><li>Explain clear strengths and learning needs using relevant evidence.</li><li>Connect future practice to equity leadership and the school's context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"131\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1735\"><p>[After the analysis of my experience in Cycle 1, a specific professional learning goal I have decided to undertake is to implement the equity gap analysis process in attendance rates with other vulnerable student groups at High School P, for example homeless and foster youth, Black students, and Student With Disabilities. My goal is to determine if these groups would also benefit from my proposed strategies, or if other contributing factors affecting their absenteeism would suggest alternative intervention strategies. My goal is to align my strategies with the school’s vision and mission, where all students are given a personalized educational experience. I am really curious to see if these different student groups have similar or different contributing factors. I think those findings could uncover a much deeper understanding of the systems currently in place at High School P. Another professional goal I have to continue my professional growth in leadership is to run for the school’s Governance Council so that I am privy to the institutional issues the school navigates and what the current practices are to address them. As a voting member of the council, I would be able to partake in decision-making discussions with colleagues and experience the democratic collaborative process of coming to a consensus when running a school. This experience would significantly help me develop my approach to building buy-in among colleagues and provide a forum where diverse perspectives are shared. This valuable experience can allow me to practice developing my ability to consider various perspectives before attempting to engage in problem solving. This would be crucial knowledge to bring to my future administrative roles. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"132\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1559\"><p>Lastly, I hope to lead my own PDSA cycle that is relative to my current position as a coordinator. I am responsible for leading a school committee that will implement a community school plan and the use of its budget to make the plan a reality. Although I am not in an administrative role, this responsibility to implement a schoolwide initiative requires me to truly tap into the critical thinking, collaborative planning, and buy-in development skills I have been exercising throughout this cycle. My first step is to plan the timeline for when the committee will meet for the rest of the school year. The time frame will consider deadlines for survey data review, program implementation goal setting, and outcome reflection before the end of the year. I will set monthly objectives based on the timeline and generate agendas with those objectives and expected measurable outcomes for review at each meeting. My goal is to be prepared for each meeting with data, necessary materials and information to make informed decisions, and always close the meetings with individual and collective action steps which we will report on in our next meeting. After each meeting, I plan to reassess our implementation plan to ensure our actions are aligned, then modify each meeting agenda if need be. I will know if I have been successful in leading this committee at the end of the year if we met the implementation plan’s goals and we have tangible evidence to show for it. I feel the work I did on this cycle has prepared me to do this work in my current position. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response proposes further equity analysis, governance participation, and leading a committee inquiry. The meeting timeline and reflection cycle make the intended next steps more concrete.</p><p>The goals would be stronger if each named the leadership skill being developed. Completing meetings or achieving a committee objective does not by itself demonstrate better interpretation of evidence or more inclusive decisions.</p><p>The analytical limitations in this cycle suggest a particularly relevant learning goal: checking indicator definitions and inferences with a knowledgeable colleague before extending the inquiry to new groups.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name the leadership skill, activity, timeframe, and feedback source for each goal.</li><li>Define evidence of improved practice, such as a corrected interpretation or a decision changed by another perspective.</li><li>Include quantitative interpretation and inquiry discipline among the learning needs supported by this example.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.8: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.8 is supported by identifiable professional goals and specific next steps. Stronger evidence connecting those goals to contextual leadership learning would improve the case for a higher level.</p></section></section></article><section id=\"version-alignment\" class=\"major-section\">\n<h2>Read the template and handbook together</h2>\n<p>This comparison uses the current Version 09 Parts A-D templates and Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide. The template organizes the response; the handbook supplies the task directions, evidence requirements, and scoring rubrics.</p>\n<div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Current template and handbook comparison\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Current template</th><th scope=\"col\">Current handbook</th><th scope=\"col\">What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part A organization</th><td>I: initial quantitative collection and group selection; II: qualitative collection; III: school commitments; IV: equity-gap analysis.</td><td>Uses the same sequence and explains the collection and analysis tasks on pp. 6-9.</td><td>The documents agree on the structure. Complete the collection tasks and use their findings in the five analysis responses.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Quantitative-data cross-reference</th><td>Part A IV.1 refers to quantitative data in \"Section II,\" although Initial Data Collection is Section I.</td><td>Repeats that cross-reference on p. 8; its initial quantitative collection is also Section I.</td><td>This is a shared internal inconsistency, not a disagreement between the documents. Retain the official prompt, address its quantitative-analysis task, and seek program clarification about the reference.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part B problem statement</th><td>Section II labels the problem-statement prompt as question 5, following four factor/need questions.</td><td>Places the problem statement under Section II on p. 13 without numbering it 5; p. 14 explains its purpose.</td><td>These refer to the same task. Use the template numbering and the handbook's explanation of a focused problem statement.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part C feedback</th><td>Question 4 asks about feedback on each strategy and the resulting adjustments.</td><td>The introduction on p. 17 explains that feedback should address appropriateness, feasibility, educational partners, and resources/services.</td><td>Use those considerations to prepare for feedback and explain the decisions that follow it.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part D reflection</th><td>Three prompts address feedback, learning with strengths/growth, and future professional learning. The written template allows up to five pages.</td><td>Uses the same three prompts on p. 20 and permits up to five written pages or five minutes of video explanation.</td><td>Answer all three prompts with specific cycle evidence. Consult the handbook when selecting the written or video format.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Locations are printed handbook page numbers. The cross-reference observation is instructional guidance, not an official CTC correction.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"A-sources\" class=\"major-section evidence-guide\">\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">Part A / Preparing the investigation</p><h2>Find the evidence before drafting</h2>\n<p>Start with the question: which student group is experiencing an unequal outcome, and what school conditions or access to support might help explain it? The CTC guide names four starting sources, five categories of quantitative evidence, and a range of qualitative approaches. Use the sources that answer your question; collecting every report is not an assessment requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>The required foundation:</strong> choose one of the six state indicators, analyze its school data across the three most recent years, identify one student group, collect and analyze at least three different qualitative sources, and connect the findings to school vision, mission, and/or goals. The guide directs candidates to select another indicator if three years of quantitative data are unavailable for the original choice. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-ctc\">CTC guide, printed pages 6-9</a>.</p>\n<h3>All six permitted state indicators</h3>\n<ul class=\"indicator-list\"><li>Chronic Absenteeism</li><li>Suspension Rate</li><li>English Learner Progress</li><li>Graduation Rate</li><li>Academic Performance</li><li>College/Career Readiness</li></ul>\n<p>The list identifies the choices for the focal indicator. Grades, climate surveys, staffing, and program participation can add relevant context; they do not replace the selected indicator and its three-year analysis.</p>\n\n<h3>All four starting sources named by CTC</h3>\n<div class=\"source-directory\">\n<section id=\"source-dashboard\"><h4>California School Dashboard</h4><p>Locate the selected school's indicator results and available student-group information. Keep the school identity, reporting year, measure, group, counts, and source notes with your working record. Compare actual measures across years and check reporting changes.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> establish the outcome pattern and explain the selected group. <strong>Part B:</strong> retain that same outcome concern while investigating factors; the pattern alone does not identify a cause.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Starting address: <code>https://caschooldashboard.org/</code>. School records may be needed to resolve missing information.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-lcap\"><h4>Local Control and Accountability Plan (LCAP)</h4><p>Obtain the district or charter school's adopted plan and relevant updates. Find the goal, student group, action, resources, and reported progress connected to the concern. The LCAP describes goals, actions, services, and expenditures. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-lcap\">CDE LCAP guidance</a>.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> compare the intended allocation and outcome with the findings. <strong>Part B:</strong> investigate whether a proposed problem fits existing priorities and resources. A planned action is not proof that support reached students.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Obtain the actual local plan from the district/charter website or office; a state template is not your school's evidence.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-eddata\"><h4>Ed-Data</h4><p>Find the school or district profile and inspect the available measures relevant to your chosen indicator and group. Preserve reporting years and source definitions, and reconcile any apparent difference with the original source.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> add useful comparison or context. <strong>Part B:</strong> use it to test a factual assumption before writing about resources or conditions. The same underlying state figures presented on two websites are not independent confirmation.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Starting address: <code>https://www.ed-data.org/</code>.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-sarc\"><h4>School Accountability Report Card (SARC)</h4><p>Obtain the school's available report cards and inspect the sections relevant to your inquiry. Record each report's publication date and the data year of the particular table; they may differ.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> use relevant school information to give the indicator analysis context. <strong>Part B:</strong> use documented conditions to frame questions about processes and resources, then verify present practice locally.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Look on the school/district website or request it from the school office. CDE's SARC starting address: <code>https://www.cde.ca.gov/ta/ac/sa/</code>.</p></section>\n</div>\n\n<section id=\"source-quantitative\"><h3>All five quantitative evidence categories in the guide</h3>\n<p>These are CTC examples of possible supporting evidence, not five additional required datasets. The examples below preserve the scope of the list on printed page 6.</p>\n<div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Quantitative evidence categories\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Category and CTC examples</th><th scope=\"col\">Use in Part A</th><th scope=\"col\">Follow-up for Part B</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Student performance: grades or promotion; end-of-course assessments; course completion; career pathway enrollment, completion, and dropout; interim/periodic assessments; graduation and college-going rates.</th><td>Clarify the learning pattern and its relationship to the selected indicator.</td><td>Investigate access to learning, pathways, or support. A result does not establish the quality of instruction by itself.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Student engagement: attendance and program participation, such as AP enrollment.</th><td>Compare relevant participation patterns by group and year.</td><td>Examine schedules, entry criteria, outreach, and access barriers rather than assume motivation.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Teacher qualifications: years teaching, time at the school, and assignment monitoring.</th><td>Describe relevant staffing context using appropriate records.</td><td>Examine support, continuity, or assignment processes. Do not infer an individual's competence from group statistics.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">School characteristics: average class size, demographics, and school budget.</th><td>Describe the population and relevant allocation of resources.</td><td>Check how allocation becomes usable support. A budget entry does not establish implementation or impact.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">School, teacher, and student/family/guardian culture: culture and climate data.</th><td>Investigate experiences of connection, safety, or support relevant to the indicator.</td><td>Compare perspectives and investigate a plausible mechanism without treating a perception measure as proof of cause.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n\n<section id=\"source-qualitative\"><h3>The qualitative sources and activities CTC describes</h3>\n<p>The guide names interview responses, observation notes, and document analysis as examples, and describes interviews, observations, and surveys as possible collection strategies. It also offers the four activities below. Select at least three different relevant sources and explain what each contributes; three websites repeating one dataset do not become three qualitative sources. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-ctc\">CTC guide, p. 7</a>.</p>\n<ol class=\"qualitative-options\">\n<li><strong>Observations and participant perspectives:</strong> observe in and around the school, including classrooms; use focus groups or surveys with students, teachers, families/guardians, and other representative educational partners.</li>\n<li><strong>Audits of processes and practices:</strong> examine teaching practices, academic intervention, course offerings, class schedules, family/guardian participation, professional development, or the school budget, including evidence of effectiveness related to the identified need.</li>\n<li><strong>Further educational-partner input:</strong> use surveys, interviews, or focus groups with leaders, teachers, students, families/guardians, or community members about strengths, interests, and needs, including those of the selected group.</li>\n<li><strong>Student school culture:</strong> examine course taking, extracurricular activities, discipline practices, academic and social environments, and student-engagement improvement efforts for opportunities related to the indicator and group.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The material and the analysis determine the evidence type.</strong> Numerical survey percentages are quantitative. Analyzed open responses, observation notes, and document passages may contribute qualitative evidence. Record actual findings and how you analyzed them, including differing accounts. Use appropriate access, participation, and confidentiality arrangements.</p>\n<p>The guide also calls for the school's vision, mission, and/or goals and describes checking the relationship between resource allocations and outcomes. It identifies additional inquiry such as student shadowing when evidence is insufficient. A local document can contain both numbers and narrative; analyze each for what it can establish. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-ctc\">CTC guide, p. 8</a>.</p></section>\n\n<h3>Additional sources for this inquiry</h3>\n<div class=\"source-directory\">\n<section id=\"source-wasc\"><h4>WASC materials</h4><p>Where available, request the latest school self-study, visiting committee report, schoolwide action plan, and subsequent progress report. WASC's progress guidance emphasizes examining implementation and evidence of effects on learning and well-being. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-wasc\">ACS WASC guidance</a>.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> locate relevant commitments and documented findings. <strong>Part B:</strong> examine whether a concern has already been identified, what response was planned, and what progress evidence exists. Read specific passages and their supporting evidence; the existence of a report or accreditation does not resolve the equity question.</p><p class=\"source-note\">An optional local source for this course, not a named requirement in the Cycle 1 guide. A school may not have WASC materials; use other relevant school plans and process evidence where appropriate.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-chks\"><h4>California Healthy Kids Survey (CHKS)</h4><p>CHKS addresses school climate and safety, student wellness, resiliency, and connectedness. Seek the available school/district report, administration year, participating grades, response counts/rates, and relevant item wording. CDE identifies companion staff and parent surveys within CalSCHLS. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-chks\">CDE CHKS guidance</a>.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> use relevant findings to frame questions about experience and access. <strong>Part B:</strong> examine those questions with local qualitative evidence. Preserve differences in populations and years; anonymous survey percentages cannot identify the individual students in an attendance table.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Optional supporting evidence. The sample uses a School Experience Survey; it is not identified as CHKS. Do not relabel it or present numerical CHKS results as the required qualitative sources.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-analyzer\"><h4>Equity Gap Analyzer</h4><p>Use the instructor's site to organize public evidence for the correct school: confirm the school and CDS identifier, examine the available report and timeline, and retain the actual years, values, counts, and source qualifications. Check important figures against their underlying sources and local records.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> identify a defensible pattern and questions for local inquiry. <strong>Part B:</strong> return to that pattern while investigating processes and educational need. An app-generated narrative is a starting point for analysis, not evidence that interviews occurred or that a proposed cause has been established.</p><figure class=\"analyzer-example\"><button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge saved Equity Gap Analyzer view\" title=\"Enlarge saved Analyzer view\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 1: Saved Equity Gap app school-selection screen, showing Jordan High and its CDS identifier\"><img src=\"assets/gap-app-pages/school.png\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1080\" alt=\"Saved Equity Gap app school-selection screen, showing Jordan High and its CDS identifier\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Saved October 6, 2026 public-app view. Jordan High is a separate illustration, not the High School P sample. The image is a dated reference, not a live connection.</figcaption></figure><p class=\"source-note\">Site address: <code>https://explorer.chrisboosalis.com</code>. This description uses the locally preserved app report and capture record. Live availability and interface were not verified in this review.</p></section>\n</div>\n\n<h3>A working source record</h3><p>For each source, retain its title, owner, date, exact page/table/item, population, measure, finding, limitation, and the Part A or B question it helps answer. For numerical comparisons, retain the year and denominator. For qualitative material, retain the analyzed passage or observation and the interpretation. Keep source evidence separate from your own inference.</p>\n<section class=\"source-citations\" aria-label=\"Source notes for the evidence guide\">\n<h3>Source notes</h3>\n<p id=\"source-note-ctc\"><strong>CTC:</strong> supplied <cite>CalAPA Leadership Cycle 1 Assessment Guide</cite>, Version 09, copyright 2026, printed pp. 6-9 and 13-14. All four named starting sources, all six indicators, all five quantitative categories, and the qualitative examples above were checked against that local guide. These are the guide's examples, not an exhaustive list of every acceptable source.</p>\n<p id=\"source-note-lcap\"><strong>CDE:</strong> <cite>Local Control and Accountability Plan (LCAP)</cite>, checked October 9, 2026. Source address: <code>https://www.cde.ca.gov/re/lc/</code>.</p>\n<p id=\"source-note-wasc\"><strong>ACS WASC:</strong> <cite>Reflections: Effective Mid-cycle and Other Progress Reports</cite>, Spring 2021, checked October 9, 2026. Source address: <code>https://acswasc.org/writing-effective-school-reports/</code>. The specific suggested uses in Parts A and B are instructional applications.</p>\n<p id=\"source-note-chks\"><strong>CDE:</strong> <cite>California Healthy Kids Survey</cite>, checked October 9, 2026. Source address: <code>https://www.cde.ca.gov/LS/he/at/chks.asp</code>. The comparison and interpretation cautions are instructional guidance.</p>\n</section>\n<h3>Current Part A template</h3><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure><button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Part A template page 1\" title=\"Enlarge template page 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 1: Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-1.png\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" alt=\"Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1\"></button><figcaption>Part A template / page 1</figcaption></figure><figure><button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Part A template page 2\" title=\"Enlarge template page 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 1: Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-2.png\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" alt=\"Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2\"></button><figcaption>Part A template / page 2</figcaption></figure></div></section><section id=\"limits\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>What you will submit</h2><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Part, Submission, Maximum length\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Submission</th><th scope=\"col\">Maximum length</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Data tables and equity-gap analysis</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages of responses</strong>, excluding quantitative and qualitative data tables</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Contributing factors and problem statement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Strategies, feedback, adjustments, and partner involvement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 written pages OR 5 minutes of video explanation</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These are limits for each complete part, not for each question. Submit one file per part. Written files may be DOCX, ODT, or PDF. Use the original templates with Arial 11-point type, single spacing, and one-inch margins. The original template text and your responses count toward the applicable limit, with the stated exclusion for Part A's data tables. The guide does not give charts a separate page-count exemption. Excess pages are not read or scored.</p>\n<p>First develop the evidence and reasoning each question needs; then revise for clarity and length. The teaching explanations and annotated examples on this page are longer than a candidate response. They are not a completed, page-fitted submission to copy. The Part D video option addresses the same three reflection prompts; it is not a recording of a meeting.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">CTC Cycle 1 Version 09: <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">preparing evidence, printed pp. 22-23</a>; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">submission specifications, printed pp. 24-25</a>. Part D permits asf, qt, mov, mpg, mpeg, avi, wmv, mp4, or m4v video files.</p></section><footer id=\"source-record\" class=\"sources\"><h2>Source notes</h2><p>Questions and requirements come from the current CalAPA Cycle 1 Parts A-D templates and Version 09 Performance Assessment Guide. Full rubric descriptors are reproduced below; explanations and critiques are instructional interpretations.</p><p>The CTC example was supplied for this local teaching review. Its response wording is retained. The group-selection interpolation and qualitative-table reconstruction are labeled. School records, cited research studies, and an official assessor score report were not independently obtained for the example.</p><p>Official template and handbook material copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing.</p></footer><section id=\"score-summary\" class=\"major-section\"><span id=\"final-check\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"rubric-map\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Estimated score profile</h2><p class=\"score-total\">Estimated total: 21 / 40</p><p>Each rubric is counted once across its relevant evidence. This arithmetic total is not an official score or passing determination.</p><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Rubric</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.1</th><td>3 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A1\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.2</th><td>2 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A2\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.3</th><td>2 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A5\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.4</th><td>2 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B1\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.5</th><td>2 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B5\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.6</th><td>3 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C1\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.7</th><td>4 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C4\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rubric 1.8</th><td>3 / 5</td><td><a href=\"#cycle/c1/D2\">Critique and reasons</a></td></tr></tbody></table></section><section id=\"rubric-1-1\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.1\" data-score=\"3\"><h3>Rubric 1.1: Quantitative inquiry</h3><p class=\"essential\">Based on the chosen California state indicator, how well does the candidate select and analyze quantitative data sources across the three most recent years, identify patterns and/or trends related to equity, and choose an appropriate student group?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not include quantitative data across the three most recent years. OR Patterns and trends are not identified or are irrelevant. OR Candidate does not select a student group to investigate OR selects a student group not appropriate for the identified indicator.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate selects a California state indicator* and minimally analyzes quantitative data, vaguely identifying patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate is not clear about which student group they will investigate or why the student group was selected.</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate selects a California state indicator* and analyzes quantitative data across the three most recent years, clearly identifying general patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate clearly identifies a student group to investigate further and provides a rationale for why this student group was selected.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-1\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explores additional data linked to the indicator that clearly informs their understanding of patterns and/or trends to further clarify group differences within the state indicator selected (e.g., demographic data, ethnicity, gender, language).</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant research that informs their understanding (patterns and/or trends related to equity) of the determined equity issue for the chosen student group.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">California state indicator: Chronic Absenteeism, Suspension Rate, English Learner Progress, Graduation Rate, Academic Performance, College/Career Readiness.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 10. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-2\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.2\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.2: Qualitative inquiry</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate collect and analyze relevant qualitative data from a range of at least three different qualitative* data sources and explain their relationship of the findings from the analysis to the quantitative** data findings and the identified student group equity issue?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not provide or analyze three qualitative* data sources. OR Candidate provides no or irrelevant information about the connection among qualitative and quantitative data** findings and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate collects and minimally analyzes a range of qualitative* data from at least three sources. Candidate minimally connects the qualitative data and findings to the quantitative data** findings for the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate collects and analyzes a range of relevant qualitative* data from three different sources. Candidate clearly explains the relationship among the findings from the analyses of the qualitative data, the quantitative data,** and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-2\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate collects additional qualitative data as appropriate to deepen their understanding of the California state indicator chosen and the student group equity issue, and provides a complete analysis of the relationship between their qualitative and quantitative data findings and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how their qualitative data collection strategy is responsive to the complex context in which they are working and demonstrates cultural sensitivity and an appreciation for diverse viewpoints.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Qualitative data include contextual evidence collected through methods such as interviews, focus groups, observations, and document analysis. Quantitative data express quantities, amounts, or ranges. See the full definitions on handbook p. 11.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 11. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-3\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.3\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.3: Equity gap analysis</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate conduct an equity gap analysis based on the chosen California state indicator to inform their understanding of the equity issues for the identified student group? How does this equity gap align with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue with no evidence of quantitative or qualitative data analysis. OR Candidate does not identify any patterns and/or trends in the equity gap analysis provided. OR Candidate does not provide a description of an equity gap for the student group. OR Candidate does not identify the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group based on minimal quantitative or qualitative data analysis. Candidate identifies patterns and/or trends that are not clear in the equity gap analysis provided or does not clearly describe the identified patterns and/or trends. Candidate provides a minimal description of an equity gap for the student group. Candidate minimally describes the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group clearly based on their quantitative and qualitative data analysis for the chosen state indicator. Candidate clearly describes patterns and/or trends across the quantitative and qualitative data analysis in the equity gap analysis provided. Candidate clearly identifies the alignment between the equity gap for the identified student group and the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-3\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate conducts a thorough equity gap analysis, describing a clear connection from quantitative data findings to supportive qualitative data findings, and provides a sophisticated understanding of the equity disparity identified for the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites and explains how research informs their understanding of the equity gap for the specific student group.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 12. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-4\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.4\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.4: Contributing factors</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to the identified equity gap affecting a student group and cite the research supporting their determination?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate identifies contributing factors that are biased, superficial, or irrelevant to the equity gap analysis. OR Candidate does not cite research and/or neglects to draw connections between research and contributing factors.</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate identifies potential contributing factors and minimally describes how they relate to the equity gap analysis. Candidate attempts to draw connections between research and contributing factors, but citations are not related to the equity gap.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly uses the equity gap analysis and findings from the quantitative and qualitative data analyses to determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to an equity gap affecting a student group. Candidate cites relevant research to support potential contributing factors that create or add to the identified equity gap affecting a student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-4\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains in detail (with supporting evidence from Step 1) how several contributing factors can create or add to equity differences or disparities for a student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate demonstrates a sophisticated, research-based understanding of the systemic, institutional, or structural causes of the identified single equity gap for a group of students at the school.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 15. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-5\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.5\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.5: Problem statement</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate use the equity gap analysis and identification of potential contributing factors to develop a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not use the equity gap analysis or potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement. OR Candidate’s problem statement is not responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate attempts to use the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement, but it is not clear how the problem statement supports student achievement and/or well-being of the student group. Candidate’s problem statement is only partially responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate develops a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being of the student group that clearly draws from the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors identified. Candidate’s problem statement is clearly responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-5\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains why their problem statement is responsive and feasible for the school’s culture and context.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant evidence-based practices or research on how the area of educational need has been addressed in other school settings to improve achievement and/or well-being for similar student groups.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 16. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-6\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.6\" data-score=\"3\"><h3>Rubric 1.6: Strategies and alignment</h3><p class=\"essential\">How are the strategy(ies) proposed for equitable school improvement for the student group well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis, including contributing factors, and responsive to the problem statement? How is the proposed strategy(ies) aligned to the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not propose strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement informed by the findings. OR Proposed strategy(ies) are not aligned with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate’s proposed strategy(ies) are minimally informed by the findings and provide only general reference to the equity gap analysis, contributing factors, and/or problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are partially aligned with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate’s proposed strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement for the student group are well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis and the contributing factors are responsive to the problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are clearly aligned with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-6\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides relevant strategy(ies) that strategically focus on equitable student and school improvement and represent a contextually responsive approach to addressing the equity issue or educational need.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides research-based evidence of the relevance of the proposed strategy(ies) and their implementation for improving student achievement and/or well-being for the specific student group and school.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 18. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-7\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.7\" data-score=\"4\"><h3>Rubric 1.7: Feedback and feasibility</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate apply the feedback received from administrators or educational leaders familiar with the school culture and context and describe next steps for creating educational partner buy-in and potential implications for the adjusted set of strategy(ies)?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not apply feedback from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen their proposed strategy(ies). OR Candidate states their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with little or no explanation of steps for buy-in from educational partner(s). OR Candidate does not identify anticipated implications for proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely describes feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader and makes irrelevant and/or inappropriate adjustments to proposed strategy(ies). Candidate vaguely describes their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with educational partner(s); it is not clear that educational partner(s) will have the opportunity to develop buy-in. Candidate does not clearly describe anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly applies feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen proposed strategy(ies). Candidate clearly provides relevant and appropriate next steps for creating buy-in and communicating with educational partner(s) to address the equity gap and learning need(s). Candidate clearly describes anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to the implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\" id=\"level4-7\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate seeks additional rounds of feedback from other administrators or educational partners on the revised strategy(ies) and develops a detailed strategic plan to communicate and share the plan with a diverse range of educational partners to ensure they are proposing a workable/feasible approach to addressing the equity gap and learning need.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate plans to coach educational partners to examine and address potential biases that could impact student learning and/or well-being due to identified equity gaps, including those specifically related to sources of education disadvantage or discrimination, and is transparent about the potential underlying contributing factors.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 19. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-8\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.8\" data-score=\"3\"><h3>Rubric 1.8: Leadership learning</h3><p class=\"essential\">How did the leadership feedback shape the candidate’s equity-focused strategy, build educational partner buy- in, and support their growth in addressing equity needs and gaps for the identified student group? How does the candidate reflect on their current strengths and identify future areas for growth in equity leadership?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not discuss or only mentions their selected administrator/supervisor/educational leader and/or the importance of educational partner buy-in is not discussed in the reflection. OR Candidate does not reflect on their growth as an equity-driven leader and/or does not address the equity needs or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site. OR Candidate does not refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. OR Candidate does not discuss strengths or areas for future professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate provides limited insight into why they selected the administrator/supervisor/educational leader for feedback and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in. Candidate vaguely reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and minimally discusses their ability to address the equity needs and/or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate does not clearly explain their current strengths and/or vaguely mentions future areas for growth in equity leadership.</p></section><section class=\"level estimated\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly explains their rationale for selecting the administrator/supervisor/educational leader to provide feedback on the selected strategy(ies) and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in from school personnel. Candidate clearly reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and discusses their ability to address the equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to specific, relevant evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate clearly explains their current strengths and identifies future areas for growth in equity leadership.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-8\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate’s reflection demonstrates how the school context— including social, economic, or cultural contexts— impacts their approach to providing equity-driven leadership.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate utilizes experiences from Steps 1, 2, and 3 in confronting equity issues and gaps to describe what leadership skills have been learned and how those skills may be used in the future.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 21. The highlight shows this review's estimate.</p></section></div>","search":[{"title":"Part A / I.1 / Data and student-group selection","url":"#cycle/c1/A-data","html":"<span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Data and student-group selection</h2><section class=\"question\"><span id=\"A-group\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tIdentify the one specific student group you have chosen to further investigate.</p><p class=\"task-directions\">Directions: Complete the following table. While this template reflects the categories found on the California School Dashboard, it is neither an exclusive nor a mandatory list of categories, and you may add or delete rows as needed.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Choose one state indicator, compare the school and its student groups across the three most recent years, and identify one group for further investigation. Make the table understandable: the measure, years, groups, and reporting populations should be clear. The short identification answer names the group; Part A IV.1 develops the reason for choosing it.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Reading the data accurately</summary><p>A rate is a count divided by the population eligible for that measure. Keep the numerator, denominator, reporting year, and definition together; enrollment may not be the reporting denominator. Subtract two rates to describe a percentage-point difference.</p><p>Compare both the group's trend and its difference from the comparison group. Schoolwide results include the selected group; overlapping group rows cannot be added. Annual results may involve different students, so they are not automatically a matched-cohort comparison. Missing or suppressed results are not zero.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Analyze three years of quantitative data for one state indicator.</li><li>Identify relevant patterns and explain the selected student group.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how additional data clarify group differences within the indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"11\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"694\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>California state indicator (e.g., academic performance):</p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p> [Chronic Absenteeism Rate  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>LAUSD Whole Child Integrated Data </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year </p><p> 24-25 </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Second Most Recent Year 23-24 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent </p><p>Year </p><p>22-23 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>All Students </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>33.78% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>36.39% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>35.91% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>51.09%  of 167 (53% </p><p>NC) </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>51.71% of 170 (59% </p><p>NC) </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>47.27% of 162 (56% </p><p>NC) </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Socioeconomically Disadvantaged </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>36.53% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>38.23% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>40.03% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Students with Disabilities </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>40.61% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>45.56% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>48.08% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Asian </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>20% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>14.29% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>11.54% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>African American </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>42.86% (of 23 students) </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>58.62% (of 24 students) </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>53.85% (of 25 students) </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Filipino </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>20% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>24.14% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>25.71% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Hispanic </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>33.66% </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>36.79% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>36.78% </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"13\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"236\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>SES, Academic Focus - F. My teachers make learning fun </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year </p><p>2024-2025 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Second Most </p><p>Recent Year </p><p>2023-2024 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent Year </p><p>2022-2023 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>All Students </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>46% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>41% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>37% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>50% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>53% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>45% </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"15\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"278\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>SES, Opp.For Part. - D. I participate in extra-curricular activities offered through my school.  </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year </p><p>2024-2025 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Second Most </p><p>Recent Year </p><p>2023-2024 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent Year </p><p>2022-2023 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>All Students </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>49% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>46% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>52% </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>37% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>35% </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>48% </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"17\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"509\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Student English </p><p>Language </p><p>Acquisition </p><p>Results </p><p>Summative ELPAC - ELPI scores (Source: CA Dashboard) </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Most Recent Year 2024 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Second Most </p><p>Recent Year </p><p>2023 </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Third Most Recent </p><p>Year </p><p>2022 </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>ALL English Learners </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>51% Progressed One </p><p>ELPI  Level </p><p>44.4% Maintained ELPI </p><p>Level </p><p>4.6% Decreased one </p><p>ELPI Level </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>46.7% Progressed </p><p>One ELPI Level </p><p>46.2% Maintained </p><p>ELPI Level </p><p>7.1% Decreased one </p><p>ELPI Level </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>45.6% Progressed </p><p>One ELPI Level </p><p>41.1% Maintained </p><p>ELPI Level </p><p>12.8% Decreased one ELPI Level </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><section class=\"interpolated-passage\"><h4>Student-group response</h4><p class=\"source-note\">Interpolated teaching response based on the CTC example.</p><blockquote class=\"interpolated-response\"><p>I have chosen English Learners (EL) at High School P as the one student group I will further investigate. My selected California state indicator is Chronic Absenteeism. The three years of attendance data show a consistently higher chronic absenteeism rate for EL students than for all students at the school.</p></blockquote></section></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The CTC example supplies a useful foundation: three annual attendance comparisons and a clearly selected EL population. The annual differences matter more than a rounded three-year average. The EL rate falls slightly in the final year, yet the gap widens because the schoolwide rate falls more.</p><table class=\"audit comparison-table\"><caption>Annual differences calculated from the CTC example</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>EL students</th><th>All students</th><th>Difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2022-2023</td><td>47.27%</td><td>35.91%</td><td>11.36 percentage points</td></tr><tr><td>2023-2024</td><td>51.71%</td><td>36.39%</td><td>15.32 percentage points</td></tr><tr><td>2024-2025</td><td>51.09%</td><td>33.78%</td><td>17.31 percentage points</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The table compares EL students with all students, a group that includes EL students. It is not an EL-versus-non-EL comparison. The enrollment counts also need to be checked against the population eligible for each reported measure; an apparent mismatch is a reason to investigate definitions, not proof that a rate is wrong.</p><p>The supplementary survey and language-progress tables can inform the inquiry only when their measures are interpreted correctly. A survey's remaining percentage may include neutral responses; maintaining an ELPI category does not mean a student learned nothing.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Report the annual differences in percentage points and explain both the persistent disparity and the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Identify the year, population, denominator, and source for each measure; reconcile apparent inconsistencies.</li><li>Keep the selected group consistent and explain the contribution of the additional measures in Part A IV.1.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.1: 3 / 5</h4><p>The tables and accompanying narrative establish a three-year pattern, relevant group, and rationale, supporting the overall 3 for Rubric 1.1. The additional measures are not connected clearly enough to the attendance disparity to support a confident 4. The interpolated identification sentence is excluded from this estimate.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II / Qualitative evidence table","url":"#cycle/c1/A-qualitative","html":"<span id=\"qualitative-analysis\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II / Qualitative evidence table</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">Directions: Identify each of the three qualitative sources you have selected, and record and analyze the qualitative data you have collected from each source.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record what each qualitative source revealed and explain what it contributes to understanding the selected group and indicator. A source is the person or material; an interview is a method; a finding is what was learned; analysis explains its significance and limits. Listing interview participants completes only the first part of this task.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Turning sources into findings</summary><p>Choose sources that can illuminate the selected population's experiences and the school conditions related to the indicator. Ask about concrete events: what happened, what support was available, whether it was usable, and what happened next. For documents, compare written expectations with available evidence of follow-through.</p><p>Record each source's contribution before comparing accounts. Look for recurring themes, differences, and counterexamples. A theme is an interpretation supported by evidence, not a count of interview labels. Explain whose experience is represented and what remains unknown; one account does not establish how common a condition is.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Collect and analyze at least three different, relevant qualitative sources.</li><li>Explain relationships among their findings, the quantitative data, and the equity issue.</li><li>For Level 4, deepen understanding with additional qualitative data as appropriate and provide a complete connected analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"21\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"168\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Data Sources </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Qualitative Data </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>ELD Teacher </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview  </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>EL Chronic Absentee/Student </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>PSA </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>ELAC Parent Member </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Interview </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The four perspectives are relevant, but the findings cells are empty. This makes it difficult to see what each source contributed. The narrative later supplies findings, so the problem is incomplete presentation and analysis rather than evidence that no interviews occurred.</p><h4>How to complete the findings column</h4><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching reconstruction from findings already reported in the CTC example narrative.</p><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Qualitative findings and their contribution\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Reported finding</th><th>Contribution to the inquiry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELD teacher</th><td>Frequent enrollment interrupts instructional continuity and requires repeated review.</td><td>Investigate entry routines and instructional support; this is not evidence of teacher incompetence.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Student</th><td>The student reports a difficult placement experience and improved attendance after placement changed.</td><td>Examine the individual experience without treating it as a groupwide effect.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">PSA counselor</th><td>Unstable housing interferes with a student reaching school.</td><td>Examine attendance access and support; the account does not establish how common the barrier is.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELAC parent</th><td>Bus-route changes and less frequent service make travel to school more difficult.</td><td>Investigate transportation conditions and who is affected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The findings can be organized into two lines of inquiry: instructional continuity and placement, and access to school and attendance support. Comparing these accounts is analysis. Simply counting four interviews does not show how their findings relate or how widespread each experience is.</p><p>The attendance figures establish a group disparity. These accounts suggest conditions to investigate; they do not quantify how much of that disparity each condition produced. A strong response identifies both the contribution and the limits of each source.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Populate each findings cell with the actual evidence already described in the narrative.</li><li>Compare the accounts to identify themes, differences, and questions that remain unresolved.</li><li>Use a focused follow-up, where needed, to examine an unresolved condition rather than collecting another source merely to increase the count.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.2: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.2 reflects relevant collection but only partially supported analysis and connections. Completing this table would improve traceability; the related narrative must also explain the findings accurately to establish Level 3 and then the added depth of Level 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / III.1 / School vision and mission","url":"#cycle/c1/A-mission","html":"<span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / III.1 / School vision and mission</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tInvestigate the vision, mission, and/or goals at your school and document your findings.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Investigate the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals and document the commitments relevant to your selected population. Explain why those commitments matter for that group. This question establishes the school's intentions; Part A IV.5 develops the comparison between those intentions and the equity-gap evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Document specific school commitments that can inform the equity analysis.</li><li>Connect those commitments to the selected group and indicator in the Part A analysis.</li><li>Rubric 1.3 evaluates the integrated analysis across the relevant responses, including the thorough synthesis required at Level 4.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"6\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"771\"><p>[High School P’s vision is to provide a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve college and/or career readiness upon graduation. The school’s mission is to carry out the following: 1) Maintain high expectations and provide rigorous, standards-based, and real world learning opportunities for all students. 2) Provide a safe, personalized, and small-school learning environment for all students. 3) Foster a culture, which builds the capacity of faculty and staff to serve all students. 4) Provide community-based resources to students and parents with the objective of mitigating obstacles to student learning. 5) Create a culture where stakeholders are empowered and actively engaged to affect change within the school and community. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The CTC example identifies substantive commitments: personalized education, staff capacity, community resources, and participation. The next instructional step is to explain their relevance to English learners experiencing chronic absenteeism, rather than leave that connection implicit.</p><table class=\"audit mission-connections\"><caption>Connections to investigate for the selected EL population</caption><thead><tr><th>School commitment</th><th>Relevance to this population</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Personalized education</th><td>Appropriate placement and instruction for EL students with differing language-development needs.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Staff capacity</th><td>Support for teachers managing language instruction and continuous enrollment.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Community resources</th><td>Usable assistance with reported transportation and housing barriers.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Educational partner participation</th><td>Meaningful opportunities for EL students and families to influence school decisions.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These commitments guide questions about actual access. A promise of community resources, for example, makes the transportation account relevant to examine. The promise alone cannot establish that assistance reached students or that the school failed to provide it. Identify the source and date of the adopted wording, then use actual findings when making the later comparison.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source and date of the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Select the commitments most relevant to EL students' instructional experience and attendance access.</li><li>Develop the evidence-based comparison in Part A IV.5, distinguishing intended provision from students' reported experiences.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>This passage supplies useful context. The overall 2 for Rubric 1.3 comes from weaknesses in the combined equity analysis, not from treating a mission quotation as a separately scored answer. The later responses must connect these commitments to a well-supported explanation of the disparity.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap","url":"#cycle/c1/A1","html":"<span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tReflecting upon the data collected and analyzed, what supports your decision to select this specific equity gap for your identified group of students? Share your analysis for the specific quantitative data you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template and your rationale for collecting it. How are these data relevant to understanding equity issues at the school for student groups?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why this student group and equity gap deserve investigation. Interpret the three-year quantitative pattern, explain why you collected the additional numerical data, and show what those data contribute to understanding equity at the school. A list of percentages is incomplete without the reasoning that connects them to your choice.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/version-alignment\">Template and handbook cross-reference note</a>.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Explain the three-year pattern and the rationale for selecting the group.</li><li>Show how additional quantitative findings clarify the indicator and group differences.</li><li>Distinguish observed patterns from explanations the available data do not establish.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"25\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1585\"><p>[The California indicator I chose to investigate is Chronic Absenteeism. The first data table presents chronic absenteeism rates over the course of 3 years for all students and various demographic groups.The average chronic absentee rate of all students at High School P is 35% which will serve as the baseline to compare the different student groups to determine the equity gap. To start, English Learners’ (EL) three year average rate is 50%, Socioeconomically disadvantaged students have 38%, Students with disabilities are at 45%, and Asian students have the lowest average at 15%. The African American student population has a rate of 51%, Filipino students have a 23% rate, and lastly the Hispanic group is at 36%. The pattern that stands out on this chart is that most of the subgroups improved their attendance from 2022-2023 to 2024-2025. There were small reductions in the numbers (improvement), with the exception of the EL, Asian, and African American subgroups which had an increase in the second year, but for the most part the data shows improvement when comparing the second year to last school year’s data. When comparing the average chronic absenteeism rate for all students (35%) to the average of the EL student (50%) there is a 15% equity gap. Furthermore I’m noting that there was no improvement over the course of the last three years, in fact it worsened. My rationale is that because the EL population is a larger group at High School P (~166 students) and because the equity gap has persisted, the focus group I will continue to analyze will be the EL group. </p></div><h4>Additional quantitative evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"25\" data-start=\"1585\" data-end=\"3359\"><p>To further understand this disparity over the past three years, I explored additional data points from the School Experience Survey (SES), and the California Dashboard English Learner Progress Indicator (ELPI) scores. I wanted to look at additional data to understand potential causes that have sustained the equity gap. The second chart presents data from the Academic Focus section of the SES, which asks students if their teachers make learning fun. I wanted to see if EL students find learning enjoyable, which is usually a major reason why students consistently attend school. The data shows that EL students at High School P are enjoying learning with their teachers at a much higher average rate of 49% when compared to the general population where only 41% think their teachers make learning fun. Although more of the EL population finds learning fun when compared to all students, it is possible that the remaining 51% are those struggling to be in school every day. The third chart is from the School Climate section of the SES and it presents the percentage of involvement students have in extracurricular activities. At the secondary level, students who are involved with sports and clubs often demonstrate proficient attendance to maintain competition eligibility or they are held accountable for certain club projects and responsibilities. I wanted to see if EL students participate in school groups that keep them accountable for their attendance. The data shows that ELs are less involved in these experiences at an average rate of 40% compared to the general population at 49%. This low number of involvement in the school culture may affect the connectedness ELs have with school in general which may limit the inherent interest of being in school daily.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"26\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1434\"><p> \tBecause chronic absenteeism has a negative impact on academic performance, I analyzed language acquisition progress. The fourth chart presents ELPI data from the state’s dashboard which indicates that on average only 48% of ELs are demonstrating improvement in their English language acquisition. This is significant because half of High School P’s EL population are Newcomers who are at the initial stages of English language acquisition and may still be adjusting to housing situations, culture, and possibly overcoming trauma thereby limiting their attendance and academic success. Newcomers may represent the other half of ELs that are showing limited language skill progress. It is possible that EL students get discouraged and lose interest in school when their language acquisition development is not properly supported and begin to fail academically which may cause the chronic absenteeism, but this could be interchangeable where EL students are not developing their language skills because of socioemotional or economic factors that limit their attendance. My findings align with current research that states, “The existing literature establishes a clear link between absenteeism and student performance (Liu et al., 2021).” My rationale is that it is most likely that half of chronic absentee EL students are the Newcomer subgroup who are struggling to acquire language and adjusting to a new school in a new country.  ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response names Chronic Absenteeism, selects EL students, and explains why supplementary measures were examined. Those are useful parts of a rationale. Its description of the trend needs greater precision: the annual differences widen from 11.36 to 15.32 to 17.31 percentage points, while the EL rate improves slightly in the final year.</p><p>The statement 'there is a 15% equity gap' confuses percent with percentage points. More consequentially, the response suggests that the remaining 51% of survey respondents are the absent students, and that half of chronically absent EL students are newcomers. Separate aggregate percentages do not establish either overlap.</p><p>The survey and language data may suggest questions about school experience. Explain that contribution without treating them as proof of who is absent or why. The research citation concerns a broad attendance-performance relationship; it does not resolve these local measurement and inference problems.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use annual percentage-point differences and acknowledge the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Remove unsupported claims that identify absent students from separate survey or newcomer percentages.</li><li>Explain how each additional finding changes the interpretation of the attendance disparity and identify what remains uncertain.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.1: 3 / 5</h4><p>An overall 3 for Rubric 1.1 is plausible because the indicator, three-year pattern, group selection, and rationale are evident. A 4 needs additional data that actually clarify the indicator differences; the unsupported connections in this response limit that case.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources","url":"#cycle/c1/A2","html":"<span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tDescribe the three different qualitative data sources you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template. How did these sources provide more information about this student group for the California state indicator?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>For each qualitative source, explain the perspective it provides, what you learned, and how that finding adds to your understanding of the selected group and indicator. Explain why the source was useful. A biography of each participant or a list of interviews does not show the contribution of the evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Describe at least three different qualitative sources and analyze their relevant findings.</li><li>Explain what each source adds to understanding the group and state indicator.</li><li>Use the findings together to deepen the inquiry, with further qualitative evidence as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1790\"><p>[I strategically chose to interview the following four qualitative sources to understand the equity gap for EL students. They are: ELD Teacher, EL chronic absentee student, PSA counselor, and an EL Parent who is a member of the EL Advisory Committee. The first interview was with an ELD teacher who is new to the profession and only teaches the Newcomer course which is the first course in the EL Master Plan to help ELs acquire the English language. All newcomer enrollees are placed in her class throughout the year quite frequently and she expressed how challenging it is to teach a course where there are sometimes more than 2 students being added to her classes almost every other week. Her goal is to help all students feel welcomed and included, but with new students being constantly added, she doesn’t feel there is continuity in her lessons because she has to pause or review objectives constantly. She also notices the inconsistent attendance in her class. Some of her Newcomer students are out due to doctor visits for vaccinations, their parents are ill so students must take on part-time jobs, and some come with traumatic pasts in their home country and don’t have stable housing. The teacher’s responses helped me understand that EL students struggle with developing a connection to school because many deal with significant outside factors that keep them away from school.Her experience also speaks to what Bryk, Gomez, et al highlight, “The task of managing heterogeneous classrooms is a key teaching problem, and classrooms have clearly become more complex environments (62). This systemic issue is affecting the quality of instruction and may have a detrimental effect on Newcomer’s language acquisition rate, which relates to their academic performance and attendance. </p></div><h4>Student perspective</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"1790\" data-end=\"2629\"><p>I also interviewed a recently reclassified EL student who was a chronic absentee. He shared that the reason he missed most of last year is because he didn’t like his teachers or classes. Although fluent in English, he remained classified as EL (identified in a public elementary school) due to not being reclassified at his private middle school. Upon entering High School P he was placed in ELD courses, which left him depressed and disengaged. With support from the EL Coordinator, he reclassified at the end of last year and was programmed into Mainstream English. He now attends regularly, enjoys school, and has a more positive view of his teachers and classes. What I learned is that timely reclassification is essential, preferably before high school, because course misplacement causes frustration, disengagement, and absenteeism. </p></div><h4>Counselor perspective</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"2629\" data-end=\"3276\"><p>The third interview with the PSA counselor helped me understand how issues of parent drug addiction and homelessness lead to chronic absenteeism among EL students. She shared a story of how a student and his parents were living in a car. There had been times when the student woke up in the back seat and found himself in another city, nowhere near the school. This went on for a while until the PSA found out and reported the situation to DCFS and the child went into the foster system. The PSA shared that EL students find it hard to maintain connectedness with school when they are experiencing traumatic events that cause chronic absenteeism. </p></div><h4>Parent perspective</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"28\" data-start=\"3276\" data-end=\"4021\"><p>My final interview was with an EL parent on the EL Advisory Council (ELAC). This parent brought up a concern about recent bus route changes that had an impact on students. Previously, two routes serviced the school’s neighborhood with buses picking up every 15 minutes. Now there is only one route making stops further away for some and less frequent buses often leaving students stranded or forced to wait longer than expected to board a bus. Many families cannot afford private transportation so some students stay home rather than walking 3-5 miles to school. This parent’s community insight amplified my understanding about how transportation barriers can disproportionately affect EL students and directly contribute to their absenteeism.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher, student, counselor, and parent provide distinct perspectives. The teacher describes continuous enrollment; the student describes placement; the counselor and parent describe access barriers. These findings make the source choices understandable and provide more than a list of methods.</p><p>The response becomes less convincing when one student's experience becomes a general claim that course misplacement causes absenteeism. Attribute the experience to that student and explain what it makes worth investigating. Similarly, the teacher's account supports examining instructional continuity, not assuming inadequate competence.</p><p>The strongest explanation would show how the accounts complement or challenge one another. It should also describe relevant collection arrangements and limitations, while including only the personal details needed to understand the finding.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Organize each source around its perspective, finding, contribution to the attendance inquiry, and limitation.</li><li>Qualify the placement conclusion as an individual account and identify the further evidence needed for a broader claim.</li><li>Explain how sources were selected and how collection arrangements supported relevant, accessible participation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.2: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.2 recognizes substantive findings but incomplete analysis of their relationships and limits. A clearer explanation of each source's contribution and the connections among them would strengthen the Level 3 foundation.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings","url":"#cycle/c1/A3","html":"<span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tWhat patterns and/or trends related to the California state indicator and student group equity issues did you find in the qualitative data? How do they relate to the quantitative data patterns and/or trends you identified?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Move from individual accounts to patterns across the qualitative evidence. Identify which sources support each pattern, then explain how it relates to the numerical trend. Include evidence that complicates your explanation. The task is to connect different kinds of evidence, not repeat every interview or assume that all sources confirm the same story.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubrics 1.2 and 1.3</h3><ul><li>Rubric 1.2: analyze relationships among qualitative findings and quantitative patterns.</li><li>Rubric 1.3: integrate the findings into a clear understanding of the group's equity issue.</li><li>A stronger synthesis explains both supporting evidence and the limits of the proposed interpretation.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a> / <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"30\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"862\"><p>[The first major pattern and trend that emerged from the diverse viewpoints from the qualitative data is that English learners at the school are not making sufficient progress towards becoming proficient in acquiring English language skills. Even after three years of ELD instruction, many students at High School P still struggle with developing enough English communication skills to reclassify or to successfully pass other content courses. The teacher I interviewed shared her experience teaching the Newcomer course and described how students are not learning English at an appropriate pace. This is also reflected in the quantitative data from the ELPI scores which indicate that only 48% of EL students have advanced in their proficiency levels over the past three years. This means that 52% of EL students are not improving on their proficiency levels.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"31\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"598\"><p> \tA second pattern and trend that emerged from the qualitative data is that EL students don’t feel connected to the school. Many of the Newcomer students have expressed their inability to feel connected to their new school because they can’t communicate with most of their teachers and the majority of the school population. In addition, LTEL students who feel stuck because they have not reclassified before high school often develop low self-esteem and begin to disengage. This might explain why only 40% of EL students are engaged in extra curricular activities as indicated in my third chart.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"32\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"730\"><p> \tA third pattern and trend that emerged from the qualitative data is that many of our EL students are facing significant challenges stemming from their socioeconomic status. Both the PSA counselor and ELAC parent in their interviews mentioned that many of our EL students don’t have reliable transportation and experience homelessness, which in turn impacts their attendance. EL students who are dealing with these traumatic circumstances are more than likely focused on getting by rather than being in school every day, hence the disengagement and absenteeism. This is also reflected in the quantitative data from my first chart that indicated approximately 50% of EL students are chronic absentees for the last three years.  ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The accounts support investigating at least two themes: instructional continuity and placement, and barriers to reaching school or obtaining assistance. The numerical attendance pattern makes these themes relevant, but it does not establish their prevalence or causal contribution.</p><p>The claim that 52% of EL students are not improving treats language-progress reporting categories as a complete account of learning. Maintaining a category is not equivalent to learning nothing. Annual distributions also do not necessarily follow the same students.</p><p>A useful synthesis would identify the sources behind each theme and explain their relationship to attendance. The parent and counselor accounts, for example, complicate an explanation centered on students' motivation by describing circumstances that can prevent attendance.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name each theme and identify the specific accounts supporting or complicating it.</li><li>Correct the interpretation of ELPI categories before connecting them to instructional experience.</li><li>Explain the relationship to the numerical pattern without claiming that separate datasets identify the same students.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.2: 2 / 5, Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall estimates are 2 for Rubric 1.2 and 2 for Rubric 1.3. Relevant sources and themes are present, but inaccurate quantitative interpretation and overstated connections weaken the integrated analysis needed for higher levels.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research","url":"#cycle/c1/A4","html":"<span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDefine the equity gap you identified for a student group through your quantitative and qualitative data analyses. Discuss related research and explain how this research informs and/or supports your equity gap analysis finding for the California state indicator and student group you identified.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>State precisely what the equity gap is for the selected group and indicator. Bring together the numerical disparity and the qualitative findings that help interpret it. Then explain how relevant research informs that interpretation. Research should contribute an idea or finding you can apply and examine, not serve as a quotation added after the conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Define the group's equity gap through integrated quantitative and qualitative analysis.</li><li>Explain patterns and the relationship between resources, access, and outcomes.</li><li>Discuss the research required by the prompt and explain its relevance to the selected group and indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"34\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"2477\"><p>[The equity gap I identified at High School P is related to chronic absenteeism and how it has persisted at a significantly high rate over the course of three years among the EL student population. When compared to the all students with an average rate of 35% over three years, the EL students have a much higher rate at 50% which is a 15% equity gap. The research emphasizes the need to “Examin[e] absenteeism rates among LTEL and newcomer EL subgroups is essential because in addition to the usual factors affecting absences across the k-12 span (i.e., illness, change of residence, lack of academic motivation, etc), absenteeism for these subgroups in secondary school could reflect a generalized disconnect between needs and services” (Finn et al., 2023). The qualitative findings support this idea by highlighting the importance of knowing the right resources for each subgroup. For example, Newcomers may be experiencing difficulty accessing content due to language acquisition barriers which in turn disheartens them and causes disengagement in school. These students are already having a hard time adjusting to a new country, surviving poverty, and homelessness. They would benefit from specialized social services to ensure their daily attendance in school. In addition, long term English learners fall into chronic absenteeism due to poor reclassification support and course programming that leads to lack of student interest in school. Having to take two periods of English every year limits their opportunity to take elective courses or engage in sports teams, which limits the joy of being in school. The research further posits that supporting ELs to reclassify before getting to high school can have positive outcomes and deter chronic absenteeism. For example, “We expect absenteeism to be lower among RFEP students than ELs because their educational experience is more mainstream, that is, they are less likely to face curricular tracking, labeling, and other exclusionary practices that are consistent with the EL designation…Although not all studies show positive effects of reclassification on student outcomes, a robust body of evidence indicates that reclassification has a positive impact on academic outcomes (Carlson et al., 2016).The research clearly indicates two very different approaches to addressing chronic absenteeism among EL students and it’s important to understand how to develop and sustain systems that ensure the appropriate supports. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies a persistent attendance concern and begins distinguishing different EL experiences. This is useful because a group-level rate does not imply that every member faces the same conditions.</p><p>The distinction between newcomer and other EL experiences is not supported by separate attendance analyses here. Treat it as a question to examine. Connect the actual teacher, student, parent, and counselor findings to the disparity without turning those accounts into subgroup rates or proven causes.</p><p>Research can help explain why a reported condition matters. State the relevant finding, compare its setting and population with this inquiry, and explain the limit of the application. A general citation cannot establish the local effect of a policy or service.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Define the disparity with the selected group, indicator, years, and correct numerical units.</li><li>Connect each interpretive claim to an actual quantitative or qualitative finding.</li><li>Explain the research connection and retain uncertainty where local evidence is incomplete.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.3 reflects an identifiable equity concern with an incomplete synthesis. A stronger response would make the numerical, qualitative, and resource-access relationships explicit before claiming the thorough understanding associated with Level 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments","url":"#cycle/c1/A5","html":"<span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tHow is the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school related to specific components of the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Compare the equity-gap findings with specific school commitments documented in Part A III. Explain what the school promises for this population, what the evidence suggests about students' experiences, and where the two align or appear to differ. Repeating the mission or stating that the gap conflicts with it leaves the comparison unfinished.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Connect the selected group's equity gap to specific school commitments.</li><li>Use quantitative and qualitative findings to explain the relationship.</li><li>For Level 4, provide a thorough synthesis of the disparity and the conditions that help interpret it.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"36\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1697\"><p>[The school’s vision is to provide a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve college and/or career readiness upon graduation. Unfortunately, the EL students are not benefiting from a personalized educational experience as they are being placed in ELD courses based on their grade level and not their proficiency level due to the new programing policy from the district. The support EL students are being given to help them reclassify is also not curtailed to their individual needs, which may be the reason they feel disengaged. EL students who are fluent in English and have not reclassified usually feel stuck in their courses and begin to lose interest in attaining their diploma, which starts to affect absenteeism. On the other hand, Newcomer students who have been in high school over three years and are still not fluent in English or college ready will most likely start to consider their future options and pursue that as opposed to finishing their high school career. High School P values providing a “safe, personalized, and small-school learning environment for all students” as one of its mission goals, but it’s struggling to provide a personalized learning environment for EL students which supports their diverse academic and socioemotional needs. The systems the school had in place to address chronic absenteeism did not make a dent in the EL population and the equity gap persisted. The data reveals that our most vulnerable students are slipping through the cracks and a more streamlined personalized approach to supporting EL subgroups needs to be established or revisited to ensure the school is working towards meeting its mission goals. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response usefully returns to personalized learning and community support. The placement account is relevant to the first commitment; the transportation and housing accounts are relevant to the second. These are concrete points of comparison for EL students.</p><p>The claim that existing systems 'did not make a dent' goes beyond the evidence. Persistent disparity does not show whether support was implemented, who received it, or what would have occurred without it. Describe the apparent mismatch and identify what needs verification.</p><p>The comparison should retain the selected population throughout. A mission for all students applies to EL students, but neither the mission nor one participant account establishes what every EL student received.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each relevant commitment with the particular finding that helps assess it.</li><li>Replace claims of no effect with an evidence-based account of the unresolved access or outcome concern.</li><li>Identify the service or implementation evidence needed to distinguish planned support from support received.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.3: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.3 includes a real strength here: explicit mission alignment. Unsupported conclusions about service effects and weaknesses in the wider synthesis keep that alignment from establishing a higher level by itself.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors","url":"#cycle/c1/B1","html":"<span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tWhat potential contributing factors are suggested by the data you have collected and analyzed that may have created or added to the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible conditions that may have created or contributed to the single equity gap. Draw each factor from the Part A findings and explain the school practice, arrangement, or process involved. 'Potential' matters: the evidence should justify investigating the factor without pretending that the inquiry has proved its causal effect.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Use the equity analysis to identify relevant contributing factors, including institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Connect factors to supporting inquiry evidence and relevant research across Part B.</li><li>For Level 4, explain several factors in detail using supporting evidence from Step 1.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"64\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1709\"><p>[The equity gap I identified in Step 1 at High School P is within the EL student population who have a 50% chronic absentee rate for the past three years. When compared to the general population at 35%, the equity gap is 15%. Based on the quantitative and qualitative data, the following are three potential factors which may have contributed to the equity gap. They are as follows: Contributing Factor #1: (Structural) EL Newcomer students have inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning experiences. The EL teacher at High School P has not participated in the appropriate training to teach the Newcomer course. Students are not learning at an appropriate pace and eventuallty become disheartened and disillusioned when they start failing their classes due to their language barrier. This disengagement contributes to their absenteeism. The interview with the ELD teacher revealed that she struggled to master the course content because of the continuous enrollment of students throughout the year which made it difficult to pace her instructional goals. These circumstances and her inexperience affect the language acquisition progress of EL students as well as their academic achievement in other content courses due to limited English proficiency. The quantitative data of the ELPI scores demonstrate the slow language acquisition of EL students where only 48% of them are advancing on the English Language Proficiency Assessment of California (ELPAC) exam. The slow language acquisition and poor academic achievement (falling behind on credits) among Newcomer students causes the disconnect from school and as a result they begin to explore job opportunities and stop prioritizing education.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"65\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"77\"><p>Contributing Factor #2: (Institutional) A recent change in the district’s EL </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"66\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1116\"><p>Masterplan course placement policy is not serving the needs of Long Term English Learners (LTEL). The policy change eliminated the LTEL course, which was supplemental to secondary ELA courses and designed to support EL students who were fluent in English to meet the state mandated reclassification criteria. Now, all EL students are placed in ELD courses based on their grade and not their fluency levels. This creates mixed-proficiency classes where some EL students feel alienated because the content is not at their fluency level. They feel like they have been placed in remedial classes and this affects their self-esteem. The interview with the newly reclassified/past chronic absentee student revealed that being placed in an ELD course made him feel like he was being treated as unintelligent. This led to low self-esteem, disengagement, and absenteeism. The inappropriate student learning experience is corroborated by SES quantitative data that shows only 49% of EL students find learning fun which means that 51% may be our chronic absentee students who are disengaged and dismissing school altogether.   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"67\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1343\"><p>Contributing Factor #3:  (Structural) The socioeconomic barriers of unstable housing and unreliable public transportation contribute to the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students. In her interview, the PSA counselor reported that the EL students on her caseload who are new to the country usually experience homelessness due to unstable work opportunities, or dire family relations. These traumatic experiences prevent consistent attendance which hinder the EL students’ ability to develop meaningful connections with their peers and coursework. Similarly, in the interview with the ELAC Parent I learned that the issue with unreliable public transportation in the community makes coming to school significantly challenging for EL students, who often opt to stay home instead. The quantitative data found in the SES is indicative of EL students' inability to connect to their school’s culture by way of extracurricular activities because they are not in school or prioritize getting home as soon as school lets out. The data shows that ELs are less involved in extracurricular activities at an average rate of 40% compared to the general population at 49%. This low number of involvement in the school culture may affect the connectedness ELs have with school in general which may limit the inherent interest of being in school daily.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies relevant areas to examine: instructional continuity, placement, and assistance with attendance barriers. Each can be connected to a source in Part A and to a process the school can investigate.</p><p>The statement that the teacher has not received appropriate training is not established by the reported interview. A description of frequent enrollment becomes an unsupported personnel judgment. This is especially consequential because Rubric 1.4 explicitly identifies biased or superficial factors as a Level 1 concern.</p><p>Housing and transport conditions can be outside the school's direct control. Explain the related school responsibility, such as referral, coordination, or follow-through, instead of listing the external condition as though it identifies the school process to change.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each potential factor to a named finding from Part A.</li><li>Replace the unsupported training judgment with the documented question about continuity and instructional support.</li><li>Explain the institutional or structural process involved and identify the evidence needed to test the proposed explanation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.4: 2 / 5</h4><p>The working overall estimate for Rubric 1.4 is 2 because plausible factors are present but several conclusions exceed the evidence. The unsupported personnel judgment creates a material risk of a lower score; a clearer evidence-to-factor connection is needed for Level 3.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors","url":"#cycle/c1/B2","html":"<span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tHow do these specific contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, impact student learning or well-being for the student group?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how each identified factor could affect this group's learning or well-being. Describe the pathway from the condition to a student experience and its possible consequence. Keep the explanation tied to Part A evidence; naming a factor again or saying it causes the gap does not explain its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Explain how the identified institutional or structural factors relate to the group's equity issue.</li><li>Support proposed effects with the Step 1 findings and relevant research.</li><li>For Level 4, develop detailed explanations of several factors and their contribution to disparities.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"69\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"836\"><p>[The institutional and structural contributing factors have impacted student well-being and learning by creating the 15% equity gap of chronic absenteeism among EL students for the past three years. First, the inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning has disheartened many Newcomer students at High School P over the years because they are not learning English language skills at an adequate pace. Even after two years of ELD instructions, Newcomer students find it significantly challenging to keep up with graduation requirement courses or making up credits for past classes they have failed. The language barriers that limit their academic achievement not just in ELD courses, but in all other content courses, adds to the chronic absentee equity gap of EL students who may lack motivation to be in school consistently. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"70\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"852\"><p>Second, the disservice to EL students caused by the new course placement policies of the most recent EL Master Plan have negatively impacted EL student learning and well-being by lowering the self esteem of students. With the quick roll out of the policy, teachers were not adequately prepared to scaffold and differentiate course content that would serve the needs of a mixed-proficiency class and as a result many students did not receive the targeted language supports to acquire language skills and meet reclassification criteria. These circumstances lead to the alienation of some students while the teacher teaches at the level of others. This combination of poor instructional experiences and negative perceptions undermines students' academic progress, sense of belonging, and overall well-being contributing to disengagement and absenteeism.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"71\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"663\"><p>Lastly, the socioeconomic circumstances of unstable housing and unreliable public transportation negatively impact EL students’ learning and well-being because they create significant stress that can be overbearing. The trauma of not having a place to sleep or enough to eat outweighs any academic concern which causes student disengagement from school; their main concern is survival. EL students who rely on public transportation may encounter unpredictable challenges when commuting to school every day. These transportation related stressors can contribute to heightened anxiety thereby leading students to forgo attending school to avoid these difficulties. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"72\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"309\"><p>The combined effects of language barriers, academic struggles, and socioeconomic stress can negatively impact students’ sense of belonging, self-confidence, and overall emotional well-being, which makes it harder for ELs to stay engaged in school creating the significant equity gap of chronic absenteeism. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response attempts to discuss instructional access, placement, and socioeconomic barriers. Those conditions can affect students through different pathways, so they need separate explanations.</p><p>The opening claims that the factors created the entire numerical gap. The inquiry does not estimate that contribution. A transport problem may prevent reaching school, while an instructional concern may affect the learning experience; neither pathway has been quantified by these data.</p><p>Claims about motivation, self-esteem, and achievement should be limited to the actual accounts and findings. An interpretation becomes stronger when the writer explains its support and uncertainty instead of making it sound conclusive.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain one evidence-supported pathway for each factor.</li><li>Remove the assertion that the identified factors produced the whole numerical disparity.</li><li>Distinguish attendance-access barriers from assumptions about students' motivation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.4: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.4 reflects identifiable mechanisms with insufficiently supported local conclusions. Detailed explanations can strengthen the response only when they remain grounded in the inquiry.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.3 / Using research to examine factors","url":"#cycle/c1/B3","html":"<span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.3 / Using research to examine factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tCite research related to your findings regarding contributing factors that may have created or added to the equity gap you have identified for the student group.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use research to examine the contributing factors identified from your findings. For each relevant study, explain what it found, how that finding relates to your factor, and why the connection is appropriate for this group and setting. Embed citations in the response as directed by the template and handbook.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Cite research relevant to the potential contributing factors.</li><li>Explain the connection between the research, local findings, and selected equity gap.</li><li>Support increasingly detailed understanding of the factors rather than adding citations without analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"74\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1174\"><p>[Research aligns with my findings regarding the contributing factors that add to the EL population’s chronic absentee equity gap.The first source shows that EL students do not have access to competent and rigorous learning. According to L. Santibanez and P. Gandara “Preparing teachers for an increasingly diverse classroom has been teacher education's \"central challenge\" for the past several decades (Rueda &amp; Stillman, 2012). This challenge is particularly acute in the nation’s secondary schools…ELs are a particularly vulnerable population at the secondary level as the academic and linguistic demands are greater at this level, and there is very limited time to acquire needed skills” (2018). This research highlights a significant issue that is affecting ELs across the state, and particularly Newcomers when they enroll at High School P. Their expectation is to quickly learn English to integrate into their school and culture, but their ELD teacher lacks the skills to help them learn the language. They become disheartened and discouraged as courses get more challenging and they can not keep up with the language skills demands. This has added to the equity gap.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"75\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1225\"><p>A second research source supports my findings about the inequitable district policy on course placement and how it adversely affects the chronic absenteeism equity gap particularly relating to LTEL students. According to Callahan R, et al “Under the auspices of ESL placement, when a student is identified as deficient in English, intentionally or not, [his] access to academic content is delayed until [he] is deemed sufficiently proficient in English (as measured by academic achievement in English as well as by her English language proficiency (Harklau, 1994b; Linquanti, 2001; MacSwan &amp; Rolstad, 2003) to engage with the academic content: the same academic content in which [he] is now behind as [he]has had limited exposure while learning English.” (2010) The student I interviewed experienced this type of set back due to not being reclassified while attending a private middle school. When he enrolled in High School P and was placed in an ELD course he was devastated because he felt stuck in the EL program. Although fluent in English, having to take an extra English course during the day felt burdensome and it affected his well-being, thereby creating low-self esteem that led to disengagement and absenteeism.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"76\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1803\"><p>The third research source confirms that “EL-classified students live in socioeconomic contexts that are associated with more absences: poverty, food insecurity, less parental supervision, frequent health issues, transportation issues, or familial or other obligations that necessitate students—especially older students—to stay at home and care for other children or family members, for example (Attendance Works and Everyone Graduates Center, 2017).” (Santibañez, L., Gottfried, M. A., &amp; Freeman, J. A., 2024) High School P is located in a low-income immigrant community, and 30% of its general student population is identified as living in poverty. The socioeconomic barriers of their communities are contributing factors that affect the attendance equity gap. Lastly, it is important to note research that presents the vulnerability of Newcomer EL students who are migrants and sometimes part of “The subgroup of students who are both homeless and chronically absent...For example, 9% of students who experienced homelessness have been identified as unaccompanied every year since SY 2015-16. Migratory students have accounted for 1% of students who experienced homelessness since at least SY 2014-15. While English learners steadily increased among 9 students experiencing homelessness between SYs 2014-15 and 2017-18, the annual increase was by one percentage point or less.” (National Center for Homeless Education 2022) The influx of Newcomer students to High School P is determined by external variable societal factors, which is why it is important to communicate a receptive and welcoming tone for new EL students as soon as they enroll in order to establish a sense of trust that they have a safe place they can count on when struggling with circumstances that limit their daily attendance.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response brings research into the discussion of instructional support and attendance barriers. That is necessary, but the presence of a citation does not establish that the study supports the precise local claim.</p><p>Broad findings about teacher preparation cannot prove that an individual teacher is unqualified. Similarly, research about placement or socioeconomic conditions needs a specific connection to the local evidence and an explanation of the limits of transfer.</p><p>The cited studies and the local poverty figure need verification. Check quotation accuracy, the populations studied, and the apparent copied page-number artifact before using them to support a conclusion.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify each cited study and identify its relevant finding, population, and setting.</li><li>Explain which factor the finding supports and which Part A evidence supplies the local connection.</li><li>Qualify the application where the research setting or evidence differs from this school.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.4: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.4 recognizes an attempt to use research, but several applications are broader than the local evidence permits. Clear, relevant research connections are part of the Level 3 foundation.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.4 / Identifying the educational need","url":"#cycle/c1/B4","html":"<span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.4 / Identifying the educational need</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tIdentify areas of educational need related to the single equity gap for a group of students (e.g., the school needs additional supports or materials, necessary specialists or counselors, after-school programs for students).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify what the selected group needs in order to experience more equitable learning or well-being. Describe the unmet condition indicated by the inquiry before naming a program. Supports, staffing, materials, or opportunities may be relevant, but explain the need they would address and its relationship to the single equity gap.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Draw the educational need from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Keep the need responsive to the identified student group.</li><li>Establish a focused basis for the feasible problem statement evaluated by Rubric 1.5.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"78\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"155\"><p>[In order for the High School P to address the institutional and structural causes of the 15% chronic absentee equity gap among EL students, it needs to:  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"79\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"706\"><p>Create an ELD teacher cadre with designated teachers who want to prioritize the educational needs of EL students and are vested in developing their teaching skills to help their students achieve academic success. This Professional Learning Community (PLC) can access targeted research based instructional strategies on differentiated instruction, scaffolding, and culturally and linguistically responsive pedagogy. The school should support this group with peer mentors, instructional coaches, or the Bilingual Program Coordinator to guide conscientious skill development. Better instructional quality will be evident for EL students who will value their learning experiences and continue going to school. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"80\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"516\"><p>Create a Tier 2 pull-out intervention program run by the EL Coordinator or Instructional Coach where they analyze EL student ELPAC scores to determine what domain/s is/are keeping students from meeting reclassification criteria. The small group intervention will target specific language skill development  once or twice a week for the entire semester before the new ELPAC administration in the spring. This will help LTEL students feel they matter and are cared for when it comes to their reclassification efforts. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"81\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"336\"><p>Offer culturally and linguistically relevant extended learning opportunities that expose EL students to various parts of their new city which will facilitate acquiring language in non-academic settings. These experiences will also contribute fun and new ways of learning which will inspire students to be more engaged in their school.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"82\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"695\"><p>Empower and support the Pupil Services and Attendance (PSA) counselors to create attendance intervention teams made up of teachers, academic counselors, school climate advocates, parent volunteers, and administrators who will be assigned chronic absentee students. This team will be responsible for making sure they call their student’s home when absent, welcome them back when they return to school, facilitate conversations with the student to establish trust and encourage them to continue coming to school, and/or learn about factors that limit their attendance. These concentrated efforts will ensure the most at risk students are given proper resources to address their individual needs.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response proposes a cadre, instructional arrangements, extended learning, and mentoring. These may be possible responses, but the underlying educational need becomes difficult to isolate.</p><p>For example, a teacher cadre is a delivery strategy. The need might concern dependable access to appropriate instruction during enrollment changes. Stating that condition first lets the school examine which response best fits the evidence.</p><p>The same distinction applies to mentoring: supportive relationships and timely access to assistance are different needs, even if one proposed program tries to address both. Select a defensible focus for the problem statement.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the unmet educational or access condition in plain language.</li><li>Explain the Part A evidence and contributing factors that support that need.</li><li>Reserve detailed program design for Part C after the problem has been defined.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.5: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.5 reflects a responsive concern whose need and proposed solutions are not clearly separated. A focused, evidence-based need would strengthen the foundation for a feasible Level 3 problem statement.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement","url":"#cycle/c1/B5","html":"<span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tProblem Statement: Describe the equity gap that needs to be addressed at the chosen school for the California state indicator and the identified student group’s area of need (achievement and/or well-being).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the single equity gap the school needs to address, keeping the indicator, student group, and learning or well-being need explicit. Draw the statement from the inquiry and contributing factors. Explain why the problem is responsive to the group and feasible to address within the school's actual context.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Develop a feasible problem statement from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Make the statement clearly responsive to the selected group's learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain responsiveness and feasibility in the school's culture and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"85\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"660\"><p>[The equity gap analysis I completed revealed a clear equity gap for EL students on the California state chronic absentee indicator at High School P. This group has a chronic absentee rate of 50%. When compared to the general school population which has a 35% rate, there is a clear equity gap of 15% for the three years I have analyzed. The qualitative data collection revealed themes of insufficient progress of EL students towards becoming proficient in English language skills,  EL students feeling disconnected to the school, and significant socioeconomic challenges, which added to my understanding of the patterns and trends in my equity gap analysis.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"86\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"640\"><p>This equity gap analysis led to my determination of the following contributing factors: EL Newcomer students have inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning experiences, new district course placement policy fails to meet the needs of LTEL students, and several socioeconomic barriers contribute to the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students. High School P’s inability to address these issues has sustained the equity gap for the last three years. The quantitative and qualitative findings corroborate that these issues contribute to the equity gap. The identified group’s areas of need are associated with these factors.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"87\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1547\"><p>EL students need adults at the school who are committed to growing professionally to meet their educational needs and demonstrate a vested interest in being a part of their academic achievement. Quality instructors will not only produce improved language development and academic performance, but also instill in EL students a sense of pride and belonging to a school that looks after their achievement and well-being. EL students also need adults who are willing to develop and implement intervention programs that will target supporting their reclassification attempts early on in high school. Reclassification criteria at upper grade levels is significantly more challenging compared to ninth or tenth grade. Helping students reclassify sooner than later will help them lose the sense of being stuck in the EL program and motivate them to attend school consistently and continue their education in a traditional high school program. In addition, students need school personnel mentors who will develop a trusting relationship with them through well-being check-ins, frequent family communication, and goal setting. According to a study by R. Balfanz and V. Byrnes titled “Meeting the Challenge of Combating Chronic Absenteeism” there is proof that “Quality mentoring, especially as part of a school-wide effort, can be leveraged as a strategy to improve attendance and boost academic achievement…” (2013). The chronic absentee equity gap demonstrates how EL students are falling through the cracks without proper monitoring and intervention.   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"88\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1954\"><p>In order to close the equity gap, High School P needs to create an ELD teacher cadre that is committed to teaching EL students and developing the necessary skills to meet the diverse needs of those students. This will ensure EL students that their education matters to the school therefore it should matter to them by being in school every day.  The school also needs to create a Tier 2 intervention pull-out program that provides intense individualized reclassification supports for LTEL students. This will give LTEL students the confidence to continue their reclassification efforts and motivate them to improve their attendance. Lastly, the school needs to provide extended learning opportunities to EL students to support language acquisition in a social setting, and create a cadre of designated support staff to mentor chronic absentee students. This problem statement is responsive to the school culture and context because there is a Bilingual Coordinator who can organize professional development opportunities to the willing ELD teacher cadre. Additionally, the school has a part-time intervention coordinator who can develop and implement a Tier 2 program in collaboration with the Bilingual Coordinator. High School P also counts on three PSA counselors who usually take on five or more interns. They can develop a mentoring program and recruit various willing personnel to mentor chronic absentee students. Furthermore, this problem statement is feasible for the school’s culture and context as there is enough personnel who can align their time and resources to assist the EL population. By addressing the identified needs and contributing factors, High School P can live up to its mission and vision of “providing a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve” by implementing the proposed changes. This will assist in the reduction or eliminate the 15% equity gap of chronic absenteeism among EL students.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response keeps EL students and attendance visible and attempts to discuss local resources. Those are useful foundations for a problem statement.</p><p>It then expands into several programs and assumes sufficient staff capacity. Staff roles alone do not establish available time or agreement; the later PSA feedback about recruiting mentors makes this assumption especially important to revisit.</p><p>The promise to reduce or eliminate the gap turns an intended outcome into an assurance. A focused statement can justify improvement work while acknowledging that the effect of a proposed response remains to be examined.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Reduce the statement to one evidence-supported educational need and a feasible school-level scope.</li><li>Support feasibility with actual responsibilities, time, resources, authority, and constraints.</li><li>Use percentage-point units and describe attendance improvement as an intended outcome to assess.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.5: 2 / 5</h4><p>The overall 2 for Rubric 1.5 reflects a relevant but overly broad problem and unverified feasibility assumptions. A 4 requires a convincing explanation of responsiveness and feasibility on top of the clear Level 3 problem.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 1 / Describing proposed strategies","url":"#cycle/c1/C1","html":"<span id=\"strategy-map\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 1 / Describing proposed strategies</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tFor the student equity gap you identified in Step 1, describe the potential strategies you identified for equitable school improvement and how they are to be applied.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the strategies you propose and how each would operate at the school. Connect them to the equity gap and explain the activities, responsibilities, and application well enough for the reader to understand the proposal. This is a plan for improvement; distinguish proposed actions from actions already completed.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the proposal to the evidence</summary><p>Keep the reasoning visible without repeating the full investigation. A proposed strategy responds to a supported finding or a clearly identified condition to investigate; it is not evidence that improvement has already occurred.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Connection</th><th scope=\"col\">What to explain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Finding and potential factor</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify the evidence and the school-related condition it suggests. Keep an untested explanation provisional.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Proposed action and access</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Explain what would change for the selected student group and why that change addresses the need.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Feedback and refinement</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify who examined feasibility, what they contributed, and how the proposal changed.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Remaining uncertainty</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">State what would need to be examined before claiming that the response works.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Propose strategies informed by the analysis, factors, and problem statement.</li><li>Explain how the strategies are applied and aligned with school commitments.</li><li>For Level 4, show a strategic and contextually responsive approach to the equity issue.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"93\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"355\"><p>[The equity gap I identified at High School P is related to chronic absentee rates among EL students, who averaged 50% over a period of three years. When compared to the general population at 35%, there is a clear equity gap of 15%. To help address this inequity, I have selected two focus strategies that would fit the current context of High School P.  </p></div><h4>Instructional collaboration</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"93\" data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"2712\"><p>Strategy 1: Create an ELD Teacher cadre committed to developing their teaching skills to improve the quality of instruction in ELD courses. This strategy will address the structural Contributing Factor #1 which indicates that EL Newcomer students have inequitable access to competent and rigorous learning experiences. My reasoning for doing this is based on the quantitative data from my equity gap analysis which revealed that only 48% of EL students have progressed one level on the ELPAC, meaning most are not developing their English language skills or falling behind academically. When EL students fall behind in their courses and eventually on graduation credits they begin to disengage from school which leads to chronic absenteeism. The ELD teacher cadre will allow teachers to collaborate on research-based strategies for language acquisition. The school’s Bilingual Coordinator will lead, with meetings twice a month in the fall and monthly in the spring. The Bilingual Coordinator will also curate a resource bank with SDAIE strategies, Culturally and Linguistically responsive pedagogy, and Translanguaging philosophies to guide the meeting objectives. During the meetings cadre participants will work on setting instructional goals to implement in between meetings. The ELD teacher cadre will also analyze data (attendance and academic) to understand the importance of creating learning environments that are culturally and linguistically responsive and focus on differentiation to meet the needs of every student. The idea is for the ELD teachers to get into a PDSA cycle where they plan to use the strategies, try them out with their students, evaluate the effectiveness of student engagement, then share their findings with the cadre at the next meeting. The vision for the cadre will be presented to the principal to gain approval. Once the idea is approved, it will be presented to all ELD teachers at their department meeting to gain buy-in and recruit participants. This PLC addresses the problem statement by providing ELD teachers the opportunity to hone in on their methodology and provide more rigorous high quality instruction. When EL students see an improvement in the quality of their lessons they will begin to value being in school consistently, which will reduce frustration and disengagement, thereby increasing attendance. </p></div><h4>Mentoring and support</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"93\" data-start=\"2712\" data-end=\"4483\"><p>Strategy 2: Create a mentoring and well-being check-in program for chronic absentee students. This strategy will address the structural Contributing Factor #3 which indicates that EL students face socioeconomic barriers of unstable housing and unreliable transportation. My reasoning for doing this is because the qualitative data from my equity gap analysis revealed that EL students who experience chronic absenteeism face significant socioeconomic barriers like homelessness and unstable public transportation. A structured mentoring and well-being check-in program will provide collaborative educational partner support that strengthens family contact which will build trust and strengthen students’ sense of belonging resulting in improved attendance. This mentoring program will be led by the PSA who will recruit participants by presenting attendance data to the out of class support personnel at their monthly meetings and identify the students who are in most need of individualized intervention. After giving an overview of the mentor responsibilities and expected outcomes, the PSA will recruit volunteer mentors and assign one student to each. The PSA will provide a one-page conversation guide and remain accessible to coordinate resources for students and families. This group will meet once a month for the entire school year to report on each student’s progress or lack thereof, and to bring up any questions or concerns they may have as they continue to mentor the students. This program addresses the problem statement because mentors can identify socioeconomic barriers during check-ins and connect students to school or community resources which will reduce stressors and allow students to engage in school consistently thereby reducing absenteeism.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response provides concrete operational detail: a Bilingual Coordinator, a meeting rhythm, an instructional inquiry cycle, a PSA role, and a mentoring guide. This makes the proposed work understandable.</p><p>The instructional rationale is weakened by treating ELPI category maintenance as lack of learning and by unsupported claims about competence. Correct the evidence interpretation before using it to justify the strategy.</p><p>The mentoring proposal recognizes material barriers, but a check-in does not itself provide transport or resolve housing instability. Explain the referral and follow-through process, who can provide assistance, and how the team would know that support was usable.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct the quantitative interpretation supporting the instructional proposal.</li><li>Explain why the selected strategies fit the supported need and which concerns they leave unresolved.</li><li>Specify the route from identifying a barrier to obtaining assistance and the responsibilities requiring agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.6: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.6 reflects concrete, connected strategies and school-goal alignment. A more accurate rationale and a complete account of how support reaches students would strengthen the contextually responsive Level 4 case.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem","url":"#cycle/c1/C2","html":"<span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tExplain how your potential strategies address the equity gap described in your problem statement.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why each proposed strategy is expected to address the problem statement. Describe the mechanism: what would change in students' access or experience, and how that change relates to the identified need. Use relevant evidence to justify the expectation without presenting a hoped-for outcome as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Explain how strategies respond to the identified problem and supporting findings.</li><li>Keep the mechanism of improvement relevant to the selected group and school.</li><li>Distinguish the proposal's rationale from demonstrated implementation results.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"95\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1461\"><p>[My first potential strategy addresses the equity gap in the problem statement with the idea of a cadre of ELD teachers that provides them opportunity to learn collaboratively under the guidance of the Bilingual Coordinator. It is important to invest in the professional capacity of ELD teachers because they are the key educational partners who engage with EL students every day. The way they set up their classrooms, interact with students, and scaffold and differentiate lessons is what communicates to students that they are cared for and considered; that they are seen and they matter. Lacking these qualities in ELD classrooms would contribute to the disengagement and ultimate disconnect of EL students, resulting in chronic absenteeism. For EL students to value their education, ELD teachers must be better prepared - this is a challenge not unique to High School P. The research that supports investing in this type of ELD teacher professional development and how it improves EL student engagement was written by L.Santibanez and P.Gandara who studied the “scarcity of fully authorized teachers” to teach EL students. In their qualitative work they found that “the best way to “to improve their teaching of ELs [teachers] would benefit from coaching, observing and collaborating with other teachers (PLCs).” (2018) The benefits of the ELD teacher cadre will not just be improved instruction, but more engaged EL students with proficient attendance.   \t</p></div><h4>Mentoring rationale</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"95\" data-start=\"1461\" data-end=\"2943\"><p>The second potential strategy that addresses the equity gap in the problem statement is the creation of a mentoring and well-being check-in program. It will maximize on the large human capital that exists at High School P in the large out of class support personnel such as coordinators, counselors, itinerants, and assistant principals. The PSA counselor can develop a feasible and powerful Tier 3 intervention program that can provide EL students access to socioemotional support for those students dealing with traumatic life challenges and encourage them to continue to push through on the toughest days. Mentors have the potential to build trusting relationships with students and make an impact on their educational outlook. In addition, mentors can help identify the specific needs students and their families may have in a timely manner. This allows for resources and immediate supports to be provided to ensure students are safe and able to attend school consistently and reduce chronic attendance. The research that supports this approach was conducted by R. Balfanz and V. Byrnes in New York City where they found that “In the 25% of schools with the greatest impacts, chronically absent students supported by Success Mentors gained, on average, more than a month of school.” (2013) When students feel supported they are motivated and more willing to keep trying in the face of adversity. By building these connections, the attendance gap for EL students will improve. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response connects teacher collaboration to instructional experience and mentoring to relationships and assistance. These are plausible mechanisms, but their effectiveness depends on whether the proposed changes reach the students concerned.</p><p>The mentoring quotation describes results in the highest-impact quarter of schools. Even if the original study is verified, that result is not an expected effect for High School P. Explain the conditions that support transfer and the differences that limit it.</p><p>Several sentences promise attendance improvement. Replace that certainty with an explanation of the intended pathway and the evidence that would show whether it is working.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the proposed change in student experience for each strategy.</li><li>Verify and contextualize the research, including the restriction to the highest-impact schools.</li><li>State attendance improvement as an outcome to examine and distinguish relational encouragement from material assistance.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.6: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.6 recognizes a relevant rationale. The case for 4 remains limited by uncertain local fit and overstated outcomes; additional research quotations alone would not resolve those issues.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals","url":"#cycle/c1/C3","html":"<span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tExplain how your potential strategies for improvement address or take into account the potential contributing factors—including institutional and/or structural factors—and how they align with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Make two connections for each strategy: explain which contributing factor it addresses and how it aligns with a specific school commitment. Show the chain from finding to factor to action to school goal. Explain any important factor the chosen strategies do not address.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Connect strategies to the findings and institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Explain alignment with specific elements of the school's vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Show why the approach responds to this school's equity issue and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"97\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1712\"><p>[Based on my equity gap analysis, a potential strategy that addresses Contributing Factor #1 is to create an ELD Teacher cadre committed to developing their teaching skills to improve the quality of instruction in ELD classes. The goal would be to improve ELD instruction so that students can learn more effectively which will improve their interest in the value of their education, inspire them to reengage in school, and lessen chronic absenteeism. Better trained ELD teachers will ensure that students have access to rigorous and competent learning experiences. Based on the quantitative data from my equity gap analysis, there is a significant lack of improvement in ELPI scores, and only 49% of EL students find learning fun according to the school’s SES survey. This implies that ELs are not learning adequately or finding intrinsic value in learning when they come to school, so they opt to stay home to help their families or find jobs. This data was corroborated by the qualitative data from the ELD teacher interview who shared her struggles when teaching the Newcomer EL course and from the EL students who felt he was not treated well by his ELD teacher. These circumstances have contributed to the disproportionate chronic absentee rate because EL students feel disengaged and disconnected from school. The potential strategy aligns to the school’s Mission of fostering “a culture, which builds on the capacity of faculty and staff to serve all students.” The ELD teacher cadre demonstrates commitment to teacher growth and equitable, individualized learning. By improving the EL educational experience, more EL students will remain in school consistently and improve their attendance proficiency.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"98\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1807\"><p>Based on my findings in my equity gap analysis, my strategy addresses Contributing Factor #3 by creating a mentoring and well-being check-in program for chronic absentee students. The goal of the mentoring program would be to create a consistent relationship that builds a sense of belonging. By helping EL students feel seen and valued, they will be motivated to be in school more frequently or find necessary resources to alleviate their circumstances. The qualitative data in my equity gap analysis revealed that the chronic absentee rate is affected by a significant number of EL students who face socioeconomic barriers that make it challenging to be in school every day. The most prominent barriers being experiencing homelessness and unreliable public transportation. In addition, these stressors cause EL students to feel disconnected from their education. This was corroborated by the quantitative data from the school’s SES where an average of 40% of EL students participate in extracurricular activities offered at High School P. The majority of EL students are not a part of the greater school community and chronically absent EL students may not prioritize these opportunities when questioning having a place to sleep or hoping to get home safely. High School P can make an impact on EL attendance trends by investing in a mentor program. The recommended strategy aligns with the school’s vision which is to “provide a personalized educational experience that supports each student to achieve college and career readiness upon graduation.” Each mentor will be able to provide personalized attention to their assigned student and guide them through various academic and/or personal challenges. This individualized approach would illustrate High School P’s commitment to fulfilling its vision. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response explicitly links staff collaboration to capacity building and mentoring to personalized support. These are clearer alignments than a general assertion that a strategy promotes equity.</p><p>The survey does not establish that students find little value in learning and therefore choose work over school. That assumed chain weakens the factor-to-strategy connection. Use the actual reported instructional and access concerns.</p><p>The placement concern is not clearly resolved by either proposed strategy. Explain its status instead of implying that two programs address every factor in the inquiry.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>For each strategy, identify the finding, factor, proposed action, and specific school commitment.</li><li>Remove unsupported assumptions about students' motives.</li><li>Account for the placement concern and explain how mentors would respond to needs beyond their role or resources.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.6: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.6 includes a strength here: explicit school-goal alignment. More accurate factor connections and a clear account of the strategies' scope would strengthen the Level 4 explanation.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 4 / Applying targeted feedback","url":"#cycle/c1/C4","html":"<span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 4 / Applying targeted feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDescribe the targeted feedback you received on each proposed strategy from your administrator, supervisor, or other key educational leader(s), and explain how you would adjust your proposed strategies to address the needs of the student group and inform equitable school improvement based on that feedback.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what feedback you received on each proposed strategy and what you changed, or would change, because of it. Explain the reason for each decision, including a recommendation you did not adopt. Keep the sequence visible so the reader can follow the proposal from feedback through revision.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Apply targeted feedback to strengthen the proposed strategies.</li><li>Explain appropriate adjustments and their implications for the group and school.</li><li>For Level 4, seek further feedback on revised strategies and develop a detailed communication plan across diverse educational partners.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"100\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"2022\"><p>[ \tRound 1:  I met with the school Principal at High School P and asked him for feedback on my strategies because he has been working with the PSAs for the last two years to improve chronic absenteeism at the school.  Strategy 1: The feedback was positive. The suggestions for improvement included making sure that the cadre uses ELPAC data to determine what language domains most EL students need support in. Also, to ensure that best practices for EL students are not just being developed in language courses, the principal recommended that the ELD cadre members collect evidence of the instructional strategies implementation and their results, so that at the end of the year they can put together a presentation for the entire faculty to create buy-in for a similar cadre of teachers that teach EL students in other content courses.  Strategy 2: The feedback was positive. The suggestion for improvement was to add a talking point in the check-in conversation guide about student interests in sports offered at school or possible clubs that may sound fun to them. The goal would be to connect the students with other trusting adults on campus to increase the sense of support and belonging.   \tAfter receiving feedback from the principal, I made the following adjustments: Strategy 1: Create an ELD Teacher cadre committed to developing their teaching skills to improve the quality of instruction in ELD courses. Based on the feedback I received, I will ensure that the Bilingual Coordinator has administrative access to the school’s Learning Management System (LMS) where she can create a Group for the cadre. In this digital space, the Coordinator can organize the instructional resources, lesson plans, and materials that are used and developed by the ELD cadre and invite the rest of the faculty to explore its contents. By promoting this at faculty meetings, hopefully other teachers can adopt EL strategies in their classrooms and facilitate access to improved instructional quality for EL students. Strategy 2: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"101\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"82\"><p>Create a mentoring and well-being check-in program for chronic absentee students. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"102\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"302\"><p>Based on the feedback I received, I will create a list of clubs at High School P with information on meeting times and locations. I will also create a list of sports teams, their season, and coach contact information so that mentors can easily reference the information when talking to their mentees.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"103\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1482\"><p> \tRound 2: I met with the school’s PSA counselor to share the adjusted strategies and received the following feedback: Strategy 1: This was well received and they mentioned that a lot of chronic absentee students who are facing socioeconomic challenges often think about finding a job. They recommended advising the ELD teacher cadre to frequently connect learning to work-based skills so that students find value in their learning and are more likely to be in school every day. Strategy 2: They mentioned that in the past, when these types of programs are in the initial stages of being implemented it may be a challenge to recruit the mentors because not all out of class personnel want to give up their work time. They suggested the possibility of expanding the opportunity to teachers. By giving teachers training and resources to mentor one of their students, there may be a possibility of having a larger reach. After receiving this feedback from the PSA I made additional adjustments to Strategy 1: I would enlist the support of the school’s Work Based Learning coordinator to support the cadre by offering her services of coordinating guest speakers and/or field trips that connect to any work related topics/skills that teachers may be covering. Strategy 2: I would coordinate the opportunity for the PSA and I to present the chronic EL absentee data to the entire faculty and the idea of the mentoring program. Together we would invite teachers to volunteer as mentors. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The principal-to-revision-to-PSA sequence is a substantial strength. It shows a second partner considering adjusted strategies, rather than merely two unrelated conversations.</p><p>The PSA's recruitment concern changes the assumed mentor pool and leads to a proposal to include teachers. Preserve this clear feedback-to-decision connection while recognizing that a wider recruitment pool does not establish participation.</p><p>Some other changes are less complete. The principal's recommendation about ELPAC domains and implementation evidence is not fully answered by creating an LMS group. Explain the actual analytical work the group would do and confirm the added coordinator's capacity.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each substantive recommendation to a decision, proposal change, and reason.</li><li>Explain how ELPAC domains and implementation evidence would guide the instructional work.</li><li>Distinguish completed proposal revisions from staffing, resources, and activities still awaiting agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.7: 4 / 5</h4><p>The overall 4 for Rubric 1.7 is plausible when this feedback sequence is read with C5's communication plan. It remains near the 3-to-4 boundary if the plan is insufficiently detailed or workable. A 5 would require the explicit bias-examination coaching described in the rubric.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 5 / Building participation and anticipating implications","url":"#cycle/c1/C5","html":"<span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 5 / Building participation and anticipating implications</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tDescribe steps you would take to create school-level and community educational partner buy-in to the final adjusted set of proposed strategies. What potential implications for proposed strategies do you anticipate encountering as you address the equity gap at the school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how different educational partners would become involved in the adjusted proposal and what practical consequences or constraints you anticipate. Specify opportunities for people to influence the plan, not only receive an announcement. Connect likely implementation difficulties to the steps you would take to address them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Provide relevant steps for buy-in and communication.</li><li>Anticipate realistic implications of implementation at this school.</li><li>For Level 4, develop a detailed, workable communication plan involving diverse educational partners, alongside further feedback on revisions.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"841\"><p>[In order for the adjusted strategies to be implemented successfully and truly make a difference in the EL chronic absentee equity gap, I need to generate buy-in from educational partners across the school: Teachers:The Bilingual Coordinator and I would visit an English department meeting and present data on the EL chronic absentee equity gap to create a sense of urgency for intervention. The idea of the ELD cadre, how it addresses a contributing factor, and its goals will also be shared to enlist interested volunteers who will commit to the endeavor. The importance of achieving equitable outcomes for ELs will be emphasized. For the mentoring program, the PSA and I will present the same data at a faculty meeting to ensure all educational partners understand our efforts in recruiting their participation to reduce the equity gap.  </p></div><h4>Support personnel</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"841\" data-end=\"1748\"><p>Out of Class Support personnel: To enlist the participation of the Bilingual Coordinator and the PSA in leading the ELD cadre and the mentoring program I would offer my support as co-leader so that we can share in the planning responsibilities. I would emphasize a team approach so that together we make decisions about our collaboration in an effort to meet the school’s vision and extensive mission, which include providing a personalized educational experience that supports all students to achieve and building the capacity of our teachers to serve all students. These strategies are feasible because the Bilingual Coordinator and the PSAs may already be expected to implement these types of strategies as part of their job descriptions, but have not due to time constraints and other responsibilities. With coordinated support, they may be inclined to develop and implement the recommended strategies. </p></div><h4>Families</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"1748\" data-end=\"2291\"><p>Parents: In order to get parent involvement, the Bilingual Coordinator, PSA, and I would schedule a meeting after school for all ELD parents. We would mail out letters, send home flyers, program a mass phone call and email from the principal inviting them to attend and enjoy a light dinner. At this meeting we would also present the attendance data, explain the importance of being in school every day, present our proposals for the ELD cadre and mentoring program, and kindly ask them to ensure they send their students to school every day. </p></div><h4>Students</h4><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"105\" data-start=\"2291\" data-end=\"2919\"><p>Students: To create buy-in from students I would start by introducing myself to them during their ELD periods and let them know that I’ll be visiting them often to see what and how they are learning because their education matters to me. I would make sure to identify the chronic absentee students and make sure to acknowledge when they are present and encourage them to keep returning. For the mentees, I would ensure funding to provide tangible incentives that mentors can distribute when their students reach and maintain attendance goals. These can be things like donuts, pizza lunch, movie tickets, or school merchandise.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"106\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1417\"><p>In the culture and context of this school, in order to ensure buy-in, clear communication with all educational partners is essential so everyone understands the motives behind the strategies. Both strategies are designed as collaborative team efforts to ensure transparency, feasibility, and lasting student success.The following are possible implications of my proposed strategies. Challenging implications: There may be initial enthusiasm among educators participating in the ELD cadre and the mentoring program, but ongoing commitment may decline without strong accountability and incentives. There may also be inconsistent implementation of the work expected to be done. Some may engage deeply, while others not so much, which could leave uneven student experiences. In addition, educators tend to feel overburdened sometimes which may lead to dropping out of the programs. Positive implications: improved attendance for EL students as well as increased communication with families which will increase their access to valuable resources and build trust. The regular use of data fosters a culture of evidence-based decision making. In addition, the leaders of the programs can sponsor recognition events and incentives to foster a positive campus climate where EL students feel valued. Aligning school, home, and community efforts will improve attendance and ultimately help High School P close the equity gap.  ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response differentiates teachers, support personnel, parents, and students. It also recognizes workload and implementation concerns, giving the participation plan useful specificity.</p><p>Families and students are mainly asked to support a plan already selected. Asking families to ensure attendance sits uneasily beside the reported transport barriers. Include opportunities to identify what arrangements are usable and what the proposal has missed.</p><p>Time, confidentiality, and incentives need attention. A role description does not establish spare capacity, and attendance rewards can disadvantage students whose barriers remain unresolved.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify when each group can question or change the proposal and how its feedback returns to decisions.</li><li>Provide accessible participation arrangements suited to language and scheduling needs.</li><li>Explain how workload, confidentiality, recruitment, and unequal access would change the implementation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.7: 4 / 5</h4><p>Read with C4, the working estimate for Rubric 1.7 is 4. The differentiated plan and further feedback support that judgment, but the detail and feasibility of participation remain important weaknesses. The score is an estimate, not evidence that buy-in has already occurred.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / 1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback","url":"#cycle/c1/D1","html":"<span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tProvide your rationale for the administrator, supervisor, or educational leader feedback you chose to gather in Step 3 and how the feedback impacted your approach to building positive educational partner buy-in.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why you chose these feedback partners and how their feedback changed your approach to gaining participation. Connect the partner's relevant expertise to a specific recommendation, decision, and lesson for leadership. Praise for a leader's qualities does not by itself explain the effect of the feedback.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Explain the rationale for selecting feedback partners and its effect on the approach to buy-in.</li><li>Refer to specific evidence from the cycle.</li><li>For Level 4, show how school context affects the approach to equity leadership.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"115\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"2055\"><p>[The rationale for selecting the Principal of High School P was for this expert guidance on instructional development and effective attendance interventions. He has an extensive career in education and since he started at this school two years ago, his shared experience when making important structural changes have started to show significant improvements. It was important for me to share my proposed strategies with him because I trust his feedback. His input helped shape my approach to being an effective equity-driven leader because he models it. Through our conversations I was able to broaden my perspective and be more considerate of district and state expectations while remaining collaborative by utilizing a democratic and transparent approach when asking teachers to do more for our students. His feedback impacted my approach to building buy-in in the following way: he supported the idea of the ELD teacher cadre because this type of collaborative work allows for teacher autonomy and capacity building, which supports effective educational partner buy-in. Teachers buy-in to implementing change when they feel empowered, rather than directed. In addition, as the student-centered, data-driven leader he is, his recommendation to use ELPAC data to guide the objectives of the cadre and eventually determine a measurable outcome helped see how buy-in is gained when there is tangible evidence that justifies the work that is being proposed and it’s not just someone’s prerogative to create more work for teachers. The principal’s feedback on the mentor conversation guide to include talking points about clubs and sports impacted my approach to developing strategies by keeping student data in mind. Based on my use of the SES data on student connectedness to the school’s culture, he reminded me that quantitative data can be further understood when interacting with students, particularly during the well-being check-ins. Keeping students at the center of our work has proven to be an effective way of gaining educational partner buy-in. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"116\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1770\"><p>My rationale for selecting the PSA to provide me with feedback on the two potential strategies I identified in Step 3 was because of her expertise in supporting students with chronic absenteeism and her direct experience working with families experiencing socioeconomic challenges. Her feedback was important to me because I wanted to make sure my proposed ideas are feasible and realistic. Over the course of her career she has implemented many intervention strategies that were developed collaboratively among her colleagues at various schools and some were district directives. In that time she has learned what approaches yield higher educational partner buy-in. Her feedback shaped my approach to building buy-in in important ways. For the ELD cadre she recommended that teachers keep in mind the importance of highlighting work-based skills that students can develop in each lesson. This led me to make a connection with an important resource at High School P; the new Work-Based Learning Coordinator (WBLC). By connecting the WBLC and the ELD cadre more teachers will buy-in to the idea of participating in the cadre’s work because they will feel supported by an additional person who can enhance their efforts. For the mentoring program, she noted potential challenges when recruiting out of class support personnel and suggested giving teachers the opportunity to be mentors as well. This feedback impacted my approach to developing buy-in by presenting the attendance data and the well-being mentoring program to the entire faculty to recruit volunteers. I realized that by expanding this opportunity, buy-in is strengthened when participation is distributed more broadly, making this feel like a shared responsibility rather than the job of certain people. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The principal contributes instructional expertise and the PSA contributes attendance and feasibility expertise. These are relevant reasons for seeking their perspectives.</p><p>The most convincing reflective evidence is a changed assumption: the recruitment concern led to a broader proposed mentor pool. Explain what that taught the writer about testing capacity and involving people who understand the work.</p><p>The account sometimes shifts from a revised plan to an implication that buy-in is achieved. A decision to invite more people is not evidence of their agreement. Keep the reflection consistent with the revisions actually documented in Part C.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific recommendation and decision to explain the contribution of each partner.</li><li>Identify the assumption or leadership practice that changed.</li><li>Distinguish intended participation from actual agreement and reconcile the reflection with Part C.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.8: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.8 reflects a clear feedback rationale and evidence of learning. Stronger connections between school context, changed decisions, and future practice would support a more convincing 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / 2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth","url":"#cycle/c1/D2","html":"<span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tReflect on what you have learned, including strengths and areas for growth as an equity-driven leader. Then discuss and refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 as to how you addressed equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at your school.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you learned about equity leadership, identify strengths and areas for growth, and show how your work addressed this group's equity needs. Support the reflection with specific evidence from Steps 1, 2, or 3. Describe changes in your understanding and decisions, not only the activities you completed or traits you believe you possess.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Reflect on growth in addressing the selected group's equity needs and gaps.</li><li>Use specific cycle evidence to explain strengths and areas for growth.</li><li>For Level 4, demonstrate how social, economic, or cultural context affects the leadership approach.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"118\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"564\"><p>[During this cycle, I have learned the importance of approaching my work with a conscientious focus on equity-driven ideals. As I engaged in both quantitative and qualitative data collection, my initial instinct was to propose solutions and interpretations. However, through my course readings and reflections at each step, I learned to pause and take a step back. This allowed me to broaden my perspective to understand the social and cultural context of the school, learn from others, and develop more equitable approaches to bringing about school improvement.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"119\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1313\"><p>In Step 1 I learned the importance of analyzing various data sources to find connections that may help to deepen the understanding of a specific student group. I also learned that it’s important to find the “story” the quantitative data shows before engaging in gathering qualitative data because this research should serve to fill the gaps in the quantitative data. I found that I had to be strategic about the questions I wanted to ask each of my sources. I became mindful of being culturally sensitive with my inquiries based on the context of their realities. This helped me gain a clear understanding of the equity gap and its contributing factors. This equity gap analysis also helped me understand that this is a process an equity-driven school principal should implement regularly to truly understand student progress and the various systems within a school that sustain or prevent it. In addition, when I am in the role of school principal, I will be aware of the conversations I have with all educational partners as it is valuable qualitative data that can help me interpret quantitative data, and vice versa. The concerns, ideas, and proposals brought to me by teachers, students, and parents will inform my approach to building collaborative systems that bring about and sustain school improvement.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"120\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1116\"><p>In Step 2 I learned about the responsibility of an equity-driven leader to identify equity gaps and invest the time in analyzing the institutional and structural factors that produce them. Understanding the distinction between institutional and structural factors was also key in developing my proposed strategies. For example, at High School P there are many “commonly accepted and deeply ingrained norms, values, beliefs, systems, and practices” that have been in place for many years due to minimal staff and faculty turnover. The institutional factors have established a culture of long standing practices that I believe contributed to the limited attention given to the EL population over the years thereby sustaining the equity gap. With this in mind, I realized that effective improvement would require a paradigm shift, making it essential for me to develop buy-in strategies to enlist likeminded educational partners committed to change. On the other hand, understanding what structural factors are at High School P gave me a framework to utilize the potential resources available for a potential strategy. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"121\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"77\"><p>Something else I learned during Cycle 1 from my course readings, is the term </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"122\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"633\"><p>“solutionitis” which is used in the book Learning to Improve: How America’s Schools Can Get Better at Getting Better by A. Bryk et.al (2015). This term refers to the tendency of educators to jump quickly to implementing solutions without first deeply understanding a problem. I recognized that I have done this sometimes in the past, therefore, it became imperative for me to develop disciplined awareness of this and eliminate the habit. In my equity gap analysis I developed critical skills that will help me move away from this approach and focus on evidence driven inquiry as the framework for my approach to school improvement. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"123\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"935\"><p>In Step 3 I continued to implement what I learned from my course readings in Learning to Improve by A. Bryk et.al (2015) to narrow in on the contributing factors of the EL equity gap. Using a “Fishbone Diagram” I was able to list several key factors I thought were causing the problem which then created a more broader picture of the systemic issue. After this thorough analysis, I was able to identify two strategies that could address the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students. These strategies came from a deeper understanding of High School P’s culture and context, the feasibility of implementation, and research based evidence on their effectiveness. By incorporating feedback from my principal and PSA colleague, I learned that being an equity-driven leader also means being open to refining strategies, being transparent with educational partners, and ensuring there is buy-in for implementation of new interventions.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"124\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"545\"><p>Lastly, I will continue to use the skills I developed in this cycle in the future when I become a principal. I will refine these skills presently while engaging in PDSA cycles at my school because an equity-driven leader intentionally identifies and addresses the systems that sustain inequalities through data-informed, collaborative action. I will keep my actions student-centered and data-driven, remain neutral and open-minded when gathering qualitative data, and employ democratic processes to remain transparent when looking for buy-in. ] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"126\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"817\"><p>[Based on my experience in Cycle 1, what I learned about myself is that I rely on my background knowledge and experience to immediately attempt to solve a problem. I am now aware of my “solutionitis.” What really helped me come to terms with this was comparing my thought process with my principal’s. When I would speak to him about daily school issues throughout the period of this cycle, I paid attention to his behavior and choice of words when addressing the issues I brought to him. Being neutral, transparent, and always being mindful of employing a democratic process with decision making were traits I picked up on. Considering High School P is a Pilot School, these are essential to his success as our principal. I am now mindful about how I can emulate these traits as I grow into future leadership roles.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"127\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1876\"><p>What I also learned about my areas of strength is that I am an effective data analyzer, critical thinker, and collaborative team player. My ability to work with others shaped my proposed strategies to address the chronic absentee equity gap among EL students because I believe school can bring about effective improvements when faculty and all other educational partners work together rather than individually in classrooms or offices. Another strength is my ability to share my extensive experience in education to build capacity among my colleagues while at the same time remaining open-minded to learn from new experiences and perspectives. My collaboration with my principal and PSA colleague helped reshape my strategies and it was an essential reminder that a different perspective is what helps us learn fast, which is why collaboration should be the foundation of school improvement. These strengths can help me address the equity needs for EL students with a systemic, collaborative, and reflective approach. After learning the protocol of an equity gap analysis I plan to utilize those steps to continue to examine EL student data in other California state indicators. I’d like to see if EL students are experiencing equity gaps in graduation rates, college and career readiness, and SBAC proficiency scores at High School P. If I were to find more gaps, I would bring my findings to the school’s Governance Council so that the matters can be taken up by one of its committees. As the collaborative leader that I am at my school, I would attend the committee meetings and invite other educational partners so that together we create a shared ownership of the equity gap problem. I would ensure that strategic approaches to meeting the needs of the EL population arise from these committee meetings so that this subgroup doesn’t continue to fall through the cracks.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"128\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"443\"><p>This cycle also helped me recognize my areas of growth. I’ve already mentioned my tendency to jump to solutions, so I need to continue developing disciplined awareness of this habit and eliminate it. I also need to strengthen my ability to build a deeper understanding of my school’s existing systems and culture so that I validate the work that is already being done to bring about improvement, rather than blindly suggesting my strategies.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"129\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"551\"><p>In the process of addressing the equity gap among chronic absentee EL students, I have realized the need for me to be more involved in High School P’s governing systems so that I can work on developing my leadership style. I have begun attending the Governance Council meetings and the School Culture Committee as the first steps in learning more about how my school is being led and all the educational partners that are doing work to improve it. This cycle truly helped me understand the value in understanding the school’s culture and context.   ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The reflection recognizes a tendency to propose solutions too quickly and describes learning through feedback and collaboration. The two feedback rounds provide concrete support for collaboration as a strength.</p><p>The claim of effective data analysis needs qualification. Percentage-unit errors and unsupported subgroup inferences are consequential growth areas. A credible reflection can recognize a real strength while explaining an analytical limitation and how it would be addressed.</p><p>The qualitative evidence offers a deeper reflective opportunity: transport and housing accounts can challenge an explanation centered on motivation. Show how attending to that context changes a decision about support, rather than describing context without explaining its effect on leadership.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each claimed strength or growth area with an event, decision, or finding from the cycle.</li><li>Explain one initial interpretation that changed after considering another perspective.</li><li>Show how the school's context shaped an action and define the professional practice that needs improvement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.8: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.8 recognizes evidence-based learning and identified growth areas. Parts of the reflection approach Level 4, but the connection from context to changed leadership behavior needs greater precision.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / 3 / Planning professional learning","url":"#cycle/c1/D3","html":"<span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 3 / Planning professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 1, identify specific professional learning goals for yourself and describe future steps for your professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Turn the learning from this cycle into specific professional goals and future steps. Identify the skill you need to develop, what you will do to develop it, and how you will judge your progress. A new committee role or another data project is a learning opportunity; explain the leadership growth it is intended to produce.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Identify future professional growth grounded in the cycle experience.</li><li>Explain clear strengths and learning needs using relevant evidence.</li><li>Connect future practice to equity leadership and the school's context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"131\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1735\"><p>[After the analysis of my experience in Cycle 1, a specific professional learning goal I have decided to undertake is to implement the equity gap analysis process in attendance rates with other vulnerable student groups at High School P, for example homeless and foster youth, Black students, and Student With Disabilities. My goal is to determine if these groups would also benefit from my proposed strategies, or if other contributing factors affecting their absenteeism would suggest alternative intervention strategies. My goal is to align my strategies with the school’s vision and mission, where all students are given a personalized educational experience. I am really curious to see if these different student groups have similar or different contributing factors. I think those findings could uncover a much deeper understanding of the systems currently in place at High School P. Another professional goal I have to continue my professional growth in leadership is to run for the school’s Governance Council so that I am privy to the institutional issues the school navigates and what the current practices are to address them. As a voting member of the council, I would be able to partake in decision-making discussions with colleagues and experience the democratic collaborative process of coming to a consensus when running a school. This experience would significantly help me develop my approach to building buy-in among colleagues and provide a forum where diverse perspectives are shared. This valuable experience can allow me to practice developing my ability to consider various perspectives before attempting to engage in problem solving. This would be crucial knowledge to bring to my future administrative roles. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-blocks=\"132\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"1559\"><p>Lastly, I hope to lead my own PDSA cycle that is relative to my current position as a coordinator. I am responsible for leading a school committee that will implement a community school plan and the use of its budget to make the plan a reality. Although I am not in an administrative role, this responsibility to implement a schoolwide initiative requires me to truly tap into the critical thinking, collaborative planning, and buy-in development skills I have been exercising throughout this cycle. My first step is to plan the timeline for when the committee will meet for the rest of the school year. The time frame will consider deadlines for survey data review, program implementation goal setting, and outcome reflection before the end of the year. I will set monthly objectives based on the timeline and generate agendas with those objectives and expected measurable outcomes for review at each meeting. My goal is to be prepared for each meeting with data, necessary materials and information to make informed decisions, and always close the meetings with individual and collective action steps which we will report on in our next meeting. After each meeting, I plan to reassess our implementation plan to ensure our actions are aligned, then modify each meeting agenda if need be. I will know if I have been successful in leading this committee at the end of the year if we met the implementation plan’s goals and we have tangible evidence to show for it. I feel the work I did on this cycle has prepared me to do this work in my current position. ] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response proposes further equity analysis, governance participation, and leading a committee inquiry. The meeting timeline and reflection cycle make the intended next steps more concrete.</p><p>The goals would be stronger if each named the leadership skill being developed. Completing meetings or achieving a committee objective does not by itself demonstrate better interpretation of evidence or more inclusive decisions.</p><p>The analytical limitations in this cycle suggest a particularly relevant learning goal: checking indicator definitions and inferences with a knowledgeable colleague before extending the inquiry to new groups.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name the leadership skill, activity, timeframe, and feedback source for each goal.</li><li>Define evidence of improved practice, such as a corrected interpretation or a decision changed by another perspective.</li><li>Include quantitative interpretation and inquiry discipline among the learning needs supported by this example.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score / Rubric 1.8: 3 / 5</h4><p>The overall 3 for Rubric 1.8 is supported by identifiable professional goals and specific next steps. Stronger evidence connecting those goals to contextual leadership learning would improve the case for a higher level.</p></section></section>"}],"anchors":["top","preparation","foundation","A-data","a-q1","part-a","A-group","A-qualitative","qualitative-analysis","A-mission","a-q2","A1","a-q3","A2","a-q4","A3","a-q5","A4","a-q6","A5","a-q7","B1","b-q1","part-b","B2","b-q2","B3","b-q3","B4","b-q4","B5","b-q5","C1","strategy-map","c-q1","part-c","C2","c-q2","C3","c-q3","C4","c-q4","C5","c-q5","D1","d-q1","part-d","D2","d-q2","D3","d-q3","version-alignment","A-sources","source-dashboard","source-lcap","source-eddata","source-sarc","source-quantitative","source-qualitative","source-wasc","source-chks","source-analyzer","source-note-ctc","source-note-lcap","source-note-wasc","source-note-chks","limits","source-record","score-summary","final-check","rubric-map","rubric-1-1","level4-1","rubric-1-2","level4-2","rubric-1-3","level4-3","rubric-1-4","level4-4","rubric-1-5","level4-5","rubric-1-6","level4-6","rubric-1-7","level4-7","rubric-1-8","level4-8"],"aliases":{"part-a":"A-data","a-q1":"A-data","a-q2":"A-mission","a-q3":"A1","a-q4":"A2","a-q5":"A3","a-q6":"A4","a-q7":"A5","part-b":"B1","b-q1":"B1","b-q2":"B2","b-q3":"B3","b-q4":"B4","b-q5":"B5","part-c":"C1","c-q1":"C1","c-q2":"C2","c-q3":"C3","c-q4":"C4","c-q5":"C5","part-d":"D1","d-q1":"D1","d-q2":"D2","d-q3":"D3","foundation":"preparation","rubric-map":"score-summary","final-check":"score-summary","strategy-map":"C1","qualitative-analysis":"A-qualitative"}},"c2":{"title":"Cycle 2: Understanding the Questions","html":"<div class=\"revised-cycle\"><header class=\"intro guide-heading\" id=\"top\"><h1>Cycle 2: Understanding the Questions</h1><p class=\"lead\">Cycle 2: Facilitate collaborative professional learning</p><p>Current questions, plain-language explanations, rubric components, and a critique of the CTC example. Requirements come from the supplied Version 09 templates and handbook.</p></header><details class=\"contents-index\"><summary>Questions and reference</summary><nav aria-label=\"Cycle 2 questions\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/top\" aria-current=\"location\">Introduction</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/preparation\">Before you begin</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A1\">I.1 / Investigating professional learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A2\">I.2 / Implications for facilitation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A3\">II.1 / Selecting the educational focus</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A4\">II.2 / Choosing community members</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A5\">II.3 / Understanding representation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A6\">II.4 / Explaining positionality</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A7\">II.5 / Expected benefits for students</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/B\">Planning meeting agenda</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/C\">Planning meeting minutes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D1\">II.1 / Moving from data to a problem</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D2\">II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D3\">II.3 / Identifying a change in practice</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D4\">II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D5\">III.1 / Selecting one strategy together</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D6\">III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D7\">III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D8\">III.4 / Monitoring implementation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/E1\">Implementation agenda 1</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/E2\">Implementation agenda 2</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/E3\">Implementation agenda 3</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/F1\">Implementation minutes 1</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/F2\">Implementation minutes 2</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/F3\">Implementation minutes 3</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/G\">Collaborative work products</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/H\">Implementation meeting video clips</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I1\">1 / Establishing meeting purpose</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I2\">2 / Evaluating implementation data</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I3\">3 / Reaching shared understanding</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I4\">4 / Supporting equitable participation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I5\">5 / Determining next steps together</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J1\">1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J2\">2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J3\">3 / Responding to school context</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J4\">4 / Professional learning goals</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/alignment\">Template and handbook</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/limits\">Submission requirements</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/source-record\">Source notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/scores\">Score profile</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</a></nav></details><section id=\"preparation\" class=\"preparation major-section\"><span id=\"foundation\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Before you begin</h2><p>Read the whole cycle before convening the group: investigate the educational focus and membership; plan a shared problem and strategy; implement the strategy and examine initial results in at least three implementation meetings; reflect on your facilitation. Plan evidence collection and recording permissions before the meetings occur.</p><p>Agendas show intended work, minutes show what happened, work products show what the group used or produced, and commentary explains your leadership in the clips. These are connected forms of evidence, not interchangeable assignments.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Scores are instructional estimates of the complete rubric, not separate scores for individual questions. Video-dependent estimates are provisional; recordings were not reviewed. The example is evidence to examine, not a model to copy.</p></section><article id=\"A1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Investigating professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe and analyze the role of collaborative professional learning at your school site/educational setting as it relates to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>a.\tProvide information about any formal training offered to professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting within the past three years related to collaborative professional learning, communities of practice, or group learning process.</p><p>b.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting currently engage with one another in professional learning. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of this professional learning in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>c.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators engage with one another in communities of practice. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of the community of practice approach in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>d.\tDescribe how collaborative professional learning reflects evidence-based adult learning processes and cite relevant research to support your analysis.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Answer all four subparts: training offered within three years; current professional learning and its impact; communities of practice and their impact; and the adult-learning principles that explain those practices. A community of practice involves sustained shared inquiry, not simply attendance at a meeting.</p><p>Separate what you observed or found in records from what you expect collaboration might accomplish. When impact evidence is unavailable, explain that limitation rather than presenting a benefit as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Formal training</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"10\"><p>[Within the past three years, our school provided formal training related to collaborative professional learning through the introduction of the PDSA cycle, which stands for Plan, Do, Study, Act. This training was provided last year as part of a schoolwide improvement effort focused on reading comprehension in English Language Arts. Administration presented the purpose of the PDSA cycle during professional development and explained how it supports collaborative inquiry and instructional alignment. A shared slide deck was created for the entire school, and each grade level used the same templates to identify a problem of practice, select a strategy, gather evidence, and analyze student learning. Because the slide deck was shared across the whole school, grade levels could view each other’s work and see how different teams approached the same schoolwide goal. Grade level teams completed the cycle together and documented their thinking within the shared templates. At several points during the year, teams presented their results to the staff, which helped build a common language and a more unified approach to improving reading comprehension. Although the initial training was brief, it provided a structured process for examining student learning and strengthened collaboration across the grade levels.] </p></div><h4>Current professional learning</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"5\"><p>[Professional educators at my school collaborate through several established structures that support shared decision making and instructional alignment. Regular schoolwide professional development, held two or three times each month, provides time for teachers, specialists, coordinators, and administrators to review schoolwide goals, analyze iReady and DIBELS data, prepare for state assessments, and align curriculum expectations. These collaborative structures have a positive impact on student learning by promoting shared understanding of grade-level expectations and supporting more equitable access to instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"6\"><p>Biweekly grade-level meetings during the instructional day allow teachers to discuss current data, student work, and pacing. These conversations often lead to timely reteaching and instructional adjustments, which can positively impact student learning by helping teachers respond more quickly to identified needs. The school also offers up to two hours of optional after-school collaboration each month; however, participation varies due to teacher schedules, which results in inconsistent impact across teams and limits the reach of shared planning practices. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"7\"><p>Teams are beginning to incorporate more structured data conversations, though this practice is still developing. Overall, collaboration has the potential to positively impact student learning and well-being by supporting early identification of academic needs and coordinated supports for English learners and students requiring intervention. At the same time, logistical constraints during professional development sessions limit sustained inquiry. Because teams are expected to meet only when all members are available, some grade levels engage in deeper collaboration than others. Additionally, paraprofessionals are not routinely included in these structures, which limits alignment of instructional supports and reduces the overall impact on student learning. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"8\"><p>Inconsistent use of inquiry routines across teams further contributes to variation in how instructional adjustments are made.] </p></div><h4>Communities of practice</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"12\"><p>[Professional learning has played a central role in shaping collaboration at my school. Historically, most collaboration has occurred during regular professional development meetings and biweekly grade level meetings during the instructional day. These structures have established a routine expectation that teachers meet to analyze data, discuss instruction, and align pacing. This has supported coherence across classrooms and has helped build a culture in which collaboration is viewed as part of the school’s professional norms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"13\"><p>However, the effectiveness of collaboration varies. Some professional development sessions emphasize logistics, which limits deeper inquiry. Optional after school collaboration time also creates challenges because the expectation that all team members must attend prevents some grade levels from meeting consistently. As a result, opportunities for collaboration are uneven across the school. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"14\"><p>The introduction of a schoolwide PDSA cycle last year strengthened collaborative inquiry by providing a clear structure for examining data and sharing results. The shared slide deck helped create transparency across grade levels and encouraged alignment. Overall, professional learning facilitates collaboration when it focuses on instructional improvement, yet it hinders collaboration when structures limit participation or emphasize compliance rather than inquiry.] </p></div><h4>Adult-learning analysis</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"16\"><p>[Collaborative professional learning at my school reflects several principles of evidence based adult learning. Teachers engage in work that is immediately relevant, such as analyzing current data and discussing strategies they are using with students. This aligns with Knowles’ theory of andragogy, which emphasizes that adults learn best when learning is problem-centered, relevant to their professional roles, and connected to real tasks they must perform (Knowles, 1984). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"17\"><p>Grade level collaboration also reflects social learning theory. Vygotsky and Lave and Wenger describe learning as a social process in which individuals build understanding together. During collaborative meetings and the PDSA cycle, teachers share ideas, problem solve, and develop collective expertise. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"18\"><p>The PDSA process reflects elements of effective professional learning identified by Darling Hammond and colleagues, including collaboration, focus on student outcomes, and ongoing reflection. Although not all professional development sessions fully reflect these principles, the structures that center on data and shared inquiry are consistent with research on effective adult learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response provides useful distinctions among schoolwide development, grade-level meetings, optional collaboration, and the PDSA process. It identifies uneven participation, logistical discussions, and the exclusion of paraprofessionals. Those are analytical observations, not just a schedule.</p><p>Its claims that collaboration improves equitable access and student learning need a traceable example: a decision, the resulting change in instruction, and evidence of what students subsequently did. The research discussion explains possible mechanisms, but it does not establish local effects. One training event is described; the three-year account would be clearer with the other years explicitly addressed.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the dates, participants, and purposes of relevant training across the three-year period, including years with no such training.</li><li>Compare two forms of professional learning using a concrete record of a decision, instructional follow-through, and student evidence.</li><li>Use the adult-learning ideas to examine both strengths and weaknesses of the local practices; distinguish established effects from plausible benefits.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The descriptions and analysis of participation barriers support 3. For 4, supply evidence of how different forms of professional learning affected students, not only the benefits they might produce.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Implications for facilitation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tAs a result of your investigation and given the educational setting as well as available data related to student learning and/or well-being, describe the most important issues you will need to consider in preparing to facilitate and support a community of practice to engage in collaborative professional learning.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what the investigation requires you to do as facilitator. Connect each priority to the school context, available student evidence, and this group's likely participation. Do not repeat the entire description from question 1.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"20\"><p>[Several issues must be addressed when preparing to facilitate a community of practice with the team I will be working with. The first issue is the need for consistent participation. Even within a single team, attendance can vary depending on the structure of the meeting or time of day. When all members are not present, the team cannot engage fully in shared inquiry, and this affects the group’s ability to make informed and unified decisions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"21\"><p>The second issue is the need for clearer protocols that support deeper analysis of student learning. At times, collaborative meetings rely on informal conversation and shift toward logistics rather than instructional improvement. To facilitate a community of practice effectively, I will need to implement consistent structures that guide the team to examine data, reflect on strategies, and make collective decisions based on evidence. Strengthening these routines will be essential for ensuring that collaborative time leads to meaningful improvements in student learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Consistent participation and protocols for examining student learning follow logically from the preceding investigation. The response correctly identifies conditions that could prevent the community from moving beyond logistical conversation.</p><p>The proposed response remains broad: implement structures and support attendance. Students need to see how the facilitator will protect time, keep absent voices from disappearing, and bring the discussion back to evidence of mathematics learning.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify an agreed meeting schedule and the school support needed to make participation possible.</li><li>Name the data-discussion routine and explain how it will separate observations, interpretations, and decisions.</li><li>Connect each facilitation choice to a documented concern from the investigation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.1: 3 / 5.</strong> These facilitation priorities support the overall 3 but do not supply the missing impact evidence required for 4. Make the priorities operational and connect them to the investigation.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Selecting the educational focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe the agreed-upon area of educational focus for improving student learning and/or well-being (obtained with guidance from one or more school administrators and/or teacher leaders) selected for the group’s professional learning. Be sure to include a discussion of the relevant data that supports the selection of this focus and the connection between the educational focus and the vision, mission, and/or goals in your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Name the educational focus agreed with an administrator or teacher leader, explain the student data supporting it, and connect it to an actual school commitment. The broad focus is established here; the community jointly defines the more specific problem of practice later.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Three different decisions</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Decision</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Who shapes it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Educational focus</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">The broad area of student learning or well-being that warrants inquiry.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Established with administrator or teacher-leader guidance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Problem of practice</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">A specific, changeable aspect of adult practice identified through evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Defined jointly by the community during planning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Selected strategy</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">One evidence-based response that fits the problem and setting.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Chosen jointly after considering research, evidence, and feasibility.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not arrive with every decision already made. Facilitation creates opportunities for colleagues to challenge an interpretation and influence the response.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"24\"><p>[The agreed-upon area of educational focus for this cycle is mathematics, specifically improving student performance on the Smarter Balanced Assessment. This focus was selected in collaboration with school administration after reviewing district goals and school-level SBA trend data. The district’s numeracy goal requires students in grades three through five to move an average of 40 points closer to proficiency by 2026, with targeted student groups expected to make gains of 50 points. Although our school has demonstrated steady growth and is currently performing above proficiency in mathematics, the most recent Distance from Standard score of 43.7 indicates that performance remains below the district’s projected 2025–26 target. As a result, continued acceleration in mathematics achievement is needed. Prioritizing mathematics aligns with the school’s mission to provide equitable access to rigorous instruction and supports the district’s long-term goal of preparing students for success in higher-level mathematics, including Algebra.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Mathematics is clearly identified, with administrative guidance, a Distance from Standard value of 43.7, and a district numeracy target. The mission connection is coherent.</p><p>The response does not give enough dates and comparison values to verify the claim that performance is below the projected target. A schoolwide achievement measure also needs a bridge to the particular third-grade learners whose instruction the community can influence.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the year, population, source, and comparison target for the Distance from Standard value.</li><li>Explain why the third-grade evidence warrants this focus while keeping schoolwide and grade-level results distinct.</li><li>State the relevant school commitment and the administrator or teacher-leader role in agreeing to the focus.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> This answer provides the data-to-focus and school-goal foundation for the overall 4. The later membership and prospective-benefit answers complete that judgment; this paragraph alone does not earn it.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Choosing community members</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tProvide a list of the job titles for each member of the community of practice group, detailing the specific reason(s) for each member’s inclusion and referencing the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>List each partner's job title and explain the expertise or instructional responsibility that makes that person useful for the mathematics focus. The community requires 3-5 partners in addition to the candidate. Shared availability alone is not a membership rationale.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"26\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS): Included because this teacher leads an SAS class and supports a wide range of learners within the same classroom. This perspective is valuable for selecting mathematics strategies that work for students with varied needs, which aligns with our focus on improving SBA math performance. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"27\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (SAS): Included for expertise with enrichment and accelerated instruction and for her role as the grade-level chair, which supports maintaining rigor and coordinating mathematics practices across the team. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"28\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (General Education): Included to represent the broader range of instructional levels in third grade. This teacher provides insight into scaffolds and supports needed to help students meet the mathematics proficiency target. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"29\"><p>The third grade team was selected because third grade is the first grade to take the SBA in mathematics. Successful collaboration with this group can be extended to upper grades as we continue working toward district numeracy targets.] </p></div><h4>Related evidence: member commitment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"40\"><p>[I obtained each member’s commitment during a grade level meeting by explaining the purpose of the community of practice and the connection to the schoolwide numeracy goal. All teachers agreed to participate because they saw value in improving mathematics instruction and supporting third grade students preparing for the SBA. They viewed the collaboration as an opportunity to strengthen instructional alignment.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Three third-grade teachers bring general education, enrichment, and grade-level leadership perspectives. Their direct responsibility for the students makes the group plausible. The additional commitment passage explains why they agreed to participate.</p><p>The inclusion rationale would be stronger if it distinguished the two SAS teachers' contributions and explained how language-development or disability-related perspectives would enter the inquiry. A member's ethnicity or assignment should not be treated as automatic expertise in every learner's experience.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Differentiate each teacher's specific contribution to identifying and addressing the mathematics need.</li><li>Explain how the group will obtain relevant perspectives not represented in its membership.</li><li>Keep the facilitator distinct from the three partners and retain the actual commitment process.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> The three relevant partners and differentiated inclusion reasons support the membership criterion. The whole Part A estimate is 4; purposeful inclusion of missing viewpoints needs further explanation for 5.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Understanding representation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tProvide a description of each group member’s demographics and how this reflects or is different from your school site/educational setting’s demographics.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe each member's appropriately provided demographic information and compare it with the school community. Explain representation without assuming that shared identity guarantees shared perspectives or that a person speaks for an entire group.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"33\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS) – Hispanic </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"34\"><p>This teacher’s background reflects the school’s largest demographic group, as approximately fifty nine percent of students identify as Latino. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"35\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (SAS) – White </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"36\"><p>This teacher represents a demographic group that makes up about sixteen percent of the school population. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"37\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (General Education) – Armenian </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"38\"><p>Although Armenian is not reported as a separate category in the school demographic chart, our school community includes many Armenian families. This teacher’s background aligns with a notable portion of the school community even though it is not reflected as a standalone category in the official data.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response compares three teachers with the reported student population and explicitly recognizes that Armenian identity is not separately represented in the chart. That is more useful than simply listing identities.</p><p>The categories are not directly comparable: ethnicity and ancestry may be recorded differently. The response needs the chart's date and definitions, and it should explain missing perspectives without inferring identities or attributing educational needs to demographic membership.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source, year, and category definitions for the school demographic comparison.</li><li>Use member-provided information and explain any noncomparable categories.</li><li>Describe how differing perspectives will be sought and respected in the mathematics inquiry.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> The demographic comparison supports the overall 4 when read with the other Part A evidence. Listing identities alone does not demonstrate the respectful inclusion of diverse viewpoints required for 5.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A6\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.4 / Explaining positionality</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tProvide a brief description of your relationship or positionality (e.g., coach, mentor, teacher colleague, administrator) to each member of the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain your working relationship and authority in relation to each partner. Consider how grade-level relationships, status, experience, or prior collaboration may shape participation even when you are not an evaluator.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"31\"><p>[I am a second grade teacher who works directly below this team in the grade level progression, and I collaborate with them as a colleague within the same school. My positionality is that of a peer teacher who prepares students academically before they enter third grade. Because I teach the grade level immediately preceding theirs, I have insight into students’ foundational skills and the instructional experiences that lead into third grade mathematics. I am not in a supervisory or evaluative role, which allows me to participate as an equal member of the community of practice focused on improving mathematics outcomes.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The candidate clearly identifies a peer role as a second-grade teacher preparing students for the third-grade team. The absence of supervisory authority is relevant.</p><p>Calling oneself an equal member does not eliminate differences in grade-level authority or familiarity. The question asks about each relationship; the response largely treats the three partners as one unit.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe any relevant differences in your relationship with each partner.</li><li>Explain how the grade-level chair's role or your feeder-grade position could shape discussion.</li><li>Identify one facilitation choice that protects candid participation without assuming all members feel equally comfortable.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> The peer role and feeder-grade connection support the positionality criterion. The overall 4 relies on the combined Part A evidence; a member-by-member account would make this contribution clearer.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A7\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.5 / Expected benefits for students</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tExplain how the work of this group is likely to advance conditions for students’ learning and/or well-being in relation to the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the pathway from the community's adult learning to improved conditions for students. Identify what teachers may learn or change, what students will experience differently, and why that could help with the mathematics focus. These are expected effects, not completed outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"42\"><p>[The work of this community of practice will improve student learning by strengthening the consistency and quality of mathematics instruction across third grade. Through collaborative inquiry, the team will identify strategies that address numeracy gaps and reinforce foundational skills needed for success on the Smarter Balanced Assessment. Shared planning and reflection will promote more coherent routines, aligned expectations, and targeted supports for diverse learners. As a result, students are more likely to experience consistent instruction, clearer expectations, and increased confidence in mathematics, supporting both academic growth and well-being. Additionally, this collaborative work creates structures for ongoing reflection and adjustment, allowing teachers to regularly examine student evidence and refine instruction in response to emerging needs. By engaging in shared analysis and collective decision-making, educators build a common understanding of effective practices and strengthen their capacity to respond to diverse learners. Over time, this collaborative approach supports more equitable learning conditions by ensuring instructional decisions are intentional, responsive, and grounded in shared responsibility for student progress.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Shared planning, aligned routines, targeted support, and repeated examination of evidence form a credible pathway. This passage supplies the prospective explanation that extends Rubric 2.2 beyond group selection.</p><p>The opening promise that the work will improve learning is stronger than the evidence available at this stage. Greater consistency is useful only if the agreed instruction meets students' different needs; identical instruction is not itself equitable instruction.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use prospective language for effects that have not yet been observed.</li><li>Identify the particular opportunities to learn that coordinated planning should improve.</li><li>Name the student evidence the group will later examine to see whether those opportunities and benefits occurred.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> This prospective pathway is the main Level 4 extension: it explains how the group may advance learning conditions. It should remain a reasoned expectation, not a promise of results.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\"><span id=\"b-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / Planning meeting agenda</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for the planning meeting. You may use either this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nAfter creating the agenda, use it to facilitate meetings with group members’ active involvement to review and analyze data—including student demographics, learning, academic performance, and/or well-being—and other relevant student information related to the selected area of educational focus. Record minutes during the planning meeting, describing what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>a.\tData source(s):</p><p>b.\tDiscussion of data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Prepare the meeting before it occurs. Include who will attend, the purpose, norms, data, discussion of the problem, strategy selection, implementation planning, and next actions. The prompts below are the current optional format; the agenda itself is required.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"47\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[10/13/25] </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[1:30 – 2:30 PM] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"48\"><p>PLC Norms: [Be present.] [Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] [Be open to different perspectives.] [Stay on topic.] PLC Norms: [Be present.] [Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] [Be open to different perspectives.] [Stay on topic.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"49\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Mrs. W.]  </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"51\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Focal Group of Students (i.e., common grade level or demographic): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[Third-grade students, with particular attention to those demonstrating foundational numeracy needs as identified through initial iReady BOY data.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"53\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To analyze available student performance data (including SBA and iReady) to identify a shared problem of practice and collaboratively select one evidence based instructional strategy to support third grade numeracy.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>School commitments</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"56\"><p>[The team will review the district numeracy goal requiring students in grades three through five to move an average of 40 points closer to proficiency on the SBA by 2026. The group will also revisit the school’s mission to provide equitable access to rigorous instruction and support student success in mathematics. iReady growth expectations will be referenced as ongoing indicators of progress toward these goals.] </p></div><h4>Data sources</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"59\"><p>[ •  iReady BOY Grade 3 overall placement data </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"60\"><p>iReady domain-level data (Number and Operations, Algebraic Thinking, Measurement and Data) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"61\"><p>SBA Math Distance-from-Standard trend data] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"63\"><p>[We will collaboratively review the iReady BOY data and domain information to identify trends in student performance and areas where students may need additional support. The team will examine overall placement, domain strengths, and domain gaps to determine which foundational skills may be affecting access to grade level math standards. This discussion will guide us toward identifying a shared problem of practice.] </p></div><h4>Problem and strategy</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"65\"><p>[The team will collaboratively identify a problem of practice based on patterns observed in student performance data. Discussion will focus on instructional areas that may be limiting students’ access to grade level mathematics content, particularly foundational skills that support success in later concepts. The group will work to define a problem of practice that is specific, actionable, and within the team’s instructional control.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"67\"><p>[After identifying the problem of practice, the team will explore possible strategies to address the identified need. We will consider more than one evidence based approach and discuss how each aligns with the data and the needs of our students before selecting one strategy collaboratively.] </p></div><h4>Implementation and next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"69\"><p>[Once the team selects a strategy, we will outline how we will implement it as a community of practice. This plan may include instructional routines, grouping structures, shared materials, monitoring tools, and a timeline for checking progress.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"71\"><p>[The team will determine next steps, including responsibilities, materials needed, how progress will be monitored, and the date of the next collaboration meeting.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The agenda identifies a date, participants, norms, a focal group, and relevant data sources. Its future tense correctly leaves the problem and strategy open to group discussion.</p><p>Broad items such as determining responsibilities or monitoring progress need enough time and a clear expected product. The agenda should not be treated as evidence that participants actually reached those decisions; the minutes and Part D must establish that.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Allocate realistic discussion time to data interpretation, alternatives, and implementation decisions.</li><li>Specify which dated data or student work members will examine and what questions will guide that examination.</li><li>Close with a plan to record owners, deadlines, needed support, and the next meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5. Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The agenda supports the combined planning judgment, but only the minutes and narrative can show that shared analysis and selection occurred. Stronger group-specific evidence is needed for a confident 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\"><span id=\"c-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Planning meeting minutes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: While facilitating your planning meeting with your group members, record minutes of the discussion. Be sure to describe what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part C template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the actual discussion, differing interpretations, agreements, and next steps. Show how the group moved from evidence to a changeable problem and a selected strategy. Minutes are a record of the group's reasoning, not a second agenda or a transcript of everything said.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>School commitments</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"77\"><p>[The meeting began with a review of the school’s mission to provide equitable access to rigorous, high quality instruction for all students. The teachers noted that this mission includes preparing students for future success in higher level mathematics. The group also reviewed the district numeracy goal, which requires students in grades three through five to move an average of forty points closer to proficiency on the Smarter Balanced Assessment by 2026. In addition to the district goal, the team discussed the importance of cultivating strong mathematical understanding early in the year so students can meet iReady’s typical and stretch growth expectations. The group agreed that improving foundational number sense directly supports both the school mission and district goals.] </p></div><h4>Data discussion</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"79\"><p>[Through my facilitation, the team examined the i-Ready BOY data for third grade to better understand current student performance trends. I guided teachers to first look at overall placement results, which showed that only a small percentage of students were performing on or above grade level, while the majority were performing one or more grade levels below. I prompted the group to analyze domain-level data, which revealed significant gaps in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Together, we identified that these domains are foundational for addition and subtraction with regrouping, a major focus of third-grade mathematics and a prerequisite for later mathematical success. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"80\"><p>I supported the team in connecting these data points to classroom evidence by inviting teachers to share observations and early work samples. Through this discussion, teachers noted that many students struggle with place value understanding and regrouping concepts. At the same time, teachers shared that some students are already demonstrating strong conceptual understanding and would benefit from opportunities to extend their thinking. I named this pattern for the group and emphasized that the data reflected a wide range of instructional needs within and across classrooms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"81\"><p>Through my facilitation, I helped the team recognize that the data pointed to two instructional needs occurring simultaneously: targeted support for students who require foundational skill development and intentional opportunities for enrichment for students who are ready to move beyond grade-level expectations. I guided the discussion toward the idea that addressing both needs would require greater instructional consistency and intentional planning across the grade level. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"82\"><p>As a result of this collaborative analysis, the team agreed that students will require focused, intentional instruction to accelerate progress toward both i-Ready growth goals and SBA expectations. I summarized our discussion by highlighting that the data supports the need for a structured, shared approach that allows teachers to respond to varied student needs while maintaining coherence across classrooms. This collective understanding set the foundation for identifying an instructional strategy that could support differentiated instruction and equitable access to grade-level mathematics.] </p></div><h4>Problem of practice</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"84\"><p>[Through my facilitation of data analysis and collaborative discussion, the group identified the following problem of practice: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"85\"><p>Students lack foundational number sense skills, indicating a need for teachers to provide more consistent and targeted instruction in early numeracy to support access to grade-level mathematics and progress toward iReady growth and SBA proficiency. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"86\"><p>As I guided the team in examining iReady BOY data, domain-level results, and early classroom work samples, I supported teachers in identifying patterns showing that many students were performing below grade level, particularly in areas related to number and operations that underpin addition and subtraction with regrouping. I prompted the group to consider how these foundational gaps limit students’ ability to engage with grade-level content and contribute to persistent performance differences across student groups. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"87\"><p>Through this facilitated discussion, teachers noted that while many students require foundational support, others are performing at or above grade level and need opportunities for enrichment. I helped the group recognize that this range of performance requires intentional instructional shifts, particularly in how students are grouped and how foundational skills are reinforced. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"88\"><p>The team agreed that this problem of practice is specific, actionable, and directly aligned to district and school goals for mathematics achievement. Addressing it requires intentional changes in instructional practice, including more strategic grouping and targeted support, to ensure all students have equitable access to rigorous mathematics instruction and opportunities for accelerated growth.] </p></div><h4>Strategy selection</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"90\"><p>[To address the identified problem of practice, I intentionally introduced and shared two research-based instructional strategies that could support improved numeracy outcomes. I facilitated discussion around how each strategy aligned with our student data and instructional context. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"91\"><p>First, I shared research on flexible, skill-based grouping using the NCSM article Supporting All </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"92\"><p>Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. I highlighted how this approach allows teachers to group students temporarily based on current skill needs, adjust groups as students progress, and provide targeted instruction without permanently tracking students. I prompted the team to consider how flexible grouping could support both students who need foundational instruction and those who are ready for enrichment. Teachers noted that this approach would allow them to better address the wide range of readiness levels present in their classrooms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"93\"><p>Next, I shared a second research-based strategy focused on daily math reasoning routines, drawing from the Heinemann blog Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. I guided the team in discussing how routines such as Number Talks can strengthen conceptual understanding, mathematical discourse, and number sense, particularly for English learners and students who benefit from structured opportunities to explain their thinking. Teachers discussed how these routines could complement core instruction by building reasoning and language skills. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"94\"><p>After reviewing both strategies, I facilitated a discussion to help the team determine which approach best aligned with the identified problem of practice and their current instructional realities. Through this discussion, the group collaboratively decided to implement flexible grouping as the primary strategy, recognizing that it directly supports differentiated instruction across multiple performance levels. The team agreed to refer to this approach locally as Cruz’s Math Crew, while acknowledging that it represents a research-based model of flexible, skillbased grouping supported by NCSM. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"95\"><p>Teachers expressed that this strategy was particularly appropriate because their classrooms include students with a wide range of needs, from those requiring foundational support in addition and subtraction with regrouping to those ready for more advanced problem-solving. By adopting this approach, the team agreed they could provide targeted instruction while also offering enrichment opportunities, ensuring all students have access to meaningful and appropriately challenging math instruction.] </p></div><h4>Implementation</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"97\"><p>[After the group selected flexible grouping as the strategy to address the identified problem of practice, I facilitated the development of an initial implementation plan focused on supporting students’ foundational skills in addition and subtraction with regrouping. I guided the team to narrow the instructional focus so that implementation would be manageable, coherent, and aligned across classrooms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"98\"><p>As a group, we agreed that the first cycle of Cruz’s Math Crew would target addition and subtraction with regrouping, as this skill represents a foundational prerequisite for later gradelevel work and was identified as an area of need in both assessment data and classroom observations. During the meeting, I supported the team in collaboratively creating a short preassessment designed to measure students’ understanding of place value and regrouping concepts. Teachers agreed to administer this preassessment prior to the next meeting and to use the results to inform instructional grouping. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"99\"><p>I facilitated discussion around how groups would function and emphasized that grouping would be temporary and flexible. Teachers agreed that some groups would focus on foundational numeracy skills such as composing and decomposing numbers, understanding place value relationships, and using visual or concrete models to support regrouping. Other groups would provide enrichment and extended problem solving opportunities for students who demonstrate readiness for more advanced work. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"100\"><p>To support coherence across classrooms, I guided the team in identifying shared expectations for implementation. Teachers agreed to use a consistent, protected instructional time for Cruz’s Math Crew, along with common language, routines, and visual models. I also encouraged collaborative planning and resource sharing so that instruction remained aligned while allowing teachers flexibility to respond to student needs. The group agreed that the length of the instructional cycle would be determined after reviewing preassessment data, and that a postassessment would be administered at the end of the cycle to evaluate the impact of the strategy.] </p></div><h4>Next actions</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"102\"><p>[To move the work forward, I facilitated agreement on clear next steps and responsibilities. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"103\"><p>Teachers will administer the regrouping preassessment and score it prior to the next meeting. They will bring student results, initial observations, and questions about grouping or instruction to support collaborative analysis. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"104\"><p>At the next meeting, the team will use the preassessment data to finalize instructional groupings for Cruz’s Math Crew and determine the duration of the first implementation cycle. Teachers will also identify or refine instructional materials and strategies aligned to the needs of each group. Together, the team will plan the post-assessment that will be used to measure student progress and inform future instructional decisions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"105\"><p>Throughout this process, I will continue to facilitate reflection and collaboration by prompting discussion around patterns in the data, supporting alignment across classrooms, and helping the team make shared decisions about adjustments to instruction. These next steps ensure that implementation remains responsive, data-informed, and focused on improving student access to grade-level mathematics.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The minutes describe analysis of i-Ready data, variation in readiness, comparison of flexible grouping with reasoning routines, and plans for a common assessment. They offer a coherent rationale for Cruz's Math Crew.</p><p>Repeated statements that the candidate facilitated the discussion tell less than a specific contribution that changed the decision. The record also needs clearer numbers for the named groups, concrete action owners, and an unambiguous timeline for assessment design and administration.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record specific member contributions, differing views, and how agreement was established.</li><li>Attach the actual group-level data or work used to define the need.</li><li>List each agreed action with its owner, due date, and evidence to bring back.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5. Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The recorded reasoning supports 3 across the planning evidence. Actual comparisons among student groups and more specific group-level strategy effects are needed to establish the respective Level 4 extensions.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.1 / Moving from data to a problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you: (a) worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the educational focus; (b) selected a problem of practice based on the area of educational focus; and (c) addressed how practitioners could potentially improve student learning and/or well-being by improving the current practices at your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the collaborative reasoning: which data the group examined, what members noticed, which problem they selected, and how changing current instruction could help. Keep the educational focus, problem of practice, and selected strategy distinct.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"112\"><p>[During the planning meeting, I facilitated a structured review of multiple third grade data sources connected to our mathematics focus. I began by guiding the team through iReady BOY results and SBA numeracy trend data to establish a shared understanding of current performance. I prompted teachers to examine the percentage of students performing below grade level and to look closely at domain level results, which revealed notable gaps in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. I supported the group in identifying patterns across classrooms and encouraged teachers to share observations from their daily instruction to add context to the data. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"113\"><p>To deepen the analysis, I asked guiding questions such as, “What patterns do we notice across the grade level?” and “Which foundational skills seem to be limiting students’ access to grade level math?” These questions helped teachers connect quantitative data with their classroom experiences. Teachers noted that many students struggle with composing and decomposing numbers, understanding place value, and applying regrouping strategies, which directly affects their ability to engage with grade level content. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"114\"><p>I facilitated discussion that helped the group recognize that while many students need foundational support, there are also students performing at or above grade level who require enrichment and opportunities to extend their thinking. Through this collaborative analysis, I supported the team in identifying a shared problem of practice focused on strengthening foundational number sense through more consistent and targeted instructional practices. Together, we concluded that addressing this need would require intentional instructional shifts, particularly in how students are grouped and how foundational skills are reinforced across classrooms to support progress toward iReady growth and SBA expectations.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies i-Ready and SBA evidence, guiding questions, teacher observations about place value, and the need to support both foundational and advanced learners. It connects analysis with an instructional change.</p><p>It would be stronger with one concrete example of a teacher's interpretation influencing the agreed problem. A repeated claim of collaboration is less persuasive than a visible decision trail. The problem should foreground instructional provision rather than defining students as deficient.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Show one data pattern and the member discussion that shaped the problem.</li><li>State what is inconsistent or insufficient in current instruction and what the group can change.</li><li>Connect the selected problem to the shared evidence without treating the strategy as already decided.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> Shared data analysis and recognition of a changeable practice support 3. Concrete evidence of differences across groups and their influence on the problem would strengthen the case for 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how you worked collaboratively with the group to review your collected data on student demographics, student learning and academic performance or well-being, and other relevant data differentiated by student groups.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group compared data across student groups. Identify the measures, populations, and findings, then explain what members made of the differences. Mentioning several group names does not demonstrate a differentiated analysis.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"117\"><p>[To deepen the analysis, I facilitated a review of student demographic and performance data across subgroups, including English learners, students with disabilities, and students without identified needs. We examined iReady BOY results and discussed patterns showing that a large proportion of students were performing below grade level in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Teachers noted that these gaps were evident across classrooms and student groups, suggesting a systemic instructional need rather than an isolated concern. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"118\"><p>I supported the group in analyzing how these data connected to classroom observations and student work. Teachers shared that many students struggled with place value understanding and regrouping, which limited their ability to access grade level math tasks. At the same time, teachers noted that some students were already demonstrating stronger skills and needed opportunities to extend their thinking. This discussion highlighted the wide range of learners within each classroom. </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage names English learners, students with disabilities, and students without identified needs, then reports broad gaps in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Teacher observations add context about place value and readiness.</p><p>No group-specific counts, rates, scores, or work examples are given here. The reader cannot determine whether patterns differ across groups or whether the proposed response follows from those differences. This is the central barrier to a confident Level 4 judgment for Rubric 2.3.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide the actual results for at least two relevant groups, with dates and denominators or clearly described work evidence.</li><li>Explain how overlapping group memberships and different measures affect comparison.</li><li>Show how the group used the differences to refine the problem rather than simply reporting them.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> This is the central limit on moving from 3 to 4: the response names groups but does not present their differentiated performance evidence or show how actual differences shaped the decision.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.3 / Identifying a change in practice</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe the ways you facilitated the meeting to acknowledge how the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current practices in your professional context.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how you helped the group recognize that something adults currently do can change. Name the instructional practice under discussion and show how members connected that change to the evidence. This is separate from merely identifying low performance.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"119\"><p>Through my facilitation, I prompted the team to consider how current instructional structures might be limiting our ability to respond effectively to this range of needs. I asked questions such as, “What instructional practices might help us respond more intentionally to different readiness levels?” and “How might we adjust our structures so students receive support that aligns more closely with their skill gaps?” These questions helped the group recognize that addressing the problem of practice would require shifts in how instruction is organized and delivered, particularly in how students are grouped and supported during math instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"120\"><p>As a result of this collaborative discussion, the team agreed on the following problem of practice: Students lack foundational number sense skills, indicating a need for more consistent and targeted instruction in early numeracy to support access to grade level math content and progress toward iReady growth and SBA proficiency.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The questions about instructional structures and readiness move the discussion toward practices the team can change. These passages directly address the current standalone question, so no invented response is needed.</p><p>The final problem still begins with students lacking number sense. It should identify the current instructional opportunity that is missing or inconsistent, supported by classroom evidence, rather than imply that flexible grouping is justified solely by low scores.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the current practice the team agreed was limiting access to targeted instruction.</li><li>Record what members said that showed their recognition of the needed change.</li><li>Explain why the proposed change follows from the evidence, keeping alternative explanations visible.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> The questions about instructional structures help establish the Level 3 requirement to recognize a change in practice. This does not replace the differentiated data needed for 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow does the identified problem of practice: (a) correspond to the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/educational setting; (b) reflect California state standards and/or curriculum frameworks or equivalent, or other applicable standards; and (c) address existing differences in student group performance or well-being?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three connections: school commitments, relevant standards or frameworks, and actual differences in student learning or well-being. Explain how each connection supports the selected problem, not just that the problem aligns with them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"122\"><p>[The problem of practice corresponds directly to our school’s mission of providing equitable access to rigorous instruction and preparing students for success in higher level mathematics. Focusing on foundational numeracy supports the district’s long term numeracy goals and aligns with the California Mathematics Framework, which emphasizes conceptual understanding, fluency with numbers, and flexible problem-solving. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"123\"><p>Addressing early numeracy gaps also responds to existing performance differences. Third grade data showed that students across multiple subgroups struggle with foundational skills, with students performing below grade level needing accelerated progress to meet annual growth expectations. By focusing on foundational number sense, the problem of practice aims to reduce these differences and ensure all students, including those who are already on track, receive instruction that advances their learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response links numeracy with equitable access and references the mathematics framework's conceptual understanding and flexible problem-solving. It recognizes the need to support students at different readiness levels.</p><p>The standards connection remains broad, and the group differences are asserted without comparative evidence. A named grade-level learning expectation and an example of what students can or cannot yet do would make the alignment concrete.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the relevant grade-level learning expectation and the work that reveals difficulty with it.</li><li>Connect the school goal to the instructional opportunity the group can change.</li><li>Use actual group differences to explain why the problem matters for equitable access.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> The school-goal and framework connections support the problem rationale. The combined estimate remains 3 because the performance differences are not sufficiently demonstrated.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.1 / Selecting one strategy together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tExplain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting in selecting one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice, potentially leading to improved student learning and/or well-being across student groups. Be sure to include how you facilitated the group to help make their selection.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group considered and selected one relevant evidence-based strategy. Include the alternatives, criteria, member contributions, and your facilitation. The group's decision is the object of analysis, not just your preferred strategy.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"126\"><p>[Once the problem of practice was established, I facilitated a discussion of evidence-based strategies that could address the identified numeracy gaps. I introduced two options for consideration: skill-based regrouping (academies), supported by the NCSM article Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices, and daily math reasoning routines such as </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"127\"><p>Number Talks, supported by the Heinemann article Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. I guided the group to examine how each strategy would function in practice by prompting discussion around alignment to student needs, feasibility, and the ability to differentiate instruction across multiple performance levels. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"128\"><p>To support collaborative decision making, I asked questions such as, “Which approach best targets the specific gaps we are seeing in addition and subtraction?”, “Which strategy allows us to meet the wide range of learners in our classrooms?”, and “Which approach can we implement consistently as a team?” Through this discussion, teachers noted that flexible grouping would allow them to reorganize students based on current skill needs, provide targeted instruction, and offer enrichment for students already demonstrating proficiency. The group reached consensus to select flexible grouping, which we named Cruz’s Math Crew, as the strategy most likely to address foundational numeracy gaps while supporting differentiated instruction for all learners.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example compares flexible grouping with Number Talks and reports questions about fit, feasibility, and differentiation. It explains why teachers preferred temporary skill-based groups.</p><p>The two named articles function as evidence claims in the example, not independently verified research in this review. The response needs to explain what the supporting evidence says and how the proposed mathematics instruction will work inside the groups. Regrouping students is an organizational decision; the learning opportunities within those groups matter.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the specific instructional features the selected strategy will include.</li><li>Show how members evaluated the evidence and considered an alternative.</li><li>Document how shared agreement was reached, including concerns or conditions attached to the choice.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The comparison and reported joint selection support 3. A stronger strategy description should explain the expected instructional benefit for specific groups, not just why regrouping is feasible.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D6\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the group’s rationale for selecting this evidence-based strategy. How will the group learn this strategy and how to implement it? What challenges do you anticipate the group might encounter and how could they be addressed?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the group chose the strategy, how teachers will learn to use it, and how anticipated challenges will be addressed. Prior exposure to grouping is not the same as a plan to learn a particular instructional approach.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"130\"><p>[The teachers selected the flexible grouping strategy because it allows them to differentiate more effectively for the wide range of skill levels present in each classroom. The team emphasized that their classes include students performing well below, on, and above grade level, and that regrouping into temporary skill-based groups offers the most realistic way to provide targeted instruction that meets each student’s needs. They also noted that Grades K–2 have previously implemented flexible grouping systems for ELA instruction, which provided a successful model for meeting diverse learner needs. Because of this prior experience, I am able to support the team, and they identified opportunities to seek guidance from K–2 teachers who have used the approach extensively. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"131\"><p>To support implementation, the group collaboratively created a short pre-assessment during the meeting. This assessment will determine each student’s placement within Cruz’s Math Crew by identifying gaps in addition and subtraction with regrouping, as well as underlying place value concepts. Teachers plan to administer and score the pre-assessment before the next PLC meeting so the team can use the data to design groups and determine instructional foci. The team also discussed instructional resources, routines, manipulatives, and strategies they will use, and they expressed interest in observing one another’s groups during the implementation period. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"132\"><p>Anticipated challenges include securing consistent instructional time for group implementation, maintaining the flexibility of groups as students progress, and ensuring that instruction remains aligned across classrooms. To address these concerns, the team agreed to use common routines, shared language, and regular check ins during upcoming meetings. These structures will help ensure that instruction is cohesive, groups adjust responsively based on data, and the strategy remains manageable for all teachers throughout the cycle.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies prior K-2 experience, access to experienced colleagues, a common pre-assessment, shared routines, and scheduling challenges. Those are useful implementation conditions.</p><p>Interest in observing one another is not a scheduled learning plan. The explanation also needs to show how language supports, disability accommodations, enrichment, and movement between groups will work without creating fixed tracks.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify a rehearsal, observation, or collaborative planning activity with a date and responsible person.</li><li>Define the instructional routine, evidence for changing groups, and safeguards against permanent low-expectation placement.</li><li>Match anticipated challenges for specific learners with feasible supports.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The rationale and proposed common routines support 3, but the account does not fully detail how the strategy will affect specific groups. A practical learning plan would also make the expected implementation more credible.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D7\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat improvements for student learning and/or well-being related to the problem of practice do the group expect the selected evidence-based strategy will yield?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe what different students are expected to learn or experience because of the selected strategy. Explain the instructional mechanism: what teachers will do differently and why that should address the identified need. Keep predictions distinct from outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"134\"><p>[The group expects that the flexible skill based grouping strategy will lead to several meaningful improvements in student learning. By providing targeted instruction aligned to students’ specific numeracy needs, teachers anticipate that students who struggle with regrouping in addition and subtraction will develop stronger foundational number sense, increased accuracy, and improved confidence when working with multi-digit operations. Students performing at or above grade level will also benefit from enrichment groups that extend mathematical reasoning and problem solving, ensuring all learners receive instruction that matches their current readiness level. The group believes that this strategy will lead to greater student engagement, reduced frustration during core instruction, and more equitable access to grade level content. Overall, the strategy is expected to support academic growth and enhance students’ sense of competence and well being in mathematics.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response distinguishes students needing regrouping support from those ready for enrichment. Accuracy, reasoning, confidence, and reduced frustration are plausible targets.</p><p>The benefits are mostly broad expectations. A stronger explanation would identify the actual learning task or support for each group and the evidence that would indicate success. Confidence and engagement need their own evidence; they cannot be inferred from arithmetic scores alone.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each group's observed need to a specific instructional action and expected benefit.</li><li>Identify how conceptual reasoning, procedural accuracy, and enrichment will each be examined.</li><li>Use an appropriate source for any well-being or engagement claim.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The response distinguishes broad readiness levels but gives limited detail about the instruction and expected learning in each. That makes 3 more defensible than the detailed cross-group impact explanation required for 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D8\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.4 / Monitoring implementation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow will you facilitate the group in determining a monitoring process to understand the impact of the evidence-based strategy? Describe how you and the group plan to monitor and analyze initial implementation.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group will check both whether the strategy was implemented and what students initially learned. Specify evidence, timing, interpretation, and decision rules. The monitoring process must be manageable and jointly understood.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"136\"><p>[To determine the impact of the selected strategy, I will facilitate the group in developing a clear and manageable monitoring process. During the planning meeting, the team agreed to use a collaboratively created pre-assessment as the baseline measure for regrouping students within Cruz’s Math Crew. Before the next meeting, teachers will administer and score the preassessment, and we will review the results together to finalize group placements. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"137\"><p>To monitor initial implementation, the group plans to collect and share multiple forms of evidence, including student work samples, exit tickets, observation notes, and informal checks tied to regrouping and place value skills. I will guide the team in using these data to identify patterns, adjust grouping as needed, and note early signs of progress or challenges. At the beginning of each implementation meeting, I will facilitate a brief review of the collected evidence to help the group analyze the effectiveness of instruction and determine whether any adaptations are needed. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"138\"><p>The team also agreed to establish a defined instructional window for using Cruz’s Math Crew before giving a post-assessment. This post-assessment will allow the group to compare before and after data and evaluate the strategy’s early impact on student understanding. Through structured reflection, shared data analysis, and consistent check-ins, the group will maintain a clear process for monitoring progress and adjusting instruction to support all learners.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>A common baseline, work samples, exit tickets, observation notes, and a post-assessment provide a useful outline. Reviewing evidence at every implementation meeting connects monitoring with action.</p><p>The response leaves the instructional window undefined and does not establish common scoring, matched populations, or criteria for regrouping. Later concerns about assessment rigor make these omissions consequential: a before-and-after comparison is only interpretable when the measures and populations are understood.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Agree on dates, participants, scoring criteria, and the relationship between pre- and post-assessments.</li><li>Record how often and how the strategy was used, alongside student results.</li><li>Set a decision rule for adapting instruction and specify who will bring evidence to each meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> Monitoring strengthens the credibility of the strategy plan, but does not by itself establish the group-specific impact explanation needed for 4. The present plan lacks enough precision to resolve later measurement concerns.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan discussion of evidence from the strategy's initial use, not only its preparation. Include the required meeting information, norms, evidence review, summary, and next steps. A summary item on an agenda means planning time to summarize; it does not mean writing the conclusions in advance.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the implementation meetings</summary><p>Each meeting should carry the inquiry forward: revisit the previous commitment, examine evidence from actual implementation, compare interpretations, decide what to retain or adjust, and establish responsibilities and support. The next meeting examines what followed. These are implementation meetings, not repeated planning sessions.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Record</th><th scope=\"col\">What it should make visible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Evidence returning to the group</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">What was tried, what students demonstrated, and what is still uncertain. Include difficulties and counterexamples.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Shared interpretation and decision</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">How members used the evidence, where they disagreed, and why the group retained or adjusted the approach.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Support and follow-through</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">Who will do what, what help is needed, and what evidence the group will revisit. Later minutes record what actually happened.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An agenda anticipates this work. Minutes document it; products preserve the tools or analysis the group actually used. They should agree without copying one another.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Meeting information</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"144\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Grade Level(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[3  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[11/5/25  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[2:30 – 3:30 PM  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>PLC Norms: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be present.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be open to different perspectives.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Stay on topic.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. W.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"146\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To check in on the initial implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, review early information that will be shared from the preassessment </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>and classroom observations, and collaboratively reflect on how the strategy may be supporting students with varying levels of readiness in addition and subtraction with regrouping.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Early returns and new data</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"148\"><p>[The team will share early information related to the implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"149\"><p>Whether the preassessment has been administered and initial observations teachers plan to share about student performance </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"150\"><p>Initial patterns noticed across classrooms  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"151\"><p>Observations about student readiness levels and variation within classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"152\"><p>Early experiences with launching the groups, including transitions, routines, and expectations </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"153\"><p>The discussion will focus on identifying emerging trends and questions rather than drawing conclusions, since implementation has just begun.] </p></div><h4>Planned summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"155\"><p>[As a group, we will summarize key points discussed during the meeting, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"156\"><p>Common patterns noticed across classrooms related to student understanding of addition and subtraction with regrouping </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"157\"><p>Early strengths and challenges observed during the rollout of Cruz’s Math Crew </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"158\"><p>Initial observations related to student engagement, routines, and collaboration across classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"159\"><p>Any shared questions or areas that may need continued attention as implementation moves forward </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"160\"><p>The facilitator will support the group in naming patterns, highlighting shared observations, and confirming common understanding.] </p></div><h4>Planned next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"162\"><p>[The group will identify next steps to support continued implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, which may include: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"163\"><p>Continuing to implement flexible, leveled groups focused on addition and subtraction with regrouping </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"164\"><p>Continuing to refine routines and expectations for student transitions between classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"165\"><p>Monitoring student progress and gathering additional evidence from instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"166\"><p>Bringing updated observations and student work to the next meeting </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"167\"><p>Identifying any supports or adjustments needed to strengthen implementation </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"168\"><p>The team will confirm the focus for the next meeting and clarify what data or reflections members should bring.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The agenda identifies the first implementation check-in, the named strategy, launch routines, and emerging questions. Its caution against early conclusions is appropriate.</p><p>Some planned discussion concerns whether the pre-assessment has been administered or groups have started. Clarify which implementation has actually occurred by this meeting so the discussion can examine new evidence from use of the strategy, not remain another planning session.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify what instruction will have occurred before the meeting and what new evidence members will bring.</li><li>Reserve time to distinguish launch logistics from students' mathematical understanding.</li><li>Plan to record agreed actions, support, owners, and the next review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> This agenda plans relevant activity but cannot demonstrate facilitation or shared interpretation by itself. The provisional 3 uses the later minutes and commentary; actual implementation and video evidence remain essential.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use the second agenda to revisit the first meeting's decisions and examine new evidence. Ask what was tried, what students did, and what may need adjustment. Repeating the same agenda headings is appropriate; repeating a generic discussion without follow-through is not.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Meeting information</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"173\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Grade Level(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[ 3 ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[11/19/25  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[2:30 – 3:30 PM  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>PLC Norms: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be present.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be open to different perspectives.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Stay on topic.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. W.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"175\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To reflect on early implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, analyze how students are responding within leveled groups, and determine whether instructional or grouping adjustments may be needed to better support student learning.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Early returns and new data</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"177\"><p>[The team will share early information related to the implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"178\"><p>Teachers will share observations from early Cruz’s Math Crew sessions </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"179\"><p>Discussion of how students appear to be responding within their current groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"180\"><p>Identification of early evidence of engagement, progress, or continued challenges </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"181\"><p>Consideration of whether any students may need to move between groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"182\"><p>Sharing instructional strategies being used with different groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"183\"><p>Discussion of how lessons are being planned and differentiated for each group </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"184\"><p>The discussion will focus on identifying emerging patterns and early indicators rather than making final judgments about effectiveness.] </p></div><h4>Planned summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"186\"><p>[As a group, we will summarize key themes that emerge from the discussion, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"187\"><p>Common patterns observed across classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"188\"><p>Areas of success related to student engagement and instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"189\"><p>Instructional or grouping adjustments under consideration </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"190\"><p>Confirmation of whether the current focus and strategy continue to align with student needs </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"191\"><p>The facilitator will help synthesize these themes by naming patterns across classrooms, highlighting shared observations, and checking for agreement to ensure a common understanding of how implementation is progressing.] </p></div><h4>Planned next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"193\"><p>[The group will identify next steps to support continued implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, which may include: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"194\"><p>Agreeing on potential group movement or instructional adjustments </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"195\"><p>Continuing implementation with planned lessons for each group </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"196\"><p>Monitoring student progress and engagement across groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"197\"><p>Determining whether to extend the current instructional cycle </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"198\"><p>Identifying data or student work to bring to the next meeting </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"199\"><p>The team will confirm expectations for what information to bring to the next meeting and identify any supports needed to strengthen implementation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>This agenda turns toward student responses, grouping changes, and differentiated instruction. It recognizes that early results should guide revision rather than a final effectiveness claim.</p><p>The connection to the previous meeting remains implicit. The agenda should identify which agreed actions are returning for review and the evidence needed to decide whether group movement or an extension of the cycle is warranted.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Bring forward specific actions agreed in meeting 1 and identify their owners.</li><li>Specify the work or assessment evidence needed to evaluate each proposed grouping change.</li><li>Plan discussion of different student experiences, including students not benefiting yet.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The planned revisit is relevant to the provisional 3. A 4 requires demonstrated inclusion or sustained follow-through, depending on the rubric, not simply agenda items inviting them.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan a shared examination of initial results and a decision about what comes next. Compare meaningful evidence across the implementation period and retain uncertainty about causes or measures. The final scheduled meeting should lead to specific action, not only celebration.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Meeting information</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"204\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Grade Level(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[3  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[12/10/25  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[2:30 – 3:30 PM  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>PLC Norms: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be present.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be open to different perspectives.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Stay on topic.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. W.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"206\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To review post-assessment results from the implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, examine evidence of student progress and ongoing needs, and collaboratively determine next instructional steps to strengthen math instruction across groups.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Results to examine</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"208\"><p>[The team will review post-assessment data and observations gathered after several weeks of implementing Cruz’s Math Crew. Discussion will include: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"209\"><p>Sharing post-assessment results from each classroom </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"210\"><p>Discussing general trends in student performance (e.g., maintenance, improvement, or areas of continued need) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"211\"><p>Identifying patterns related to regrouping, word problems, and foundational skills </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"212\"><p>Sharing observations about how students are responding to the group structure and instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"213\"><p>Considering variation across groups and levels] </p></div><h4>Planned synthesis</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"215\"><p>[As a group, we will synthesize key takeaways from the discussion, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"216\"><p>Common patterns observed across classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"217\"><p>Reflections on how students are responding to Cruz’s Math Crew </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"218\"><p>Strengths of the current implementation </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"219\"><p>Questions or areas that may need further attention or refinement </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"220\"><p>Shared understandings that emerge from the discussion </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"221\"><p>The facilitator will support the group in naming patterns across classrooms and confirming a shared understanding of student progress and areas for refinement.] </p></div><h4>Planned next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"223\"><p>[The team will identify and agree on next instructional actions, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"224\"><p>Discussing potential instructional adjustments based on post-assessment results </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"225\"><p>Considering whether to extend or refine the current cycle </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"226\"><p>Identifying possible focus areas for upcoming instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"227\"><p>Discussing how future preassessments might be refined to better target standards </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"228\"><p>Determining what data or reflections members should bring to the next meeting] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The agenda appropriately includes post-assessment results, variation across groups, continuing needs, and refinement of future assessments. It leaves conclusions open for group discussion.</p><p>The phrase maintenance or improvement needs separate categories in the evidence brought to the meeting. A combined count can hide students who made no measured gain, while class averages can hide learners whose performance declined.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Prepare separate counts for improved, unchanged, and lower scores, with the matched student denominator.</li><li>Bring the assessment tasks and scoring criteria so rigor and alignment can be examined.</li><li>Plan to decide which instruction continues, changes, or ends and how the next decision will be monitored.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The agenda creates an opportunity to examine results and decide next steps. The provisional 3 depends on evidence of what the group actually did; a plan for synthesis is not evidence of consensus.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Document what the first implementation meeting actually established: evidence from instruction, what is working, needed changes, member reflections, and agreed support. Include enough specificity for the next meeting to check follow-through.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What is going well</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"240\"><p>[Teachers reported that the initial implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew has begun smoothly. All participants administered the preassessment focused on addition and subtraction with regrouping. Teachers shared that student performance varied widely across classrooms, confirming the need for flexible grouping. Several teachers noted that while some students demonstrated strength with addition, many struggled with subtraction with regrouping, particularly when word problems were involved. English Learners were identified as needing additional support with academic language embedded in problem solving. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"241\"><p>Teachers also shared early successes related to implementation logistics. Students were able to transition between classrooms, expectations were clearly communicated, and routines such as name tags and lining up procedures supported smoother movement. Teachers noted that practicing transitions and clearly teaching expectations helped students feel more comfortable during rotations. I highlighted these early successes as indicators that the structure was supporting access and predictability for students.] </p></div><h4>What needs adjustment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"243\"><p>[Teachers noted that some procedural elements still need refinement, including ensuring consistent use of name tags and reinforcing expectations across classrooms. A need was identified to continue supporting students who struggle with language demands in math, especially during word problems. Teachers also discussed the importance of continued communication among staff when students require additional behavioral or safety supports, such as notifying colleagues about students who may elope. These adjustments were viewed as necessary to strengthen consistency and safety as implementation continues.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"245\"><p>I prompted discussion about how these adjustments could be addressed through clearer communication routines and shared expectations, and I documented agreed upon areas for follow up.] </p></div><h4>Participant reflections</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"247\"><p>[I prompted reflection by asking teachers to share observations from the preassessment and their early experiences implementing Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers reflected that the preassessment outcomes were largely consistent with expectations and confirmed gaps that had been previously suspected based on iReady and SBA data. They noted that working collaboratively and sharing expectations increased their confidence in implementing the strategy and helped ensure consistency across classrooms. Several teachers shared that students appeared to enjoy learning with different teachers, which positively impacted engagement. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"248\"><p>I acknowledged the team’s decision to name the strategy Cruz’s Math Crew and highlighted how connecting the work to an existing schoolwide character and growth mindset framework supported student buy-in. I reinforced the importance of using shared language and common expectations so students would understand the purpose of the groups and experience predictable routines across classrooms. Teachers reflected that this shared approach helped students transition more smoothly and begin to take ownership of expectations. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"249\"><p>To support implementation, I encouraged teachers to communicate proactively about student needs, particularly for students requiring additional behavioral or academic supports. Teachers discussed how sharing information about students, such as behavioral considerations or transition needs, helped ensure consistency and safety across classrooms. I affirmed these practices as effective collaborative supports and emphasized the importance of continued communication as the groups evolve.] </p></div><h4>Summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"251\"><p>[During this meeting, the team reviewed early implementation data from the Cruz’s Math Crew strategy, including preassessment results and initial observations from the first days of grouping. Teachers confirmed that student performance aligned with patterns previously identified through iReady and SBA data, particularly in addition and subtraction with regrouping. The group discussed early successes, such as increased student engagement, clearer expectations, and smoother transitions supported by shared norms and language. Teachers also noted the importance of communication and collaboration when supporting students with diverse academic and behavioral needs.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"253\"><p>I supported the group in synthesizing patterns across classrooms and clarifying areas that required continued attention. Overall, the team affirmed continued implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew and agreed to refine routines and supports as implementation continues.] </p></div><h4>Next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"255\"><p>[The team agreed to continue implementing Cruz’s Math Crew and to refine procedures for transitions and expectations. Teachers will continue using the preassessment data to inform grouping and will monitor student progress during instruction. Prior to the next meeting, teachers will gather observations, student work, and notes on how students are responding to instruction. I will support the team by facilitating ongoing check-ins, helping organize shared observations, and guiding reflection during the next meeting. At the next PLC, we will review early progress, discuss any needed adjustments to grouping or instruction, and determine next instructional steps to better support students who require additional scaffolding or enrichment.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The minutes describe pre-assessment use, student transitions, name tags, routines, and early differentiation. They show that the group noticed practical access issues during implementation.</p><p>A smooth launch is not yet evidence of improved mathematics learning. The broad references to observations and follow-up would be more useful with a specific student-work pattern and an assigned action. The facilitator's account should distinguish what teachers reported from what the candidate directly observed.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record one concrete early learning pattern alongside the launch logistics.</li><li>Distinguish member reports, documents examined, and your own observations.</li><li>Assign the agreed procedural or instructional changes to named roles and set a review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The minutes supply implementation context for the provisional 3. They need clearer student-learning evidence, agreed support, and follow-through, alongside the video demonstration, to support a higher judgment.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what the group learned after trying the strategy and carrying out prior decisions. Record the evidence behind adjustments, including student movement between groups, instructional supports, and the support you agreed to provide.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What is going well</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"260\"><p>[I facilitated a discussion in which teachers shared early observations from implementing Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers reported that students were generally responding positively to the grouping structure and were motivated by working with different teachers. Several participants noted that students appeared excited about rotations and looked forward to math group time. Teachers shared that some students demonstrated strong performance, particularly in addition with regrouping, and were ready for increased challenge. Others described success using visual models, gestures, and step by step representations to support students when solving word problems. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"261\"><p>I highlighted these examples to reinforce how intentional grouping and instructional planning were supporting student engagement and access to learning. I also named these as early indicators of positive impact on student experience. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"262\"><p>Teachers also shared that students in advanced groups were eager to engage in higher-level problem solving and were motivated by being challenged. I acknowledged this as evidence that flexible grouping was allowing students to access instruction at an appropriate level. I summarized that across classrooms, teachers were seeing early positive engagement and responsiveness to instruction, even though students were working at different levels.] </p></div><h4>What needs adjustment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"264\"><p>[I guided the group to reflect on areas needing refinement. Teachers shared that some students continued to struggle with word problems, particularly those involving academic language or comparison situations. Several teachers noted that students needed additional support with place value concepts, especially understanding ones, tens, and hundreds when regrouping. I prompted discussion around instructional adjustments, and teachers described adapting their instruction by incorporating more visual representations, modeling, gestures, and explicit sequencing of steps. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"265\"><p>Teachers also noted the need to adjust group placement as students made progress. I acknowledged that movement between groups is an expected and important part of flexible grouping and emphasized that regrouping based on ongoing evidence is a strength of the strategy rather than a challenge. I also facilitated discussion around pacing, as teachers shared that some students were ready to move forward while others required additional time and practice. Toward the end of the discussion, the group agreed that the current cycle might need to be extended to allow students sufficient time to develop understanding before moving on. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"266\"><p>I supported the group in identifying concrete areas for refinement, including pacing, regrouping decisions, and instructional scaffolds, and emphasized that adjustment is an expected and productive part of implementation rather than a sign of difficulty.] </p></div><h4>Participant reflections</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"268\"><p>[I prompted reflection by asking teachers to share what they were noticing about student learning and engagement during implementation. Teachers reflected that students appeared motivated by the structure of Cruz’s Math Crew and were excited to rotate to different classrooms. They noted that working with different teachers increased engagement and encouraged students to take ownership of their learning. Several teachers shared that students benefited from explicit modeling, visuals, and hands-on supports, particularly when solving word problems or working through regrouping steps. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"269\"><p>I acknowledged these reflections and emphasized that the team’s collaboration and willingness to share observations was strengthening implementation. I highlighted that teachers were actively adjusting instruction based on student needs, such as moving students between groups, modifying tasks, and selecting strategies aligned to each group’s level. I also noted that teachers were beginning to plan intentionally for each group by identifying appropriate tasks, levels of challenge, and instructional supports.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"270\"><p>I emphasized that this level of collaborative reflection demonstrates shared ownership for student learning and supports more equitable instructional decision making across classrooms.] </p></div><h4>Summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"272\"><p>[During this meeting, I facilitated a collaborative review of early implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers shared observations about student engagement, progress, and instructional needs across groups. The group discussed how students were responding to rotations, how instructional strategies such as visual models and explicit modeling were supporting understanding, and how some students were ready for increased challenge while others required continued foundational support. We discussed the importance of flexible movement between groups and acknowledged that differences in student progress were expected.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"274\"><p>I helped the group synthesize patterns across classrooms and connect observations to instructional decisions, reinforcing the importance of monitoring impact over time rather than making isolated judgments. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"276\"><p>Overall, the discussion reflected strong collaboration, shared responsibility, and growing alignment in instructional planning.] </p></div><h4>Next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"278\"><p>[I guided the group in identifying next steps to support continued implementation. Teachers agreed to extend the current Cruz’s Math Crew cycle to allow additional time for students to develop understanding. We agreed to continue monitoring student progress and to adjust group placement as needed based on ongoing observations. Teachers will continue planning targeted lessons for each group, including foundational practice, visual modeling, and enrichment tasks aligned to student needs.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"280\"><p>I clarified expectations for documentation and evidence to bring to the next meeting and reiterated my role in supporting coordination, reflection, and adjustment across the team. At the next PLC, we will review updated observations, discuss the impact of continued instruction, and determine whether additional refinements or transitions to new content are appropriate.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The record describes students' differing readiness, advanced learners' engagement, continued difficulties, and possible grouping adjustments. It recognizes that flexible placement should change as learning changes.</p><p>Statements about strong collaboration and responsiveness need concrete support. The reader needs to know what work justified a grouping decision, which action from meeting 1 was completed, and what changed for a student after it.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the evidence supporting at least one group movement or instructional adjustment.</li><li>Report completion and results of actions agreed in the previous meeting.</li><li>Record who will provide the agreed support and what evidence will return in meeting 3.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> Specific adjustment ideas support the provisional 3. The record needs clearer connections to prior actions and evidence of whose views shaped the discussion for the Level 4 extensions.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the group's interpretation of initial results, including successes, continuing needs, limitations, and next actions. Show how members reached a shared conclusion rather than simply declaring that the strategy worked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What is going well</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"285\"><p>[I facilitated a review of post-assessment data to examine the impact of Cruz’s Math Crew after several weeks of implementation. Teachers shared quantitative results indicating that a large majority of students maintained or improved their performance. One teacher reported that 14 of 17 students showed growth, while another shared that 17 of 20 students maintained or improved. Teachers noted that many students increased by one to two performance points, suggesting meaningful academic progress during the instructional cycle. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"286\"><p>I supported the group in identifying patterns across classrooms and noted that flexible grouping allowed teachers to provide instruction that was better aligned to students’ readiness levels. Teachers shared that students who were ready for challenge benefited from more complex tasks, while others made gains through targeted instruction focused on regrouping and place value. I named these patterns as early evidence of instructional impact and affirmed that the strategy was positively influencing how students accessed and engaged with math instruction across groups. I highlighted these outcomes as evidence that the strategy positively impacted student learning for a wide range of learners.] </p></div><h4>What needs adjustment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"288\"><p>[I guided the team in reflecting on areas where adjustments were needed. Teachers discussed that some students, particularly those with gaps in foundational place value understanding, did not show the same level of growth and would benefit from continued targeted support. Teachers also reflected on the post-assessment itself, noting that portions of the assessment may not have been well aligned to students’ instructional experiences and may have been overly complex for some groups. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"289\"><p>I prompted discussion around instructional pacing and assessment design, and teachers agreed that future pre- and post-assessments should be more intentionally aligned to specific standards and skill progressions. Teachers also noted the importance of monitoring group placement more frequently to ensure students are moved sooner when additional support or challenge is needed. I emphasized that identifying these needs for adjustment reflects responsive instruction and strengthens the team’s ability to refine practice based on evidence rather than assumptions.] </p></div><h4>Participant reflections</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"291\"><p>[I prompted reflection by asking teachers to consider how the strategy influenced both student learning and their instructional decision making. Teachers reflected that Cruz’s Math Crew allowed them to address student needs more efficiently by narrowing the range of skills within each group. Several teachers shared that working with students at similar readiness levels increased their ability to provide focused instruction and respond quickly to misconceptions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"292\"><p>I acknowledged teachers’ reflections and emphasized how their collaborative analysis of data supported more responsive instruction. Teachers reflected that reviewing data together increased their confidence in making instructional adjustments, including regrouping students, modifying tasks, and planning targeted lessons. I emphasized that this collaborative reflection strengthened shared responsibility for student outcomes and supported more equitable instructional decision making across classrooms. I reinforced that this shared reflection demonstrated collective responsibility for student outcomes and strengthened instructional coherence across classrooms.] </p></div><h4>Summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"294\"><p>[During this meeting, I facilitated a collaborative analysis of post-assessment data to evaluate the impact of Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers shared evidence of student growth across groups and identified trends indicating that flexible grouping supported improved access to instruction. The team discussed both successes and challenges related to student progress, assessment alignment, and instructional pacing. I supported the group in synthesizing findings and recognizing that while most students benefited from the strategy, continued adjustments were needed to support students with persistent gaps. I guided the team in connecting evidence of impact to future instructional decisions rather than viewing results in isolation. The discussion reflected thoughtful use of data, shared accountability, and a focus on continuous improvement.] </p></div><h4>Next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"296\"><p>[I guided the group in identifying next steps based on the data analysis. Teachers agreed to continue using Cruz’s Math Crew as an instructional structure while shifting focus to new grade level standards, including multiplication and division. The team agreed to revise future preassessments to align more closely with specific standards and to use released or standardsbased items to better measure student readiness. Teachers also agreed to continue providing targeted small-group support for students who require additional instruction in addition and subtraction skills. I clarified expectations for continued monitoring of student progress and for bringing updated instructional plans and assessment tools to the next meeting. At the next meeting, the team will review updated instructional plans and determine how the revised assessment process supports ongoing implementation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The minutes include post-assessment review, unresolved needs, assessment alignment, and plans to continue targeted instruction. Recognition of a mismatch between instruction and an assessment is an important finding.</p><p>It also limits the strength of the outcome claim. The group should identify which assessment produced which result and separate maintenance from growth. General agreement to continue Cruz's Math Crew needs a more explicit account of who receives which support next.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link each reported result to the exact assessment, scoring method, and group of students.</li><li>Record the differing interpretations and how the group resolved or retained uncertainty.</li><li>Specify the next instructional response for students needing reinforcement and those ready to extend learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The recorded decisions support a provisional 3, but measurement ambiguities and an incomplete action trail constrain a higher judgment. The clips must confirm the shared interpretation and facilitation.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"G\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"G\"><span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Collaborative work products</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit key collaborative work products (up to 10 pages) used during the implementation meetings (e.g., lesson plans, student work products, instructional materials, assessment tools, data or evidence collected).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 19; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit actual products used or created in the community's work, within the handbook's page limit. Explain their purpose, when the group used them, and which decisions they support. A product can document planning, implementation, or a result, but those functions should not be confused.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"401\"><p>1: PLC Regrouping Planning Document (2 pages) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"402\"><p>This document was collaboratively developed by the PLC to establish a shared instructional focus for teaching addition and subtraction with regrouping and to guide lesson planning, task selection, and analysis of student understanding. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"403\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-403-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"406\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-406-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"411\"><p>PLC Meeting Minutes #1 – Pre-Assessment Design &amp; Instructional Logistics </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"412\"><p>These meeting minutes document collaborative planning related to the creation of a pre-assessment and the establishment of instructional logistics for implementing small group math instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"414\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-414-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"415\"><p>PLC Meeting Minutes #2 – Pre-Assessment Scoring, Group Formation &amp; Instructional Roles </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"416\"><p>These meeting minutes capture the PLC’s collaborative analysis of pre-assessment data, the formation of instructional groups, and the assignment of teaching roles to support targeted instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"417\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-417-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"421\"><p>Pre-/Post-Assessment (Blank Copy, 2 pages) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"422\"><p>This assessment was administered both before and after instruction to monitor student understanding of regrouping and to inform instructional decisions and reflection. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"424\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-424-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"430\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-430-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"431\"><p>5: Class-Level Pre/Post Assessment Summary </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"432\"><p>This class-level summary of pre- and post-assessment results was referenced during Video Clip 3 as part of a broader discussion of student outcomes and assessment rigor, while maintaining student anonymity. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"439\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-439-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"442\"><p>Work Product 6: Post-Assessment Referenced in Video Clip 3 (Blank Copy) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"443\"><p>This post-assessment was discussed during Video Clip 3 as part of a reflective conversation about alignment between instructional focus and assessment rigor. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"445\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-445-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example includes a planning document, additional meeting records, assessment tasks, and a class-level result summary. These make the strategy and monitoring process more inspectable than the narrative alone.</p><p>The 17 visible pre/post score pairs in Work Product 5 appear to show 13 improved, 2 unchanged, and 2 lower scores: 15 of 17 maintained or improved. The yellow heading says 14 of 17. Reconcile that discrepancy with the underlying records before using the summary. This screenshot does not display all 85 students mentioned in the reflection, so it cannot independently verify the broader 72-of-85 statement.</p><p>A blank assessment establishes what was asked, not how students answered. The separately identified post-assessment discussed for rigor should not be treated as interchangeable with the common measure without explanation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Label each product with its date, purpose, group use, and relationship to a specific decision.</li><li>Distinguish blank tools, completed work, aggregated results, and the assessment that raised alignment concerns.</li><li>Reconcile the visible score pairs, the 14-of-17 heading, and the broader 72-of-85 report; distinguish growth, maintenance, and decline.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> These artifacts support the provisional 3 by making the planning and assessment process inspectable. They do not resolve the numerical inconsistency or replace the required evidence of facilitation in video.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"H\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"H\"><span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Implementation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the videos of the implementation meetings where you and the individuals participating are seen and heard, select and submit 3 video clips, each up to 5 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 5 minutes of each video clip submitted), that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Setting the Purpose and Data Review (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you set the purpose for the meeting and how you facilitate the community of practice in analyzing the data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy. • Video Clip 2: Professional Collaboration (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates your facilitation of the community of practice and document their agreements, their decisions, and how they arrive at a consensual understanding of the initial implementation results. Also include how you facilitate the community of practice to (a) respect diverse viewpoints, (b) respond to individual and collective feedback, (c) maintain the group’s focus and energy, and (d) monitor progress toward goals of the meetings.</p><p>Video Clip 3: Results and Next Steps (up to 5 minutes) • Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you facilitate the community of practice in reporting and discussing the results of their implementation of the evidenced-based strategy and jointly plan for next steps/between meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 20 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit the three required clips from authentic implementation meetings: setting purpose and examining data; professional collaboration; and results with jointly planned next steps. The handbook specifies up to five minutes for each clip. The written commentary must refer to what is actually visible and audible.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><p class=\"scope-note\">The source commentary refers to video clips, but those recordings were not available for this review. No example video or invented transcript is substituted.</p></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Part I describes three clips and supplies timestamps, but a written account cannot establish visibility, audibility, authentic participation, or whether the described interaction falls within the submitted clips.</p><p>The absence of recordings from this review is not a finding that the candidate failed to submit them. It is a limit on this review. No definitive video-dependent score can be assigned here.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual recordings against the three required functions and the time limits.</li><li>Verify that the candidate and required participants can be seen and heard and that permissions are in place.</li><li>Ensure each commentary claim aligns to its cited clip and timestamp; do not stage or script missing evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: Not independently assessable without the video. Rubric 2.6: Not independently assessable without the video.</strong> The written commentary supports provisional estimates elsewhere, but the clips themselves cannot be judged from the source document. No score is assigned to an unseen recording.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Establishing meeting purpose</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you set the purpose for the meetings/reviewed the problem of practice with the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use a specific clip moment to explain how you established the meeting's purpose and connected it to the problem of practice. Include what you did, why, and the response or change in the discussion that supports your interpretation of its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"301\"><p>[In Video Clip 1, I intentionally opened the meeting by welcoming the team and naming the purpose as a check in on our initial implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew (0:00–0:28). I reviewed the norms we had previously agreed on, including being present, coming prepared, being open to different perspectives, and staying on topic, and I asked for confirmation before moving forward (0:29–0:53). This helped establish shared expectations and created a respectful, collaborative environment for discussion. By beginning with norms and purpose, I ensured that participants understood both how we would work together and why the meeting mattered, which helped anchor the discussion in collective responsibility rather than individual classroom concerns. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"302\"><p>I then revisited the problem of practice by grounding the conversation in our shared data sources and prior planning decisions. I referenced our review of SBA and iReady data and named the instructional focus on addition and subtraction with regrouping as a foundational skill students need to access grade level math (0:55–1:26). I explicitly articulated the problem of practice by stating that students need more targeted and differentiated instruction in foundational numeracy skills in order to make progress toward grade level expectations (1:26– 1:40). I also restated the agreed upon strategy of flexible leveled grouping through Cruz’s Math Crew and confirmed shared commitment to that approach by asking whether everyone was still comfortable moving forward with it (1:40–2:16). This framing reinforced the connection between adult instructional practices and student outcomes and helped maintain a consistent throughline across meetings. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"303\"><p>In Video Clip 3, the clip opens with me again naming the purpose of the meeting by stating that we had been implementing Cruz’s Math Crew for several weeks and that our goal was to understand the impact of the strategy and determine next steps based on post assessment data (0:00–0:18). I explicitly framed the conversation around learning from evidence, saying that we were looking to understand “the impact that this strategy has had and then talk about our next steps.” This repeated framing helped create continuity across meetings and reinforced that each session was part of an ongoing improvement cycle rather than a standalone discussion.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies openings, welcomes, purpose statements, and returns to the shared mathematics problem. References to individual clips help a reader locate the claimed actions.</p><p>An opening statement establishes intent, not impact by itself. Explain how a member's response or the ensuing data discussion shows that the group understood and used the purpose.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Select a precise moment that includes both the purpose-setting action and the group's response.</li><li>Explain why that framing was needed at that point in implementation.</li><li>Describe its observable effect without assuming that a welcome or nod demonstrates shared understanding.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> Purpose-setting and the stated reasons are consistent with 3 in the written account. Level 4 requires purposeful inclusion of all members across the meeting evidence, not just a clear opening.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Evaluating implementation data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe how you and the community of practice evaluated data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group interpreted evidence from using the strategy. Distinguish baseline information, implementation observations, and post-assessment findings. Show your role in helping members examine what the evidence does and does not establish.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"305\"><p>[Across the three clips, I facilitated a progression from examining baseline data to reviewing implementation evidence and finally analyzing post-assessment results. I intentionally supported the group in moving beyond reporting scores to interpreting what the data suggested about instruction, so that data functioned as a tool for refinement rather than evaluation of students. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"306\"><p>In Video Clip 1, I prompted teachers to share what they noticed from the preassessment used to inform grouping (2:17–2:39). Teachers described patterns of student performance, noting that subtraction with regrouping and word problems were particularly challenging (2:44–4:12). As teachers shared, I supported collective sense making by naming patterns across classrooms and elevating equity considerations, particularly the language demands of word problems for English Learners (4:13–4:41). This helped shift the discussion from individual classroom observations to a shared understanding of student needs. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"307\"><p>In Video Clip 2, the focus shifted from baseline data to evidence gathered during implementation. The clip opens with me asking whether any adjustments were needed based on what teachers were seeing in their groups (0:00–0:09). Teachers then shared instructional evidence, such as noticing students who needed additional challenge and should move groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"308\"><p>(0:12–0:31), as well as students who struggled with comparison language in word problems (0:34–0:47). Teachers described specific strategies they were using, including visual representations, gestures, and breaking problems into steps (0:49–1:20). Others noted student motivation and engagement when working at an appropriate level of challenge (1:27–1:58). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"309\"><p>As these examples emerged, I supported evaluation of the strategy by naming that flexible grouping allowed teachers to notice differences in readiness and respond accordingly. I summarized that movement between groups was expected and productive, noting that “it makes sense that we might have some movement” as students progress or reveal additional needs (4:27–4:45). This helped the group view instructional adjustment as evidence of responsiveness and effective implementation rather than inconsistency. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"310\"><p>In Video Clip 3, the group evaluated post-assessment data more formally. Teachers shared quantitative outcomes such as “14 out of 17 students maintained or improved” and “17 out of 20 maintained or improved” (0:19–1:21). They also noted that many students increased by one to two points and identified reasons why some students showed less growth, such as gaps in place value understanding (0:41–1:21). I supported interpretation of this data by helping the group identify patterns and implications, stating that a “large percentage of students benefited from the strategy” while acknowledging that some students still needed additional support (2:48–3:01). Through this process, the group evaluated both effectiveness and limitations of the strategy using multiple sources of evidence.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The account moves from pre-assessment patterns through instructional adjustments to post-assessment discussion. It names concrete issues such as comparison language, visual supports, and differing progress.</p><p>Baseline data can explain initial group placement, but they are not results of the strategy. The analysis should identify the changing evidence and the exact group conclusions, especially where assessment alignment or combined maintenance-and-growth counts complicate interpretation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which evidence at each timestamp is baseline, implementation, or outcome evidence.</li><li>Report the actual comparison, including students with no gain or a lower score.</li><li>Explain the question or facilitation move that helped the group test an interpretation rather than merely share results.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The narrative describes evaluation and adaptation consistent with a provisional 3. A higher judgment needs clearer evidence of participation, shared interpretation, and continuing action rather than unqualified success claims.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Reaching shared understanding</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any agreements or decisions made by the group and how you facilitated the community of practice to arrive at a consensual understanding of any initial implementation results.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the agreement and how members arrived at it. Identify the evidence, alternatives or concerns, and your action in checking understanding. Agreement is not established solely because the facilitator summarized a conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"312\"><p>[In Video Clip 1, I supported consensus by first confirming shared understanding of the problem of practice and strategy before moving into analysis (0:29–2:16). After teachers described preassessment patterns, I synthesized their observations and named common areas of need such as subtraction with regrouping and language demands in word problems (4:13–4:41). This synthesis helped the group reach a shared understanding of what the data suggested and what to monitor moving forward. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"313\"><p>In Video Clip 2, I facilitated agreement around instructional adjustments and grouping decisions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"314\"><p>Teachers discussed moving students between groups when readiness levels changed (0:12– 0:31, 2:27–3:27). I reinforced this as an expected feature of flexible grouping, stating that it made sense to adjust placement as students progressed or revealed new needs (4:27–4:45). By reframing regrouping as responsive rather than corrective, I supported consensus around continuing the strategy with flexibility. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"315\"><p>In Video Clip 3, I facilitated consensus around initial outcomes and next steps by helping the group interpret post-assessment results together. After teachers shared that many students demonstrated growth while others continued to need support, I summarized the emerging agreement that the strategy was having a positive impact while still requiring targeted differentiation. For example, after I asked, “What do we think based on the results? What do we think our next steps should be?” (3:37–3:41), a teacher affirmed that the grouping structure made it easier to address specific needs because there was not such a wide range of readiness levels within one group (3:43–4:10) and noted that we may need to adjust how we design the preassessment next cycle to be more specific (4:14–4:26). I then supported consensus by summarizing the team’s shared direction that many students were ready to move forward to new skills while some would continue to benefit from additional support through small groups or push in supports (4:28–4:46). I confirmed alignment by restating the next steps and linking them directly to the data and teacher reflections before the clip ends (4:46–4:59). This helped the group move from individual observations to a shared understanding of how to respond instructionally, ensuring our next steps reflected collective input and maintained a focus on meeting student needs across readiness levels.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage identifies grouping changes, instructional refinements, and discussion of results. It explains the candidate's efforts to frame regrouping as a purposeful part of the strategy.</p><p>The account would be more convincing with a specific exchange showing how different views were reconciled and how each member's understanding was checked. A statement that the group reached consensus needs a visible basis.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State one precise decision and the evidence used to make it.</li><li>Describe a differing view or uncertainty and how it influenced the decision.</li><li>Identify the clip moment where members confirmed the shared interpretation or retained an unresolved question.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported decisions support a provisional 3. Show the actual process of checking each viewpoint and carrying agreed actions forward to support the Level 4 extensions.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 4 / Supporting equitable participation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tDescribe how you</p><p>valued and respected diverse viewpoints,</p><p>responded to individual and collective feedback,</p><p>maintained the group’s focus and energy, and</p><p>monitored progress toward the goals of the meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four components: diverse viewpoints, individual and collective feedback, group focus and energy, and progress toward the meeting goals. Analyze the effect of your actions on participation and reasoning, not simply your use of polite language.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"318\"><p>[Across all three clips, I intentionally used facilitation moves that elevated teacher voice while maintaining focus on the purpose of each meeting. I viewed differences in perspective as assets for instructional problem solving rather than barriers to agreement. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"319\"><p>In Video Clip 1, I began by reinforcing norms that emphasized openness and respect for different perspectives (0:29–0:52). I invited each teacher to share observations rather than privileging one voice (2:27–2:40). When teachers raised different concerns, such as subtraction difficulty or language barriers for English Learners, I acknowledged each perspective and integrated them into a shared synthesis (4:13–4:41). This demonstrated that multiple viewpoints contributed to a fuller understanding of student needs. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"320\"><p>In Video Clip 2, I maintained focus by opening the clip with a clear guiding question about adjustments based on observations (0:00–0:09). I actively responded to individual contributions by asking follow up questions and clarifying meaning, such as when a teacher referred to a group as “the most difficult,” and I checked for shared understanding by reframing the comment in terms of level of challenge and instructional support needed (1:50–1:57). I also monitored progress toward meeting goals by periodically summarizing what we were learning, including that different groups required different supports and that movement between groups was expected (4:27–5:00). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"321\"><p>In Video Clip 3, I sustained focus by clearly stating that the purpose was to review postassessment data and determine next steps (0:00–0:18). I listened as teachers raised concerns about assessment difficulty and students who did not show as much growth (1:32–2:46). I then validated these perspectives while also highlighting strengths by summarizing that “a large percentage of students really benefited from the strategy,” which helped balance challenges with evidence of impact (2:48–3:01). I then redirected the conversation toward action by asking what steps should come next (3:37–3:41). These moves helped balance affirmation, accountability, and forward momentum.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example discusses norms, guiding questions, affirmation, and redirection toward action. Those are relevant facilitation moves, and the commentary gives reasons for using them.</p><p>Respectful responses to those already speaking do not establish that every member was encouraged to contribute. The Level 4 extension requires purposeful solicitation of diverse viewpoints, including quieter or differing perspectives, with evidence that those contributions were taken seriously.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify who contributed at the cited moments and how you invited perspectives not yet heard.</li><li>Show how feedback changed the discussion or decision, rather than only noting that it was acknowledged.</li><li>Connect a specific focus or pacing adjustment to progress on the meeting's goal.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> Norms, affirmation, and redirection support a provisional 3. A 4 requires evidence that the candidate actively sought varied views and encouraged every member to contribute.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 5 / Determining next steps together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tDescribe how you collaborated with the group to determine any next steps for implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show how evidence led the group to agree on its next actions. Explain what the candidate and members will do, how the plan responds to different needs, and how later evidence will reveal whether the response was useful.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"323\"><p>[Across the three meetings, I intentionally guided the group from evidence toward shared action through questioning, summarizing, and confirming consensus. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"324\"><p>In Video Clip 1, after discussing preassessment results, I shifted the conversation toward implementation by asking whether groups had begun meeting and how often (4:44–4:58). This helped connect early data to instructional action and set expectations for ongoing monitoring. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"325\"><p>In Video Clip 2, collaboration focused on refining instruction during implementation. Teachers identified concrete adjustments such as regrouping students, increasing visual supports, using gestures, and reinforcing place value concepts (0:12–1:21; 2:27–3:52). I validated these ideas and summarized that regrouping and instructional adjustment were intentional features of the strategy rather than deviations from it (4:27–4:45). This helped the team view flexibility as part of effective implementation. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"326\"><p>In Video Clip 3, collaboration centered on post-assessment outcomes and future planning. After reviewing growth data and discussing students who demonstrated different levels of progress, I guided the group to identify next steps by asking what actions would best support students who still needed reinforcement while also responding to students who were ready to move forward (3:34–3:41). Teachers agreed that small group support should continue for students who required additional practice with addition and subtraction, while most students were ready to begin transitioning into multiplication and division concepts (4:27–4:44). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"327\"><p>In addition, teachers identified the need to refine future pre- and post-assessments so they more precisely aligned to the targeted standards and better captured student understanding (4:15–4:25). I summarized these decisions to confirm shared understanding and collective responsibility before closing the discussion. Across all meetings, next steps were co-constructed through dialogue, grounded in evidence, and aligned to the shared goal of improving student access to grade level math instruction.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response gives concrete directions: continue support for regrouping, move ready students toward multiplication and division, refine assessment design, and adjust supports. These are more useful than an undifferentiated promise to continue meeting.</p><p>The account needs a clearer thread from one meeting's agreement to completed action and new evidence at the next. This distinction matters for Level 4, which extends the analysis beyond a single well-described closing discussion.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each next step to a stated finding and the students it is intended to support.</li><li>Record the responsible person, needed support, timeline, and evidence for the next review.</li><li>Trace at least one action from agreement to implementation to the following meeting's interpretation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The jointly described instructional directions support a provisional 3. For 4, trace how decisions were consistently carried forward within and between meetings.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tRefer to specific evidence (including agendas, minutes, group work products, implementation data, videos, and/or participant feedback) to analyze how your leadership actions influenced the community of practice’s progress toward its goal. Reflect on initial implementation results and feedback from the group to evaluate your strengths and areas for growth as a facilitator. Make explicit connections between the evidence, your leadership skills, equitable&nbsp;facilitation practices, and your next steps for professional growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Bring the evidence, your leadership actions, initial results, member feedback, strengths, growth needs, equitable facilitation, and professional next steps into one analysis. The question asks what the evidence teaches you about your leadership, not only whether the group completed its work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Leadership actions and evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"333\"><p>[Throughout Leadership Cycle 2, my leadership actions supported collaborative professional learning by intentionally designing structures and resources that guided the Community of Practice from planning to implementation and reflection. My facilitation focused on preparing the conditions for collaboration so that members could engage meaningfully in addressing the identified problem of practice: improving student understanding of addition and subtraction with regrouping through targeted small-group instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"334\"><p>After the initial planning meeting, I facilitated a subsequent planning focused Community of Practice meeting in which the group collaboratively developed a shared pre-assessment and clarified instructional logistics, including a consistent weekly schedule for small group instruction (Work Product 2). By ensuring that assessment tools, timelines, and instructional focus were clarified prior to implementation, members were able to concentrate on analyzing student needs and preparing for instruction rather than logistical barriers. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"335\"><p>While I was not present for the subsequent data analysis meeting, evidence from the meeting minutes shows that the structures established during planning supported continued progress. Teachers collaboratively scored the pre-assessment, identified patterns in student understanding, and formed instructional groups aligned to identified needs (Work Product 3). This demonstrates that the planning structures I facilitated supported distributed leadership and sustained collaboration. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"336\"><p>Evidence from Video Clip 1 shows teachers referencing shared instructional practices during early implementation, indicating coherence across classrooms. Initial impact on student learning was monitored using the collaboratively designed assessment as both a pre- and post-measure (Work Product 4). In Video Clip 3, teachers referenced class level pre- and post-assessment results (Work Product 5) to reflect on student outcomes and engaged in discussion about assessment rigor and instructional alignment. These conversations demonstrate progress toward the group’s goal of monitoring initial impact on student learning and using evidence to refine instructional practice.] </p></div><h4>Results, feedback, strengths, and growth</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"347\"><p>[A key strength in my facilitation during Leadership Cycle 2 was intentional preparation and structure. By anticipating instructional and logistical needs and designing shared tools in advance, I supported meaningful collaboration and sustained progress even when I was not physically present. Evidence from the meeting minutes and video discussions shows that the group was able to analyze data, form instructional groups, and engage in reflective dialogue aligned to the shared instructional goal. Early implementation results further reflected these strengths: post-assessment data showed that 72 out of 85 students (approximately 85%) maintained or improved their performance following implementation. Among students who demonstrated growth, gains of one to two points on an eight-point assessment represent approximately a 12.5% to 25% improvement. Early implementation results, including the group’s ability to collaboratively analyze data and adjust instruction, reflected strengths in my leadership related to clarity of purpose, advance planning, and the use of shared structures to support collective decision making. This indicates that the foundational planning supported distributed leadership and continuity of practice. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"348\"><p>Another strength was creating a safe space for reflection and professional dialogue. Video evidence shows that teachers were willing to engage honestly in discussion about instructional challenges, including assessment alignment. In Video Clip 3, the group reflected on a postassessment that exceeded the level of rigor of the instruction, using this moment as an opportunity to analyze alignment rather than assign blame. Teachers’ willingness to engage openly in this conversation served as feedback on my facilitation, indicating that the structure and tone of the meetings supported trust, shared ownership, and professional risk taking. When asked about my leadership and facilitation skills, the group provided positive feedback, noting that the meetings felt well structured, collaborative, and supportive of honest instructional reflection, and several team members expressed interest in having me facilitate future meetings. This feedback affirmed that my facilitation structures supported productive adult learning, and it also pushes me to keep strengthening my practice by improving real time synthesis and making consensus points even clearer for the team. This reinforced for me that effective leadership involves creating conditions where misalignment can be examined openly and used as a learning opportunity. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"349\"><p>An area for growth I identified was synthesizing multiple teacher contributions in real time. During discussions, I sometimes found it challenging to hold all of the details shared by each teacher and immediately name patterns across responses. Reviewing the video recordings alongside the meeting minutes helped me recognize moments when slowing the conversation, paraphrasing, or using visible note taking structures could have supported more accurate synthesis and collective sense making. This feedback highlights a growth area in my leadership related to pacing and strategic summarization, which I plan to strengthen by intentionally pausing discussions and using shared visual tools during meetings. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"350\"><p>I also recognized that equitable facilitation requires purposeful listening. Video review helped me see that ensuring all contributions are heard, accurately represented, and synthesized is critical for honoring diverse perspectives and preventing dominant voices from shaping conclusions prematurely. This reinforced listening as an equity focused leadership practice rather than simply a facilitation skill. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"351\"><p>Finally, reviewing the meeting minutes reinforced the importance of documentation as a leadership tool. The minutes not only captured decisions but also supported continuity of the work across meetings. Moving forward, I would be more intentional about using shared documentation during meetings to capture emerging patterns and next steps in real time, supporting clarity and alignment for all members of the community of practice.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The combined passages connect planning tools, teacher ownership, reported feedback, and the candidate's difficulty synthesizing multiple contributions. They identify a real developmental need rather than presenting an entirely successful self-portrait.</p><p>The claim that 72 of 85 students maintained or improved is not an 85% improvement rate. On an eight-point assessment, a one- or two-point gain is 12.5 or 25 percentage points of the maximum score, not necessarily a 12.5% or 25% relative improvement. Assessment alignment concerns further limit attribution to the strategy.</p><p>Positive feedback about structure is relevant, but it should be distinguished from the candidate's interpretation of teachers' willingness to speak. Analyze whose contributions shaped decisions and how the proposed synthesis routines would address a specific inequity in participation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate improved, unchanged, and lower scores; state the score scale and correct the units for gains.</li><li>Use a specific member comment and a meeting event to explain a strength and a growth need.</li><li>Connect the proposed changes in listening, synthesis, and documentation to equitable participation and a way to evaluate your growth.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The connected analysis of results, feedback, and growth supports 3. Correcting the outcome claims and examining equitable influence across the inquiry would strengthen the deeper analysis needed for 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tRefer to specific evidence (including the agendas and minutes, group work products and initial implementation results, videos and commentary, and/or group feedback on the process) of how effectively you maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity (e.g., respect for diverse viewpoints) as you worked with members of the community of practice.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze how you acted professionally and equitably throughout the inquiry. Use decisions or difficult moments to show how you treated disagreement, represented evidence accurately, and shared influence. Listing norms is not the same as demonstrating their use.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"338\"><p>[I maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity by intentionally modeling respectful, data centered facilitation and supporting collaborative decision making grounded in student needs rather than assumptions. After the initial planning meeting, I facilitated a subsequent planning focused Community of Practice meeting in which I structured the agenda and discussion to ensure that all members had opportunities to contribute to assessment design and instructional planning (Work Product 2). Decisions during this meeting were made collaboratively and anchored in shared student data, reinforcing a professional learning environment grounded in evidence rather than personal opinion. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"339\"><p>Equity was further supported through the establishment of shared norms for professional dialogue, including being open to different perspectives, maintaining focused and respectful discussion, and grounding instructional decisions in shared student data, as outlined in the planning meeting agenda and reinforced across subsequent meeting agendas. These norms supported inclusive participation and helped ensure that diverse instructional perspectives were heard and considered while maintaining alignment to the group’s shared instructional goal. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"340\"><p>During subsequent implementation discussions, I continued to demonstrate integrity by facilitating reflective dialogue that honored professional expertise and encouraged honest examination of practice. In Video Clip 3, when a post-assessment was identified as exceeding the level of rigor of the instruction (Work Product 6), I guided the conversation toward analyzing instructional alignment and identifying next steps rather than assigning blame. This facilitation move supported an environment of trust and professionalism in which challenges could be discussed openly as opportunities for learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response cites shared planning, norms, and a productive response to a mismatch between instruction and assessment. Treating that mismatch as a learning opportunity rather than blaming a teacher is a useful example.</p><p>The analysis remains broad about whose viewpoints mattered and whether participation was consistently equitable. Integrity also requires limiting outcome claims when the assessment evidence is uncertain, not only maintaining a respectful tone.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Analyze a specific difficult exchange or disagreement and its effect on participation.</li><li>Show how members' contributions changed a decision across more than one stage of the cycle.</li><li>Explain how assessment limitations changed what you were willing to claim about learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The non-blaming discussion of assessment alignment supports 3. A 4 needs a deeper, sustained analysis of professionalism, integrity, and equitable influence across the process.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Responding to school context</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of a community of practice at your school site/educational setting? What actions did you take to respond to these contextually based challenges or supports?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which features of school culture or scheduling supported or constrained the work, what you did in response, and what resulted. Distinguish the group's independent work from meetings you personally facilitated.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"342\"><p>[Several aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of the Community of Practice during Leadership Cycle 2. One key contextual factor was limited instructional time, which required careful planning and intentional use of existing structures. This influenced the decision to establish a consistent weekly time for small group instruction that aligned with the school schedule and instructional priorities, allowing implementation to occur without disrupting core instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"343\"><p>Another important contextual factor was the structure of grade level collaboration time at the site. Grade level teams have built in bi-monthly collaboration time while students attend physical education. Because I am not a member of this grade level, I was unable to attend the second PLC meeting, which occurred during this scheduled time. Rather than discouraging the team from meeting without me, I intentionally supported their decision to use this time to continue the work. Teachers were motivated and eager to move forward with scoring the pre-assessment and forming instructional groups, and I viewed this initiative as a positive indicator of ownership within the community of practice. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"344\"><p>This decision was supported by a broader school culture that values collaboration and distributed leadership. Although I was not present for one PLC meeting, teachers were able to continue data analysis and instructional planning independently. I responded to this context by ensuring that planning structures established earlier were clear and robust, and by supporting alignment and reflection during subsequent implementation discussions rather than attempting to reassert control over the process. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"345\"><p>Variation in teachers’ experience with regrouping instruction also influenced facilitation. I anticipated this challenge by centering discussions on shared student data and instructional evidence, which helped maintain focus on instructional problem solving rather than deficit based explanations. This approach supported equitable dialogue and reinforced a shared responsibility for addressing student needs across classrooms.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response honestly acknowledges that the candidate could not attend a grade-level data meeting and describes teachers continuing the work. This can show ownership and distributed responsibility.</p><p>It should not imply that an absent candidate demonstrated facilitation at that meeting. Clarify whether this was an additional planning/data session or one of the required implementation meetings, and distinguish records reviewed afterward from firsthand observations.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide a dated chronology separating additional planning sessions from the required implementation meetings.</li><li>Identify which meetings you attended and what you learned from records of others.</li><li>Explain how support before and after the independent session affected the work without claiming actions you did not perform.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The contextual reflection and acknowledgement of absence contribute to 3. Clarifying the meeting chronology and the limits of firsthand evidence is important to a stronger analysis of integrity.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 4 / Professional learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 2, identify specific professional learning goals and describe your next steps for growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Set specific professional learning goals derived from the cycle's evidence. Explain what you will practice or study, where you will apply it, what feedback you will seek, and how you will judge progress.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"353\"><p>[Based on my analysis of Leadership Cycle 2, my professional learning goals directly build on the strengths and areas for growth identified through meeting minutes and video reflection. One goal is to strengthen my ability to support collective sense making by improving how I synthesize teacher input in real time. While intentional preparation and structure supported productive collaboration, I recognize the need to more consistently slow discussions, paraphrase contributions, and visibly document key ideas during meetings. As a next step, I plan to incorporate structured note taking tools and intentional pauses during facilitation to support clearer pattern identification and more equitable representation of all voices. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"354\"><p>A second professional learning goal is to further develop shared leadership within the community of practice. While foundational planning supported continuity of practice, I want to more intentionally release responsibility to members by creating opportunities for teachers to lead portions of meetings, facilitate data discussions, or summarize group thinking. Moving forward, I plan to gather input from members about their interest in leadership roles and design agendas that distribute facilitation responsibilities. This approach builds on the distributed leadership that emerged during Cycle 2 and supports deeper engagement and ownership of the work. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"355\"><p>Additionally, I will strengthen my facilitation by using shared documentation during meetings to capture key insights and next steps in real time and by embedding brief opportunities for participant feedback to inform facilitation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Real-time synthesis, shared documentation, and distributed facilitation follow from the reflection. The response moves beyond generic goals such as becoming a better leader.</p><p>The plan still needs a timeframe and evidence of change. Simply adding a tool or assigning a member a role does not establish that understanding or equitable participation improved.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Turn each goal into a specific practice to try in an identified future meeting or inquiry cycle.</li><li>Specify the feedback and meeting evidence that will show whether the practice helped.</li><li>Set a review point to examine progress and revise the professional learning plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The professional goals follow logically from the identified growth needs and contribute to 3. More operational goals are useful, but Level 4 also requires the deeper analysis of equitable leadership across the inquiry.</p></section></section></article><section id=\"alignment\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Read the template and handbook together</h2><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\"><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Current template</th><th>Current handbook</th><th>Meaning for the student</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part A inquiry</td><td>Question I.1 separates formal training, professional learning, communities of practice, and adult-learning research.</td><td>Investigation directions and Rubric 2.1 connect practices to student learning or well-being (pp. 6-8, 11).</td><td>Answer each subpart, then explain relationships. Meeting frequency is not evidence of impact.</td></tr><tr><td>Part D numbering</td><td>The two sections are labeled II and III.</td><td>Planning directions label the corresponding sections I and II (pp. 14-15).</td><td>Match by task and section title. Preserve the supplied template labels; seek program clarification rather than renumbering official text.</td></tr><tr><td>Agendas and minutes</td><td>Parts B, C, E, and F offer optional meeting-record formats.</td><td>Planning records and at least three implementation agendas and minutes are required (pp. 13-15, 18-19).</td><td>Optional format does not mean optional evidence. Actual implementation must occur while the implementation meetings are being conducted.</td></tr><tr><td>Video commentary</td><td>Part I provides five prompts and clip, timestamp, action, reason, and impact fields.</td><td>All five prompts must be addressed at least once across three clips, with aligned commentary (pp. 21-22).</td><td>Organize the evidence by the actual clip moments. This review groups passages by question for teaching; it does not replace the submission fields.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflection</td><td>Part J has four questions; question 1 includes results, feedback, strengths, growth, equity, and next steps.</td><td>Reflection directions and Rubric 2.7 assess the connections among evidence and leadership learning (pp. 26-27).</td><td>Bring the related reflection passages together. A separate account of success does not replace analysis of feedback or growth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note\">Page references are printed handbook pages. These are instructional observations, not official CTC corrections.</p></section><section id=\"limits\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Submission requirements</h2><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>A</th><td>Context, educational focus, and community of practice</td><td>Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>Planning meeting agenda</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>C</th><td>Planning meeting minutes</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>D</th><td>Problem of practice and selected strategy</td><td>Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>E</th><td>Implementation meeting agendas</td><td>At least 3 meetings; all agendas in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>F</th><td>Implementation meeting minutes</td><td>At least 3 meetings; all minutes in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>G</th><td>Collaborative group work products</td><td>Up to 10 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>H</th><td>Three implementation meeting video clips</td><td>Exactly 3 video files; up to 5 minutes each</td></tr><tr><th>I</th><td>Commentary on implementation meeting clips</td><td>Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>J</th><td>Reflective narrative</td><td>Up to 4 written pages OR 5 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Meeting-record templates marked optional may be replaced by an appropriate local format; the required evidence is not optional. Consult the current handbook for recording permissions, technical specifications, and submission requirements.</p></section><section id=\"source-record\" class=\"sources\"><h2>Source notes</h2><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf\">Current Cycle 2 handbook</a>. Official material copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Original response wording and document images from the supplied CTC example are retained; formatting and placement are adapted. Example references to research are preserved, but those studies and the school records were not independently verified. No assessor score report or video recording was available.</p><ul class=\"template-links\"><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S1_A_ContextEdFocusCommunityPrac.docx\">Part A template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_B_PlanningMeetingAgenda_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part B template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_C_PlanningMeetingMinutes_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part C template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_D_ProbPracDescStrategy.docx\">Part D template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part E template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_F_MinutesImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part F template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_I_CommentaryImplementationMeetings.docx\">Part I template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\">Part J template</a></li></ul><p>Companion reviews: <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Cycle 1</a> | <a href=\"#cycle/c3\">Cycle 3</a>.</p></section><section id=\"scores\" class=\"major-section\"><span id=\"final-check\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"rubric-map\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Estimated score profile</h2><p>Each rubric is counted once across its relevant evidence. No total or passing prediction is offered because required video evidence was not reviewed.</p><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Rubric</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Basis and limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>2.1</th><td>3 / 5</td><td>The example describes several structures and analyzes barriers to collaboration. Level 3 is the stronger fit; evidence that different forms actually affected student learning is too general for a confident 4.</td></tr><tr><th>2.2</th><td>4 / 5</td><td>Across Part A, the mathematics focus, school goal, three partners, and membership rationale are clear. The explanation of shared planning and targeted support addresses the Level 4 extension. A demographic list does not yet establish the intentional inclusion and respect for diverse viewpoints needed for 5.</td></tr><tr><th>2.3</th><td>3 / 5</td><td>The planning record explains shared analysis and a shift toward targeted instruction. Naming English learners and students with disabilities without their actual comparative results leaves the Level 4 evidence incomplete.</td></tr><tr><th>2.4</th><td>3 / 5</td><td>The group compares two approaches, selects flexible grouping, and explains expected benefits. The account needs more specific instructional actions and expected effects for identified groups to support a confident 4.</td></tr><tr><th>2.5</th><td>3 / 5, provisional</td><td>The written commentary identifies facilitation moves, reasons, and timestamps consistent with Level 3. A 4 requires visible, purposeful inclusion of all members. The actual demonstrations and clip alignment cannot be verified without the recordings.</td></tr><tr><th>2.6</th><td>3 / 5, provisional</td><td>The written record connects results with regrouping and future instruction. It does not consistently trace each agreed action to evidence brought back at the next meeting, so 3 is more defensible than 4. Video confirmation remains necessary.</td></tr><tr><th>2.7</th><td>3 / 5</td><td>The reflection connects planning, member feedback, synthesis difficulties, and professional goals. Level 4 needs deeper evidence of equitable participation throughout the process, and the learning claims need correction where maintenance is counted with improvement.</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section id=\"rubric-2-1\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how do these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not describe the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. OR Candidate does not analyze how these practices relate to student learning and/or well- being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate minimally describes the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a vague analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate provides a clear detailed description of the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a clear analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains the relationship between different forms of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how these practices have impacted student learning and/or well-being, providing evidence from observations, documentation, surveys, student work, and/or staff consultations.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate critiques collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting, referring to evidence-based adult learning processes and/or research that supports or refutes the practices and outlines implications for facilitating a community of practice at their school site/educational setting.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 11.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-2\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate select an area of educational focus based on student data that corresponds to the vision, mission, and/or goals of their school site/educational setting? How does the candidate choose a group of educators/educational partners to participate in a community of practice in relation to the selected area of educational focus?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not select or selects an area of educational focus that is not based on student data or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate’s school site/educational setting. OR Candidate provides little detail about demographic characteristics and/or does not provide reasons for member inclusion in the community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate selects and minimally describes an area of educational focus loosely based on student data and/or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate’s school site/educational setting. Candidate lists group members and provides unclear or irrelevant details of their demographics and characteristics; rationale for member inclusion in the community of practice is not clear.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate selects an area of educational focus, clearly referring to data on student learning and/or well-being, and explains the connection to the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate’s school site/educational setting. Candidate clearly describes community of practice group members by demographic characteristics, current job titles, assignments, and positional relationships and provides clear reasons for each member’s inclusion in the group in relation to the selected area of educational focus.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate articulates how the work of the group is likely to advance conditions for student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides a rationale for including the specific members in their community of practice to ensure diverse viewpoints are represented and respected, leading to supports for all students to learn and to experience a positive sense of well-being.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 12.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-3\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><p>Essential Question: Using the agreed-upon area of educational focus as a starting point, how does the candidate work collaboratively with the group to review data sets and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being? How does the candidate work with the group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current instructional practice at their school site/educational setting?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not involve the group in analyzing data sets to select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. OR Candidate provides no evidence that they worked collaboratively with the group to select the problem of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate minimally involves the group in the analysis of data sets and/or the selection of the problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate minimally involves the group in the selection of the problem of practice and/or minimally describes how the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly explains how they worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the selected educational focus and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate clearly describes how they worked collaboratively with group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate differentiates the academic performance for more than one student group and/or issue of well-being, providing supporting student data/evidence and explains how they facilitated the group to collaboratively address these differences in defining the problem of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains in detail both how they facilitated the collaborative process with the group members to analyze student data/evidence and why specific facilitation skills were used.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-4\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy to address the selected problem of practice? How does the candidate describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate does not describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate minimally discusses the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate clearly describes the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes in detail how the collaboratively determined evidence-based strategy will have a positive impact on student learning and/or well-being across specific student groups.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate thoroughly explains potential implementation implications and/or challenges across multiple student groups and suggests feasible next steps for each group, as appropriate.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 17.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-5\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze a variety of their facilitation skills of group learning— including documenting group decisions, supporting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy— while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, feedback, and progress during the implementation meetings?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not demonstrate facilitation skills during the implementation meetings. OR Candidate’s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group learning or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate demonstrates minimal facilitation skills that are vaguely supportive of group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate’s commentary does not clearly provide an analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly demonstrates facilitation skills that support group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, including documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate’s commentary provides a clear analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate purposefully seeks diverse viewpoints and clearly encourages all members of the community to speak and share ideas during meetings.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses their commentary to critique their facilitation skills used during the implementation meetings based on group learning processes and/or research that supports facilitation practices.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) • Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) • Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 23.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-6\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate facilitate a discussion that leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results? How does the candidate collaboratively lead the group in jointly determining next steps?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not facilitate a discussion about implementation results. OR Next steps are not determined. OR Candidate’s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group discussion or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate facilitates a discussion that does not clearly lead to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. It is not clear that the candidate works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine the next steps. Candidate’s commentary provides a vague analysis of their facilitation of group discussions.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate facilitates a discussion that clearly leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. Candidate clearly works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine next steps. Candidate’s commentary provides a clear analysis of their facilitation of the group discussions.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate consistently addresses initial results and next steps to inform the collaborative implementation process with group members both within and between meetings.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate collaborates with the group to adjust the implementation strategy as needed in response to its challenges or successes.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) • Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) • Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1B CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 24.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-7\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate reflect upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills and practices to make specific connections to their strengths and areas for growth, and identify next steps, referring to specific evidence from any of the four steps? How does the candidate demonstrate a variety of leadership skills used to equitably facilitate a community of practice?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate provides no connection to initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make specific connections to their strengths or areas for growth, or next steps related to leadership practices. OR Candidate does not include specific evidence from any of the cycle’s four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. OR Candidate does not describe their leadership practice using an equitable leadership approach to facilitate a community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely or inaccurately reflects upon initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and/or next steps. Candidate vaguely refers to evidence from any of the cycle’s four steps to support their reflections and/or inform their analysis. Candidate vaguely demonstrates their capacity to facilitate a community of practice. It is not clear that the candidate has an equitable approach to leadership practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly reflects upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills, making specific connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and next steps. Candidate clearly refers to specific, appropriate evidence from any of the four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. Candidate clearly demonstrates how their leadership practice equitably facilitated a community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides a deep analysis that illustrates how they maintained professionalism and integrity as well as employed an equitable leadership approach consistently throughout the community of practice inquiry process.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate consults evidence- based practices and/or research related to equitable leadership development, adult learning, and/or group facilitation, then uses the information to analyze their own leadership practices and set professional goals during this cycle.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 5 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 27.</p></section></div>","search":[{"title":"Part A / I.1 / Investigating professional learning","url":"#cycle/c2/A1","html":"<span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Investigating professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe and analyze the role of collaborative professional learning at your school site/educational setting as it relates to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>a.\tProvide information about any formal training offered to professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting within the past three years related to collaborative professional learning, communities of practice, or group learning process.</p><p>b.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting currently engage with one another in professional learning. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of this professional learning in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>c.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators engage with one another in communities of practice. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of the community of practice approach in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>d.\tDescribe how collaborative professional learning reflects evidence-based adult learning processes and cite relevant research to support your analysis.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Answer all four subparts: training offered within three years; current professional learning and its impact; communities of practice and their impact; and the adult-learning principles that explain those practices. A community of practice involves sustained shared inquiry, not simply attendance at a meeting.</p><p>Separate what you observed or found in records from what you expect collaboration might accomplish. When impact evidence is unavailable, explain that limitation rather than presenting a benefit as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Formal training</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"10\"><p>[Within the past three years, our school provided formal training related to collaborative professional learning through the introduction of the PDSA cycle, which stands for Plan, Do, Study, Act. This training was provided last year as part of a schoolwide improvement effort focused on reading comprehension in English Language Arts. Administration presented the purpose of the PDSA cycle during professional development and explained how it supports collaborative inquiry and instructional alignment. A shared slide deck was created for the entire school, and each grade level used the same templates to identify a problem of practice, select a strategy, gather evidence, and analyze student learning. Because the slide deck was shared across the whole school, grade levels could view each other’s work and see how different teams approached the same schoolwide goal. Grade level teams completed the cycle together and documented their thinking within the shared templates. At several points during the year, teams presented their results to the staff, which helped build a common language and a more unified approach to improving reading comprehension. Although the initial training was brief, it provided a structured process for examining student learning and strengthened collaboration across the grade levels.] </p></div><h4>Current professional learning</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"5\"><p>[Professional educators at my school collaborate through several established structures that support shared decision making and instructional alignment. Regular schoolwide professional development, held two or three times each month, provides time for teachers, specialists, coordinators, and administrators to review schoolwide goals, analyze iReady and DIBELS data, prepare for state assessments, and align curriculum expectations. These collaborative structures have a positive impact on student learning by promoting shared understanding of grade-level expectations and supporting more equitable access to instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"6\"><p>Biweekly grade-level meetings during the instructional day allow teachers to discuss current data, student work, and pacing. These conversations often lead to timely reteaching and instructional adjustments, which can positively impact student learning by helping teachers respond more quickly to identified needs. The school also offers up to two hours of optional after-school collaboration each month; however, participation varies due to teacher schedules, which results in inconsistent impact across teams and limits the reach of shared planning practices. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"7\"><p>Teams are beginning to incorporate more structured data conversations, though this practice is still developing. Overall, collaboration has the potential to positively impact student learning and well-being by supporting early identification of academic needs and coordinated supports for English learners and students requiring intervention. At the same time, logistical constraints during professional development sessions limit sustained inquiry. Because teams are expected to meet only when all members are available, some grade levels engage in deeper collaboration than others. Additionally, paraprofessionals are not routinely included in these structures, which limits alignment of instructional supports and reduces the overall impact on student learning. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"8\"><p>Inconsistent use of inquiry routines across teams further contributes to variation in how instructional adjustments are made.] </p></div><h4>Communities of practice</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"12\"><p>[Professional learning has played a central role in shaping collaboration at my school. Historically, most collaboration has occurred during regular professional development meetings and biweekly grade level meetings during the instructional day. These structures have established a routine expectation that teachers meet to analyze data, discuss instruction, and align pacing. This has supported coherence across classrooms and has helped build a culture in which collaboration is viewed as part of the school’s professional norms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"13\"><p>However, the effectiveness of collaboration varies. Some professional development sessions emphasize logistics, which limits deeper inquiry. Optional after school collaboration time also creates challenges because the expectation that all team members must attend prevents some grade levels from meeting consistently. As a result, opportunities for collaboration are uneven across the school. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"14\"><p>The introduction of a schoolwide PDSA cycle last year strengthened collaborative inquiry by providing a clear structure for examining data and sharing results. The shared slide deck helped create transparency across grade levels and encouraged alignment. Overall, professional learning facilitates collaboration when it focuses on instructional improvement, yet it hinders collaboration when structures limit participation or emphasize compliance rather than inquiry.] </p></div><h4>Adult-learning analysis</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"16\"><p>[Collaborative professional learning at my school reflects several principles of evidence based adult learning. Teachers engage in work that is immediately relevant, such as analyzing current data and discussing strategies they are using with students. This aligns with Knowles’ theory of andragogy, which emphasizes that adults learn best when learning is problem-centered, relevant to their professional roles, and connected to real tasks they must perform (Knowles, 1984). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"17\"><p>Grade level collaboration also reflects social learning theory. Vygotsky and Lave and Wenger describe learning as a social process in which individuals build understanding together. During collaborative meetings and the PDSA cycle, teachers share ideas, problem solve, and develop collective expertise. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"18\"><p>The PDSA process reflects elements of effective professional learning identified by Darling Hammond and colleagues, including collaboration, focus on student outcomes, and ongoing reflection. Although not all professional development sessions fully reflect these principles, the structures that center on data and shared inquiry are consistent with research on effective adult learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response provides useful distinctions among schoolwide development, grade-level meetings, optional collaboration, and the PDSA process. It identifies uneven participation, logistical discussions, and the exclusion of paraprofessionals. Those are analytical observations, not just a schedule.</p><p>Its claims that collaboration improves equitable access and student learning need a traceable example: a decision, the resulting change in instruction, and evidence of what students subsequently did. The research discussion explains possible mechanisms, but it does not establish local effects. One training event is described; the three-year account would be clearer with the other years explicitly addressed.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the dates, participants, and purposes of relevant training across the three-year period, including years with no such training.</li><li>Compare two forms of professional learning using a concrete record of a decision, instructional follow-through, and student evidence.</li><li>Use the adult-learning ideas to examine both strengths and weaknesses of the local practices; distinguish established effects from plausible benefits.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The descriptions and analysis of participation barriers support 3. For 4, supply evidence of how different forms of professional learning affected students, not only the benefits they might produce.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.2 / Implications for facilitation","url":"#cycle/c2/A2","html":"<span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Implications for facilitation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tAs a result of your investigation and given the educational setting as well as available data related to student learning and/or well-being, describe the most important issues you will need to consider in preparing to facilitate and support a community of practice to engage in collaborative professional learning.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what the investigation requires you to do as facilitator. Connect each priority to the school context, available student evidence, and this group's likely participation. Do not repeat the entire description from question 1.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"20\"><p>[Several issues must be addressed when preparing to facilitate a community of practice with the team I will be working with. The first issue is the need for consistent participation. Even within a single team, attendance can vary depending on the structure of the meeting or time of day. When all members are not present, the team cannot engage fully in shared inquiry, and this affects the group’s ability to make informed and unified decisions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"21\"><p>The second issue is the need for clearer protocols that support deeper analysis of student learning. At times, collaborative meetings rely on informal conversation and shift toward logistics rather than instructional improvement. To facilitate a community of practice effectively, I will need to implement consistent structures that guide the team to examine data, reflect on strategies, and make collective decisions based on evidence. Strengthening these routines will be essential for ensuring that collaborative time leads to meaningful improvements in student learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Consistent participation and protocols for examining student learning follow logically from the preceding investigation. The response correctly identifies conditions that could prevent the community from moving beyond logistical conversation.</p><p>The proposed response remains broad: implement structures and support attendance. Students need to see how the facilitator will protect time, keep absent voices from disappearing, and bring the discussion back to evidence of mathematics learning.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify an agreed meeting schedule and the school support needed to make participation possible.</li><li>Name the data-discussion routine and explain how it will separate observations, interpretations, and decisions.</li><li>Connect each facilitation choice to a documented concern from the investigation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.1: 3 / 5.</strong> These facilitation priorities support the overall 3 but do not supply the missing impact evidence required for 4. Make the priorities operational and connect them to the investigation.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.1 / Selecting the educational focus","url":"#cycle/c2/A3","html":"<span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Selecting the educational focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe the agreed-upon area of educational focus for improving student learning and/or well-being (obtained with guidance from one or more school administrators and/or teacher leaders) selected for the group’s professional learning. Be sure to include a discussion of the relevant data that supports the selection of this focus and the connection between the educational focus and the vision, mission, and/or goals in your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Name the educational focus agreed with an administrator or teacher leader, explain the student data supporting it, and connect it to an actual school commitment. The broad focus is established here; the community jointly defines the more specific problem of practice later.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Three different decisions</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Decision</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Who shapes it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Educational focus</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">The broad area of student learning or well-being that warrants inquiry.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Established with administrator or teacher-leader guidance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Problem of practice</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">A specific, changeable aspect of adult practice identified through evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Defined jointly by the community during planning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Selected strategy</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">One evidence-based response that fits the problem and setting.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Chosen jointly after considering research, evidence, and feasibility.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not arrive with every decision already made. Facilitation creates opportunities for colleagues to challenge an interpretation and influence the response.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"24\"><p>[The agreed-upon area of educational focus for this cycle is mathematics, specifically improving student performance on the Smarter Balanced Assessment. This focus was selected in collaboration with school administration after reviewing district goals and school-level SBA trend data. The district’s numeracy goal requires students in grades three through five to move an average of 40 points closer to proficiency by 2026, with targeted student groups expected to make gains of 50 points. Although our school has demonstrated steady growth and is currently performing above proficiency in mathematics, the most recent Distance from Standard score of 43.7 indicates that performance remains below the district’s projected 2025–26 target. As a result, continued acceleration in mathematics achievement is needed. Prioritizing mathematics aligns with the school’s mission to provide equitable access to rigorous instruction and supports the district’s long-term goal of preparing students for success in higher-level mathematics, including Algebra.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Mathematics is clearly identified, with administrative guidance, a Distance from Standard value of 43.7, and a district numeracy target. The mission connection is coherent.</p><p>The response does not give enough dates and comparison values to verify the claim that performance is below the projected target. A schoolwide achievement measure also needs a bridge to the particular third-grade learners whose instruction the community can influence.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the year, population, source, and comparison target for the Distance from Standard value.</li><li>Explain why the third-grade evidence warrants this focus while keeping schoolwide and grade-level results distinct.</li><li>State the relevant school commitment and the administrator or teacher-leader role in agreeing to the focus.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> This answer provides the data-to-focus and school-goal foundation for the overall 4. The later membership and prospective-benefit answers complete that judgment; this paragraph alone does not earn it.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.2 / Choosing community members","url":"#cycle/c2/A4","html":"<span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Choosing community members</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tProvide a list of the job titles for each member of the community of practice group, detailing the specific reason(s) for each member’s inclusion and referencing the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>List each partner's job title and explain the expertise or instructional responsibility that makes that person useful for the mathematics focus. The community requires 3-5 partners in addition to the candidate. Shared availability alone is not a membership rationale.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"26\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS): Included because this teacher leads an SAS class and supports a wide range of learners within the same classroom. This perspective is valuable for selecting mathematics strategies that work for students with varied needs, which aligns with our focus on improving SBA math performance. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"27\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (SAS): Included for expertise with enrichment and accelerated instruction and for her role as the grade-level chair, which supports maintaining rigor and coordinating mathematics practices across the team. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"28\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (General Education): Included to represent the broader range of instructional levels in third grade. This teacher provides insight into scaffolds and supports needed to help students meet the mathematics proficiency target. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"29\"><p>The third grade team was selected because third grade is the first grade to take the SBA in mathematics. Successful collaboration with this group can be extended to upper grades as we continue working toward district numeracy targets.] </p></div><h4>Related evidence: member commitment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"40\"><p>[I obtained each member’s commitment during a grade level meeting by explaining the purpose of the community of practice and the connection to the schoolwide numeracy goal. All teachers agreed to participate because they saw value in improving mathematics instruction and supporting third grade students preparing for the SBA. They viewed the collaboration as an opportunity to strengthen instructional alignment.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Three third-grade teachers bring general education, enrichment, and grade-level leadership perspectives. Their direct responsibility for the students makes the group plausible. The additional commitment passage explains why they agreed to participate.</p><p>The inclusion rationale would be stronger if it distinguished the two SAS teachers' contributions and explained how language-development or disability-related perspectives would enter the inquiry. A member's ethnicity or assignment should not be treated as automatic expertise in every learner's experience.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Differentiate each teacher's specific contribution to identifying and addressing the mathematics need.</li><li>Explain how the group will obtain relevant perspectives not represented in its membership.</li><li>Keep the facilitator distinct from the three partners and retain the actual commitment process.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> The three relevant partners and differentiated inclusion reasons support the membership criterion. The whole Part A estimate is 4; purposeful inclusion of missing viewpoints needs further explanation for 5.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.3 / Understanding representation","url":"#cycle/c2/A5","html":"<span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Understanding representation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tProvide a description of each group member’s demographics and how this reflects or is different from your school site/educational setting’s demographics.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe each member's appropriately provided demographic information and compare it with the school community. Explain representation without assuming that shared identity guarantees shared perspectives or that a person speaks for an entire group.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"33\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS) – Hispanic </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"34\"><p>This teacher’s background reflects the school’s largest demographic group, as approximately fifty nine percent of students identify as Latino. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"35\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (SAS) – White </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"36\"><p>This teacher represents a demographic group that makes up about sixteen percent of the school population. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"37\"><p>Third Grade Teacher (General Education) – Armenian </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"38\"><p>Although Armenian is not reported as a separate category in the school demographic chart, our school community includes many Armenian families. This teacher’s background aligns with a notable portion of the school community even though it is not reflected as a standalone category in the official data.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response compares three teachers with the reported student population and explicitly recognizes that Armenian identity is not separately represented in the chart. That is more useful than simply listing identities.</p><p>The categories are not directly comparable: ethnicity and ancestry may be recorded differently. The response needs the chart's date and definitions, and it should explain missing perspectives without inferring identities or attributing educational needs to demographic membership.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source, year, and category definitions for the school demographic comparison.</li><li>Use member-provided information and explain any noncomparable categories.</li><li>Describe how differing perspectives will be sought and respected in the mathematics inquiry.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> The demographic comparison supports the overall 4 when read with the other Part A evidence. Listing identities alone does not demonstrate the respectful inclusion of diverse viewpoints required for 5.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.4 / Explaining positionality","url":"#cycle/c2/A6","html":"<span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.4 / Explaining positionality</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tProvide a brief description of your relationship or positionality (e.g., coach, mentor, teacher colleague, administrator) to each member of the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain your working relationship and authority in relation to each partner. Consider how grade-level relationships, status, experience, or prior collaboration may shape participation even when you are not an evaluator.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"31\"><p>[I am a second grade teacher who works directly below this team in the grade level progression, and I collaborate with them as a colleague within the same school. My positionality is that of a peer teacher who prepares students academically before they enter third grade. Because I teach the grade level immediately preceding theirs, I have insight into students’ foundational skills and the instructional experiences that lead into third grade mathematics. I am not in a supervisory or evaluative role, which allows me to participate as an equal member of the community of practice focused on improving mathematics outcomes.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The candidate clearly identifies a peer role as a second-grade teacher preparing students for the third-grade team. The absence of supervisory authority is relevant.</p><p>Calling oneself an equal member does not eliminate differences in grade-level authority or familiarity. The question asks about each relationship; the response largely treats the three partners as one unit.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe any relevant differences in your relationship with each partner.</li><li>Explain how the grade-level chair's role or your feeder-grade position could shape discussion.</li><li>Identify one facilitation choice that protects candid participation without assuming all members feel equally comfortable.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> The peer role and feeder-grade connection support the positionality criterion. The overall 4 relies on the combined Part A evidence; a member-by-member account would make this contribution clearer.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.5 / Expected benefits for students","url":"#cycle/c2/A7","html":"<span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.5 / Expected benefits for students</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tExplain how the work of this group is likely to advance conditions for students’ learning and/or well-being in relation to the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the pathway from the community's adult learning to improved conditions for students. Identify what teachers may learn or change, what students will experience differently, and why that could help with the mathematics focus. These are expected effects, not completed outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"42\"><p>[The work of this community of practice will improve student learning by strengthening the consistency and quality of mathematics instruction across third grade. Through collaborative inquiry, the team will identify strategies that address numeracy gaps and reinforce foundational skills needed for success on the Smarter Balanced Assessment. Shared planning and reflection will promote more coherent routines, aligned expectations, and targeted supports for diverse learners. As a result, students are more likely to experience consistent instruction, clearer expectations, and increased confidence in mathematics, supporting both academic growth and well-being. Additionally, this collaborative work creates structures for ongoing reflection and adjustment, allowing teachers to regularly examine student evidence and refine instruction in response to emerging needs. By engaging in shared analysis and collective decision-making, educators build a common understanding of effective practices and strengthen their capacity to respond to diverse learners. Over time, this collaborative approach supports more equitable learning conditions by ensuring instructional decisions are intentional, responsive, and grounded in shared responsibility for student progress.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Shared planning, aligned routines, targeted support, and repeated examination of evidence form a credible pathway. This passage supplies the prospective explanation that extends Rubric 2.2 beyond group selection.</p><p>The opening promise that the work will improve learning is stronger than the evidence available at this stage. Greater consistency is useful only if the agreed instruction meets students' different needs; identical instruction is not itself equitable instruction.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use prospective language for effects that have not yet been observed.</li><li>Identify the particular opportunities to learn that coordinated planning should improve.</li><li>Name the student evidence the group will later examine to see whether those opportunities and benefits occurred.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.2: 4 / 5.</strong> This prospective pathway is the main Level 4 extension: it explains how the group may advance learning conditions. It should remain a reasoned expectation, not a promise of results.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / Planning meeting agenda","url":"#cycle/c2/B","html":"<span id=\"b-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / Planning meeting agenda</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for the planning meeting. You may use either this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nAfter creating the agenda, use it to facilitate meetings with group members’ active involvement to review and analyze data—including student demographics, learning, academic performance, and/or well-being—and other relevant student information related to the selected area of educational focus. Record minutes during the planning meeting, describing what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>a.\tData source(s):</p><p>b.\tDiscussion of data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Prepare the meeting before it occurs. Include who will attend, the purpose, norms, data, discussion of the problem, strategy selection, implementation planning, and next actions. The prompts below are the current optional format; the agenda itself is required.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"47\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[10/13/25] </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[1:30 – 2:30 PM] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"48\"><p>PLC Norms: [Be present.] [Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] [Be open to different perspectives.] [Stay on topic.] PLC Norms: [Be present.] [Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] [Be open to different perspectives.] [Stay on topic.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"49\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Mrs. W.]  </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"51\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Focal Group of Students (i.e., common grade level or demographic): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[Third-grade students, with particular attention to those demonstrating foundational numeracy needs as identified through initial iReady BOY data.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"53\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To analyze available student performance data (including SBA and iReady) to identify a shared problem of practice and collaboratively select one evidence based instructional strategy to support third grade numeracy.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>School commitments</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"56\"><p>[The team will review the district numeracy goal requiring students in grades three through five to move an average of 40 points closer to proficiency on the SBA by 2026. The group will also revisit the school’s mission to provide equitable access to rigorous instruction and support student success in mathematics. iReady growth expectations will be referenced as ongoing indicators of progress toward these goals.] </p></div><h4>Data sources</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"59\"><p>[ •  iReady BOY Grade 3 overall placement data </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"60\"><p>iReady domain-level data (Number and Operations, Algebraic Thinking, Measurement and Data) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"61\"><p>SBA Math Distance-from-Standard trend data] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"63\"><p>[We will collaboratively review the iReady BOY data and domain information to identify trends in student performance and areas where students may need additional support. The team will examine overall placement, domain strengths, and domain gaps to determine which foundational skills may be affecting access to grade level math standards. This discussion will guide us toward identifying a shared problem of practice.] </p></div><h4>Problem and strategy</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"65\"><p>[The team will collaboratively identify a problem of practice based on patterns observed in student performance data. Discussion will focus on instructional areas that may be limiting students’ access to grade level mathematics content, particularly foundational skills that support success in later concepts. The group will work to define a problem of practice that is specific, actionable, and within the team’s instructional control.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"67\"><p>[After identifying the problem of practice, the team will explore possible strategies to address the identified need. We will consider more than one evidence based approach and discuss how each aligns with the data and the needs of our students before selecting one strategy collaboratively.] </p></div><h4>Implementation and next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"69\"><p>[Once the team selects a strategy, we will outline how we will implement it as a community of practice. This plan may include instructional routines, grouping structures, shared materials, monitoring tools, and a timeline for checking progress.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"71\"><p>[The team will determine next steps, including responsibilities, materials needed, how progress will be monitored, and the date of the next collaboration meeting.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The agenda identifies a date, participants, norms, a focal group, and relevant data sources. Its future tense correctly leaves the problem and strategy open to group discussion.</p><p>Broad items such as determining responsibilities or monitoring progress need enough time and a clear expected product. The agenda should not be treated as evidence that participants actually reached those decisions; the minutes and Part D must establish that.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Allocate realistic discussion time to data interpretation, alternatives, and implementation decisions.</li><li>Specify which dated data or student work members will examine and what questions will guide that examination.</li><li>Close with a plan to record owners, deadlines, needed support, and the next meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5. Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The agenda supports the combined planning judgment, but only the minutes and narrative can show that shared analysis and selection occurred. Stronger group-specific evidence is needed for a confident 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / Planning meeting minutes","url":"#cycle/c2/C","html":"<span id=\"c-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Planning meeting minutes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: While facilitating your planning meeting with your group members, record minutes of the discussion. Be sure to describe what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part C template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the actual discussion, differing interpretations, agreements, and next steps. Show how the group moved from evidence to a changeable problem and a selected strategy. Minutes are a record of the group's reasoning, not a second agenda or a transcript of everything said.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>School commitments</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"77\"><p>[The meeting began with a review of the school’s mission to provide equitable access to rigorous, high quality instruction for all students. The teachers noted that this mission includes preparing students for future success in higher level mathematics. The group also reviewed the district numeracy goal, which requires students in grades three through five to move an average of forty points closer to proficiency on the Smarter Balanced Assessment by 2026. In addition to the district goal, the team discussed the importance of cultivating strong mathematical understanding early in the year so students can meet iReady’s typical and stretch growth expectations. The group agreed that improving foundational number sense directly supports both the school mission and district goals.] </p></div><h4>Data discussion</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"79\"><p>[Through my facilitation, the team examined the i-Ready BOY data for third grade to better understand current student performance trends. I guided teachers to first look at overall placement results, which showed that only a small percentage of students were performing on or above grade level, while the majority were performing one or more grade levels below. I prompted the group to analyze domain-level data, which revealed significant gaps in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Together, we identified that these domains are foundational for addition and subtraction with regrouping, a major focus of third-grade mathematics and a prerequisite for later mathematical success. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"80\"><p>I supported the team in connecting these data points to classroom evidence by inviting teachers to share observations and early work samples. Through this discussion, teachers noted that many students struggle with place value understanding and regrouping concepts. At the same time, teachers shared that some students are already demonstrating strong conceptual understanding and would benefit from opportunities to extend their thinking. I named this pattern for the group and emphasized that the data reflected a wide range of instructional needs within and across classrooms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"81\"><p>Through my facilitation, I helped the team recognize that the data pointed to two instructional needs occurring simultaneously: targeted support for students who require foundational skill development and intentional opportunities for enrichment for students who are ready to move beyond grade-level expectations. I guided the discussion toward the idea that addressing both needs would require greater instructional consistency and intentional planning across the grade level. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"82\"><p>As a result of this collaborative analysis, the team agreed that students will require focused, intentional instruction to accelerate progress toward both i-Ready growth goals and SBA expectations. I summarized our discussion by highlighting that the data supports the need for a structured, shared approach that allows teachers to respond to varied student needs while maintaining coherence across classrooms. This collective understanding set the foundation for identifying an instructional strategy that could support differentiated instruction and equitable access to grade-level mathematics.] </p></div><h4>Problem of practice</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"84\"><p>[Through my facilitation of data analysis and collaborative discussion, the group identified the following problem of practice: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"85\"><p>Students lack foundational number sense skills, indicating a need for teachers to provide more consistent and targeted instruction in early numeracy to support access to grade-level mathematics and progress toward iReady growth and SBA proficiency. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"86\"><p>As I guided the team in examining iReady BOY data, domain-level results, and early classroom work samples, I supported teachers in identifying patterns showing that many students were performing below grade level, particularly in areas related to number and operations that underpin addition and subtraction with regrouping. I prompted the group to consider how these foundational gaps limit students’ ability to engage with grade-level content and contribute to persistent performance differences across student groups. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"87\"><p>Through this facilitated discussion, teachers noted that while many students require foundational support, others are performing at or above grade level and need opportunities for enrichment. I helped the group recognize that this range of performance requires intentional instructional shifts, particularly in how students are grouped and how foundational skills are reinforced. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"88\"><p>The team agreed that this problem of practice is specific, actionable, and directly aligned to district and school goals for mathematics achievement. Addressing it requires intentional changes in instructional practice, including more strategic grouping and targeted support, to ensure all students have equitable access to rigorous mathematics instruction and opportunities for accelerated growth.] </p></div><h4>Strategy selection</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"90\"><p>[To address the identified problem of practice, I intentionally introduced and shared two research-based instructional strategies that could support improved numeracy outcomes. I facilitated discussion around how each strategy aligned with our student data and instructional context. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"91\"><p>First, I shared research on flexible, skill-based grouping using the NCSM article Supporting All </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"92\"><p>Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. I highlighted how this approach allows teachers to group students temporarily based on current skill needs, adjust groups as students progress, and provide targeted instruction without permanently tracking students. I prompted the team to consider how flexible grouping could support both students who need foundational instruction and those who are ready for enrichment. Teachers noted that this approach would allow them to better address the wide range of readiness levels present in their classrooms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"93\"><p>Next, I shared a second research-based strategy focused on daily math reasoning routines, drawing from the Heinemann blog Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. I guided the team in discussing how routines such as Number Talks can strengthen conceptual understanding, mathematical discourse, and number sense, particularly for English learners and students who benefit from structured opportunities to explain their thinking. Teachers discussed how these routines could complement core instruction by building reasoning and language skills. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"94\"><p>After reviewing both strategies, I facilitated a discussion to help the team determine which approach best aligned with the identified problem of practice and their current instructional realities. Through this discussion, the group collaboratively decided to implement flexible grouping as the primary strategy, recognizing that it directly supports differentiated instruction across multiple performance levels. The team agreed to refer to this approach locally as Cruz’s Math Crew, while acknowledging that it represents a research-based model of flexible, skillbased grouping supported by NCSM. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"95\"><p>Teachers expressed that this strategy was particularly appropriate because their classrooms include students with a wide range of needs, from those requiring foundational support in addition and subtraction with regrouping to those ready for more advanced problem-solving. By adopting this approach, the team agreed they could provide targeted instruction while also offering enrichment opportunities, ensuring all students have access to meaningful and appropriately challenging math instruction.] </p></div><h4>Implementation</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"97\"><p>[After the group selected flexible grouping as the strategy to address the identified problem of practice, I facilitated the development of an initial implementation plan focused on supporting students’ foundational skills in addition and subtraction with regrouping. I guided the team to narrow the instructional focus so that implementation would be manageable, coherent, and aligned across classrooms. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"98\"><p>As a group, we agreed that the first cycle of Cruz’s Math Crew would target addition and subtraction with regrouping, as this skill represents a foundational prerequisite for later gradelevel work and was identified as an area of need in both assessment data and classroom observations. During the meeting, I supported the team in collaboratively creating a short preassessment designed to measure students’ understanding of place value and regrouping concepts. Teachers agreed to administer this preassessment prior to the next meeting and to use the results to inform instructional grouping. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"99\"><p>I facilitated discussion around how groups would function and emphasized that grouping would be temporary and flexible. Teachers agreed that some groups would focus on foundational numeracy skills such as composing and decomposing numbers, understanding place value relationships, and using visual or concrete models to support regrouping. Other groups would provide enrichment and extended problem solving opportunities for students who demonstrate readiness for more advanced work. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"100\"><p>To support coherence across classrooms, I guided the team in identifying shared expectations for implementation. Teachers agreed to use a consistent, protected instructional time for Cruz’s Math Crew, along with common language, routines, and visual models. I also encouraged collaborative planning and resource sharing so that instruction remained aligned while allowing teachers flexibility to respond to student needs. The group agreed that the length of the instructional cycle would be determined after reviewing preassessment data, and that a postassessment would be administered at the end of the cycle to evaluate the impact of the strategy.] </p></div><h4>Next actions</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"102\"><p>[To move the work forward, I facilitated agreement on clear next steps and responsibilities. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"103\"><p>Teachers will administer the regrouping preassessment and score it prior to the next meeting. They will bring student results, initial observations, and questions about grouping or instruction to support collaborative analysis. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"104\"><p>At the next meeting, the team will use the preassessment data to finalize instructional groupings for Cruz’s Math Crew and determine the duration of the first implementation cycle. Teachers will also identify or refine instructional materials and strategies aligned to the needs of each group. Together, the team will plan the post-assessment that will be used to measure student progress and inform future instructional decisions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"105\"><p>Throughout this process, I will continue to facilitate reflection and collaboration by prompting discussion around patterns in the data, supporting alignment across classrooms, and helping the team make shared decisions about adjustments to instruction. These next steps ensure that implementation remains responsive, data-informed, and focused on improving student access to grade-level mathematics.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The minutes describe analysis of i-Ready data, variation in readiness, comparison of flexible grouping with reasoning routines, and plans for a common assessment. They offer a coherent rationale for Cruz's Math Crew.</p><p>Repeated statements that the candidate facilitated the discussion tell less than a specific contribution that changed the decision. The record also needs clearer numbers for the named groups, concrete action owners, and an unambiguous timeline for assessment design and administration.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record specific member contributions, differing views, and how agreement was established.</li><li>Attach the actual group-level data or work used to define the need.</li><li>List each agreed action with its owner, due date, and evidence to bring back.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5. Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The recorded reasoning supports 3 across the planning evidence. Actual comparisons among student groups and more specific group-level strategy effects are needed to establish the respective Level 4 extensions.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.1 / Moving from data to a problem","url":"#cycle/c2/D1","html":"<span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.1 / Moving from data to a problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you: (a) worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the educational focus; (b) selected a problem of practice based on the area of educational focus; and (c) addressed how practitioners could potentially improve student learning and/or well-being by improving the current practices at your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the collaborative reasoning: which data the group examined, what members noticed, which problem they selected, and how changing current instruction could help. Keep the educational focus, problem of practice, and selected strategy distinct.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"112\"><p>[During the planning meeting, I facilitated a structured review of multiple third grade data sources connected to our mathematics focus. I began by guiding the team through iReady BOY results and SBA numeracy trend data to establish a shared understanding of current performance. I prompted teachers to examine the percentage of students performing below grade level and to look closely at domain level results, which revealed notable gaps in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. I supported the group in identifying patterns across classrooms and encouraged teachers to share observations from their daily instruction to add context to the data. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"113\"><p>To deepen the analysis, I asked guiding questions such as, “What patterns do we notice across the grade level?” and “Which foundational skills seem to be limiting students’ access to grade level math?” These questions helped teachers connect quantitative data with their classroom experiences. Teachers noted that many students struggle with composing and decomposing numbers, understanding place value, and applying regrouping strategies, which directly affects their ability to engage with grade level content. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"114\"><p>I facilitated discussion that helped the group recognize that while many students need foundational support, there are also students performing at or above grade level who require enrichment and opportunities to extend their thinking. Through this collaborative analysis, I supported the team in identifying a shared problem of practice focused on strengthening foundational number sense through more consistent and targeted instructional practices. Together, we concluded that addressing this need would require intentional instructional shifts, particularly in how students are grouped and how foundational skills are reinforced across classrooms to support progress toward iReady growth and SBA expectations.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies i-Ready and SBA evidence, guiding questions, teacher observations about place value, and the need to support both foundational and advanced learners. It connects analysis with an instructional change.</p><p>It would be stronger with one concrete example of a teacher's interpretation influencing the agreed problem. A repeated claim of collaboration is less persuasive than a visible decision trail. The problem should foreground instructional provision rather than defining students as deficient.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Show one data pattern and the member discussion that shaped the problem.</li><li>State what is inconsistent or insufficient in current instruction and what the group can change.</li><li>Connect the selected problem to the shared evidence without treating the strategy as already decided.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> Shared data analysis and recognition of a changeable practice support 3. Concrete evidence of differences across groups and their influence on the problem would strengthen the case for 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data","url":"#cycle/c2/D2","html":"<span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how you worked collaboratively with the group to review your collected data on student demographics, student learning and academic performance or well-being, and other relevant data differentiated by student groups.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group compared data across student groups. Identify the measures, populations, and findings, then explain what members made of the differences. Mentioning several group names does not demonstrate a differentiated analysis.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"117\"><p>[To deepen the analysis, I facilitated a review of student demographic and performance data across subgroups, including English learners, students with disabilities, and students without identified needs. We examined iReady BOY results and discussed patterns showing that a large proportion of students were performing below grade level in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Teachers noted that these gaps were evident across classrooms and student groups, suggesting a systemic instructional need rather than an isolated concern. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"118\"><p>I supported the group in analyzing how these data connected to classroom observations and student work. Teachers shared that many students struggled with place value understanding and regrouping, which limited their ability to access grade level math tasks. At the same time, teachers noted that some students were already demonstrating stronger skills and needed opportunities to extend their thinking. This discussion highlighted the wide range of learners within each classroom. </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage names English learners, students with disabilities, and students without identified needs, then reports broad gaps in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Teacher observations add context about place value and readiness.</p><p>No group-specific counts, rates, scores, or work examples are given here. The reader cannot determine whether patterns differ across groups or whether the proposed response follows from those differences. This is the central barrier to a confident Level 4 judgment for Rubric 2.3.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide the actual results for at least two relevant groups, with dates and denominators or clearly described work evidence.</li><li>Explain how overlapping group memberships and different measures affect comparison.</li><li>Show how the group used the differences to refine the problem rather than simply reporting them.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> This is the central limit on moving from 3 to 4: the response names groups but does not present their differentiated performance evidence or show how actual differences shaped the decision.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.3 / Identifying a change in practice","url":"#cycle/c2/D3","html":"<span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.3 / Identifying a change in practice</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe the ways you facilitated the meeting to acknowledge how the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current practices in your professional context.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how you helped the group recognize that something adults currently do can change. Name the instructional practice under discussion and show how members connected that change to the evidence. This is separate from merely identifying low performance.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"119\"><p>Through my facilitation, I prompted the team to consider how current instructional structures might be limiting our ability to respond effectively to this range of needs. I asked questions such as, “What instructional practices might help us respond more intentionally to different readiness levels?” and “How might we adjust our structures so students receive support that aligns more closely with their skill gaps?” These questions helped the group recognize that addressing the problem of practice would require shifts in how instruction is organized and delivered, particularly in how students are grouped and supported during math instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"120\"><p>As a result of this collaborative discussion, the team agreed on the following problem of practice: Students lack foundational number sense skills, indicating a need for more consistent and targeted instruction in early numeracy to support access to grade level math content and progress toward iReady growth and SBA proficiency.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The questions about instructional structures and readiness move the discussion toward practices the team can change. These passages directly address the current standalone question, so no invented response is needed.</p><p>The final problem still begins with students lacking number sense. It should identify the current instructional opportunity that is missing or inconsistent, supported by classroom evidence, rather than imply that flexible grouping is justified solely by low scores.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the current practice the team agreed was limiting access to targeted instruction.</li><li>Record what members said that showed their recognition of the needed change.</li><li>Explain why the proposed change follows from the evidence, keeping alternative explanations visible.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> The questions about instructional structures help establish the Level 3 requirement to recognize a change in practice. This does not replace the differentiated data needed for 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences","url":"#cycle/c2/D4","html":"<span id=\"d-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow does the identified problem of practice: (a) correspond to the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/educational setting; (b) reflect California state standards and/or curriculum frameworks or equivalent, or other applicable standards; and (c) address existing differences in student group performance or well-being?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three connections: school commitments, relevant standards or frameworks, and actual differences in student learning or well-being. Explain how each connection supports the selected problem, not just that the problem aligns with them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"122\"><p>[The problem of practice corresponds directly to our school’s mission of providing equitable access to rigorous instruction and preparing students for success in higher level mathematics. Focusing on foundational numeracy supports the district’s long term numeracy goals and aligns with the California Mathematics Framework, which emphasizes conceptual understanding, fluency with numbers, and flexible problem-solving. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"123\"><p>Addressing early numeracy gaps also responds to existing performance differences. Third grade data showed that students across multiple subgroups struggle with foundational skills, with students performing below grade level needing accelerated progress to meet annual growth expectations. By focusing on foundational number sense, the problem of practice aims to reduce these differences and ensure all students, including those who are already on track, receive instruction that advances their learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response links numeracy with equitable access and references the mathematics framework's conceptual understanding and flexible problem-solving. It recognizes the need to support students at different readiness levels.</p><p>The standards connection remains broad, and the group differences are asserted without comparative evidence. A named grade-level learning expectation and an example of what students can or cannot yet do would make the alignment concrete.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the relevant grade-level learning expectation and the work that reveals difficulty with it.</li><li>Connect the school goal to the instructional opportunity the group can change.</li><li>Use actual group differences to explain why the problem matters for equitable access.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.3: 3 / 5.</strong> The school-goal and framework connections support the problem rationale. The combined estimate remains 3 because the performance differences are not sufficiently demonstrated.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.1 / Selecting one strategy together","url":"#cycle/c2/D5","html":"<span id=\"d-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.1 / Selecting one strategy together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tExplain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting in selecting one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice, potentially leading to improved student learning and/or well-being across student groups. Be sure to include how you facilitated the group to help make their selection.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group considered and selected one relevant evidence-based strategy. Include the alternatives, criteria, member contributions, and your facilitation. The group's decision is the object of analysis, not just your preferred strategy.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"126\"><p>[Once the problem of practice was established, I facilitated a discussion of evidence-based strategies that could address the identified numeracy gaps. I introduced two options for consideration: skill-based regrouping (academies), supported by the NCSM article Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices, and daily math reasoning routines such as </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"127\"><p>Number Talks, supported by the Heinemann article Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. I guided the group to examine how each strategy would function in practice by prompting discussion around alignment to student needs, feasibility, and the ability to differentiate instruction across multiple performance levels. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"128\"><p>To support collaborative decision making, I asked questions such as, “Which approach best targets the specific gaps we are seeing in addition and subtraction?”, “Which strategy allows us to meet the wide range of learners in our classrooms?”, and “Which approach can we implement consistently as a team?” Through this discussion, teachers noted that flexible grouping would allow them to reorganize students based on current skill needs, provide targeted instruction, and offer enrichment for students already demonstrating proficiency. The group reached consensus to select flexible grouping, which we named Cruz’s Math Crew, as the strategy most likely to address foundational numeracy gaps while supporting differentiated instruction for all learners.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example compares flexible grouping with Number Talks and reports questions about fit, feasibility, and differentiation. It explains why teachers preferred temporary skill-based groups.</p><p>The two named articles function as evidence claims in the example, not independently verified research in this review. The response needs to explain what the supporting evidence says and how the proposed mathematics instruction will work inside the groups. Regrouping students is an organizational decision; the learning opportunities within those groups matter.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the specific instructional features the selected strategy will include.</li><li>Show how members evaluated the evidence and considered an alternative.</li><li>Document how shared agreement was reached, including concerns or conditions attached to the choice.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The comparison and reported joint selection support 3. A stronger strategy description should explain the expected instructional benefit for specific groups, not just why regrouping is feasible.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy","url":"#cycle/c2/D6","html":"<span id=\"d-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the group’s rationale for selecting this evidence-based strategy. How will the group learn this strategy and how to implement it? What challenges do you anticipate the group might encounter and how could they be addressed?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the group chose the strategy, how teachers will learn to use it, and how anticipated challenges will be addressed. Prior exposure to grouping is not the same as a plan to learn a particular instructional approach.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"130\"><p>[The teachers selected the flexible grouping strategy because it allows them to differentiate more effectively for the wide range of skill levels present in each classroom. The team emphasized that their classes include students performing well below, on, and above grade level, and that regrouping into temporary skill-based groups offers the most realistic way to provide targeted instruction that meets each student’s needs. They also noted that Grades K–2 have previously implemented flexible grouping systems for ELA instruction, which provided a successful model for meeting diverse learner needs. Because of this prior experience, I am able to support the team, and they identified opportunities to seek guidance from K–2 teachers who have used the approach extensively. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"131\"><p>To support implementation, the group collaboratively created a short pre-assessment during the meeting. This assessment will determine each student’s placement within Cruz’s Math Crew by identifying gaps in addition and subtraction with regrouping, as well as underlying place value concepts. Teachers plan to administer and score the pre-assessment before the next PLC meeting so the team can use the data to design groups and determine instructional foci. The team also discussed instructional resources, routines, manipulatives, and strategies they will use, and they expressed interest in observing one another’s groups during the implementation period. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"132\"><p>Anticipated challenges include securing consistent instructional time for group implementation, maintaining the flexibility of groups as students progress, and ensuring that instruction remains aligned across classrooms. To address these concerns, the team agreed to use common routines, shared language, and regular check ins during upcoming meetings. These structures will help ensure that instruction is cohesive, groups adjust responsively based on data, and the strategy remains manageable for all teachers throughout the cycle.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies prior K-2 experience, access to experienced colleagues, a common pre-assessment, shared routines, and scheduling challenges. Those are useful implementation conditions.</p><p>Interest in observing one another is not a scheduled learning plan. The explanation also needs to show how language supports, disability accommodations, enrichment, and movement between groups will work without creating fixed tracks.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify a rehearsal, observation, or collaborative planning activity with a date and responsible person.</li><li>Define the instructional routine, evidence for changing groups, and safeguards against permanent low-expectation placement.</li><li>Match anticipated challenges for specific learners with feasible supports.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The rationale and proposed common routines support 3, but the account does not fully detail how the strategy will affect specific groups. A practical learning plan would also make the expected implementation more credible.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits","url":"#cycle/c2/D7","html":"<span id=\"d-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat improvements for student learning and/or well-being related to the problem of practice do the group expect the selected evidence-based strategy will yield?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe what different students are expected to learn or experience because of the selected strategy. Explain the instructional mechanism: what teachers will do differently and why that should address the identified need. Keep predictions distinct from outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"134\"><p>[The group expects that the flexible skill based grouping strategy will lead to several meaningful improvements in student learning. By providing targeted instruction aligned to students’ specific numeracy needs, teachers anticipate that students who struggle with regrouping in addition and subtraction will develop stronger foundational number sense, increased accuracy, and improved confidence when working with multi-digit operations. Students performing at or above grade level will also benefit from enrichment groups that extend mathematical reasoning and problem solving, ensuring all learners receive instruction that matches their current readiness level. The group believes that this strategy will lead to greater student engagement, reduced frustration during core instruction, and more equitable access to grade level content. Overall, the strategy is expected to support academic growth and enhance students’ sense of competence and well being in mathematics.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response distinguishes students needing regrouping support from those ready for enrichment. Accuracy, reasoning, confidence, and reduced frustration are plausible targets.</p><p>The benefits are mostly broad expectations. A stronger explanation would identify the actual learning task or support for each group and the evidence that would indicate success. Confidence and engagement need their own evidence; they cannot be inferred from arithmetic scores alone.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each group's observed need to a specific instructional action and expected benefit.</li><li>Identify how conceptual reasoning, procedural accuracy, and enrichment will each be examined.</li><li>Use an appropriate source for any well-being or engagement claim.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> The response distinguishes broad readiness levels but gives limited detail about the instruction and expected learning in each. That makes 3 more defensible than the detailed cross-group impact explanation required for 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.4 / Monitoring implementation","url":"#cycle/c2/D8","html":"<span id=\"d-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.4 / Monitoring implementation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow will you facilitate the group in determining a monitoring process to understand the impact of the evidence-based strategy? Describe how you and the group plan to monitor and analyze initial implementation.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group will check both whether the strategy was implemented and what students initially learned. Specify evidence, timing, interpretation, and decision rules. The monitoring process must be manageable and jointly understood.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"136\"><p>[To determine the impact of the selected strategy, I will facilitate the group in developing a clear and manageable monitoring process. During the planning meeting, the team agreed to use a collaboratively created pre-assessment as the baseline measure for regrouping students within Cruz’s Math Crew. Before the next meeting, teachers will administer and score the preassessment, and we will review the results together to finalize group placements. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"137\"><p>To monitor initial implementation, the group plans to collect and share multiple forms of evidence, including student work samples, exit tickets, observation notes, and informal checks tied to regrouping and place value skills. I will guide the team in using these data to identify patterns, adjust grouping as needed, and note early signs of progress or challenges. At the beginning of each implementation meeting, I will facilitate a brief review of the collected evidence to help the group analyze the effectiveness of instruction and determine whether any adaptations are needed. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"138\"><p>The team also agreed to establish a defined instructional window for using Cruz’s Math Crew before giving a post-assessment. This post-assessment will allow the group to compare before and after data and evaluate the strategy’s early impact on student understanding. Through structured reflection, shared data analysis, and consistent check-ins, the group will maintain a clear process for monitoring progress and adjusting instruction to support all learners.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>A common baseline, work samples, exit tickets, observation notes, and a post-assessment provide a useful outline. Reviewing evidence at every implementation meeting connects monitoring with action.</p><p>The response leaves the instructional window undefined and does not establish common scoring, matched populations, or criteria for regrouping. Later concerns about assessment rigor make these omissions consequential: a before-and-after comparison is only interpretable when the measures and populations are understood.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Agree on dates, participants, scoring criteria, and the relationship between pre- and post-assessments.</li><li>Record how often and how the strategy was used, alongside student results.</li><li>Set a decision rule for adapting instruction and specify who will bring evidence to each meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.4: 3 / 5.</strong> Monitoring strengthens the credibility of the strategy plan, but does not by itself establish the group-specific impact explanation needed for 4. The present plan lacks enough precision to resolve later measurement concerns.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / Implementation agenda 1","url":"#cycle/c2/E1","html":"<span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan discussion of evidence from the strategy's initial use, not only its preparation. Include the required meeting information, norms, evidence review, summary, and next steps. A summary item on an agenda means planning time to summarize; it does not mean writing the conclusions in advance.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the implementation meetings</summary><p>Each meeting should carry the inquiry forward: revisit the previous commitment, examine evidence from actual implementation, compare interpretations, decide what to retain or adjust, and establish responsibilities and support. The next meeting examines what followed. These are implementation meetings, not repeated planning sessions.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Record</th><th scope=\"col\">What it should make visible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Evidence returning to the group</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">What was tried, what students demonstrated, and what is still uncertain. Include difficulties and counterexamples.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Shared interpretation and decision</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">How members used the evidence, where they disagreed, and why the group retained or adjusted the approach.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Support and follow-through</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">Who will do what, what help is needed, and what evidence the group will revisit. Later minutes record what actually happened.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An agenda anticipates this work. Minutes document it; products preserve the tools or analysis the group actually used. They should agree without copying one another.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Meeting information</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"144\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Grade Level(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[3  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[11/5/25  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[2:30 – 3:30 PM  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>PLC Norms: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be present.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be open to different perspectives.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Stay on topic.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. W.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"146\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To check in on the initial implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, review early information that will be shared from the preassessment </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>and classroom observations, and collaboratively reflect on how the strategy may be supporting students with varying levels of readiness in addition and subtraction with regrouping.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Early returns and new data</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"148\"><p>[The team will share early information related to the implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"149\"><p>Whether the preassessment has been administered and initial observations teachers plan to share about student performance </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"150\"><p>Initial patterns noticed across classrooms  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"151\"><p>Observations about student readiness levels and variation within classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"152\"><p>Early experiences with launching the groups, including transitions, routines, and expectations </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"153\"><p>The discussion will focus on identifying emerging trends and questions rather than drawing conclusions, since implementation has just begun.] </p></div><h4>Planned summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"155\"><p>[As a group, we will summarize key points discussed during the meeting, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"156\"><p>Common patterns noticed across classrooms related to student understanding of addition and subtraction with regrouping </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"157\"><p>Early strengths and challenges observed during the rollout of Cruz’s Math Crew </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"158\"><p>Initial observations related to student engagement, routines, and collaboration across classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"159\"><p>Any shared questions or areas that may need continued attention as implementation moves forward </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"160\"><p>The facilitator will support the group in naming patterns, highlighting shared observations, and confirming common understanding.] </p></div><h4>Planned next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"162\"><p>[The group will identify next steps to support continued implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, which may include: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"163\"><p>Continuing to implement flexible, leveled groups focused on addition and subtraction with regrouping </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"164\"><p>Continuing to refine routines and expectations for student transitions between classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"165\"><p>Monitoring student progress and gathering additional evidence from instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"166\"><p>Bringing updated observations and student work to the next meeting </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"167\"><p>Identifying any supports or adjustments needed to strengthen implementation </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"168\"><p>The team will confirm the focus for the next meeting and clarify what data or reflections members should bring.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The agenda identifies the first implementation check-in, the named strategy, launch routines, and emerging questions. Its caution against early conclusions is appropriate.</p><p>Some planned discussion concerns whether the pre-assessment has been administered or groups have started. Clarify which implementation has actually occurred by this meeting so the discussion can examine new evidence from use of the strategy, not remain another planning session.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify what instruction will have occurred before the meeting and what new evidence members will bring.</li><li>Reserve time to distinguish launch logistics from students' mathematical understanding.</li><li>Plan to record agreed actions, support, owners, and the next review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> This agenda plans relevant activity but cannot demonstrate facilitation or shared interpretation by itself. The provisional 3 uses the later minutes and commentary; actual implementation and video evidence remain essential.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / Implementation agenda 2","url":"#cycle/c2/E2","html":"<span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use the second agenda to revisit the first meeting's decisions and examine new evidence. Ask what was tried, what students did, and what may need adjustment. Repeating the same agenda headings is appropriate; repeating a generic discussion without follow-through is not.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Meeting information</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"173\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Grade Level(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[ 3 ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[11/19/25  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[2:30 – 3:30 PM  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>PLC Norms: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be present.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be open to different perspectives.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Stay on topic.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. W.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"175\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To reflect on early implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, analyze how students are responding within leveled groups, and determine whether instructional or grouping adjustments may be needed to better support student learning.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Early returns and new data</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"177\"><p>[The team will share early information related to the implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"178\"><p>Teachers will share observations from early Cruz’s Math Crew sessions </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"179\"><p>Discussion of how students appear to be responding within their current groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"180\"><p>Identification of early evidence of engagement, progress, or continued challenges </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"181\"><p>Consideration of whether any students may need to move between groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"182\"><p>Sharing instructional strategies being used with different groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"183\"><p>Discussion of how lessons are being planned and differentiated for each group </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"184\"><p>The discussion will focus on identifying emerging patterns and early indicators rather than making final judgments about effectiveness.] </p></div><h4>Planned summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"186\"><p>[As a group, we will summarize key themes that emerge from the discussion, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"187\"><p>Common patterns observed across classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"188\"><p>Areas of success related to student engagement and instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"189\"><p>Instructional or grouping adjustments under consideration </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"190\"><p>Confirmation of whether the current focus and strategy continue to align with student needs </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"191\"><p>The facilitator will help synthesize these themes by naming patterns across classrooms, highlighting shared observations, and checking for agreement to ensure a common understanding of how implementation is progressing.] </p></div><h4>Planned next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"193\"><p>[The group will identify next steps to support continued implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, which may include: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"194\"><p>Agreeing on potential group movement or instructional adjustments </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"195\"><p>Continuing implementation with planned lessons for each group </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"196\"><p>Monitoring student progress and engagement across groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"197\"><p>Determining whether to extend the current instructional cycle </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"198\"><p>Identifying data or student work to bring to the next meeting </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"199\"><p>The team will confirm expectations for what information to bring to the next meeting and identify any supports needed to strengthen implementation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>This agenda turns toward student responses, grouping changes, and differentiated instruction. It recognizes that early results should guide revision rather than a final effectiveness claim.</p><p>The connection to the previous meeting remains implicit. The agenda should identify which agreed actions are returning for review and the evidence needed to decide whether group movement or an extension of the cycle is warranted.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Bring forward specific actions agreed in meeting 1 and identify their owners.</li><li>Specify the work or assessment evidence needed to evaluate each proposed grouping change.</li><li>Plan discussion of different student experiences, including students not benefiting yet.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The planned revisit is relevant to the provisional 3. A 4 requires demonstrated inclusion or sustained follow-through, depending on the rubric, not simply agenda items inviting them.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / Implementation agenda 3","url":"#cycle/c2/E3","html":"<span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan a shared examination of initial results and a decision about what comes next. Compare meaningful evidence across the implementation period and retain uncertainty about causes or measures. The final scheduled meeting should lead to specific action, not only celebration.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Meeting information</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"204\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Grade Level(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Date: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Meeting Start and End Time: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[3  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[12/10/25  ] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[2:30 – 3:30 PM  ] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>PLC Norms: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be present.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Come prepared and be ready to collaborate.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Be open to different perspectives.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"5\"><p>[Stay on topic.] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Meeting Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>Names of Participants: </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Their Role in Your Educational Context: </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. W.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher &amp; Grade Level Chair </p><p>(SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mrs. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (SAS)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Mr. A.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Third Grade Teacher (General Education)] </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\"><p>[Ms. K.] </p></td><td colspan=\"2\"><p>[Second Grade Teacher &amp; Facilitator] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"206\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>Meeting Goal(s): </p></td><td colspan=\"1\"><p>[To review post-assessment results from the implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew, examine evidence of student progress and ongoing needs, and collaboratively determine next instructional steps to strengthen math instruction across groups.] </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Results to examine</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"208\"><p>[The team will review post-assessment data and observations gathered after several weeks of implementing Cruz’s Math Crew. Discussion will include: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"209\"><p>Sharing post-assessment results from each classroom </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"210\"><p>Discussing general trends in student performance (e.g., maintenance, improvement, or areas of continued need) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"211\"><p>Identifying patterns related to regrouping, word problems, and foundational skills </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"212\"><p>Sharing observations about how students are responding to the group structure and instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"213\"><p>Considering variation across groups and levels] </p></div><h4>Planned synthesis</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"215\"><p>[As a group, we will synthesize key takeaways from the discussion, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"216\"><p>Common patterns observed across classrooms </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"217\"><p>Reflections on how students are responding to Cruz’s Math Crew </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"218\"><p>Strengths of the current implementation </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"219\"><p>Questions or areas that may need further attention or refinement </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"220\"><p>Shared understandings that emerge from the discussion </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"221\"><p>The facilitator will support the group in naming patterns across classrooms and confirming a shared understanding of student progress and areas for refinement.] </p></div><h4>Planned next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"223\"><p>[The team will identify and agree on next instructional actions, including: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"224\"><p>Discussing potential instructional adjustments based on post-assessment results </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"225\"><p>Considering whether to extend or refine the current cycle </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"226\"><p>Identifying possible focus areas for upcoming instruction </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"227\"><p>Discussing how future preassessments might be refined to better target standards </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"228\"><p>Determining what data or reflections members should bring to the next meeting] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The agenda appropriately includes post-assessment results, variation across groups, continuing needs, and refinement of future assessments. It leaves conclusions open for group discussion.</p><p>The phrase maintenance or improvement needs separate categories in the evidence brought to the meeting. A combined count can hide students who made no measured gain, while class averages can hide learners whose performance declined.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Prepare separate counts for improved, unchanged, and lower scores, with the matched student denominator.</li><li>Bring the assessment tasks and scoring criteria so rigor and alignment can be examined.</li><li>Plan to decide which instruction continues, changes, or ends and how the next decision will be monitored.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The agenda creates an opportunity to examine results and decide next steps. The provisional 3 depends on evidence of what the group actually did; a plan for synthesis is not evidence of consensus.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / Implementation minutes 1","url":"#cycle/c2/F1","html":"<span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Document what the first implementation meeting actually established: evidence from instruction, what is working, needed changes, member reflections, and agreed support. Include enough specificity for the next meeting to check follow-through.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What is going well</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"240\"><p>[Teachers reported that the initial implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew has begun smoothly. All participants administered the preassessment focused on addition and subtraction with regrouping. Teachers shared that student performance varied widely across classrooms, confirming the need for flexible grouping. Several teachers noted that while some students demonstrated strength with addition, many struggled with subtraction with regrouping, particularly when word problems were involved. English Learners were identified as needing additional support with academic language embedded in problem solving. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"241\"><p>Teachers also shared early successes related to implementation logistics. Students were able to transition between classrooms, expectations were clearly communicated, and routines such as name tags and lining up procedures supported smoother movement. Teachers noted that practicing transitions and clearly teaching expectations helped students feel more comfortable during rotations. I highlighted these early successes as indicators that the structure was supporting access and predictability for students.] </p></div><h4>What needs adjustment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"243\"><p>[Teachers noted that some procedural elements still need refinement, including ensuring consistent use of name tags and reinforcing expectations across classrooms. A need was identified to continue supporting students who struggle with language demands in math, especially during word problems. Teachers also discussed the importance of continued communication among staff when students require additional behavioral or safety supports, such as notifying colleagues about students who may elope. These adjustments were viewed as necessary to strengthen consistency and safety as implementation continues.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"245\"><p>I prompted discussion about how these adjustments could be addressed through clearer communication routines and shared expectations, and I documented agreed upon areas for follow up.] </p></div><h4>Participant reflections</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"247\"><p>[I prompted reflection by asking teachers to share observations from the preassessment and their early experiences implementing Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers reflected that the preassessment outcomes were largely consistent with expectations and confirmed gaps that had been previously suspected based on iReady and SBA data. They noted that working collaboratively and sharing expectations increased their confidence in implementing the strategy and helped ensure consistency across classrooms. Several teachers shared that students appeared to enjoy learning with different teachers, which positively impacted engagement. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"248\"><p>I acknowledged the team’s decision to name the strategy Cruz’s Math Crew and highlighted how connecting the work to an existing schoolwide character and growth mindset framework supported student buy-in. I reinforced the importance of using shared language and common expectations so students would understand the purpose of the groups and experience predictable routines across classrooms. Teachers reflected that this shared approach helped students transition more smoothly and begin to take ownership of expectations. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"249\"><p>To support implementation, I encouraged teachers to communicate proactively about student needs, particularly for students requiring additional behavioral or academic supports. Teachers discussed how sharing information about students, such as behavioral considerations or transition needs, helped ensure consistency and safety across classrooms. I affirmed these practices as effective collaborative supports and emphasized the importance of continued communication as the groups evolve.] </p></div><h4>Summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"251\"><p>[During this meeting, the team reviewed early implementation data from the Cruz’s Math Crew strategy, including preassessment results and initial observations from the first days of grouping. Teachers confirmed that student performance aligned with patterns previously identified through iReady and SBA data, particularly in addition and subtraction with regrouping. The group discussed early successes, such as increased student engagement, clearer expectations, and smoother transitions supported by shared norms and language. Teachers also noted the importance of communication and collaboration when supporting students with diverse academic and behavioral needs.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"253\"><p>I supported the group in synthesizing patterns across classrooms and clarifying areas that required continued attention. Overall, the team affirmed continued implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew and agreed to refine routines and supports as implementation continues.] </p></div><h4>Next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"255\"><p>[The team agreed to continue implementing Cruz’s Math Crew and to refine procedures for transitions and expectations. Teachers will continue using the preassessment data to inform grouping and will monitor student progress during instruction. Prior to the next meeting, teachers will gather observations, student work, and notes on how students are responding to instruction. I will support the team by facilitating ongoing check-ins, helping organize shared observations, and guiding reflection during the next meeting. At the next PLC, we will review early progress, discuss any needed adjustments to grouping or instruction, and determine next instructional steps to better support students who require additional scaffolding or enrichment.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The minutes describe pre-assessment use, student transitions, name tags, routines, and early differentiation. They show that the group noticed practical access issues during implementation.</p><p>A smooth launch is not yet evidence of improved mathematics learning. The broad references to observations and follow-up would be more useful with a specific student-work pattern and an assigned action. The facilitator's account should distinguish what teachers reported from what the candidate directly observed.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record one concrete early learning pattern alongside the launch logistics.</li><li>Distinguish member reports, documents examined, and your own observations.</li><li>Assign the agreed procedural or instructional changes to named roles and set a review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The minutes supply implementation context for the provisional 3. They need clearer student-learning evidence, agreed support, and follow-through, alongside the video demonstration, to support a higher judgment.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / Implementation minutes 2","url":"#cycle/c2/F2","html":"<span id=\"f-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what the group learned after trying the strategy and carrying out prior decisions. Record the evidence behind adjustments, including student movement between groups, instructional supports, and the support you agreed to provide.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What is going well</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"260\"><p>[I facilitated a discussion in which teachers shared early observations from implementing Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers reported that students were generally responding positively to the grouping structure and were motivated by working with different teachers. Several participants noted that students appeared excited about rotations and looked forward to math group time. Teachers shared that some students demonstrated strong performance, particularly in addition with regrouping, and were ready for increased challenge. Others described success using visual models, gestures, and step by step representations to support students when solving word problems. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"261\"><p>I highlighted these examples to reinforce how intentional grouping and instructional planning were supporting student engagement and access to learning. I also named these as early indicators of positive impact on student experience. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"262\"><p>Teachers also shared that students in advanced groups were eager to engage in higher-level problem solving and were motivated by being challenged. I acknowledged this as evidence that flexible grouping was allowing students to access instruction at an appropriate level. I summarized that across classrooms, teachers were seeing early positive engagement and responsiveness to instruction, even though students were working at different levels.] </p></div><h4>What needs adjustment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"264\"><p>[I guided the group to reflect on areas needing refinement. Teachers shared that some students continued to struggle with word problems, particularly those involving academic language or comparison situations. Several teachers noted that students needed additional support with place value concepts, especially understanding ones, tens, and hundreds when regrouping. I prompted discussion around instructional adjustments, and teachers described adapting their instruction by incorporating more visual representations, modeling, gestures, and explicit sequencing of steps. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"265\"><p>Teachers also noted the need to adjust group placement as students made progress. I acknowledged that movement between groups is an expected and important part of flexible grouping and emphasized that regrouping based on ongoing evidence is a strength of the strategy rather than a challenge. I also facilitated discussion around pacing, as teachers shared that some students were ready to move forward while others required additional time and practice. Toward the end of the discussion, the group agreed that the current cycle might need to be extended to allow students sufficient time to develop understanding before moving on. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"266\"><p>I supported the group in identifying concrete areas for refinement, including pacing, regrouping decisions, and instructional scaffolds, and emphasized that adjustment is an expected and productive part of implementation rather than a sign of difficulty.] </p></div><h4>Participant reflections</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"268\"><p>[I prompted reflection by asking teachers to share what they were noticing about student learning and engagement during implementation. Teachers reflected that students appeared motivated by the structure of Cruz’s Math Crew and were excited to rotate to different classrooms. They noted that working with different teachers increased engagement and encouraged students to take ownership of their learning. Several teachers shared that students benefited from explicit modeling, visuals, and hands-on supports, particularly when solving word problems or working through regrouping steps. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"269\"><p>I acknowledged these reflections and emphasized that the team’s collaboration and willingness to share observations was strengthening implementation. I highlighted that teachers were actively adjusting instruction based on student needs, such as moving students between groups, modifying tasks, and selecting strategies aligned to each group’s level. I also noted that teachers were beginning to plan intentionally for each group by identifying appropriate tasks, levels of challenge, and instructional supports.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"270\"><p>I emphasized that this level of collaborative reflection demonstrates shared ownership for student learning and supports more equitable instructional decision making across classrooms.] </p></div><h4>Summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"272\"><p>[During this meeting, I facilitated a collaborative review of early implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers shared observations about student engagement, progress, and instructional needs across groups. The group discussed how students were responding to rotations, how instructional strategies such as visual models and explicit modeling were supporting understanding, and how some students were ready for increased challenge while others required continued foundational support. We discussed the importance of flexible movement between groups and acknowledged that differences in student progress were expected.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"274\"><p>I helped the group synthesize patterns across classrooms and connect observations to instructional decisions, reinforcing the importance of monitoring impact over time rather than making isolated judgments. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"276\"><p>Overall, the discussion reflected strong collaboration, shared responsibility, and growing alignment in instructional planning.] </p></div><h4>Next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"278\"><p>[I guided the group in identifying next steps to support continued implementation. Teachers agreed to extend the current Cruz’s Math Crew cycle to allow additional time for students to develop understanding. We agreed to continue monitoring student progress and to adjust group placement as needed based on ongoing observations. Teachers will continue planning targeted lessons for each group, including foundational practice, visual modeling, and enrichment tasks aligned to student needs.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"280\"><p>I clarified expectations for documentation and evidence to bring to the next meeting and reiterated my role in supporting coordination, reflection, and adjustment across the team. At the next PLC, we will review updated observations, discuss the impact of continued instruction, and determine whether additional refinements or transitions to new content are appropriate.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The record describes students' differing readiness, advanced learners' engagement, continued difficulties, and possible grouping adjustments. It recognizes that flexible placement should change as learning changes.</p><p>Statements about strong collaboration and responsiveness need concrete support. The reader needs to know what work justified a grouping decision, which action from meeting 1 was completed, and what changed for a student after it.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the evidence supporting at least one group movement or instructional adjustment.</li><li>Report completion and results of actions agreed in the previous meeting.</li><li>Record who will provide the agreed support and what evidence will return in meeting 3.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> Specific adjustment ideas support the provisional 3. The record needs clearer connections to prior actions and evidence of whose views shaped the discussion for the Level 4 extensions.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / Implementation minutes 3","url":"#cycle/c2/F3","html":"<span id=\"f-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the group's interpretation of initial results, including successes, continuing needs, limitations, and next actions. Show how members reached a shared conclusion rather than simply declaring that the strategy worked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What is going well</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"285\"><p>[I facilitated a review of post-assessment data to examine the impact of Cruz’s Math Crew after several weeks of implementation. Teachers shared quantitative results indicating that a large majority of students maintained or improved their performance. One teacher reported that 14 of 17 students showed growth, while another shared that 17 of 20 students maintained or improved. Teachers noted that many students increased by one to two performance points, suggesting meaningful academic progress during the instructional cycle. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"286\"><p>I supported the group in identifying patterns across classrooms and noted that flexible grouping allowed teachers to provide instruction that was better aligned to students’ readiness levels. Teachers shared that students who were ready for challenge benefited from more complex tasks, while others made gains through targeted instruction focused on regrouping and place value. I named these patterns as early evidence of instructional impact and affirmed that the strategy was positively influencing how students accessed and engaged with math instruction across groups. I highlighted these outcomes as evidence that the strategy positively impacted student learning for a wide range of learners.] </p></div><h4>What needs adjustment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"288\"><p>[I guided the team in reflecting on areas where adjustments were needed. Teachers discussed that some students, particularly those with gaps in foundational place value understanding, did not show the same level of growth and would benefit from continued targeted support. Teachers also reflected on the post-assessment itself, noting that portions of the assessment may not have been well aligned to students’ instructional experiences and may have been overly complex for some groups. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"289\"><p>I prompted discussion around instructional pacing and assessment design, and teachers agreed that future pre- and post-assessments should be more intentionally aligned to specific standards and skill progressions. Teachers also noted the importance of monitoring group placement more frequently to ensure students are moved sooner when additional support or challenge is needed. I emphasized that identifying these needs for adjustment reflects responsive instruction and strengthens the team’s ability to refine practice based on evidence rather than assumptions.] </p></div><h4>Participant reflections</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"291\"><p>[I prompted reflection by asking teachers to consider how the strategy influenced both student learning and their instructional decision making. Teachers reflected that Cruz’s Math Crew allowed them to address student needs more efficiently by narrowing the range of skills within each group. Several teachers shared that working with students at similar readiness levels increased their ability to provide focused instruction and respond quickly to misconceptions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"292\"><p>I acknowledged teachers’ reflections and emphasized how their collaborative analysis of data supported more responsive instruction. Teachers reflected that reviewing data together increased their confidence in making instructional adjustments, including regrouping students, modifying tasks, and planning targeted lessons. I emphasized that this collaborative reflection strengthened shared responsibility for student outcomes and supported more equitable instructional decision making across classrooms. I reinforced that this shared reflection demonstrated collective responsibility for student outcomes and strengthened instructional coherence across classrooms.] </p></div><h4>Summary</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"294\"><p>[During this meeting, I facilitated a collaborative analysis of post-assessment data to evaluate the impact of Cruz’s Math Crew. Teachers shared evidence of student growth across groups and identified trends indicating that flexible grouping supported improved access to instruction. The team discussed both successes and challenges related to student progress, assessment alignment, and instructional pacing. I supported the group in synthesizing findings and recognizing that while most students benefited from the strategy, continued adjustments were needed to support students with persistent gaps. I guided the team in connecting evidence of impact to future instructional decisions rather than viewing results in isolation. The discussion reflected thoughtful use of data, shared accountability, and a focus on continuous improvement.] </p></div><h4>Next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"296\"><p>[I guided the group in identifying next steps based on the data analysis. Teachers agreed to continue using Cruz’s Math Crew as an instructional structure while shifting focus to new grade level standards, including multiplication and division. The team agreed to revise future preassessments to align more closely with specific standards and to use released or standardsbased items to better measure student readiness. Teachers also agreed to continue providing targeted small-group support for students who require additional instruction in addition and subtraction skills. I clarified expectations for continued monitoring of student progress and for bringing updated instructional plans and assessment tools to the next meeting. At the next meeting, the team will review updated instructional plans and determine how the revised assessment process supports ongoing implementation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The minutes include post-assessment review, unresolved needs, assessment alignment, and plans to continue targeted instruction. Recognition of a mismatch between instruction and an assessment is an important finding.</p><p>It also limits the strength of the outcome claim. The group should identify which assessment produced which result and separate maintenance from growth. General agreement to continue Cruz's Math Crew needs a more explicit account of who receives which support next.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link each reported result to the exact assessment, scoring method, and group of students.</li><li>Record the differing interpretations and how the group resolved or retained uncertainty.</li><li>Specify the next instructional response for students needing reinforcement and those ready to extend learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The recorded decisions support a provisional 3, but measurement ambiguities and an incomplete action trail constrain a higher judgment. The clips must confirm the shared interpretation and facilitation.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part G / Collaborative work products","url":"#cycle/c2/G","html":"<span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Collaborative work products</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit key collaborative work products (up to 10 pages) used during the implementation meetings (e.g., lesson plans, student work products, instructional materials, assessment tools, data or evidence collected).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 19; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit actual products used or created in the community's work, within the handbook's page limit. Explain their purpose, when the group used them, and which decisions they support. A product can document planning, implementation, or a result, but those functions should not be confused.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"401\"><p>1: PLC Regrouping Planning Document (2 pages) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"402\"><p>This document was collaboratively developed by the PLC to establish a shared instructional focus for teaching addition and subtraction with regrouping and to guide lesson planning, task selection, and analysis of student understanding. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"403\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-403-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 1</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"406\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-406-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 1: regrouping planning document, page 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"411\"><p>PLC Meeting Minutes #1 – Pre-Assessment Design &amp; Instructional Logistics </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"412\"><p>These meeting minutes document collaborative planning related to the creation of a pre-assessment and the establishment of instructional logistics for implementing small group math instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"414\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-414-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 2: pre-assessment and logistics meeting record</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"415\"><p>PLC Meeting Minutes #2 – Pre-Assessment Scoring, Group Formation &amp; Instructional Roles </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"416\"><p>These meeting minutes capture the PLC’s collaborative analysis of pre-assessment data, the formation of instructional groups, and the assignment of teaching roles to support targeted instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"417\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-417-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 3: scoring and grouping meeting record</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"421\"><p>Pre-/Post-Assessment (Blank Copy, 2 pages) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"422\"><p>This assessment was administered both before and after instruction to monitor student understanding of regrouping and to inform instructional decisions and reflection. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"424\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-424-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 1</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"430\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-430-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 4: common pre/post assessment, page 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"431\"><p>5: Class-Level Pre/Post Assessment Summary </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"432\"><p>This class-level summary of pre- and post-assessment results was referenced during Video Clip 3 as part of a broader discussion of student outcomes and assessment rigor, while maintaining student anonymity. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"439\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-439-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 5: visible pre/post scores for 17 students</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"442\"><p>Work Product 6: Post-Assessment Referenced in Video Clip 3 (Blank Copy) </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"443\"><p>This post-assessment was discussed during Video Clip 3 as part of a reflective conversation about alignment between instructional focus and assessment rigor. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"445\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 2: Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle2/block-445-0.jpg\" alt=\"Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Work Product 6: assessment discussed for alignment and rigor</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example includes a planning document, additional meeting records, assessment tasks, and a class-level result summary. These make the strategy and monitoring process more inspectable than the narrative alone.</p><p>The 17 visible pre/post score pairs in Work Product 5 appear to show 13 improved, 2 unchanged, and 2 lower scores: 15 of 17 maintained or improved. The yellow heading says 14 of 17. Reconcile that discrepancy with the underlying records before using the summary. This screenshot does not display all 85 students mentioned in the reflection, so it cannot independently verify the broader 72-of-85 statement.</p><p>A blank assessment establishes what was asked, not how students answered. The separately identified post-assessment discussed for rigor should not be treated as interchangeable with the common measure without explanation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Label each product with its date, purpose, group use, and relationship to a specific decision.</li><li>Distinguish blank tools, completed work, aggregated results, and the assessment that raised alignment concerns.</li><li>Reconcile the visible score pairs, the 14-of-17 heading, and the broader 72-of-85 report; distinguish growth, maintenance, and decline.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> These artifacts support the provisional 3 by making the planning and assessment process inspectable. They do not resolve the numerical inconsistency or replace the required evidence of facilitation in video.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part H / Implementation meeting video clips","url":"#cycle/c2/H","html":"<span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Implementation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the videos of the implementation meetings where you and the individuals participating are seen and heard, select and submit 3 video clips, each up to 5 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 5 minutes of each video clip submitted), that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Setting the Purpose and Data Review (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you set the purpose for the meeting and how you facilitate the community of practice in analyzing the data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy. • Video Clip 2: Professional Collaboration (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates your facilitation of the community of practice and document their agreements, their decisions, and how they arrive at a consensual understanding of the initial implementation results. Also include how you facilitate the community of practice to (a) respect diverse viewpoints, (b) respond to individual and collective feedback, (c) maintain the group’s focus and energy, and (d) monitor progress toward goals of the meetings.</p><p>Video Clip 3: Results and Next Steps (up to 5 minutes) • Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you facilitate the community of practice in reporting and discussing the results of their implementation of the evidenced-based strategy and jointly plan for next steps/between meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 20 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit the three required clips from authentic implementation meetings: setting purpose and examining data; professional collaboration; and results with jointly planned next steps. The handbook specifies up to five minutes for each clip. The written commentary must refer to what is actually visible and audible.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><p class=\"scope-note\">The source commentary refers to video clips, but those recordings were not available for this review. No example video or invented transcript is substituted.</p></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Part I describes three clips and supplies timestamps, but a written account cannot establish visibility, audibility, authentic participation, or whether the described interaction falls within the submitted clips.</p><p>The absence of recordings from this review is not a finding that the candidate failed to submit them. It is a limit on this review. No definitive video-dependent score can be assigned here.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual recordings against the three required functions and the time limits.</li><li>Verify that the candidate and required participants can be seen and heard and that permissions are in place.</li><li>Ensure each commentary claim aligns to its cited clip and timestamp; do not stage or script missing evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: Not independently assessable without the video. Rubric 2.6: Not independently assessable without the video.</strong> The written commentary supports provisional estimates elsewhere, but the clips themselves cannot be judged from the source document. No score is assigned to an unseen recording.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 1 / Establishing meeting purpose","url":"#cycle/c2/I1","html":"<span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Establishing meeting purpose</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you set the purpose for the meetings/reviewed the problem of practice with the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use a specific clip moment to explain how you established the meeting's purpose and connected it to the problem of practice. Include what you did, why, and the response or change in the discussion that supports your interpretation of its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"301\"><p>[In Video Clip 1, I intentionally opened the meeting by welcoming the team and naming the purpose as a check in on our initial implementation of Cruz’s Math Crew (0:00–0:28). I reviewed the norms we had previously agreed on, including being present, coming prepared, being open to different perspectives, and staying on topic, and I asked for confirmation before moving forward (0:29–0:53). This helped establish shared expectations and created a respectful, collaborative environment for discussion. By beginning with norms and purpose, I ensured that participants understood both how we would work together and why the meeting mattered, which helped anchor the discussion in collective responsibility rather than individual classroom concerns. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"302\"><p>I then revisited the problem of practice by grounding the conversation in our shared data sources and prior planning decisions. I referenced our review of SBA and iReady data and named the instructional focus on addition and subtraction with regrouping as a foundational skill students need to access grade level math (0:55–1:26). I explicitly articulated the problem of practice by stating that students need more targeted and differentiated instruction in foundational numeracy skills in order to make progress toward grade level expectations (1:26– 1:40). I also restated the agreed upon strategy of flexible leveled grouping through Cruz’s Math Crew and confirmed shared commitment to that approach by asking whether everyone was still comfortable moving forward with it (1:40–2:16). This framing reinforced the connection between adult instructional practices and student outcomes and helped maintain a consistent throughline across meetings. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"303\"><p>In Video Clip 3, the clip opens with me again naming the purpose of the meeting by stating that we had been implementing Cruz’s Math Crew for several weeks and that our goal was to understand the impact of the strategy and determine next steps based on post assessment data (0:00–0:18). I explicitly framed the conversation around learning from evidence, saying that we were looking to understand “the impact that this strategy has had and then talk about our next steps.” This repeated framing helped create continuity across meetings and reinforced that each session was part of an ongoing improvement cycle rather than a standalone discussion.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies openings, welcomes, purpose statements, and returns to the shared mathematics problem. References to individual clips help a reader locate the claimed actions.</p><p>An opening statement establishes intent, not impact by itself. Explain how a member's response or the ensuing data discussion shows that the group understood and used the purpose.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Select a precise moment that includes both the purpose-setting action and the group's response.</li><li>Explain why that framing was needed at that point in implementation.</li><li>Describe its observable effect without assuming that a welcome or nod demonstrates shared understanding.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> Purpose-setting and the stated reasons are consistent with 3 in the written account. Level 4 requires purposeful inclusion of all members across the meeting evidence, not just a clear opening.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 2 / Evaluating implementation data","url":"#cycle/c2/I2","html":"<span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Evaluating implementation data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe how you and the community of practice evaluated data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group interpreted evidence from using the strategy. Distinguish baseline information, implementation observations, and post-assessment findings. Show your role in helping members examine what the evidence does and does not establish.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"305\"><p>[Across the three clips, I facilitated a progression from examining baseline data to reviewing implementation evidence and finally analyzing post-assessment results. I intentionally supported the group in moving beyond reporting scores to interpreting what the data suggested about instruction, so that data functioned as a tool for refinement rather than evaluation of students. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"306\"><p>In Video Clip 1, I prompted teachers to share what they noticed from the preassessment used to inform grouping (2:17–2:39). Teachers described patterns of student performance, noting that subtraction with regrouping and word problems were particularly challenging (2:44–4:12). As teachers shared, I supported collective sense making by naming patterns across classrooms and elevating equity considerations, particularly the language demands of word problems for English Learners (4:13–4:41). This helped shift the discussion from individual classroom observations to a shared understanding of student needs. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"307\"><p>In Video Clip 2, the focus shifted from baseline data to evidence gathered during implementation. The clip opens with me asking whether any adjustments were needed based on what teachers were seeing in their groups (0:00–0:09). Teachers then shared instructional evidence, such as noticing students who needed additional challenge and should move groups </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"308\"><p>(0:12–0:31), as well as students who struggled with comparison language in word problems (0:34–0:47). Teachers described specific strategies they were using, including visual representations, gestures, and breaking problems into steps (0:49–1:20). Others noted student motivation and engagement when working at an appropriate level of challenge (1:27–1:58). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"309\"><p>As these examples emerged, I supported evaluation of the strategy by naming that flexible grouping allowed teachers to notice differences in readiness and respond accordingly. I summarized that movement between groups was expected and productive, noting that “it makes sense that we might have some movement” as students progress or reveal additional needs (4:27–4:45). This helped the group view instructional adjustment as evidence of responsiveness and effective implementation rather than inconsistency. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"310\"><p>In Video Clip 3, the group evaluated post-assessment data more formally. Teachers shared quantitative outcomes such as “14 out of 17 students maintained or improved” and “17 out of 20 maintained or improved” (0:19–1:21). They also noted that many students increased by one to two points and identified reasons why some students showed less growth, such as gaps in place value understanding (0:41–1:21). I supported interpretation of this data by helping the group identify patterns and implications, stating that a “large percentage of students benefited from the strategy” while acknowledging that some students still needed additional support (2:48–3:01). Through this process, the group evaluated both effectiveness and limitations of the strategy using multiple sources of evidence.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The account moves from pre-assessment patterns through instructional adjustments to post-assessment discussion. It names concrete issues such as comparison language, visual supports, and differing progress.</p><p>Baseline data can explain initial group placement, but they are not results of the strategy. The analysis should identify the changing evidence and the exact group conclusions, especially where assessment alignment or combined maintenance-and-growth counts complicate interpretation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which evidence at each timestamp is baseline, implementation, or outcome evidence.</li><li>Report the actual comparison, including students with no gain or a lower score.</li><li>Explain the question or facilitation move that helped the group test an interpretation rather than merely share results.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The narrative describes evaluation and adaptation consistent with a provisional 3. A higher judgment needs clearer evidence of participation, shared interpretation, and continuing action rather than unqualified success claims.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 3 / Reaching shared understanding","url":"#cycle/c2/I3","html":"<span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Reaching shared understanding</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any agreements or decisions made by the group and how you facilitated the community of practice to arrive at a consensual understanding of any initial implementation results.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the agreement and how members arrived at it. Identify the evidence, alternatives or concerns, and your action in checking understanding. Agreement is not established solely because the facilitator summarized a conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"312\"><p>[In Video Clip 1, I supported consensus by first confirming shared understanding of the problem of practice and strategy before moving into analysis (0:29–2:16). After teachers described preassessment patterns, I synthesized their observations and named common areas of need such as subtraction with regrouping and language demands in word problems (4:13–4:41). This synthesis helped the group reach a shared understanding of what the data suggested and what to monitor moving forward. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"313\"><p>In Video Clip 2, I facilitated agreement around instructional adjustments and grouping decisions. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"314\"><p>Teachers discussed moving students between groups when readiness levels changed (0:12– 0:31, 2:27–3:27). I reinforced this as an expected feature of flexible grouping, stating that it made sense to adjust placement as students progressed or revealed new needs (4:27–4:45). By reframing regrouping as responsive rather than corrective, I supported consensus around continuing the strategy with flexibility. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"315\"><p>In Video Clip 3, I facilitated consensus around initial outcomes and next steps by helping the group interpret post-assessment results together. After teachers shared that many students demonstrated growth while others continued to need support, I summarized the emerging agreement that the strategy was having a positive impact while still requiring targeted differentiation. For example, after I asked, “What do we think based on the results? What do we think our next steps should be?” (3:37–3:41), a teacher affirmed that the grouping structure made it easier to address specific needs because there was not such a wide range of readiness levels within one group (3:43–4:10) and noted that we may need to adjust how we design the preassessment next cycle to be more specific (4:14–4:26). I then supported consensus by summarizing the team’s shared direction that many students were ready to move forward to new skills while some would continue to benefit from additional support through small groups or push in supports (4:28–4:46). I confirmed alignment by restating the next steps and linking them directly to the data and teacher reflections before the clip ends (4:46–4:59). This helped the group move from individual observations to a shared understanding of how to respond instructionally, ensuring our next steps reflected collective input and maintained a focus on meeting student needs across readiness levels.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage identifies grouping changes, instructional refinements, and discussion of results. It explains the candidate's efforts to frame regrouping as a purposeful part of the strategy.</p><p>The account would be more convincing with a specific exchange showing how different views were reconciled and how each member's understanding was checked. A statement that the group reached consensus needs a visible basis.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State one precise decision and the evidence used to make it.</li><li>Describe a differing view or uncertainty and how it influenced the decision.</li><li>Identify the clip moment where members confirmed the shared interpretation or retained an unresolved question.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported decisions support a provisional 3. Show the actual process of checking each viewpoint and carrying agreed actions forward to support the Level 4 extensions.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 4 / Supporting equitable participation","url":"#cycle/c2/I4","html":"<span id=\"i-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 4 / Supporting equitable participation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tDescribe how you</p><p>valued and respected diverse viewpoints,</p><p>responded to individual and collective feedback,</p><p>maintained the group’s focus and energy, and</p><p>monitored progress toward the goals of the meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four components: diverse viewpoints, individual and collective feedback, group focus and energy, and progress toward the meeting goals. Analyze the effect of your actions on participation and reasoning, not simply your use of polite language.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"318\"><p>[Across all three clips, I intentionally used facilitation moves that elevated teacher voice while maintaining focus on the purpose of each meeting. I viewed differences in perspective as assets for instructional problem solving rather than barriers to agreement. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"319\"><p>In Video Clip 1, I began by reinforcing norms that emphasized openness and respect for different perspectives (0:29–0:52). I invited each teacher to share observations rather than privileging one voice (2:27–2:40). When teachers raised different concerns, such as subtraction difficulty or language barriers for English Learners, I acknowledged each perspective and integrated them into a shared synthesis (4:13–4:41). This demonstrated that multiple viewpoints contributed to a fuller understanding of student needs. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"320\"><p>In Video Clip 2, I maintained focus by opening the clip with a clear guiding question about adjustments based on observations (0:00–0:09). I actively responded to individual contributions by asking follow up questions and clarifying meaning, such as when a teacher referred to a group as “the most difficult,” and I checked for shared understanding by reframing the comment in terms of level of challenge and instructional support needed (1:50–1:57). I also monitored progress toward meeting goals by periodically summarizing what we were learning, including that different groups required different supports and that movement between groups was expected (4:27–5:00). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"321\"><p>In Video Clip 3, I sustained focus by clearly stating that the purpose was to review postassessment data and determine next steps (0:00–0:18). I listened as teachers raised concerns about assessment difficulty and students who did not show as much growth (1:32–2:46). I then validated these perspectives while also highlighting strengths by summarizing that “a large percentage of students really benefited from the strategy,” which helped balance challenges with evidence of impact (2:48–3:01). I then redirected the conversation toward action by asking what steps should come next (3:37–3:41). These moves helped balance affirmation, accountability, and forward momentum.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example discusses norms, guiding questions, affirmation, and redirection toward action. Those are relevant facilitation moves, and the commentary gives reasons for using them.</p><p>Respectful responses to those already speaking do not establish that every member was encouraged to contribute. The Level 4 extension requires purposeful solicitation of diverse viewpoints, including quieter or differing perspectives, with evidence that those contributions were taken seriously.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify who contributed at the cited moments and how you invited perspectives not yet heard.</li><li>Show how feedback changed the discussion or decision, rather than only noting that it was acknowledged.</li><li>Connect a specific focus or pacing adjustment to progress on the meeting's goal.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.5: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> Norms, affirmation, and redirection support a provisional 3. A 4 requires evidence that the candidate actively sought varied views and encouraged every member to contribute.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 5 / Determining next steps together","url":"#cycle/c2/I5","html":"<span id=\"i-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 5 / Determining next steps together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tDescribe how you collaborated with the group to determine any next steps for implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show how evidence led the group to agree on its next actions. Explain what the candidate and members will do, how the plan responds to different needs, and how later evidence will reveal whether the response was useful.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"323\"><p>[Across the three meetings, I intentionally guided the group from evidence toward shared action through questioning, summarizing, and confirming consensus. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"324\"><p>In Video Clip 1, after discussing preassessment results, I shifted the conversation toward implementation by asking whether groups had begun meeting and how often (4:44–4:58). This helped connect early data to instructional action and set expectations for ongoing monitoring. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"325\"><p>In Video Clip 2, collaboration focused on refining instruction during implementation. Teachers identified concrete adjustments such as regrouping students, increasing visual supports, using gestures, and reinforcing place value concepts (0:12–1:21; 2:27–3:52). I validated these ideas and summarized that regrouping and instructional adjustment were intentional features of the strategy rather than deviations from it (4:27–4:45). This helped the team view flexibility as part of effective implementation. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"326\"><p>In Video Clip 3, collaboration centered on post-assessment outcomes and future planning. After reviewing growth data and discussing students who demonstrated different levels of progress, I guided the group to identify next steps by asking what actions would best support students who still needed reinforcement while also responding to students who were ready to move forward (3:34–3:41). Teachers agreed that small group support should continue for students who required additional practice with addition and subtraction, while most students were ready to begin transitioning into multiplication and division concepts (4:27–4:44). </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"327\"><p>In addition, teachers identified the need to refine future pre- and post-assessments so they more precisely aligned to the targeted standards and better captured student understanding (4:15–4:25). I summarized these decisions to confirm shared understanding and collective responsibility before closing the discussion. Across all meetings, next steps were co-constructed through dialogue, grounded in evidence, and aligned to the shared goal of improving student access to grade level math instruction.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response gives concrete directions: continue support for regrouping, move ready students toward multiplication and division, refine assessment design, and adjust supports. These are more useful than an undifferentiated promise to continue meeting.</p><p>The account needs a clearer thread from one meeting's agreement to completed action and new evidence at the next. This distinction matters for Level 4, which extends the analysis beyond a single well-described closing discussion.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each next step to a stated finding and the students it is intended to support.</li><li>Record the responsible person, needed support, timeline, and evidence for the next review.</li><li>Trace at least one action from agreement to implementation to the following meeting's interpretation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.6: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The jointly described instructional directions support a provisional 3. For 4, trace how decisions were consistently carried forward within and between meetings.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth","url":"#cycle/c2/J1","html":"<span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tRefer to specific evidence (including agendas, minutes, group work products, implementation data, videos, and/or participant feedback) to analyze how your leadership actions influenced the community of practice’s progress toward its goal. Reflect on initial implementation results and feedback from the group to evaluate your strengths and areas for growth as a facilitator. Make explicit connections between the evidence, your leadership skills, equitable&nbsp;facilitation practices, and your next steps for professional growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Bring the evidence, your leadership actions, initial results, member feedback, strengths, growth needs, equitable facilitation, and professional next steps into one analysis. The question asks what the evidence teaches you about your leadership, not only whether the group completed its work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Leadership actions and evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"333\"><p>[Throughout Leadership Cycle 2, my leadership actions supported collaborative professional learning by intentionally designing structures and resources that guided the Community of Practice from planning to implementation and reflection. My facilitation focused on preparing the conditions for collaboration so that members could engage meaningfully in addressing the identified problem of practice: improving student understanding of addition and subtraction with regrouping through targeted small-group instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"334\"><p>After the initial planning meeting, I facilitated a subsequent planning focused Community of Practice meeting in which the group collaboratively developed a shared pre-assessment and clarified instructional logistics, including a consistent weekly schedule for small group instruction (Work Product 2). By ensuring that assessment tools, timelines, and instructional focus were clarified prior to implementation, members were able to concentrate on analyzing student needs and preparing for instruction rather than logistical barriers. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"335\"><p>While I was not present for the subsequent data analysis meeting, evidence from the meeting minutes shows that the structures established during planning supported continued progress. Teachers collaboratively scored the pre-assessment, identified patterns in student understanding, and formed instructional groups aligned to identified needs (Work Product 3). This demonstrates that the planning structures I facilitated supported distributed leadership and sustained collaboration. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"336\"><p>Evidence from Video Clip 1 shows teachers referencing shared instructional practices during early implementation, indicating coherence across classrooms. Initial impact on student learning was monitored using the collaboratively designed assessment as both a pre- and post-measure (Work Product 4). In Video Clip 3, teachers referenced class level pre- and post-assessment results (Work Product 5) to reflect on student outcomes and engaged in discussion about assessment rigor and instructional alignment. These conversations demonstrate progress toward the group’s goal of monitoring initial impact on student learning and using evidence to refine instructional practice.] </p></div><h4>Results, feedback, strengths, and growth</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"347\"><p>[A key strength in my facilitation during Leadership Cycle 2 was intentional preparation and structure. By anticipating instructional and logistical needs and designing shared tools in advance, I supported meaningful collaboration and sustained progress even when I was not physically present. Evidence from the meeting minutes and video discussions shows that the group was able to analyze data, form instructional groups, and engage in reflective dialogue aligned to the shared instructional goal. Early implementation results further reflected these strengths: post-assessment data showed that 72 out of 85 students (approximately 85%) maintained or improved their performance following implementation. Among students who demonstrated growth, gains of one to two points on an eight-point assessment represent approximately a 12.5% to 25% improvement. Early implementation results, including the group’s ability to collaboratively analyze data and adjust instruction, reflected strengths in my leadership related to clarity of purpose, advance planning, and the use of shared structures to support collective decision making. This indicates that the foundational planning supported distributed leadership and continuity of practice. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"348\"><p>Another strength was creating a safe space for reflection and professional dialogue. Video evidence shows that teachers were willing to engage honestly in discussion about instructional challenges, including assessment alignment. In Video Clip 3, the group reflected on a postassessment that exceeded the level of rigor of the instruction, using this moment as an opportunity to analyze alignment rather than assign blame. Teachers’ willingness to engage openly in this conversation served as feedback on my facilitation, indicating that the structure and tone of the meetings supported trust, shared ownership, and professional risk taking. When asked about my leadership and facilitation skills, the group provided positive feedback, noting that the meetings felt well structured, collaborative, and supportive of honest instructional reflection, and several team members expressed interest in having me facilitate future meetings. This feedback affirmed that my facilitation structures supported productive adult learning, and it also pushes me to keep strengthening my practice by improving real time synthesis and making consensus points even clearer for the team. This reinforced for me that effective leadership involves creating conditions where misalignment can be examined openly and used as a learning opportunity. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"349\"><p>An area for growth I identified was synthesizing multiple teacher contributions in real time. During discussions, I sometimes found it challenging to hold all of the details shared by each teacher and immediately name patterns across responses. Reviewing the video recordings alongside the meeting minutes helped me recognize moments when slowing the conversation, paraphrasing, or using visible note taking structures could have supported more accurate synthesis and collective sense making. This feedback highlights a growth area in my leadership related to pacing and strategic summarization, which I plan to strengthen by intentionally pausing discussions and using shared visual tools during meetings. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"350\"><p>I also recognized that equitable facilitation requires purposeful listening. Video review helped me see that ensuring all contributions are heard, accurately represented, and synthesized is critical for honoring diverse perspectives and preventing dominant voices from shaping conclusions prematurely. This reinforced listening as an equity focused leadership practice rather than simply a facilitation skill. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"351\"><p>Finally, reviewing the meeting minutes reinforced the importance of documentation as a leadership tool. The minutes not only captured decisions but also supported continuity of the work across meetings. Moving forward, I would be more intentional about using shared documentation during meetings to capture emerging patterns and next steps in real time, supporting clarity and alignment for all members of the community of practice.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The combined passages connect planning tools, teacher ownership, reported feedback, and the candidate's difficulty synthesizing multiple contributions. They identify a real developmental need rather than presenting an entirely successful self-portrait.</p><p>The claim that 72 of 85 students maintained or improved is not an 85% improvement rate. On an eight-point assessment, a one- or two-point gain is 12.5 or 25 percentage points of the maximum score, not necessarily a 12.5% or 25% relative improvement. Assessment alignment concerns further limit attribution to the strategy.</p><p>Positive feedback about structure is relevant, but it should be distinguished from the candidate's interpretation of teachers' willingness to speak. Analyze whose contributions shaped decisions and how the proposed synthesis routines would address a specific inequity in participation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate improved, unchanged, and lower scores; state the score scale and correct the units for gains.</li><li>Use a specific member comment and a meeting event to explain a strength and a growth need.</li><li>Connect the proposed changes in listening, synthesis, and documentation to equitable participation and a way to evaluate your growth.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The connected analysis of results, feedback, and growth supports 3. Correcting the outcome claims and examining equitable influence across the inquiry would strengthen the deeper analysis needed for 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity","url":"#cycle/c2/J2","html":"<span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tRefer to specific evidence (including the agendas and minutes, group work products and initial implementation results, videos and commentary, and/or group feedback on the process) of how effectively you maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity (e.g., respect for diverse viewpoints) as you worked with members of the community of practice.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze how you acted professionally and equitably throughout the inquiry. Use decisions or difficult moments to show how you treated disagreement, represented evidence accurately, and shared influence. Listing norms is not the same as demonstrating their use.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"338\"><p>[I maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity by intentionally modeling respectful, data centered facilitation and supporting collaborative decision making grounded in student needs rather than assumptions. After the initial planning meeting, I facilitated a subsequent planning focused Community of Practice meeting in which I structured the agenda and discussion to ensure that all members had opportunities to contribute to assessment design and instructional planning (Work Product 2). Decisions during this meeting were made collaboratively and anchored in shared student data, reinforcing a professional learning environment grounded in evidence rather than personal opinion. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"339\"><p>Equity was further supported through the establishment of shared norms for professional dialogue, including being open to different perspectives, maintaining focused and respectful discussion, and grounding instructional decisions in shared student data, as outlined in the planning meeting agenda and reinforced across subsequent meeting agendas. These norms supported inclusive participation and helped ensure that diverse instructional perspectives were heard and considered while maintaining alignment to the group’s shared instructional goal. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"340\"><p>During subsequent implementation discussions, I continued to demonstrate integrity by facilitating reflective dialogue that honored professional expertise and encouraged honest examination of practice. In Video Clip 3, when a post-assessment was identified as exceeding the level of rigor of the instruction (Work Product 6), I guided the conversation toward analyzing instructional alignment and identifying next steps rather than assigning blame. This facilitation move supported an environment of trust and professionalism in which challenges could be discussed openly as opportunities for learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response cites shared planning, norms, and a productive response to a mismatch between instruction and assessment. Treating that mismatch as a learning opportunity rather than blaming a teacher is a useful example.</p><p>The analysis remains broad about whose viewpoints mattered and whether participation was consistently equitable. Integrity also requires limiting outcome claims when the assessment evidence is uncertain, not only maintaining a respectful tone.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Analyze a specific difficult exchange or disagreement and its effect on participation.</li><li>Show how members' contributions changed a decision across more than one stage of the cycle.</li><li>Explain how assessment limitations changed what you were willing to claim about learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The non-blaming discussion of assessment alignment supports 3. A 4 needs a deeper, sustained analysis of professionalism, integrity, and equitable influence across the process.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 3 / Responding to school context","url":"#cycle/c2/J3","html":"<span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Responding to school context</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of a community of practice at your school site/educational setting? What actions did you take to respond to these contextually based challenges or supports?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which features of school culture or scheduling supported or constrained the work, what you did in response, and what resulted. Distinguish the group's independent work from meetings you personally facilitated.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"342\"><p>[Several aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of the Community of Practice during Leadership Cycle 2. One key contextual factor was limited instructional time, which required careful planning and intentional use of existing structures. This influenced the decision to establish a consistent weekly time for small group instruction that aligned with the school schedule and instructional priorities, allowing implementation to occur without disrupting core instruction. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"343\"><p>Another important contextual factor was the structure of grade level collaboration time at the site. Grade level teams have built in bi-monthly collaboration time while students attend physical education. Because I am not a member of this grade level, I was unable to attend the second PLC meeting, which occurred during this scheduled time. Rather than discouraging the team from meeting without me, I intentionally supported their decision to use this time to continue the work. Teachers were motivated and eager to move forward with scoring the pre-assessment and forming instructional groups, and I viewed this initiative as a positive indicator of ownership within the community of practice. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"344\"><p>This decision was supported by a broader school culture that values collaboration and distributed leadership. Although I was not present for one PLC meeting, teachers were able to continue data analysis and instructional planning independently. I responded to this context by ensuring that planning structures established earlier were clear and robust, and by supporting alignment and reflection during subsequent implementation discussions rather than attempting to reassert control over the process. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"345\"><p>Variation in teachers’ experience with regrouping instruction also influenced facilitation. I anticipated this challenge by centering discussions on shared student data and instructional evidence, which helped maintain focus on instructional problem solving rather than deficit based explanations. This approach supported equitable dialogue and reinforced a shared responsibility for addressing student needs across classrooms.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response honestly acknowledges that the candidate could not attend a grade-level data meeting and describes teachers continuing the work. This can show ownership and distributed responsibility.</p><p>It should not imply that an absent candidate demonstrated facilitation at that meeting. Clarify whether this was an additional planning/data session or one of the required implementation meetings, and distinguish records reviewed afterward from firsthand observations.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide a dated chronology separating additional planning sessions from the required implementation meetings.</li><li>Identify which meetings you attended and what you learned from records of others.</li><li>Explain how support before and after the independent session affected the work without claiming actions you did not perform.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The contextual reflection and acknowledgement of absence contribute to 3. Clarifying the meeting chronology and the limits of firsthand evidence is important to a stronger analysis of integrity.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 4 / Professional learning goals","url":"#cycle/c2/J4","html":"<span id=\"j-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 4 / Professional learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 2, identify specific professional learning goals and describe your next steps for growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Set specific professional learning goals derived from the cycle's evidence. Explain what you will practice or study, where you will apply it, what feedback you will seek, and how you will judge progress.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"353\"><p>[Based on my analysis of Leadership Cycle 2, my professional learning goals directly build on the strengths and areas for growth identified through meeting minutes and video reflection. One goal is to strengthen my ability to support collective sense making by improving how I synthesize teacher input in real time. While intentional preparation and structure supported productive collaboration, I recognize the need to more consistently slow discussions, paraphrase contributions, and visibly document key ideas during meetings. As a next step, I plan to incorporate structured note taking tools and intentional pauses during facilitation to support clearer pattern identification and more equitable representation of all voices. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"354\"><p>A second professional learning goal is to further develop shared leadership within the community of practice. While foundational planning supported continuity of practice, I want to more intentionally release responsibility to members by creating opportunities for teachers to lead portions of meetings, facilitate data discussions, or summarize group thinking. Moving forward, I plan to gather input from members about their interest in leadership roles and design agendas that distribute facilitation responsibilities. This approach builds on the distributed leadership that emerged during Cycle 2 and supports deeper engagement and ownership of the work. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"355\"><p>Additionally, I will strengthen my facilitation by using shared documentation during meetings to capture key insights and next steps in real time and by embedding brief opportunities for participant feedback to inform facilitation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Real-time synthesis, shared documentation, and distributed facilitation follow from the reflection. The response moves beyond generic goals such as becoming a better leader.</p><p>The plan still needs a timeframe and evidence of change. Simply adding a tool or assigning a member a role does not establish that understanding or equitable participation improved.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Turn each goal into a specific practice to try in an identified future meeting or inquiry cycle.</li><li>Specify the feedback and meeting evidence that will show whether the practice helped.</li><li>Set a review point to examine progress and revise the professional learning plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 2.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The professional goals follow logically from the identified growth needs and contribute to 3. More operational goals are useful, but Level 4 also requires the deeper analysis of equitable leadership across the inquiry.</p></section></section>"}],"anchors":["top","preparation","foundation","A1","a-q1","part-a","A2","a-q2","A3","a-q3","A4","a-q4","A5","a-q5","A6","a-q6","A7","a-q7","B","b-q6","b-q5","b-q4","b-q3","b-q2","b-q1","part-b","C","c-q6","c-q5","c-q4","c-q3","c-q2","c-q1","part-c","D1","d-q1","part-d","D2","d-q2","D3","d-q3","D4","d-q4","D5","d-q5","D6","d-q6","D7","d-q7","D8","d-q8","E1","e-q1","part-e","E2","e-q2","E3","e-q3","F1","f-q1","part-f","F2","f-q2","F3","f-q3","G","g-q1","part-g","H","h-q1","part-h","I1","i-q1","part-i","I2","i-q2","I3","i-q3","I4","i-q4","I5","i-q5","J1","j-q1","part-j","J2","j-q2","J3","j-q3","J4","j-q4","alignment","limits","source-record","scores","final-check","rubric-map","rubric-2-1","rubric-2-2","rubric-2-3","rubric-2-4","rubric-2-5","rubric-2-6","rubric-2-7"],"aliases":{"part-a":"A1","a-q1":"A1","a-q2":"A2","a-q3":"A3","a-q4":"A4","a-q5":"A5","a-q6":"A6","a-q7":"A7","part-b":"B","b-q1":"B","b-q2":"B","b-q3":"B","b-q4":"B","b-q5":"B","b-q6":"B","part-c":"C","c-q1":"C","c-q2":"C","c-q3":"C","c-q4":"C","c-q5":"C","c-q6":"C","part-d":"D1","d-q1":"D1","d-q2":"D2","d-q3":"D3","d-q4":"D4","d-q5":"D5","d-q6":"D6","d-q7":"D7","d-q8":"D8","part-e":"E1","e-q1":"E1","e-q2":"E2","e-q3":"E3","part-f":"F1","f-q1":"F1","f-q2":"F2","f-q3":"F3","part-g":"G","g-q1":"G","part-h":"H","h-q1":"H","part-i":"I1","i-q1":"I1","i-q2":"I2","i-q3":"I3","i-q4":"I4","i-q5":"I5","part-j":"J1","j-q1":"J1","j-q2":"J2","j-q3":"J3","j-q4":"J4","foundation":"preparation","rubric-map":"scores","final-check":"scores"}},"c3":{"title":"Cycle 3: Understanding the Questions","html":"<div class=\"revised-cycle\"><header class=\"intro guide-heading\" id=\"top\"><h1>Cycle 3: Understanding the Questions</h1><p class=\"lead\">Cycle 3: Support teacher growth through coaching</p><p>Current questions, plain-language explanations, rubric components, and a critique of the CTC example. Requirements come from the supplied Version 09 templates and handbook.</p></header><details class=\"contents-index\"><summary>Questions and reference</summary><nav aria-label=\"Cycle 3 questions\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/top\" aria-current=\"location\">Introduction</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/preparation\">Before you begin</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A1\">I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A2\">I.2 / Explaining processes and documents</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A3\">I.3 / Teacher input and ownership</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A4\">I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A5\">I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A6\">II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A7\">II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A8\">II.3 / Anticipating challenges</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/B\">1 / Classroom context and learning goals</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/C\">Teacher lesson plan and student-work description</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/D\">Pre-observation meeting video clips</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E1\">1 / Teacher self-assessment</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E2\">2 / Reviewing the lesson together</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E3\">3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E4\">4 / Agreeing on observation evidence</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/F\">CSTP-focused observation notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/G\">Student work from the observed lesson</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/H\">Post-observation meeting video clips</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/I1\">1 / Jointly assessing student learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/I2\">2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/I3\">3 / Learning from teacher feedback</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/J1\">1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/J2\">2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/J3\">3 / Equity and continuous improvement</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/alignment\">Template and handbook</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/limits\">Submission requirements</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/source-record\">Source notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/scores\">Score profile</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</a></nav></details><section id=\"preparation\" class=\"preparation major-section\"><span id=\"foundation\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Before you begin</h2><p>Read the whole cycle before meeting the volunteer teacher: investigate school practices and teacher experience; jointly plan a CSTP-focused observation; observe instruction and collect student work; jointly examine the evidence and next steps; reflect on your coaching.</p><p>Plan to observe the lesson live, collecting evidence while instruction occurs, and then use the recording for joint review. The handbook calls for an observation of at least 20 minutes and recording its full duration. Plan permissions, the two pre-observation clips, post-observation clips, and student-work collection beforehand. Coaching decisions should remain jointly owned by you and the teacher.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Scores are instructional estimates of the complete rubric, not separate scores for individual questions. Video-dependent estimates are provisional; recordings were not reviewed. The example is evidence to examine, not a model to copy.</p></section><article id=\"A1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tOutline how often during the year teachers are coached, observed, or provided instructional feedback and what the intended outcomes are for the school, teachers, and students. After reviewing current practices at this school, provide an analysis of the effectiveness of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain frequency, procedures, and intended outcomes for the school, teachers, and students, then evaluate effectiveness using available evidence. Distinguish evaluation, walkthroughs, and voluntary instructional coaching; they may serve different purposes and create different relationships.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"4\"><p>[Certificated teachers are required to participate in a formal yearly evaluation process that is conducted with the site administrator which involves an initial pre-observation conference, two observations, and post-observation conferences. During the initial conference, the teacher consults with the site administrator and chooses one or two California Standards for the Teaching Profession (CSTP) elements that they would like to focus on in their teaching practice, using district-provided materials that detail teaching standards. The selected CSTPs are also a focusing tool that administrators use during the two observations that are conducted during the school year, so that they can give specific instructional feedback. The formal observations are conducted during the fall semester and during the spring semester, and the post-observation conferences are conducted within 5 days of the observations. During the post-observation conferences, teachers receive instructional feedback about their employment of teaching practices during the observations, with a focus on the CSTPs that were selected in the beginning of the evaluation process. The intended outcomes of this formal evaluation process are: (1) to ensure that the school maintains a high-quality certificated teaching staff that are equipped to advance student achievement, and (2) to inform teachers of their teaching practices and facilitate their ongoing growth as educators. There are also informal observations, or walkthroughs, that are conducted a few times a year with site administration, and there does not appear to be any pre- or post-observation processes associated with these types of walkthroughs. However, the existing evaluation coaching cycles and informal observation practices have not appeared to make an impact on student achievement as measured by the 3rd, 4th, and 5th grade SBAC ELA and Math data from the last few years. For example, the ELA proficiency for grades 3-5 has steadily declined; from 2023 to 2025, the percentage of students who met proficiency declined from 34% to 18%. The Math proficiency for grades 3-5 declined from 2023 to 2025, from 24% of 3rd, 4th, and 5th graders meeting proficiency to 17% of 3rd, 4th, and 5th graders meeting proficiency. Furthermore, this is the first year that instructional coaching is launching at the district, and this elementary school is one of four school sites that has received a full-time instructional coach for this current school year. For coaching cycles to be successful, the instructional coach and the teacher must develop a trusting and collaborative working relationship. Teachers can self-select into coaching cycles that consist of collaborative meetings in which the teacher and the coach establish a collaborative focus based on student strengths and needs. The cycle begins with the teacher and coach jointly setting goals with the CSTPs as a guide, co-planning for instruction with resources, and jointly selecting what types of student data to collect for later analysis. The teacher and instructional coach also decide whether the lesson will be taught solely by the teacher and the instructional coach will observe, or if the instructional coach will model and the teacher observes, or if they will co-teach the lesson together and note their observations during the post-observation meeting. After the lesson and observation, the teacher and instructional coach will meet to analyze student data and observation data, reflect and adjust, celebrate impact, and plan next steps. Given that the instructional coaching role and its processes are early in implementation at this school site, a data analysis later next year with SBAC ELA and Math student achievement data and various sources of additional quantitative and qualitative data will be necessary to use when measuring the effectiveness of the instructional coaching implementation. The formal evaluation process with the site administrator and the instructional coaching cycle with the instructional coach share practices in the pre-observation, observation, and post-observation activities that address principles of adult learning theory. Aguilar (2024) noted several principles of adult learning theory, which emphasize the importance of trusting relationships, building upon adult’s previous learning experiences and their agency in learning, and creating opportunities for adults to apply their knowledge and skills in teaching so that they can receive relevant feedback. During preobservation meetings, teachers exercise their agency in learning as they select the CSTPs and the content area that they would like to practice and receive feedback on and apply their knowledge and skills to design lessons that incorporate strategies that address their students’ specific strengths and needs. The observations are guided by the goals and observational data that are collaboratively agreed upon by the teacher and the observer. In the post-observation meetings with the site administrator, teachers receive timely feedback on their instructional practice which creates opportunities to sustain new learning and transfer it into their daily practice. In the post-observation meetings with the instructional coach, teachers engage in discussion that centers around student needs and frames the “why” of learning in relation to student needs and problem-solving, which attends to adult learning principles that acknowledge how adults have a problem-centered orientation to learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response gives concrete timing for formal observations and feedback, describes a newly introduced coaching role, and distinguishes teacher choice in coaching from formal evaluation. This is a substantial account of local practices.</p><p>Declining schoolwide proficiency does not establish that observation or evaluation caused the decline or had no benefit. Those rates may involve changing cohorts and many influences. The new coaching model also cannot be judged by results collected before its implementation. Teacher participation, changes in practice, and student work would provide more direct evidence.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate established evaluation practices from the new coaching model and identify their timelines.</li><li>Describe what the achievement trend establishes and what it cannot establish about coaching effectiveness.</li><li>Use available teacher feedback, observation records, and changes in instruction to evaluate the process more directly.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The detailed account of practices, goals, and limitations supports 3. A stronger explanation of how teacher-specific practices build trust is needed before the adult-learning discussion can support a higher level.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Explaining processes and documents</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the processes, forms, and/or documents that are used to guide pre-observation meetings, collect observation data, and focus post-observation meetings (e.g., teacher response forms, observation tools, student work products, next steps for professional growth). If the school does not conduct teacher observations and uses a different approach to provide instructional feedback to teachers, provide a detailed description of the process(es) and forms used.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how documents are used before, during, and after observation. Identify what each tool records, how it connects to the CSTP focus, and how teachers use the resulting evidence. Naming a form is not a description of the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"6\"><p>[During the pre-observation meeting for the formal yearly evaluation process, the teacher and the administrator complete a district-provided digital form that includes the selected CSTP element/s, the procedures of the observation, and the context of the observation. The site administrator uses the district-provided digital form to collect and record observation data, which is also included in the post-observation conference to discuss the observation and provide instructional feedback.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies a district digital form that follows the observation cycle and includes the selected CSTP elements and context. That gives the process a coherent structure.</p><p>It says little about the form's evidence fields, teacher self-assessment, student work, or the way post-observation decisions are documented. It also does not clearly explain whether informal coaching uses the same tools as formal evaluation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe the relevant fields and who completes or reviews them at each stage.</li><li>Explain how observational evidence is separated from interpretation or evaluation.</li><li>Show how the tool connects CSTP evidence, student learning, feedback, and next professional steps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> This brief account supports only part of the combined 3. Describe how the documents actually focus evidence and learning; naming the digital form is not enough to strengthen the analysis.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.3 / Teacher input and ownership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tExplain the level of input teachers have on the focus or purpose of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which decisions teachers influence, how they exercise that choice, and any limits on it. Distinguish selecting from options offered by an administrator from genuinely shaping the purpose, evidence, and goals of coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"8\"><p>[During the formal evaluation process, teachers select one or two California Standards for the </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"9\"><p>Teaching Profession (CSTP) elements that they would like to focus on so that they can receive focused instructional feedback after the observations. After selecting the CSTPs, teachers choose a content area to design and implement a lesson in and select the student work products that will be collected during the observation. For coaching cycles with the instructional coach, teachers collaborate with the coach and jointly set the goals for the cycle, co-plan instruction, and select the focus of the instructional feedback with the CSTPs as a tool to guide this selection.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies meaningful choices: CSTP elements, content area, student products, and jointly established goals. These details help explain how teachers participate.</p><p>For the Level 4 extension, explain how these opportunities respond to teachers' different needs and build trust with administrators. Describing a choice mechanism is not the same as showing its effect on the professional relationship.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a concrete example of teacher input changing the observation or feedback focus.</li><li>Explain how confidentiality, role clarity, and teacher choice operate in the local process.</li><li>Connect these practices to trust and teacher-specific needs using available evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> Teacher choice is clear, but its relationship to unique needs and trust remains underdeveloped. That is the main distinction between the combined Level 3 estimate and a confident 4.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tExplain the implications for this cycle based on the analysis of the current role of coaching practices (at the school) and the background and experience of the volunteer teacher.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you will retain, adapt, or clarify because of the school's practices and this teacher's experience. The task asks for implications, not merely a statement that the teacher already knows the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"11\"><p>[This coaching cycle shares similar pre-observation, observation, and post-observation components to previous coaching cycles that the volunteer teacher has participated in. Since the volunteer teacher also has experience with identifying CSTP elements that will guide the focus of the observations and instructional feedback processes, they will continue to engage with the selection and application of these elements during this coaching cycle.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response recognizes that the volunteer has experience with the three-stage process and CSTP selection. That is relevant background.</p><p>It does not explain what this familiarity changes in the candidate's approach. A teacher who knows the formal process may still need assurance about a non-evaluative partnership or more individualized support with student-work analysis.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which familiar routines can be used and which features of this cycle need clarification.</li><li>Connect a known teacher experience or need to a specific coaching decision.</li><li>Explain how you will check that the teacher understands and agrees to the purpose and evidence collection.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> This response is thin on implications; the fuller plan in II.2 helps support the combined 3. Explain what will change because of this teacher's history and context.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tHow do the CSTPs guide or play a role in teacher growth and development at the school? (If the school uses another set of standards, provide a crosswalk to the CSTPs for the element[s] you have identified.)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the standards guide ongoing growth: identifying a need, focusing evidence, interpreting practice, and deciding next steps. If a different local framework is used, provide the required crosswalk for the selected elements.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"13\"><p>[The CSTPs play a role in teacher growth and development because teachers select CSTP elements during the formal yearly evaluation process with the site administrator and during informal coaching cycles with the instructional coach.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The answer correctly states that CSTP elements are selected for evaluation and coaching. It is too brief to show how they guide development beyond that selection.</p><p>A standard becomes useful when it helps a teacher and coach decide what to notice and how to interpret it. The later focus on routines and access to mathematical learning can illustrate that function, but should not be substituted for an account of the school's actual practice.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain how the local process uses an element to focus observation evidence and feedback.</li><li>Describe how the same focus informs a professional learning decision.</li><li>Provide a crosswalk only if a different framework is actually used; do not invent one.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> This answer identifies CSTP use but gives little analysis. The combined 3 relies on the more detailed procedures and coaching plan elsewhere in Part A.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A6\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tProvide information about the volunteer teacher’s:</p><p>a.\tprofessional experience as a teacher (including years of service, school site[s], grades and content taught, languages)</p><p>b.\tcurrent assignment (grade level[s], content area, course titles)</p><p>c.\tpast experiences with coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p><p>d.\tknowledge and use of the CSTP to guide their growth and development as a teacher at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four subparts: professional experience and languages, current assignment, previous coaching experiences, and knowledge and use of the CSTPs. Explain the background accurately enough to support the individualized plan in the next question.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Professional experience</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"17\"><p>[The volunteer teacher has completed 2 years of service as a full-time certificated teacher, and this year will be her 3rd year serving as a classroom teacher at this current TK-5 elementary school. Her first year of certificated teaching was in another district in the XXX area, where she taught a 4th and 5th grade combination class at an elementary school. Her second year of certificated teaching was completed at the current elementary school that she teaches at, and she taught 5th grade last year. The volunteer teacher is fluent in the English language, and she also has speaking and listening fluency in the Cantonese language.] </p></div><h4>Current assignment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"19\"><p>[The volunteer teacher is currently teaching a 5th grade multiple subject class.] </p></div><h4>Previous coaching</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"21\"><p>[The volunteer teacher has had previous experiences with coaching and instructional feedback in previous teaching positions. During her student teaching, she participated in a mentoring group with 3 other student teachers and a mentoring experienced educator, where they met weekly to plan lessons and conduct pre-observation planning. The student teachers in the group took turns observing each other with the mentor and conducted post-observation meetings to debrief and receive instructional feedback. The volunteer teacher also participated in two years of the beginning teacher induction program; the first year of induction was completed in X and the second year of induction was completed in this current school district. The volunteer teacher participated in a district science coaching program with her grade level team; the coaching experience consisted of pre-observation and lesson planning meetings, observations, and postobservation reflections.] </p></div><h4>Knowledge and use of the CSTPs</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"23\"><p>[The volunteer teacher has applied the CSTPs to guide her growth and development during her student teaching experiences and during the first two years that she taught as a certificated teacher, both at the previous elementary school site in X and in the current school site she is teaching at. The volunteer teacher will also continue to apply the CSTPs to guide her growth and professional development as a teacher this year during the formal yearly evaluation process with the site administrator.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response describes the teacher's early career, fifth-grade assignment, Cantonese speaking and listening fluency, induction, peer observation, and science coaching. These details provide a useful foundation.</p><p>The chronology is slightly confusing: being in a third year of teaching is not necessarily a third year at the same site when the first year was elsewhere. Experience with a coaching program also does not tell the reader which aspects the teacher found useful or difficult.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Clarify total teaching experience separately from years at the current school.</li><li>Include the teacher's account of what prior coaching helped with and what remained a need.</li><li>Use the background to inform the next answer rather than assuming experience eliminates the need for orientation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The teacher background supports the individualized-plan requirement within the overall 3. Clarify the chronology and show how reported experiences change the plan.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A7\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how the information you gathered about the school context and the volunteer teacher’s experiences informs your plans for conducting a coaching cycle, including how you will:</p><p>a.\torient the volunteer teacher to participate in your coaching process</p><p>b.\tparticipate in a pre-observation meeting</p><p>c.\tprepare for the observation and collection of evidence (including student work)</p><p>d.\tjointly conduct (with the volunteer teacher) the post-observation meeting</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the school and teacher information shapes four connected steps: orientation, pre-observation meeting, observation and evidence collection, and the joint post-observation meeting. State the purpose of each step and the decisions you will make together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Orientation</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"26\"><p>[I will give an overview of the coaching process during our initial conversation about her participation in this coaching cycle, where we will co-determine a date and time for our preobservation meeting. During this initial meeting, I will review the three major components and activities of the coaching cycle: (1) Pre-observation meeting; (2) Observation; and (3) Postobservation. Since my volunteer teacher has had a few years of experience with coaching cycles and has participated in past coaching processes, I will ask her facilitative and clarifying questions to connect her past experiences with coaching to the upcoming coaching cycle that we will participate in together. I will also make sure that the volunteer teacher understands the time commitments and video logistics associated with each component; for example, I will make sure that the volunteer teacher understands and agrees to the length of the pre-observation meeting (which will be about 30 minutes) and to the videos that we will record of the preobservation meeting.] </p></div><h4>Pre-observation meeting</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"28\"><p>[During the pre-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher and I will meet during a time that works best for us to discuss the instructional components to the lesson that I will be observing and to identify students’ assets and needs. Moreover, we will jointly select one or two CSTP elements and student work products that will be the focus for the data collected during the observation and this data will be shared during the post-observation meeting. I will use facilitative and clarifying prompts during our conversation to guide us towards these topics. To summarize our conversation, I will review the CSTP elements and student work products that we jointly selected to gather evidence during the observation. I will also review the coaching and observation process that involves: (1) obtaining the necessary permissions to video record the lesson observation with the volunteer teacher and her students, (2) confirming the length of the lesson observation, (3) confirming a date and time to meet for the post-observation conference and the details on how to prepare for the conference in which I will share a secure link in a shared digital folder for the volunteer teacher to access the lesson observation video and view it before our conference, and (4) detailing the components of the post-observation conference, which will include the volunteer teacher and I watching the video of the lesson observation together and having a conversation about the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment of their instruction and assessment implementation during the lesson, effectiveness of their teaching practices during the lesson, jointly determining next steps, resources, and professional development for the volunteer teacher to engage in. I will also obtain feedback from the volunteer teacher about what was helpful or not helpful during the coaching process to help me determine my own strengths and areas of growth.] </p></div><h4>Observation and evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"30\"><p>[To prepare for the observation and collection of evidence during the observation, the volunteer teacher and I will confirm the date, time, and duration of the observation. We will review the observation processes, which include conducting and video recording the classroom observation of the volunteer teacher’s lesson with her 5th grade students. We will ensure that all the necessary protocols and permissions are in place for those who will be in the video recording. I will develop an observation tool to collect data during the lesson.] </p></div><h4>Post-observation meeting</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"32\"><p>[During the post-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher and I will watch the video recording of the lesson and engage in a thoughtful and productive conversation to jointly identify teacher strengths and needs based on CSTP-specific observation evidence, the video, and student work. During the video, we will analyze the effectiveness of the teaching practices employed by the volunteer teacher during the lesson. We will also jointly analyze student work and other data related to the lesson to co-determine next steps for the volunteer teacher’s professional growth. I will use facilitative, clarifying, and reflective questions to guide the collaborative conversation and to jointly identify instructional strengths, areas of growth, resources, and additional coaching support based on the CSTP-related observation data and feedback, student work, and lesson video. Furthermore, I will ask the volunteer teacher to share feedback on my coaching skills by asking her what was helpful or not helpful during the coaching and observation cycle; this feedback will help me reflect on my own strengths and areas for growth regarding my coaching.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The plan is detailed about logistics, permissions, CSTP selection, video access, student work, questioning, and teacher feedback. It keeps the post-observation discussion collaborative.</p><p>Much of the detail would fit any teacher. Strengthen the link to this teacher's experience and anticipated needs, and specify how the observation tool will capture learning rather than only visible behavior. Plan to observe instruction as it occurs, collect focused notes, and record the observation for later joint review; those are distinct activities in the handbook's sequence.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each major coaching choice to the teacher's background or a stated need.</li><li>Jointly define what evidence will address both the CSTP focus and lesson learning goals, including student work.</li><li>Confirm live observation, duration, recording permissions, secure handling, and shared review of the evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The four-stage plan is detailed enough to support 3. Stronger teacher-specific connections and an explanation of trust-building would help establish the Level 4 extension.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A8\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Anticipating challenges</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any challenges you may face in conducting a coaching cycle with the volunteer teacher and provide strategies for addressing these challenges.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible challenges for this teacher and context, explain why each may occur, and give a practical response. Tie the strategy to the challenge rather than listing generic good coaching practices.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"34\"><p>[During the pre-observation part of the coaching cycle, I may encounter challenges with establishing clear, specific goals for the observation. To address this challenge, I will use plan facilitative and clarifying questions to focus our collaborative conversation on identifying: (1) specific, measurable learning goals, strengths and assets of students, and evidence/student work samples (2) CSTPs that will guide the collection of observational data and student work products. During the observation, I may face challenges with collecting specific observational data related to the CSTPs and student work samples. To address this challenge, I will prepare an observational tool that helps me collect evidence that is related and focused on the one or two CSTP elements that the volunteer teacher and I agreed upon. In the post-observation meeting, it may be challenging to foster a learning conversation that includes multiple pieces of evidence. To mitigate this challenge, I will plan facilitative questions that helps me foster a collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher, and I will also prepare notes that cite CSTP-specific observation evidence, the observation video, and student work that highlights teacher strengths and areas for growth.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response anticipates vague goals, unfocused evidence collection, and difficulty discussing multiple sources. Preparing questions and a focused observation tool are sensible responses.</p><p>The plan needs a clearer link to the actual teacher and lesson. It should also explain what the coach will do if evidence complicates the selected focus or if the teacher interprets the same evidence differently.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Ground each anticipated challenge in the school context or teacher's reported experience.</li><li>Specify an observable goal and the evidence fields that will keep the observation focused.</li><li>Plan how to invite disagreement and revise interpretations jointly without taking ownership away from the teacher.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The planned responses to focused challenges support the combined 3. Their value would be clearer if linked to this teacher's actual needs rather than a general coaching process.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\"><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / 1 / Classroom context and learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tClassroom context, including:</p><p>a.\ttopic of lesson, learning and instructional goals, students’ prior knowledge, and what was taught in the prior lesson</p><p>b.\tclassroom setting (face-to-face, online, or hybrid) and organization for learning (whole class, small groups, individual practice, hybrid)</p><p>c.\tdescription of students served, including their assets and learning needs (e.g., grade level, demographics, student assets [cultural and/or linguistic] and interests, learning supports and adaptations)</p><p>d.\tmaterials and/or resources used during the lesson by the teacher or students to enhance or extend learning</p><p>e.\tother relevant classroom context information (e.g., time of day for the lesson, time of year, support staff that are in the room during the lesson, materials used, safety issues, bell schedule, parent support)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all five components: the lesson and prior learning; the setting and organization; students' assets and learning needs; materials; and other relevant context. Explain information that affects instruction and observation, not just demographic totals.</p><p>Distinguish class enrollment from attendance in the observed lesson and distinguish student engagement from achievement of the mathematical goal.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>What each form of evidence contributes</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it establishes</th><th scope=\"col\">Its limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Lesson plan</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Intended learning, instruction, supports, and student work.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">It does not establish what happened during instruction.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and lesson recording</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Specific teaching actions and student responses.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Events require interpretation; participation alone does not establish learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">What particular learners demonstrated on the task.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">A few examples do not establish a whole-class pattern or the cause of a result.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Meeting clips and commentary</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">The coaching interaction and an explanation of leadership decisions.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Commentary cannot establish an interaction absent from the submitted evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Lesson and prior learning</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"43\"><p>[Lesson 11: Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1, is a lesson in the 5th grade Math Unit 2: Fractions as Quotients and Fraction Multiplication from the district-adopted math curriculum. The purpose of the lesson is for students to find the area of a rectangle when one of the side lengths is a fraction greater than 1. The lesson’s instructional goals are: (1) Students describe (orally and in writing) how different equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a given rectangle with fractional sides greater than 1 and (2) Explain (orally) strategies used to determine the area of a rectangle with fractional side lengths and represent the area with a multiplication expression. Before this lesson, students practiced with visual models to multiply fractions by whole numbers and found the area of rectangles when one of the side lengths was a fraction less than 1. They also had practice using these visual models to explain (orally and in writing) their reasoning. For example, they used diagrams of rectangles to explain why ⅔ x 4 = (2x4) x ⅓. Using area diagrams, students apply this prior knowledge, reasoning, and strategies to this lesson to find the area of a rectangle with a fractional side greater than 1. These lessons address the following California mathematics standards in the Numbers and Operations </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"44\"><p>Fractions domains.] </p></div><h4>Setting</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"46\"><p>[This whole-group lesson was taught in a self-contained classroom for in-person learning. There were a total of 17 students present in the classroom, and students were sitting at their desks. The desks were arranged in pairs, with students facing the digital display that showed digital contents of the lesson and the items displayed under the document camera.] </p></div><h4>Students and learning needs</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"48\"><p>[The volunteer teacher’s fifth-grade class consists of 22 total students, 13 males and 9 females this year. Regarding student assets, the volunteer teacher described how her students are very friendly and they enjoy participating in class and in school events. 7 students in the classroom have individualized learning plans (IEPs) and receive additional push-in and pull-out support from specialized staff. 14 students are identified as multilingual, or English learners. According to the volunteer teacher, the students enjoy participating in class activities and discussions. All </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"53\"><p>22 students come from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds: 19 Hispanic/Latine students, 1 multiracial student, 1 Pacific Islander student, and 1 Filipino student.] </p></div><h4>Materials</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"55\"><p>[During the lesson, the teacher used a large-screen television (digital display) connected to the computer and to the document camera to present digital learning materials for all parts of the lesson.] </p></div><h4>Other relevant context</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"57\"><p>[The lesson was taught in the morning, beginning at 9:30am and concluding at 10:35am, in the beginning of October during the fall semester. There were 2 paraprofessional staff members in the room who were supporting 3 students with their Individualized Education Plan accommodations and supports. Each student had their own consumable math workbook that accompanied the district-approved math curriculum and had individual access to all material components of the math lesson. There were no safety issues, no major disruptions, and no major changes to the school’s bell schedule that day (it was a minimum day to accommodate parent-teacher conferences).] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response clearly identifies the fractional-area lesson, oral and written goals, prior work with fractions, classroom arrangement, resources, and adult support. It distinguishes 22 enrolled students from 17 present.</p><p>The description of assets is largely that students are friendly and enjoy participation. It needs more specific evidence of the knowledge, languages, strategies, or experiences the lesson can draw on. The overlap among the 14 multilingual or English learner students and seven students with IEPs is not explained; those categories should not be added together.</p><p>Later commentary focuses heavily on routines. The context should help the reader understand how those routines support mathematical explanation for these particular learners, not merely a quiet room.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify specific assets and learning needs relevant to explaining fractional area, using teacher or student evidence.</li><li>Explain the planned language supports, adaptations, and adult roles without inferring needs from demographic categories.</li><li>Maintain the distinction between 22 enrolled, 17 present, and the number of work samples actually analyzed.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> This context supports the provisional 4 when combined with the reported pre-observation dialogue. The narrative alone cannot demonstrate two-way coaching; more specific assets and supports would deepen the conversation.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\"><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Teacher lesson plan and student-work description</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit a copy of the volunteer teacher’s lesson plan, including the student work product description.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 11; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Provide the volunteer teacher's actual plan and a description of the student work that will show learning. Explain how the work relates to the lesson goals. An adopted curriculum plan establishes intended instruction, not proof of what occurred.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"61\"><p>Volunteer Teacher Lesson Plan: Unit 2 Lesson 11-Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1 The volunteer teacher’s lesson plan is from the adopted math curriculum, and the volunteer teacher utilized the attached lesson plan, Unit 2, Lesson 11: Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1, to teach the lesson plan during their lesson observation. The lesson plan is represented in the images on pages 2-9 of this document. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"63\"><p>Description of Student Work Sample: The student work sample is the Cool Down from Unit 2 </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"64\"><p>Lesson 11: Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1, from the adopted 5th grade math curriculum. This particular cool down addresses 5th grade California Common Core standard 5.NF.B.4.b, and is a formative assessment given at the end of a math lesson. The student work sample Find the Area consists of two items: (1) “Write a multiplication expression to represent the area of the shaded region” and (2) “What is the area of the shaded region?” The cool down assessment is also shown on page 9 of this document. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"66\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-66-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"67\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-67-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"68\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-68-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"69\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-69-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"70\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-70-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"71\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-71-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"72\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-72-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"73\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-73-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example includes the adopted fractional-side-length lesson and identifies a two-item cool down asking for a multiplication expression and area. The materials make the intended content visible.</p><p>The oral explanation goal is not fully represented by a written answer alone. The pre-observation discussion should establish what evidence of reasoning will be collected, how the teacher's actual adaptations will be documented, and how the work relates to the chosen CSTP focus.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the actual lesson adaptations agreed with the teacher and distinguish them from the published plan.</li><li>Specify what successful mathematical reasoning would look or sound like.</li><li>Connect the planned work and observation evidence to both the learning goals and the selected CSTP element.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The plan supports the provisional 4 across Parts B-E, but it is not separately scored. The clips must demonstrate how the teacher and coach jointly used it to decide the focus and evidence.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / Pre-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Select and submit 2 video clips, each up to 6 minutes, that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Teacher Needs, Lesson Plan Review, and CSTP Selection (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates: • Your coaching, including asking prompting and clarifying questions, that leads to the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment of instructional needs and challenges based on the lesson plan they intend to teach and the student work product that will be the outcome of the lesson; student assets and needs; and classroom context</p><p>Supporting the teacher to then identify one or two elements of one of the six CSTP domains for which they would like observation data collected to guide the post-observation meeting • Video Clip 2: Observation Process and CSTP Data (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates:  Your review of the coaching and observation process with the teacher • Your review of the CSTP–element-focused data you will collect during the observation to share and discuss in the post-observation meeting, in addition to the student work products and other relevant data shared by the teacher</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 12 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit two clips, up to six minutes each, showing the required pre-observation discussion. The first concerns teacher needs, the lesson, and CSTP selection; the second concerns the observation process and evidence to collect. Joint decisions must be visible in the actual exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><p class=\"scope-note\">The source commentary refers to video clips, but those recordings were not available for this review. No example video or invented transcript is substituted.</p></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The written example supplies a detailed account of questions, replies, and selected evidence, but the recordings were not available for this review. The account alone cannot establish that both clips contain every required component or that the interaction was genuinely two-way.</p><p>Use the commentary critique below to examine reasoning, while reserving judgment about demonstrated practice until the clips can be watched.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify the two clips against the distinct functions and six-minute limits in the current handbook.</li><li>Ensure the teacher's input and the jointly agreed focus and evidence are visible and audible.</li><li>Check every commentary timestamp against the submitted clips and keep the interaction authentic.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: Not independently assessable without the video.</strong> The written commentary is consistent with a provisional 4 elsewhere in this review. The actual dialogue, clip alignment, and joint decisions remain unverified without the recordings.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 1 / Teacher self-assessment</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Self-Assessment: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher determined their instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element(s).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the teacher identified instructional needs and challenges and how your listening and questioning helped connect them to the CSTP focus. Analyze the teacher's thinking and the effect of your coaching, not only the questions you planned to ask.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"79\"><p>[To collaboratively determine the volunteer teacher’s instructional needs and challenges based on a CSTP element, I wanted to begin with facilitative questions that would help the volunteer teacher identify the instructional goals of the lesson. For example, from minute 0:00 to minute 0:42 in video clip 1, I ask facilitative questions such as “What are the main learning goals of the lesson?” and use paraphrasing language such as, “So it sounds like there are speaking and writing domains in your learning goals?”, that aimed to facilitate a 2-way conversation in which the volunteer teacher identified the learning goals. When the volunteer teacher identified how the math learning goals included speaking, listening, and writing instructional goals, I recognized how these various goals would relate to upcoming topics in our collaborative conversation, such as identifying students’ instructional needs and challenges that also related to the CSTP element that would be the focus of our data collection. To get to these topics, I wanted to ask facilitative questions that would encourage the volunteer teacher to identify her students’ assets and needs and also identify aspects of the classroom context. For example, from minute 3:39 to minute 4:20 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher identifies a particular student cultural asset when she says, “They love participating…” and also identifies students’ instructional needs concerning student engagement when she says, “I think that something that needs support in this classroom would be for them to stay engaged with the material because it is quite challenging---fractions is not super easy.” Here, I recognized that supporting student engagement would connect with the CSTP element that we collaboratively identified later in the conversation, as this is related to the pedagogical strategies that a teacher may implement to increase student engagement and support students with their learning when the content of the lesson may be challenging. This means that as a coach, I was able to facilitate the conversation towards co-determining a CSTP element to focus on because I knew that I had to practice active and engaged listening early in the conversation to identify the specific aspects of the learning goals and instructional needs and challenges that would later connect to a collaboration process with the volunteer teacher to create a plan for the CSTP-element-focused observation.  Looking back at this moment in our pre-observation conversation, I see how I was able to make these connections because I used facilitative questions to draw out specific areas of need in regards to learning goals, student assets and needs, and the learning context, and I listened carefully to the volunteer teacher as she shared the math learning goals, students’ assets and needs, and details about the classroom context.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"80\"><p>I also recognized how the classroom context would be related to determining instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element that we identified for focused data collection during the observation, so I used facilitative questions to invite the teacher to share more about the classroom context. For example, from minute 4:21 to minute 4:28 in video clip 1, I ask the volunteer teacher to describe the learning environment and her students’ classroom culture, and at minute 4:30 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher identifies a positive behavioral goals that she is supporting the class with for the school year, such as “raising their hands” and “listening to one person at a time.” By identifying these instructional needs and challenges during this part of the conversation, we established a rationale for the CSTP element that we collaboratively identified later in the meeting. At minute 4:38 in video clip 1, I use facilitative questions to invite the volunteer teacher to choose a CSTP element that she is interested in receiving feedback on. Later in the conversation, I use facilitative questions and paraphrasing language to confirm the CSTP-element-focused data that would be collected during the lesson, such as in minute 5:04 in video clip 1, I ask, “Which element or elements align with areas that you’re interested in getting feedback on during this lesson?” and at minute 5:12 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “Since I am a newer teacher I do want to keep working on classroom management and I think 2.6 here makes sense because I was talking about wanting to support positive behavior, such as making sure that one person is speaking at a time.” Here, the volunteer teacher identified CSTP element 2.6 which related to the previous topics in our pre-observation meeting that identified instructional needs and challenges. Looking back, I see how my coaching decisions to use facilitative questions, prompts, and paraphrasing language invited the volunteer teacher to identify which CSTP element would relate to implementing strategies that supported all of her students with positive behaviors so that they could access the learning goals and outcomes.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage reports questions about goals, student needs, classroom culture, and the teacher's interest in routines. It explains how active listening helped the coach connect those topics to CSTP selection.</p><p>The evidence would be easier to follow with fewer repeated statements of intention and more attention to what the teacher's response changed. There is also an internal clip-reference inconsistency: the assets discussion is attributed to clip 2 here but to clip 1 in the lesson-review passage. The actual recordings must resolve it.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct clip references by checking the recordings, rather than inferring the intended timestamp.</li><li>Identify the teacher's specific self-assessed need and show how it influenced the jointly selected focus.</li><li>Explain the impact of a facilitative question using the teacher's resulting reasoning, not merely agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported questions draw out specific needs and support a provisional 4. Resolve the clip-reference inconsistency and use the actual teacher response to demonstrate impact.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 2 / Reviewing the lesson together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tLesson Plan Review: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher reviewed the lesson plan. Think about:</p><p>a.\tstudent work product,</p><p>b.\tclassroom context, and</p><p>c.\tstudents’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Connect the student work, classroom context, and student assets and needs to the lesson review. Show how your questions helped the teacher clarify what students should learn and how the planned evidence will reveal it.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Student work and success evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"83\"><p>[To determine the student work product or the success criteria that would provide evidence that the students met the learning and instructional goals of the lesson, it was important to first collaboratively identify the main learning goals. At minute 0:00-0:10 in video clip 1, I ask, “What are the main learning goals for your lesson and what do you hope students will be able to do at the end?” At minute 0:10 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responded and detailed two learning goals: “There will be two learning goals. The first one will be describing orally and in writing how different equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a given rectangle with fractional side lengths greater than 1. And the second one is explain orally strategies used to determine the area of a rectangle with fraction side lengths and represent the area with a multiplication expression.” At minute 0:37 in video clip 1, I clarify, “So it sounds like there are speaking and writing domains in your learning goals?” At minute 0:42 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes.” At minute 0:48 in video clip 1, I ask, “How do these particular goals in this lesson (it says lesson 11, right), how do they connect with what students have already learned going into this lesson?” At minute 0:58 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responds, “So in previous lessons, students have multiplied fractions by whole numbers and they have found the area of rectangles with one of the side lengths when the fraction was less than 1.” At minute 1:12 in video clip 1, I ask, “How will you know if the students met the learning goals by the end of the lesson? What are some student products we’re hoping that would show that they met the learning goal?” At minute 1:23 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “I hope to see that they are talking with each other using certain sentence frames to explain orally, and also for them to be able to write the multiplication expression to describe certain diagrams of rectangles.” I recognized that the volunteer teacher’s response was connected to the main learning goals, but I wanted to use facilitative questioning to help the volunteer teacher identify specific student work products that would show how they met the writing instructional goals of the lesson. This means that at this moment in our conversation, I was listening actively for the volunteer teacher to share work products that met the speaking and writing domains in the learning goals that she stated earlier and I was also actively listening for another form of formative assessment that aligned with the learning goals and the lesson plan that she shared. Looking back at this moment in the conversation, I wanted to use facilitative questions or specific examples that were evidence-based student work products. At minute 1:43 in video clip 1, I ask, “Are there any exit tickets or cool downs or anything like that, that are going to show that they met the outcomes?” At minute 1:52 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “Absolutely!” and at minute 1:53 in video clip 1, I say, “Tell me about that.” At minute 1:55 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “In this lesson, there is a cool down which is known as an exit ticket and they will be asked to write a multiplication expression to represent the area of a shaded region, and to tell us what the area is.”] </p></div><h4>Classroom context</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"85\"><p>[I wanted to invite the classroom teacher to describe the learning context so that we could determine components of the learning environment that would connect to our discussion about the CSTP-element-focused data. At minute 4:21 in video clip 1, I ask, “How would you describe the classroom culture or the learning environment for your students this year?” At minute 4:30 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responds, “This year we’re working on raising their hands when we have a comment or listening to one person at a time and I think we’re still working on that.” Looking back at this moment in our conversation, I recognized how her description of the classroom context identified positive behaviors that her students were working on, which demonstrated a specific area of need that we could later connect to our collaboration on a </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"86\"><p>CSTP element that aligned with this need.] </p></div><h4>Student assets and needs</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"88\"><p>[I wanted to invite the classroom teacher to share information about the students’ cultural and linguistic assets and their learning needs, so that we could relate this important contextual information to our selection of the CSTP-element-focused data. At minute 3:39 in video clip 1, I ask, “In terms of your students, what strengths do they bring to the classroom culturally and linguistically and are there any students who might need significant scaffolding or supports?” At minute 3:53 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responds, “My class is, has a lot of great ideas. They love participating, so that’s one of their cultural assets.” At minute 4:05 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher identifies potential student supports and says, “I think that something that needs support in this classroom would be for them to stay engaged with the material because it is quite challenging---fractions is not super easy.” Looking back at this moment in our conversation, I recognized how her identification of a specific student need connected to our collaborative selection of a CSTP element that was focused on creating and maintaining effective environments for student learning, especially when students engaged with challenging academic concepts.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The cool-down discussion is concrete: the coach asks about success evidence and the teacher identifies the multiplication expression and area task. This connects the plan to an inspectable work product.</p><p>The assets discussion remains at the level of students enjoying participation, while the main need is described as staying engaged with challenging fractions. Enjoying participation can be a strength, but it does not by itself identify a specific cultural or linguistic asset. The conversation needs to explore how students' knowledge and language resources can support mathematical explanation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the success criteria for the work and the evidence needed for the oral as well as written goals.</li><li>Draw out specific relevant student assets and supports instead of treating demographic labels or enthusiasm as sufficient.</li><li>Show how a contextual detail changed the lesson discussion or observation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported questioning about goals and work supports a provisional 4 across Part E. The shallow asset discussion limits a stronger claim that the conversation deepened equitable opportunities for all learners.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tCSTP Selection: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher selected one or two CSTP elements based on the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment and students’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the selected element fits the teacher's self-assessment and students' needs, and how the selection was genuinely joint. The current handbook specifies one or two elements within the same CSTP domain.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"90\"><p>[While we examined the detailed district guide that described the CSTPs, I used facilitative questions to invite the volunteer teacher to select one or two CSTP elements that she was interested in focusing on, based on her self-assessment and students’ assets and learning needs. At minute 4:38 in video clip 1, I say, “Now we’re going to move into selecting one or two CSTP, or California Standards for the Teaching Profession elements that match the areas of teaching that you’re most interested in exploring or getting feedback on.” At minute 5:00 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher and I are looking at a physical copy of the district’s detailed CSTP guide that outlines all of the CSTPs, and I say, “So here are the CSTPs and the standards. Which element or elements align with areas that you’re interested in getting feedback on during this lesson?” At minute 5:12 in video clip 1, while looking at the district’s detailed CSTP descriptions and pointing to the specific CSTP, the volunteer teacher says, “Since I am a newer teacher I do want to keep working on classroom management and I think 2.6 here makes sense because I was talking about wanting to support positive behavior, such as making sure that one person is speaking at a time.” Looking back at this moment in our conversation, I recognize how my decision to invite the volunteer teacher to select a CSTP that she was interested in getting feedback on connected with my previous decisions to facilitate the conversation in a way that started with eliciting the volunteer teacher’s thinking about student learning goals, student assets and needs, and classroom context. These previous decisions to begin the preobservation meeting with these topics led to our collaboration to jointly select a CSTP element that attended to student learning needs and the instructional strategies that the volunteer teacher identified as the focal aspects of collecting evidence and feedback during the observation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher selects the routines and positive-behavior focus and explains a personal growth interest. The coach connects that choice to earlier discussion rather than simply assigning a standard.</p><p>The analysis should go one step further: how will routines make mathematical learning more accessible, and what evidence will distinguish access from compliance? Hand-raising and turn-taking can support discussion but do not establish understanding of fractional area.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link the selected element to the specific lesson goals and student needs discussed earlier.</li><li>Identify observable teaching practices and student responses that will provide relevant evidence.</li><li>Preserve the teacher's rationale and agency while jointly testing whether the focus is sufficiently precise.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The teacher's reported selection and rationale support a provisional 4 alongside the other conversation evidence. Deeper inquiry into how routines support different learners would be needed for 5.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 4 / Agreeing on observation evidence</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tCoaching and Observation Process: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher collaboratively reviewed the coaching and observation process, and the CSTP–element-focused data to be collected during the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the jointly agreed process, duration, focus, and evidence collection. Include teacher practices, student responses, and student work. Analyze how the conversation produced shared understanding rather than presenting logistics as a checklist.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"92\"><p>[To collaboratively review the coaching and observation process, I wanted to confirm with the volunteer teacher how long the observation would be and confirm the CSTP-element focused data to be collected during the lesson. From minute 0:00 to minute 0:18 in video clip 2, I say, “Ok, just to wrap up this pre-observation meeting, I just want to close with an overview of the coaching cycle that we’re going to go through and make sure that we understand like how long the observation’s gonna be, right? So how long is this lesson?” At minute 0:19 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher checks her lesson plan and says, “It will be an hour and 10 minutes…closer to an hour.” At minute 0:27 in video clip 2, I nod my head as I write down notes and say, “An hour? Ok, that sounds good!” At this point in the conversation, I wanted to confirm the main points of our collaborative pre-observation meeting to make sure that I wasn’t missing any details or information that we would need as we scheduled the logistics of the observation. At minute 0:31 in video clip 2, I say, “In this pre-observation meeting we talked about all of the instructional components to the lesson [volunteer teacher nods in agreement], we talked about your students and their learning goals and their assets and their needs [volunteer teacher nods and says, “Mmmhmmm” in agreement], we collaborated for the focus of the data collected [volunteer teacher nods head in agreement]…and did we say it was going to be CSTP 2.6?” When I mention the specific CSTP 2.6 at minute 0:48 in video clip 2 and ask a clarifying question, I am pointing at the district CSTP guide that is between the volunteer teacher and I, and as she looks at the CSTP that I point to in the district guide, the volunteer teacher says, “Yes! Correct.” To further clarify the CSTP that we collaboratively chose, at minute 0:53 in video clip 2, I read the CSTP 2.6 aloud as it is written in the district guide: “Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn [the volunteer teacher nods head in agreement].” At this point in the conversation, I wanted to confirm the observation data that would provide evidence for CSTP 2.6 and wanted to paraphrase what the volunteer teacher had shared with me in the preobservation conference. At minute 1:02 in video clip 2, I ask, “And the observation data that would show this is…students…you emphasized them being able to show you different kinds of positive behavior for listening?” Immediately after I ask the volunteer teacher this question, at minute 1:18 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher nods in agreement and smiles. Looking back at this point, I recognize that I wanted to use facilitative questions to elicit the volunteer teacher’s response and confirmation of the specific behaviors that would serve as evidence or data related to the CSTP element. At minute 1:18 in video clip 2, I ask, “Remind me again…what those behaviors, listening behaviors, would be?” At minute 1:23 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “Right, some listening behaviors like raising their hand, being able to clarify other people’s thoughts or add on instead of repeating, and you know, even if it’s repeating, saying that, oh, I’m repeating from this certain individual...” [At this point, I nod my head in agreement and say, “Yeah!” and continue to listen to the volunteer teacher as she is expressing her ideas about the data that we should collect during the observation that relates to CSTP 2.6]. At minute 1:38 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “And equity sticks, I think, could also help with that, to make sure that everyone has a voice.” At this point, I wanted to affirm and use paraphrase what I had heard to let the volunteer know that I was actively listening to her response, and at minute 1:44 in video clip 2, I say, “Alright! So I’ll also be…not only looking out for what the students are doing and how they’re showing these positive behaviors, but also the strategies that you’re using as a teacher [the volunteer teacher says, “Yup! Mmhmm” and nods her head in agreement] to elicit [the volunteer teacher says, “Yes!” and smiles] those positive behaviors and maintaining that effective learning environment for all student learning.” At minute 2:00 in video clip 2, I am looking down at the district’s CSTP guide and citing CSTP 2.6, and the volunteer teacher is also looking at the CSTP 2.6 as I cite it during the conversation. At minute 2:04 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher confirms our review of the CSTP-element focused data that we collaboratively generated by saying, “Yeah!” and at minute 2:05 in video clip 2, I confirm by saying, “Ok sounds good.” To continue to review the coaching and observation process, at minute 2:07 in video clip 2 I say, “After the observation we’re going to review the video [the volunteer teacher says, “Ok”] during our post-observation conference as well as the student work products [the volunteer teacher says “Ok”] and other relevant data that matched the CSTP element and we’ll analyze it together.” To confirm, at minute 2:27 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “Ok, sounds good!” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"93\"><p>I wanted to use facilitative questions to have the volunteer teacher identify data to collect during the lesson that would highlight the CSTP 2.6 element. At minute 5:30 in video clip 1, I ask, “Which types of observation evidence would best showcase this element that we’re selecting here?” and I point to CSTP 2.6 in the district CSTP guide. At minute 5:40 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “I think…noticing they’re raising their hand and not speaking while others are speaking. Maybe having them repeat what each other says, to work on listening skills.” Here, the volunteer teacher identified observable behavioral indicators of positive behavior, as detailed in the district description of CSTP 2.6.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response gives detailed dialogue about lesson length, listening behaviors, participation routines, teacher strategies, and joint review of video and student work. The teacher contributes examples rather than only receiving a plan.</p><p>Some questions invite confirmation of the coach's proposed interpretation. The plan would be stronger if it identified how evidence will be recorded and how students' reasoning and access will be distinguished from visible on-task behavior. Long transcript-like passages need analysis of the consequential decisions, not repeated descriptions of nodding.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the agreed evidence categories and how the observation tool will capture them.</li><li>Identify a teacher contribution that changed or refined the plan.</li><li>Confirm the live observation and subsequent joint video/work review, and explain how the plan addresses the mathematical goals.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported two-way clarification of data and process supports a provisional 4. Confirm it in the clips and distinguish eliciting the teacher's thinking from obtaining agreement to the coach's interpretation.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / CSTP-focused observation notes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Collect specific evidence during the live observation and connect it to the agreed CSTP element and lesson goals. Separate observed actions and student responses from your interpretation or proposed alternative. Useful notes allow the teacher to examine a claim, not merely accept your judgment.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>From observation to a coaching question</summary><p>Keep three moves distinct: describe an event, offer a cautious interpretation tied to the agreed focus, and invite the teacher to examine it. A question should allow a genuine alternative interpretation.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Observed event</th><th scope=\"col\">Cautious interpretation</th><th scope=\"col\">Question for joint examination</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Observed event\">Illustrative only: a student reports finishing, but the collected response states an answer without explaining the reasoning.</td><td data-label=\"Cautious interpretation\">Completion did not establish understanding in this instance. The record does not establish the student's effort or the teacher's intention.</td><td data-label=\"Question for joint examination\">What does this response show about understanding, and what further evidence would help us decide?</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching demonstration, not an event from the CTC example or a substitute for observation notes. Connect your actual evidence to the CSTP element and lesson goal selected with the teacher.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"137\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>CSTP: CSTP 2 Creating and Maintaining Effective Environments for Student Learning </p><p>Element: 2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn </p><p>Teacher’s Goal or Focus Question (from Pre-Observation): Classroom Management </p><p>Make sure that one person is speaking at a time </p><p>Wanting to support positive behavior </p><p>Students are raising their hands and not speaking when others are speaking </p><p>Having them repeat what each other says (Clarifying and repeating) </p><p>Listening skills (look for these student behaviors) </p><p>Instructional Goal 1: Students describe (orally and in writing) how different equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a given rectangle with fractional sides greater than 1. </p><p>Instructional Goal 2: Students explain (orally) strategies used to determine the area of a rectangle with fractional side lengths and represent the area with a multiplication expression. </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"138\"><p>Collection of Data/Evidence </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"139\"><p>Teacher opens lesson by displaying the student learning goal (Let’s find the area of more rectangles) on the digital display. Invites students to participate by asking, “Can someone read what we’re doing today?” Multiple students raise their hands to volunteer. When a student begins reading the lesson goal and needs assistance, teacher starts to read the learning goal with the student and asks the student if they have their glasses and if they can see what is displayed. Reminds students that the learning goal is also in their individual workbooks that contain all of the student materials needed for the lesson. When multiple students begin talking at once, teacher reminds students, “One voice at a time.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"141\"><p>Missed opportunity: To ensure clarity of student learning goals in the beginning of the lesson, teacher can provide a connection between prior learning goals and the current lesson’s learning goals. Teacher can use question prompts to elicit student responses that can connect to prior knowledge and practice with concepts connected to the lesson goals. Teacher can also provide success criteria in the beginning of the lesson that connect with current lesson’s learning goals, so students will know how they will be demonstrating their understanding and learning by the end of the lesson. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"143\"><p>Teacher shares that they are moving onto the warm-up now and displays warm-up materials on the digital display. Teacher shares, “Let me explain what we’re doing. I see some people already trying and it’s ok but let me just tell us what we’re doing.”  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"144\"><p>While previewing the true/false warm-up activity with the students, asks a clarifying question, “What does true mean?” and reminds students to raise their hands to offer a response. Teacher reviews the three parts of the activity, invites students to try the activity, and sets a timer while saying, “I’m going to give us a minute and see what we get. I’m going to walk around and make sure you are doing it.” Teacher circulates the room, confers with students, and shares reminders to ensure that the learning environment is quiet during this time. Some students ask, “Are you going to pick lucky ducks?” Towards the end of the minute, teacher confirms with students that she is “picking lucky ducks” to share out (“Lucky Ducks” is a digital student randomizer used often in the classroom to encourage student engagement and participation). [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"146\"><p>Teacher uses digital student randomizer during review of warm-up math activities, and 4 different students are chosen to participate and share their reasoning. As students are sharing their thinking, other students in the class appear to listen to the student who is sharing by turning their heads towards that student. Teacher also supports by scripting what the student is sharing on the whiteboard. The teacher monitors student engagement and lets a student know that they are next to share. When a student in the back of the room appears to be disengaged and starts to make noise, the teacher addresses the student and lets the student know that she will expect the student to repeat what the other student is sharing out: “We’re working on our listening skills.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] When the student shares that they can’t hear what the student in the front of the class is saying, teacher asks the student if they could speak a little louder so the students in the back of the room could hear them and encourages the student to speak with confidence. When the next student is chosen to participate by the digital student randomizer, teacher encourages student to also explain their reasoning to support why they chose true or false and offers a sentence starter: “This statement is false because…”. As students share their reasoning, other students in the class appear to quietly listen and some students look over at the students who are taking turns speaking. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"148\"><p>Teacher shares that they are moving on to Activity 1 and reminds students of the learning goal for that lesson. Teacher displays materials for Activity 1 on the digital display and reminds students of yesterday’s exit ticket that connects with this current lesson: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"149\"><p>“Remember yesterday’s cool down, we did quite well! Can we keep it up? There’s another cool down today!” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher displays the diagram for the activity, holds up the student workbook displaying the corresponding material, and reminds students that the same diagram is on page 55 in their individual workbooks. Teacher walks around the classroom, asks students to point to the diagram, and shares that she is looking for students to point to diagram to show that they are engaged in this part of the activity. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Asks students, “What are we doing with this problem?” as they indicate to the first math prompt posed on the corresponding page. A student volunteers to read the prompt aloud, and as the student is reading the prompt, the class is sitting quietly and looking at the prompt in the workbooks.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"151\"><p>Teacher continues Activity 1 and asks students questions to facilitate dialogue about the diagram. One student raises their hand (this student has already volunteered earlier during the lesson). After initiating 10 seconds of wait time, the teacher uses a student randomizer paper tool to choose a student to participate. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] As each student participates and shares thinking when responding to teacher’s facilitative and clarifying questions, other students in the class appear to listen to responses. One student raises their hand and offers an explanation for the diagram. As student is sharing and teacher is using facilitative and clarifying questions, the students appear to listen to conversation and attend to the diagram parts that teacher is emphasizing during the conversation. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher notices another student raise their hand and invites student to share their thinking. Student shares their thinking and teacher confirms by indicating to the diagram. Teacher provides clear directions for expectations for students to continue the rest of the activity, reminds students about the purpose and learning goals of the activity, and makes connections between today’s activity and yesterday’s lesson goals. Teacher states the time length that students have to work on the activity and sets timer: “I’m going to give us 5 minutes, how does that sound, to work on this.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher asks clarifying question about the class procedure to use if students need help: “If you really don’t know, what do you do?” Students reply: “Ask 3 people!” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] After the timer starts, students immediately start working on activity, individually and in pairs. Teacher monitors and encourages students to be specific when asking peers for help: “Ask specifically—what are you looking for?” Teacher encourages students to read the directions again to help them ask specific questions for help from their peers. Teacher walks around the room, listening to student conversations and occasionally stops to have 1:1 conversations with students. Students asking peers, “Can you help me?” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"153\"><p>Teacher gives an attention signal to clarify expectations for the activity. Teacher moves to the back of the room and says, “Flat tire!” Students say, “Ssssssssss” and turn their attention towards the teacher. Teachers asks students a clarifying question about the directions for the activity. A few students raise their hands to offer clarifying answers to the question. Teacher encourages students to use additional activity resources in their workbooks to help them with the math activity. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Students raise their hands to ask the teacher clarifying questions, and teacher immediately acknowledges and responds. Students continue to work on the activity, individually and in pairs. Teacher circulates around the room and supports students 1:1 during the activity. Teacher announces, “I see someone with an expression already!” Teacher continues to observe, and walk around room, encouraging students and checking in with students. When a student begins distractive behaviors, teacher immediately walks over, confers with student, and encourages them to talk to their partner; teacher encourages the partner student, “Why don’t you try explaining to him…” and indicates the math activity. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"155\"><p>Teacher gives a signal to class about the next transition after this activity. Teacher says, “Thirty more seconds and then we’re going to share what we thought of.” Teacher encourages students to write down their thinking. When timer signal indicates the end of the designated time for the activity, the teacher says, “Flat tire!” and the students immediately respond by saying , “Ssssss” and turn their attention to the teacher. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher acknowledges that the math activity can feel challenging, but asks if she can choose a student to share their thinking with the class. A few students raise their hands. Teacher notices a student volunteering (who has not participated yet during the lesson) and asks her to come up to the digital display to share her thinking. As student walks up to the front of the room, teacher encourages class to support their peer as she comes up to share her thinking. Teacher says, “If you guys are great supporters of [student name], you should be listening.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher shares that she is going to use the digital student randomizer to choose the next student to participate and share their thinking about the math activity. While student is sharing math thinking on the digital display, students in the class are listening and teacher is standing near a group of students on the side of the room as she facilitates discussion. Teacher encourages students to be supportive of student sharing by counting with her as she marks the diagram and explains her thinking. The class immediately begins to participate and count with student who is sharing. Some students ask clarifying questions during the discussion. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"157\"><p>27:00 Teacher provides guidance on how students can explain their math thinking in writing. Teacher writes a sentence frame on the board, “This expression matches the diagram because __________.” A few students in class write the sentence frame in their workbooks, and teacher shares, “I see [student name 1] writing it down, and I see [student name 2] writing it down. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"158\"><p>28:00 Students work on the activity independently and in pairs, and some students walk up to the teacher to confer. When a student says, “This is so hard,” teacher encourages students to look at the diagram, shares that one of their peers has solved it, and encourages students: “I see a lot of you writing it down. Someone has already solved it. So if one of you can do it, all of you can do it.” As students continue to work on the activity, teacher monitors and says to a student, “I see you’ve got it!” and encourages the student to talk to another student and share their thinking. “Talk to each other.” Teacher confers with a student pair and then one student walks up to teacher and confers. Some students walk across the room to talk to other students; some of these conversations do not appear to be focused on the math activity. Teacher says, “Some of us are trying really hard, and some of us are not trying to learn this because we’re off topic.”  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"160\"><p>36:00 Teacher signals a transition from this part of the activity. Teacher says, “Alright, everyone back in their seats in 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2…” Students quickly move about the room to find their seats, and one student says, “C’mon, sit down!” Teacher says, “I’m going to take off a letter because people are talking.” 2 students are in the front of the room and one student in class says, “C’mon, stop talking!” Students are annotating on the digital diagram, and most of class is quiet. It doesn’t appear that all students are attending to the digital display, and teacher says, “I’m sorry, some of us are not listening. How do I know? Your attention is not on the screen, your attention is outside for some of you, you don’t have learning bodies…better! [Student name] turn around, please. The screen is that way.” Students at the front continue to explain math thinking with digital diagram, and teacher asks facilitative and clarifying questions. Teacher encourages student to show their thinking on the screen. Invites another student to join student who is sharing in the front and teacher thanks student for coming up and sharing.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"162\"><p>41:00 When another student who is sitting in the back gets up to sharpen pencil and stands at the sharpener for more than 20 seconds sharpening pencil, teacher reminds student to ask for another pencil. Student asks for a pencil. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] As student in the front continues to share their math thinking, teacher walks over to student at the sharpener and quietly confers with them. Student quietly stands with teacher, teacher hands student a pencil, and the student goes back to their desk. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"164\"><p>42:00 Student continuing to share their thinking at the front of the room, and helps student refer to digital diagram to show their thinking. Teacher asks class if they have any questions about the student’s thinking. When a student asks a question in class and their voice volume is low, the teacher asks the student to speak a little louder because it’s hard to listen. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] When the students ask for the student who is sharing to explain it again, the teacher immediately responds and asks the student to share again and “speak a little bit louder, please.” The student who is sharing begins to explain their thinking again, and students in class are looking at the screen as the student annotates on the diagram. After the student explains, the teacher asks the student to label the diagram: “Can you label the 14? Just count 14 to show us what you mean.” The teacher confirms the counting and labeling, and points out how the width and length matches the expression, and thanks the student for sharing. [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"166\"><p>43:30 Teacher signals a transition to the next activity in the lesson. “Solve for A or C. Find the area.” The teacher modified the activity to create entry points for students into the activity after determining the need for more support based on student work in the previous part of the lesson. The teacher circulates the classroom and monitors while students work on the task. Some students immediately bring materials to work in established groups. [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"168\"><p>44:50 Teacher gives time signal to indicate how much time students have left to complete the task: “I’m going to give us 1 more minute to solve for A or C.” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"169\"><p>46:30 Teacher gives signal that task time is over and guides students to transition. Teacher says, “Ok, back at your seats in 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.” [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"171\"><p>47:00 Teacher guides students to transition into sharing math thinking around the task. Teacher asks, “Did anyone get to solve these expressions and would like to show us? [Student name], come up.” Teacher invites a student who has not shared yet to come up and show their thinking on the whiteboard. Student begins writing on the board to share math thinking. Teacher monitors class as the student is writing on board, and then says, “Sorry [student name] someone is being disrespectful right now, so [another student name] I’m going to ask you to repeat [student name’s] thinking.”  [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"172\"><p>48:00 Teacher steps in to support student who is explaining their thinking: As student is sharing their thinking in front of the class, teacher displays the student’s workbook and diagram under the document camera to help the student explain their thinking with the diagram visual. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher asks, “Is it one twothirds or how many are there?” Student says, “4.” Teacher helps student annotate the diagram to help explain their thinking.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"173\"><p>48:40 Teacher checks in with student who was asked to explain their thinking after the previous student. Teacher asks student to explain how the previous student got the area of the diagram. Student begins to explain how the other student explained their thinking. Teacher thanks the student for explaining.  [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"175\"><p>Missed opportunity: To further clarify how the student determined the side lengths, teacher could ask facilitative questions that clarify how the student determined which numbers to multiply to get the area of the rectangle. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"177\"><p>49:20 Teacher invites another student to explain their math thinking in the activity. The teacher maintains student engagement and says, “I’m going to randomly pick someone to share what you heard from [student name].” The teacher facilitates and uses detailed descriptions when the student is explaining their thinking, to make the math thinking visible. The teacher chooses a student to paraphrase what the student did to solve the area, and asks, “What did [student name] do?” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] The student says that they didn’t hear the student, so the teacher asks the student to explain again and speak a little louder so everyone can hear. The student explains again, and the other student paraphrases what they said.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"179\"><p>51:00 Teacher transitions students to the last activity of the lesson (the cool down, or exit ticket). Teacher says, “I want you to take out your packet now.” Student begins looking for the packet, and then teacher says, “Everyone take it out in a minute!” and puts a minute on the timer. Teacher announces, “Page 79!” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"181\"><p>Missed opportunity: Teacher can state the expectation for students to have all materials ready at the beginning of the lesson, and practice this expectation to make it a routine. This can reduce the amount of time students spend looking for materials and increase time on task. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"183\"><p>53:00 Teacher displays the cool down/exit ticket task on a digital display and explains directions to complete the task.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"185\"><p>Missed opportunity: Teacher can ask students facilitative questions such as, “What do you notice?” to prompt students to identify what the task is asking them to do. This can promote student engagement and ownership of task.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"186\"><p>Missed opportunity to check for understanding and circle back to the lesson goals: Teacher could ask students what they learned how to do and practice during the lesson, which strategies they used to identify the area, and transfer this to the formative assessment task (the exit ticket) and the success criteria for students to show their understanding on this task. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"188\"><p>53:38 Teacher highlights how this task connects to a previous task. Teacher says, “It’s similar to yesterday’s–and then you’re going to solve for it, find the area.” Teacher gives directions on what students can do after they finish (read quietly).  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"190\"><p>Missed opportunity: Teacher can have these tasks (maybe a choice menu for additional tasks to do when finished) posted in the room to make it a routine. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"192\"><p>Students work on exit tickets for the last 10 minutes of class. </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The notes document routines, teacher language, participation, transitions, and specific missed opportunities. They connect classroom management with time and access to the mathematical task and include concrete examples that can anchor feedback.</p><p>Labels such as missed opportunity are interpretations. The underlying event must remain clear, followed by an explanation of how a different action might support learning. Some successful behavior routines are described without evidence that students understood the mathematics; that distinction should be retained.</p><p>This record supports a stronger analysis than a generic observation checklist. To extend it further, examine how the teacher responded to whole-class, small-group, and individual needs, including adaptations that did not produce the intended access.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Keep time, observed teacher action, student response, and interpretation distinguishable.</li><li>Link each proposed missed opportunity to the lesson learning goal, not just a preference for a different routine.</li><li>Compare responses across different learners and connect the relevant observations to actual work samples.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The concrete notes and identified missed opportunities support a provisional 4 with the commentary. The record is less systematic about adaptations across whole-class, small-group, and individual learning needs, as required for 5.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"G\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"G\"><span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Student work from the observed lesson</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use actual work produced during the lesson and discussed with the teacher. Identify the task, the success criteria, what the work demonstrates, and what remains uncertain. A correct answer, an incorrect answer, and visible reasoning can each provide different information.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"97\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-97-0.jpg\" alt=\"Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"98\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-98-0.jpg\" alt=\"Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"99\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-99-0.jpg\" alt=\"Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Three photographed work samples receive marks of 2/2, 1/2, and 0/2. They provide a useful contrast: the middle sample has an accepted area answer but an incorrect expression, while the last has errors in both. The marks and written reasoning should be examined separately; a total score does not explain the misconception.</p><p>The sample set should be identified clearly: why these three examples were discussed and whether they are typical or selected cases. The teacher's later statement that only two students earned full credit does not mean that every other student learned nothing; item-level reasoning matters. The three photographs alone do not establish the distribution of results for the class.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use anonymous, stable sample labels that also appear in the commentary and observation discussion.</li><li>Apply clear criteria to identify the demonstrated strategy, correct reasoning, and specific misconception.</li><li>State the number of works examined and avoid generalizing a few selected examples to the entire class.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: 4 / 5, provisional. Rubric 3.4: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The work makes learning inspectable, but the score depends on how it is analyzed with observation and coaching evidence. Three samples alone cannot establish the quality of the entire conversation or the class result.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"H\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"H\"><span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Post-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the video of the post-observation meeting where you and the volunteer teacher are seen and heard, select 1 to 3 video clips. Your 1 to 3 video clips may total up to 15 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 15 minutes of video recording submitted).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 17; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit one to three clips, totaling up to fifteen minutes, from the joint post-observation meeting. The evidence should show examination of the lesson video and student work, discussion of practice, shared next steps, and teacher feedback on your coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><p class=\"scope-note\">The source commentary refers to video clips, but those recordings were not available for this review. No example video or invented transcript is substituted.</p></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The narrative reports a substantive discussion, but the actual recordings were not reviewed. It cannot independently establish how much the teacher led the interpretation, whether the referenced work and lesson video were jointly examined, or whether all cited moments appear within the submitted limits.</p><p>The provisional scores below judge the written account against the criteria without treating it as a substitute for the demonstration.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual clips against the current handbook's content and duration requirements.</li><li>Verify the evidence of joint viewing, teacher reasoning, shared next steps, and feedback.</li><li>Confirm the commentary's precise clip locations and do not manufacture dialogue to fill a gap.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: Not independently assessable without the video. Rubric 3.4: Not independently assessable without the video. Rubric 3.5: Not independently assessable without the video.</strong> The written account permits provisional judgments about the described reasoning, but not an independent score for unseen clips. Review the recordings before treating the estimates as confirmed demonstrations.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Jointly assessing student learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Assessment of Student Learning:</p><p>Describe how you supported the volunteer teacher in assessing student learning during the CSTP-focused lesson.</p><p>Describe how you shared the evidence of CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher.</p><p>Describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-analyzed the video of the lesson, student work, and/or other data (if collected) from the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three components: how you supported the teacher's assessment of learning, shared CSTP-focused evidence, and jointly analyzed the lesson video and student work. Explain how your coaching helped the teacher interpret evidence, not simply that you looked at it together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Supporting assessment of learning</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"106\"><p>[During the lesson, the volunteer teacher assessed student learning by monitoring student engagement behaviors to ensure that they were participating while the class practiced strategies to meet the math learning goals. The volunteer teacher explained how it was important for her to see that students were using the strategy of counting the side lengths of the rectangles correctly so that they could also write the correct math expression to solve for the area of the rectangle, and employ these strategies after the lesson when they completed their exit tickets, or the formative assessment after the lesson. For example, at minute 1:16 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “I was counting with them to ensure that they were all counting the side lengths, not just the presenter, and that everyone else was also engaged and wanting to count their lengths and in order to write their expressions.” At minute 1:30 in video clip 1, I ask a clarifying question to connect the volunteer teacher’s teaching decision of practicing this strategy to the formative assessment: “That was part of their exit ticket, for them to show that they met the learning goal and be able to count those.” At minutes 1:36 and 1:38 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes.” I extend the discussion of this strategy of counting the side lengths of the rectangle at minute 1:38 in video clip 1 when I ask, “How did that instructional strategy that you employed there, that you used…How did you see that show up in the student work after the lesson?” Referring to the formative assessments completed by students after the lesson, at minute 1:50 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “In their cooldowns, right? Here it seems like there were 2 students, one being the one who asked a question, which is that she couldn’t hear the presenter, so she ended up doing really well, both the expression, writing the expression and finding the area.” At minute 2:09 in video clip 1, I confirm, “Which were the learning goals of the lesson,”] and the volunteer teacher agrees. At minute 2:11 in video clip 1, I continue to jointly look at the students’ formative assessments (exit tickets/cool down) with the volunteer teacher, and the volunteer teacher says, “Yes, there are two students who got 100% because of the expressions that they had written. So, they somehow found the area.” At minute 2:31 in video clip 1, I ask, “Were they able to use that strategy that you were showing in the video?” At minute 2:34 in video clip 1, while looking through multiple student work samples, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yeah! Honestly, a lot, most of them counted 11…. counting the lengths.” Here, the volunteer teacher was referring to student work samples that showed students correctly counting 11 fractional pieces that represented one side length of the given rectangle, which also showed evidence of students who employed the strategy taught by the volunteer teacher in the lesson video.]  </p></div><h4>Sharing observation evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"108\"><p>[During our post-observation meeting, as the volunteer teacher and I watched the video of the lesson, I paused the video at certain moments to engage the volunteer teacher in a conversation about CSTP-focused data that we viewed in the video, and I also shared evidence from my notes. The CSTP-focused data related to CSTP 2.6: Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn, from the district’s CSTP guide that we used to jointly choose the CSTP to focus on. For example, at minute 0:00-0:03 in video clip 1, I begin by asking the volunteer teacher to “explain what’s happening in this part of the lesson here”. At minute 0:03 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “So I have a student up on the at the board showing her work, and there was another student in the audience asking a question, and she I couldn't hear her from the back, so I asked her to speak a little louder. And then, you know, the student in the back actually couldn't hear what the presenter was saying so I asked her to also speak a little louder. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"109\"><p>So, I'm trying to have them practice this routine of speaking louder in the classroom.” At minute 0:41, I said, “In my notes, I also noted that that you were employing a support to ensure engagement and positive behavior that went along with the CSTP.” Another example of how I shared CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher occurred at minute 5:03 in video clip 1 when I said, “I see you also, I made in my notes, you’re circulating around the room, watching each student group do the activity. And I noted that that is also evidence of the CSTP in ensuring that there's a positive behavior so that all of them could attend to the task and their learning task.” I also shared notes that corresponded to moments in the lesson video in which the volunteer teacher employed strategies such as routines, procedures, norms and supports that were related to CSTP-focused data. For example, while discussing an instructional strategy (counting the lengths of the rectangle) employed by the volunteer teacher to ensure student engagement and employ supports for students to practice math strategies that would help them access the lesson’s learning goal, at minute 2:48 in video clip 1, I said, “I noticed you enlarged the screen for them to be able to count along with you and other students who were presenting. And I noted that in my notes as well.”] </p></div><h4>Joint analysis</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"111\"><p>[The volunteer teacher and I watched the lesson video together, frequently pausing the video to discuss CSTP-related evidence and to examine students’ formative assessments (exit tickets/cool downs). For example, while examining student work, at minute 3:02 in video clip 1, I said, “Yeah, I see students notating the parts and circling them in their student work, in their exit tickets,” and the volunteer teacher agrees, and at minute 3:11 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “Yeah. And I do see like, dots of them counting,” and at minute 3:14 in video clip 1, I agree and say, “Oh yeah, using that strategy that was shown during the lesson,” as I point to the lesson video on the computer screen. At the points that we paused the video to discuss what we were observing in the video, I posed facilitative questions to invite the volunteer teacher to share what they were observing in the video and their thinking about instructional decisions that they made during the lesson, For example, at minute 3:26 in video clip 1, I said, “Here in the lesson, right, I'm noticing that you displayed the next part of the activity, and there were four items…” and at minute 3:34 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes, with the expressions,” and at minute 3:37 in video clip 1, I share, “I noticed a modification. Can you tell me about how you changed that part of activity to support students?”  The volunteer teacher shared her rationale for her decision making at minute 3:46 in video clip 1: “Originally, they were asked to choose the expressions that matches the situation of the area, but I gave them the expressions instead of having them deduce, because I felt like that would have been quite challenging for certain individuals, most of them.” I responded immediately at minute 4:12 in video clip 1, I asked, “Was time a factor, too, in terms of the lesson?”, and at minute 4:17 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirmed, “Definitely,” and I asked her to tell me more about that. At minute 4:22 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher shared, “I think we just finished the first activity in 44- or 44-minute mark right here, yeah, and math is normally about an hour or so.” I share an observation about this part of the lesson video at minute 4:33 in video clip 1 I said, “so you adjusted, knowing that they would need to also have time to complete an exit ticket?” and the volunteer teacher immediately confirmed at minute 4:39 in video clip 1, “Yes, yeah.” I continue clarifying at minute 4:40 in video clip 1 and said, “so making that adjustment gave them more support in at least being able to access the activity and attempt it for more practice before the exit ticket,” and while I shared this, the volunteer teacher confirmed this observation at minutes 4:47, 4:49, and 5:51 in video clip 1 by saying, “Yes, yep, yep.”] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage connects notes about instructional supports with the lesson video and marks on exit tickets. The teacher explains why she adjusted an activity and the coach invites further reasoning. This is relevant evidence of a learning conversation.</p><p>The account sometimes moves too quickly from engagement to learning. Dots or counting marks can show use of a strategy without proving that the expression or area is correct. Giving students expressions rather than asking them to derive them changes the demand of the task; the joint analysis should examine what learning can still be inferred.</p><p>The strongest next step is to trace a learning goal from the pre-observation discussion through observed instruction to a specific feature of student work, then explain how the teacher's interpretation changed.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the work sample, criterion, and mathematical inference supporting each claim of learning.</li><li>Examine how the in-the-moment task modification affected what the work can demonstrate.</li><li>Connect the teacher's interpretation with the prior goal and the relevant observation evidence, and explain your coaching contribution.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: 4 / 5, provisional. Rubric 3.4: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported use of video, notes, and work supports the combined provisional judgments. The strongest remaining need is a deeper connection between prior learning goals, the adapted task, and what the student work actually demonstrates.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tEffectiveness of Teaching Practices Including Next Steps for Volunteer Teacher Growth:</p><p>Describe how you facilitated a joint discussion of the effectiveness of the volunteer teacher’s teaching practices to support student learning, identifying any missed opportunities.</p><p>After discussing the volunteer teacher’s effective teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-determined possible next steps of the volunteer teacher’s professional growth in the identified CSTP element(s) (what you will do and what the volunteer teacher will do).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze the joint discussion of successful practices and missed opportunities, then explain the next professional steps you co-determined. Include what the teacher will do, what you will do, the resources or support, and the evidence behind the plan.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Making joint next steps concrete</summary><p>A useful next step connects an identified learning need to a teacher action, your coaching support, appropriate resources, and evidence to revisit. Explain how both adults shaped the decision rather than presenting advice as an agreement.</p><p>Distinguish the next lesson from the observed lesson: a proposed check for understanding has not happened yet. Identify what the teacher will try, what you will help prepare or examine, and how the subsequent discussion could determine whether the change addressed the need.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Effectiveness and missed opportunities</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"114\"><p>[While watching the video of the lesson together, the volunteer teacher and I discussed the CSTP-focused data and observed teaching practices that supported student learning. I used facilitative prompts and questions to engage the volunteer teacher in reflections on her teaching practices that supported student engagement, positive behaviors, and student learning towards the learning goals of the lesson. For example, to give feedback on effective practices that I observed during the lesson, as we watched certain parts of the video lesson, I referred to my notes to confirm the video evidence of effective teaching practices. For example, at minute 1:37 in video 2, I referred to my observation notes as we were watching the video lesson and said, “Here, I noted that you were connecting the previous learning and activities to the current task, and that's a signal that also supports them to access the learning goals is to connect the current learning to the previous learning.” At minutes 1:44 and 1:54 in video 2, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes.” I also used facilitative prompts and questions to guide the volunteer teacher to identify missed opportunities to support student learning. For example, at minute 0:00 in video clip 2, I ask, “what are you noticing about this part of the lesson, when they're transitioning?” and at minute 0:08 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “That it's taking a while and that it is not a routine…someone asking, ‘Take what out?’, not knowing that it is the end of a lesson and we have our cool down…that signal…”. I immediately acknowledge the volunteer teacher’s observation, and at minute 0:30 in video clip 2, I ask, “Could this become a routine and be taught as a routine?” At minute 0:31 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “Yes.” I immediately followed up with a facilitative question to invite the volunteer teacher to reflect on strategies that would help the students employ more positive behaviors at a recurring part of the math lesson in which they have their materials ready to engage in the formative assessment or cool down/exit ticket part of the math lessons. At minute 0:33 in video clip 2 I ask, “How would you set it up and practice this with the students for next time?” and at minute 0:40 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher reflects, “I'm thinking that maybe there should be just like that signal, like after we finish the synthesis, I say, ‘Okay, we're done with the synthesis’ (that word synthesis) and we're doing our cool down, then they take it out. Or I could have them take it out like as a morning routine in the morning and have it ready on their desk.” To affirm the volunteer’s teacher reflection and add on, at minute 1:05 in video clip 2 I say, “Like already, have the page up somewhere visible, and that could eventually become a routine, and be part of the environment that ensures positive behavior for all.” The volunteer teacher confirms and agrees at minute 1:18 in video clip 2, they share, “Yeah. And I've turned those cool downs into packets already, so that they could have it easily accessible. Organization is something that I’m trying to also…”. Immediately I confirm hearing when the volunteer teacher shares a possible next step for professional growth and at minute 1:29 in video clip 2, I repeat, “Organization”, and nod my head, and then at minute 1:31 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yeah, because I see a few people like, where's my packet?” and I immediately affirm, “Oh, okay!” to let the volunteer teacher know that I understood the video evidence that she cited to inform her next steps. There were also moments while reviewing student work that initiated conversation about missed opportunities and next steps. For example, at minute 2:06 in video 2, as the volunteer teacher is examining student work, she said, “He was so close. He counted four wholes here, but three. So, because of that, he solved the area wrong. So he just wrote the wrong expression, but he knew how to solve for the area.” I immediately respond to the volunteer teacher’s observation about the student’s work, and at minute 2:23 in video 2 I ask, “Is there a point in the lesson that maybe you feel like you could have scaffolded that part to support students using that strategy?” The volunteer teacher responds at minute 2:33 in video 2 the volunteer teacher reflects, “I think it's also a routine, having them get into a routine of double checking their work. Like, even during the year, during certain tests, I'm like, double check you guys. It's something that I'm still trying to make it…I'm trying to normalize that it's okay to go back and double check your answers. Okay, because they just want to get it done. And they're like, I'm done, okay, and move on, type of thing. So that's something I would like to reinforce my classroom.” At minute 3:07 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher continues, “There’s something else I notice,” and motions to the lesson video on the computer. I immediately respond with a facilitative question to initiate further reflection from the volunteer teacher, and at minute 3:09 in video clip 2, I ask,” Yeah. What else did you notice?” At minute 3:11 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher said, “The routine of cool down itself as well. They're not super quiet, even though I keep saying it's an independent work, it's telling me what you know, not what someone else knows. But it doesn't seem to be the most quiet that it could be.” I immediately identify the volunteer teacher’s reflection as an indicator of a next step to increase the effectiveness of her teaching practices towards ensuring that all students in her class are employing positive behaviors for learning during the independent work task of the cool down, or formative assessment task, at the end of the math lessons.] </p></div><h4>Joint next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"116\"><p>[After the volunteer teacher and I discussed the teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, I used facilitative prompts, questions, and clarifying language to help us co-determine possible next steps for growth in the identified CSTP element (2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn). For example, after the volunteer teacher shared her observation that all students in the lesson video were not employing expected behaviors during the cool down, at minute 3:33 in video 2, I said, “That would be a next step, too! So, I'm hearing you say that your next steps would be working on certain parts of this math lesson of this new math curriculum to eventually become routines.” At minute 3:50 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes, yes!” I continue facilitating, and at minute 3:53 in video clip 2, I ask, “In order to make these things routine…so what kinds of things would be your next steps to do?” At minute 3:59 in video 2, the volunteer teacher offers her reflections on next steps to address this instructional need, and said, “Maybe really talking about the expectations of what each part of a lesson looks like. For example, warm up, expectations, activity, expectations, to cool down expectations, and then what to do after a cool down.” To further encourage reflection on next steps, at minute 4:25 in video 2, I asked, “Is there anything in the classroom environment that you could add to support with making those routines?” The volunteer teacher immediately responded at minute 4:32 in video 2, and said, “Yeah, I use a timer very often in my classroom. Yeah, like you have one minute to think quietly to yourself.” After I shared that I also observed this and noted the volunteer teacher’s use of the timer, the volunteer shared that it was helpful. At minute 4:47 in video 2, I affirm and encourage the volunteer teacher’s employment of this instructional strategy, and said, “You were able to get through all of the parts too. Yeah, you know, from the warm up to the activities to the very end to wrap it up, to see what they're thinking and what they walked away with.” The volunteer teacher confirmed her rationale for employing the use of the timer, and at minute 5:01 in video clip 2, she said, “Yes, yes. That's very important to me that even if I like skip one activity, I still need to get to the cool down to see where they're at.” As the volunteer teacher and I moved towards the latter parts of our postobservation meeting, I wanted to facilitate a discussion to summarize and clarify the volunteer teacher’s next steps for professional growth related to the CSTP element 2.6 (Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn), as well as clarify how I could support these next steps. At minute 0:00 in video clip 3, I said, “For next steps in just continuing to grow and develop towards employing classroom routines, procedures and norms, the CSTP element, it says employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn from our conversation, I heard you mention making certain things routines. Can you just summarize that for me and tell me what kinds of things you want to work on making routines?” At minute 0:34 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher says, “Yeah, from warm up expectations, to activity expectations, to synthesis, to cool down expectations and what to do after the cool down is something I want to work on building in my class.” At this moment in the conversation, I wanted to refer to an earlier moment in our discussion when the volunteer teacher mentioned wanting to work on student organization to support positive behaviors and to ensure a climate in which all students can learn, related to the CSTP element. At minute 0:53 in video clip 3, I said, “I also heard you mention wanting to work on supporting students organization, yeah, tell me more about that.” The volunteer teacher responded at minute 1:03 in video clip 3, and said, “It’s about getting their notebooks, math notebooks, out at the beginning of a lesson, and their math workbooks. There are math workbooks for every unit, and we are in our second unit now, and there are about six to eight workbooks, because six to eight units, so I want them to be able to get out the right workbook, and the rest should go home and stay with them, because it would be too many workbooks in their desk if they keep it all for the year. And their cool down packets should stay in their folders, but some of them are not in their folders, so then they lose it.” At this point, I asked a facilitative question to elicit strategies from the volunteer teacher, and at minute 1:46 in video 3 I ask, “Yeah. How would you support those students that are still losing those things?” The volunteer teacher immediately responded with a possible strategy and next steps, and at minute 1:51 in video 3, she said, “I keep it possibly, like I have this student who has two right here,” and she indicated to the student work that had duplicates because that student had lost their packet. I wanted to point out that her possible strategy could work by mentioning what I noticed, and at minute 1:57 in video 3, I said, “I noticed that there’s just a few, right, to support…that could be a next step for sure, to help students with organization.” Later in the conversation, as I asked for feedback, I also offered my next steps to ensure my support with my volunteer teacher’s professional growth, and at minute 2:51 in video clip 3, I said, “I am going to share all these notes with you too, so you can see all the details of where the CSTP elements were and everything.”] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher notices transition difficulties and identifies possible routines; the conversation also examines a student's counting error and organization needs. These examples are more useful than broad advice about classroom management.</p><p>The next-step discussion largely identifies intentions: clarify expectations, use routines, organize materials, and share notes. It does not fully specify the resources, coaching support, or follow-up needed to enact and examine those changes. The later request for differentiation resources reinforces that gap.</p><p>The mathematical error also deserves careful analysis. Reminding students to double-check may help, but it does not by itself address a misconception about side lengths or area. Connect the professional learning plan to the diagnosed need rather than treating all errors as inattention.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish a behavioral or organizational barrier from a mathematical misconception using the work.</li><li>Specify a jointly chosen practice, resource, and coaching action, with responsibilities and a review date.</li><li>Identify how the plan addresses particular students' learning needs and what new evidence will show whether it helps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.4: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 3.5: 2 / 5, provisional.</strong> The distinction matters: the learning conversation supports a provisional 3 on Rubric 3.4, while the missing specificity of resources and follow-up support leaves Rubric 3.5 at a provisional 2.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Learning from teacher feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tVolunteer Teacher Feedback on Candidate’s Coaching Practices:</p><p>Describe the feedback the volunteer teacher provided regarding your coaching skills during the coaching and observation cycle, including what they found helpful and/or not helpful. Explain how this feedback informs your understanding of your strengths and areas for growth as an instructional coach.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Report what the teacher found helpful and what was missing or unhelpful, then explain what that feedback reveals about your coaching strengths and growth. The current question explicitly asks for your interpretation, not just a transcript of the feedback exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What was helpful</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"119\"><p>[To elicit feedback from the volunteer teacher about my coaching skills, at minute 2:08 in video clip 3, I said, “Lastly, I want to get some feedback from you as my volunteer teacher about the coaching process. I want to know what was helpful and what wasn't helpful.” At minute 2:21 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher said, “Well, first off, so thank you so much for helping and being here in my classroom. Because, you know, I always say it takes a village to make sure to help this class, but I think what was really helpful is for you to be in the classroom to observe and see what's going on in the class, so that you can give feedback and as to how I can improve, or what I can do to help these the students.”] </p></div><h4>What was missing</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"121\"><p>[At minute 3:03 in video clip 3, I asked, “What was something that maybe during the coaching process, you maybe would have wanted that was missing from this observation cycle?” At first, the volunteer teacher took some time to think about the question and wasn’t sure how to respond, and at minute 3:18 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher said, “Maybe we could…” As she hesitated, at minute 3:23 in video clip 3, I offered, “Like resources, or additional professional development…” and immediately at minute 3:25 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher agreed and said, “Oh yeah!” Immediately, at minute 3:27 in video clip 3, I ask, “In what areas do you think?” At minute 3:28 in video clip 3 the volunteer teacher immediately responded, “Classroom management, especially with this class, or how to differentiate the curriculum even more.” I immediately affirmed the volunteer teacher’s request, and at minute 3:38 in video 3, the volunteer teacher said, “Looking at this cool down now, yeah, what do I how do I move on from this? Only two students got 100% but this is data, and then what do we do with that data?” At minute 3:53 in video clip 3, I affirmed the volunteer teacher’s possible next steps and said, “Yeah, definitely, yeah. So using data to inform your next steps of instruction, yeah? Is something, yeah, I would love to help with that.” At minute 4:09 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher confirmed, “It’s always nice to have someone come in and see it in a new perspective.”]  </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher values having another person observe and offer perspective, then asks for resources on classroom management, differentiation, and using assessment data. These comments give the coach a substantive direction for growth.</p><p>The passages report the exchange but leave much of its interpretation to Part J. Here, briefly explain the strength that is supported and the unmet need that calls for change. The coach suggests resources while the teacher is hesitating; analyze how that prompt may have shaped the feedback rather than assuming it was entirely unprompted.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish the teacher's specific feedback from the options suggested by the coach.</li><li>Explain what the comments establish about a coaching strength and an unmet support need.</li><li>State how future coaching will address that need, reserving the fuller reflection for Part J.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.6: 4 / 5.</strong> The combined Part J reflection supports a 4 for Rubric 3.6. This passage supplies the feedback, but needs its own brief interpretation to fully answer current Part I question 3; it is not a separately scored answer.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tBased on your experience in this coaching cycle, and the feedback you elicited from the volunteer teacher, reflect on your instructional coaching abilities during the post-observation meeting in Step 3. Refer to the volunteer teacher’s feedback/input and evaluate your strengths and areas for growth in coaching and observing a teacher’s practice and providing instructional feedback in relation to the agreed-upon CSTP element(s). Explain why you think you should work on them.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Evaluate your coaching using the teacher's feedback and the cycle evidence. Explain why each strength or growth area matters for the agreed CSTP focus, and how your future approach will change. A hypothetical question is a proposed improvement, not something you actually asked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"126\"><p>[During the post-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher shared how the coaching cycle was helpful and shared what she thought would have been helpful to include in the coaching cycle. The volunteer teacher shared that the observation process was helpful because I was present in her class for the observation, as she shared in video clip 3 at minute 2:08 when she said, “Thank you so much for helping and being here in my classroom…I think what was really helpful is for you to be in the classroom to observe and see what’s going on in the class, so that you can give feedback and as to how I can improve, or what I can do to help the students.” Looking back, I would have asked a follow-up question after she shared that, such as, “What specific feedback was especially helpful to you during this particular coaching cycle, in regard to the CSTP we chose and the instructional goals of the lesson?” Another follow-up question I could have asked at that moment was, “What feedback was particularly helpful that you might incorporate into your teaching practice next?” In future coaching cycles, I want to make sure that I ask follow-up, facilitative questions that clarify the specific coaching moves that were especially helpful to the volunteer teacher. I think that it is important for me to work on this because I believe that it will help me to build a stronger coaching relationship with my volunteer teachers during future coaching cycles, and better understand what coaching moves and feedback were specifically helpful.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"127\"><p>To ask for feedback on what was not helpful during the coaching cycle, in video clip 3 at minute 3:03 I asked the volunteer teacher, “What was something that maybe during the coaching process, you maybe would have wanted that was missing from this observation cycle?” At minute 3:28 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher shared that she would have liked additional resources on classroom management, or curriculum differentiation, when she said, “Classroom management, especially with this class, or how to differentiate the curriculum even more.” At that moment, I affirmed the volunteer teacher’s request, and made the decision to give 10 seconds of wait time as I noticed the volunteer teacher looking over the student’s work, and then in video clip 3 at minute 3:38 the volunteer teacher continued, “Looking at this cool down now, yeah, what do I how do I move on from this? Only two students got 100% but this is data, and then what do we do with that data?” I immediately acknowledged the volunteer teacher and asked a clarifying question in video clip 3 at minute 3:53: “So using data to inform your next steps of instruction? I would love to help with that.” Looking back, I would have incorporated more time during the pre-observation meeting to carefully look over the lesson plan and facilitate a more detailed conversation with the volunteer teacher about differentiating instruction. I might have asked questions such as, “Are there any students who need significant scaffolding or extra challenges/extensions? How can we plan for this?” Through a collaborative process, the volunteer teacher and I could identify specific moments in the math lesson to scaffold learning for the identified students, plan specific question prompts to ask during the activities, or prepare resources or supplemental activities that would help students strengthen their conceptual understanding related to the math learning objectives. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"128\"><p>In the post-observation meeting, I used active listening strategies such as paraphrasing, affirmations, and requests for clarification to communicate with the volunteer teacher. For example, in the post-observation video clip 1 at minute 1:16, the volunteer teacher shared how it was important for her to engage her students in practicing the strategy to count the side lengths during the lesson, I asked a clarifying question to help connect the volunteer teacher’s teaching strategy to the students’ formative assessment. After I asked the clarifying question with this purpose, the volunteer teacher turned her attention to connecting this teaching decision to examine student work, as seen in post-observation video clip 1 at minute 1:50, when she looked for evidence of the students applying this strategy in their cool downs/exit tickets, or the formative assessments of their learning. This demonstrated my coaching decisions to use active listening strategies (to ask relevant clarifying questions) was a strength during the coaching cycle because I was able to reflect what the volunteer teacher shared with me and help her make connections between her teaching decisions and the evidence in the students’ work towards meeting the instructional goals of the lesson. Using active listening strategies like paraphrasing, affirmations, and request for clarification while communicating with volunteer teachers during coaching cycles is a strength that I would like to continue to employ and to develop because these strategies also create space and time for volunteer teachers to reflect on their thinking, decision making, and pedagogy. I want to continue to work on asking follow-up questions during coaching conversations so that I can obtain specific details related to a volunteer teacher’s goals for further development and growth of their teaching practice, and also gain specific feedback on which parts of the coaching cycle were helpful and not helpful so that I can continue to develop and grow in my effectiveness as an instructional leader.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response examines helpful observation, missed follow-up questions, the request for resources, and the need to plan differentiation more deliberately. It identifies concrete changes to future pre- and post-observation conversations.</p><p>The distinction between reported actions and retrospective alternatives is mostly clear and worth preserving. Stronger analysis would address the limited resource follow-through and the effect of leading prompts, then define how the coach will know whether the proposed changes improve teacher ownership and instructional decision-making.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each growth area to a specific teacher comment or observable coaching moment.</li><li>Specify the resource or follow-up support that will accompany better questioning.</li><li>Define how feedback from a future cycle will show whether the revised approach is more useful.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.6: 4 / 5.</strong> The extensive examination of helpful and missing support, with concrete changes to future meetings, supports 4. Research or evidence-based practices would need to explain teacher or adult learning to establish 5.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tReflect on and refer to evidence from Steps 1–3 to demonstrate how you were able to facilitate and maintain a two-way conversation throughout the coaching, observation, and instructional feedback cycle. How were you able to encourage teacher voice and ownership of their strengths and areas for growth based on evidence of practice in relation to the CSTP element(s), the video of instruction, and student work product(s)?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use evidence across the cycle to explain how the teacher retained voice and ownership in identifying strengths, needs, and next steps. Asking questions is not sufficient by itself: show how the teacher's thinking influenced the work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"130\"><p>[After investigating the current coaching, observation, and instructional feedback practices at my current school site in Step 1, it was evident that there were few opportunities during the year for teachers to participate in regular instructional feedback cycles. As I engaged in a coaching cycle with my participating teacher during Steps 1-3, it was important for me to clearly understand the components and purposes for each meeting during the coaching cycle so that I could anticipate how to ask facilitative questions to encourage the volunteer teacher to authentically share their thoughts and reflections. During the pre-observation conference, I used facilitative prompts and questions to encourage collaborative conversation to draw out instructional needs and challenges based on a CSTP element that would become the focus of data collection and analysis during and after the observation. I also asked facilitative questions during the preobservation meeting to help the volunteer teacher identify specific student learning goals for the lesson, describe the classroom context, and identify student assets and needs. For example, in video clip 1 at minute 3:39 during the pre-observation conference, I asked the volunteer teacher to describe her students’ cultural and linguistic strengths and to identify challenges that students may have during the lesson, and after sharing her students’ strengths (“they love participating”), she shared that her students may need support with staying engaged with the lesson because the instructional focus on fractions is a challenging math concept for her students. As a coach and facilitator, at this moment I recognized how the volunteer teacher’s identification of this prominent student need would later connect to a later part of our conversation where we would jointly identify a CSTP element that she would want feedback on. To encourage my volunteer teacher’s agency in attending to the anticipated challenges and supports that she identified here, I kept this information in mind as I facilitated a collaborative selection of the CSTP element as seen in video clip 1 at minute 5:00 during the pre-observation meeting when I said, “So here are the CSTPs and the standards. Which element or elements align with areas that you’re interested in getting feedback on during this lesson?” At minute 5:12 in video clip 1 of the preobservation meeting, the volunteer teacher said, “Since I am a newer teacher, I do want to keep working on classroom management and I think 2.6 here makes sense because I was talking about wanting to support positive behavior, such as making sure that one person is speaking at a time.” In this moment of the pre-observation meeting, I recognized how her previous description of the classroom context, student assets and needs, and the instructional goals connected to the selection of the CSTP element that I invited her to select. In the preobservation meeting, I also invited the volunteer teacher to identify the student work products that we would collect to provide evidence of students growth towards the learning goals, as seen in video clip 1 at minute 5:30 when I asked, “Which types of observation evidence would best showcase this element that we’re selecting here?” and the volunteer teacher identified observable behavioral data. I also used facilitative questions to draw out specific student work products, such as the cool down or exit ticket as seen in video clip 1 at minute 1:43 when I asked, “Are there any exit tickets or cool downs or anything like that, that are going to show that they met the outcomes?” and the volunteer teacher replied, “In this lesson, there is a cool down which is known as an exit ticket and they will be asked to write a multiplication expression to represent the area of a shaded region, and to tell us what the area is.” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"131\"><p>Later during the post-observation meeting, I was able to encourage the volunteer teacher to authentically voice their strengths and areas of growth by employing active listening strategies such as paraphrasing, affirmations, and requests for clarification, and asking facilitative questions. For example, in video clip 2 during the post-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher shared how she observed students who needed support with employing positive behaviors during the cool down/exit ticket, and at minute 3:33 in video clip 2 I said, ““That would be a next step, too! So, I'm hearing you say that your next steps would be working on certain parts of this math lesson of this new math curriculum to eventually become routines!” and the volunteer teacher immediately confirmed. To elicit teacher agency and reflection, at minute 3:53 in video clip 2 during the post-observation meeting, I asked, ““In order to make these things routine…so what kinds of things would be your next steps to do?” After I asked this question, the volunteer teacher shared her next steps and ideas to employ to practice the expected positive behaviors during the exit tickets with her students, such as having discussions about the expectations during this time, supporting students with organizing their materials for learning, and helping students distinguish specific expectations during each of the major moments of the math lessons in their new math curriculum. I also encouraged teacher agency and voice during the post-observation meeting when I asked clarifying questions to encourage the volunteer teacher to share how her teaching of math strategies connected to how the students employed those strategies in the formative assessments, or cool downs/exit tickets, after the lesson. For example, at minute 1:30 in video clip 1 during the post-observation meeting, I asked, ““That was part of their exit ticket, for them to show that they met the learning goal and be able to count those?” and the volunteer teacher confirmed, “yes,” and as we continued watching the lesson video, at minute 1:38 in video clip 1 I asked, “How did that instructional strategy that you employed there, that you used…How did you see that show up in the student work after the lesson?” This question prompted the volunteer teacher to look through the student work samples and at minute 2:34 in video clip 1, she shared, ““Yeah! Honestly, a lot, most of them counted 11…. counting the lengths.” This meant that she was able to connect her decisions to teach specific math strategies to how her students employed those strategies in their own work products.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response cites teacher selection of the CSTP focus, questions about student evidence, and teacher-generated routines. It explains why listening and follow-up mattered.</p><p>The analysis would be more balanced if it examined an exchange where the coach's wording narrowed the answer or supplied the conclusion. Repeated yes responses may signal agreement, but they are weaker evidence of ownership than an explanation, disagreement, or alternative initiated by the teacher.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a teacher-originated interpretation that changed your plan or understanding.</li><li>Analyze one moment where a leading prompt or rapid summary may have limited the conversation.</li><li>Explain how the partnership supported the teacher's capacity to interpret evidence independently.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.6: 4 / 5.</strong> This partnership evidence contributes to the combined 4 with question 1. It would be stronger if it examined moments that limited teacher ownership as carefully as the successful exchanges.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Equity and continuous improvement</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tInformed by a continuous improvement mindset and focus on equitable leadership, reflect on your role (or future role) as an equity-driven school leader. Discuss the potential impact of supporting teacher growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what this cycle taught you about equitable instructional leadership and the potential of coaching to support teacher growth. Connect your leadership stance to actual teacher feedback, classroom evidence, and student access. Potential impact should not be written as a guaranteed outcome.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><ul><li>Reflect on your role as an instructional coach through continuous improvement and equitable leadership.</li><li>Explain the potential impact of coaching, observation, or feedback on teacher growth.</li><li>For Level 4, support the reflection with specific evidence from this cycle; Level 5 adds relevant evidence-based practices or research.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"133\"><p>[As an equity-driven school leader, I am committed to supporting the growth and well-being of the stakeholders in our school community so that we can meet the needs of all our students every day. Supporting teacher’s growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback is effective because it is ongoing and differentiated sustained learning that directly improves the academic and socioemotional learning outcomes for our students. By employing transformational instructional coaching that is grounded in equity, teachers can be supported in developing their instructional skill set, expand their capacity to reflect on their instructional decision making, and develop their effectiveness and resilience as teachers (Aguilar, 2024). Collaborative coaching is focused on aligning student, school, and teacher goals and employs a facilitative process that encourages teacher agency, reflection, and actions based on CSTP observational evidence and on student work that is collected and analyzed during coaching cycles. Cultivating teacher agency is an important component to the instructional feedback cycle, as evident in the ways that we invite our teachers to share what they would like to focus on and co-construct goals during the process (Aguilar, 2024). As an instructional leader, I understand how impactful coaching cycles are on the growth of our teachers to collaboratively participate in making decisions on their own learning, to understand the whys of the learning, and to meet their personal and professional needs. By committing to regular collaborative coaching cycles, school leaders engage in active listening, facilitating, observing, and analyzing in partnership with teachers, and build collaborative and trusting coaching relationships with teachers that positively impact the learning and growth of all students.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response clearly values teacher agency, sustained learning, reflection, and trusting partnerships. It uses a research reference to explain why coaching can support growth.</p><p>It does not sufficiently use this cycle's evidence to analyze equity. The teacher's request for differentiation support and the limited success on the cool down offer a concrete opportunity: whose needs remained unmet, what did the coaching reveal, and what would the leader do next? General claims that coaching directly improves outcomes overstate what this one cycle establishes.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific teacher comment and student-work finding to explain an equity issue revealed by the cycle.</li><li>Describe how your leadership response would improve access while preserving teacher agency.</li><li>Distinguish the potential benefits of coaching from outcomes demonstrated in this evidence set.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The clear leadership stance and potential benefits support 3. Specific evidence from this coaching cycle is needed for 4; a general research citation cannot replace it.</p></section></section></article><section id=\"alignment\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Read the template and handbook together</h2><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\"><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Current template</th><th>Current handbook</th><th>Meaning for the student</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CSTPs in Part A</td><td>Section I question 5 explicitly asks how CSTPs guide teacher growth, including a crosswalk if another framework is used.</td><td>The investigation directions discuss CSTPs and an alternative aligned framework in prose; the numbered list on p. 7 contains four questions.</td><td>Answer all five questions in the current template. Read the handbook prose as well as its numbered list.</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-observation evidence</td><td>Parts B and E cover context and four commentary prompts.</td><td>The handbook also requires the teacher's lesson plan and two pre-observation clips, up to six minutes each (pp. 11-14).</td><td>A written explanation does not replace the plan or the visible joint decision-making in the clips.</td></tr><tr><td>CSTP focus</td><td>Part E asks for one or two CSTP elements linked to self-assessment and students' needs.</td><td>The pre-observation directions specify one or two elements within the same domain (p. 12).</td><td>Use the handbook to complete the template's instruction; establish the focus jointly.</td></tr><tr><td>Observation and evidence</td><td>There is no separate narrative template for Parts F and G.</td><td>Conduct a classroom observation of at least 20 minutes, record its full duration, and collect focused evidence during the observation (p. 16).</td><td>Plan to observe live and preserve CSTP-focused notes and actual student work. The handbook treats the observation and later joint video review as distinct steps.</td></tr><tr><td>Post-observation feedback</td><td>Part I question 3 asks what the teacher found helpful or unhelpful and what that reveals about your coaching.</td><td>The handbook repeats this analytical task and connects teacher feedback with reflection (pp. 17-19, 23-24).</td><td>Report the feedback, interpret it, and explain the change it calls for. A thank-you statement alone is not the analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflection format</td><td>Part J is a written template with a four-page maximum.</td><td>The handbook also permits up to six minutes of verbal or ASL reflection (pp. 23-25).</td><td>Use the limit for the chosen format. The three reflection questions still apply.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note\">Page references are printed handbook pages. These are instructional observations, not official CTC corrections.</p></section><section id=\"limits\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Submission requirements</h2><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>A</th><td>School coaching practices and the volunteer teacher</td><td>Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>Classroom context, lesson, and observation</td><td>Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>C</th><td>Volunteer teacher lesson plan and work-product description</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>D</th><td>Two pre-observation meeting clips</td><td>Exactly 2 video files; up to 6 minutes each</td></tr><tr><th>E</th><td>Commentary on the pre-observation meeting</td><td>Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>F</th><td>Observation notes and CSTP-focused evidence</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>G</th><td>Student work used in the post-observation meeting</td><td>At least 1 example; 1 file; document or video; no maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>H</th><td>Post-observation meeting clips</td><td>1-3 clips; each at least 1 minute; up to 15 minutes TOTAL</td></tr><tr><th>I</th><td>Commentary on the post-observation meeting</td><td>Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>J</th><td>Reflective narrative</td><td>Up to 4 written pages OR 6 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Meeting-record templates marked optional may be replaced by an appropriate local format; the required evidence is not optional. Consult the current handbook for recording permissions, technical specifications, and submission requirements.</p></section><section id=\"source-record\" class=\"sources\"><h2>Source notes</h2><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf\">Current Cycle 3 handbook</a>. Official material copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Original response wording and document images from the supplied CTC example are retained; formatting and placement are adapted. Example references to research are preserved, but those studies and the school records were not independently verified. No assessor score report or video recording was available.</p><ul class=\"template-links\"><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S1_A_CoachingObsFeedbackPracVolTeacher.docx\">Part A template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_B_ClassroomContextLessonObservation.docx\">Part B template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_E_CommentaryPreObservationMeeting.docx\">Part E template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S3_I_CommentaryPostObservationMeeting.docx\">Part I template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\">Part J template</a></li></ul><p>Companion reviews: <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Cycle 1</a> | <a href=\"#cycle/c2\">Cycle 2</a>.</p></section><section id=\"scores\" class=\"major-section\"><span id=\"final-check\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"rubric-map\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Estimated score profile</h2><p>Each rubric is counted once across its relevant evidence. No total or passing prediction is offered because required video evidence was not reviewed.</p><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Rubric</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Basis and limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>3.1</th><td>3 / 5</td><td>The example gives detailed school procedures and a coherent plan for an experienced participant in coaching. Teacher choice is clear, but how school practices actually build trust and meet unique needs is less developed. A research citation alone does not establish the missing Level 4 analysis.</td></tr><tr><th>3.2</th><td>4 / 5, provisional</td><td>The reported questions, teacher replies, and resulting observation decisions support a provisional 4. Some prompts are leading, and the asset discussion is broad; deeper attention to equitable opportunities for all students would be needed for 5. The videos must confirm the described exchange.</td></tr><tr><th>3.3</th><td>4 / 5, provisional</td><td>The notes identify concrete routines, student responses, and missed opportunities tied to the lesson. This supports a provisional 4 alongside the commentary. The record needs more systematic analysis across whole-class, small-group, and individual needs for 5; video alignment is unverified.</td></tr><tr><th>3.4</th><td>3 / 5, provisional</td><td>The commentary reports joint examination of notes, video, and exit tickets and identifies strengths and missed opportunities. Links to prior goals and demonstrated mathematical understanding are not consistently deep enough for 4. This is a written-evidence estimate pending video review.</td></tr><tr><th>3.5</th><td>2 / 5, provisional</td><td>Teacher-generated next steps are present, but the resource and follow-up support plan remains underspecified. Sharing notes and offering future help do not clearly establish the complete Level 3 requirement. Level 2 is the cautious provisional fit until the full video and support evidence are reviewed.</td></tr><tr><th>3.6</th><td>4 / 5</td><td>The reflection examines both helpful coaching and the request for stronger resources and differentiation. It identifies missed follow-up questions and concrete changes to future meetings, supporting 4. Level 5 requires research or evidence-based practices used to explain teacher development or adult learning, not only named techniques.</td></tr><tr><th>3.7</th><td>3 / 5</td><td>The response clearly explains a leadership stance and potential benefits of coaching. It remains largely general rather than using this cycle's teacher feedback and student work to analyze equity. The research citation does not bypass the specific-evidence requirement for 4.</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section id=\"rubric-3-1\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school with the volunteer teacher, and explain the implications of how current practices impact their approach to conducting a coaching cycle?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not provide an explanation of the purpose or processes for coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school. Candidate outlines a plan for the coaching cycle with little or no details OR does not outline a plan.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate lists the teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school, providing limited details about the processes used. The purpose for conducting these processes is unclear. Candidate outlines a vague plan for the coaching cycle with minimal details about implications of each step of the coaching cycle and/or observation based on the volunteer teacher’s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly describes and analyzes the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback, discussing the purpose and intended goals of the practices for both teachers and students. Candidate clearly describes a plan for and the implications of each step of the coaching cycle and observation based on the volunteer teacher’s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes how teachers participate in the selection and purpose of the instructional feedback practices at the school to meet the unique needs of the teachers and how the practices build trust between the teachers and school administrators.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate discusses relevant evidence-based practices and/or adult learning theory to explain how school practices of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback support ongoing teacher development and benefit students’ learning and well- being.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part A: Written Narrative: Coaching, Observation, and/or Instructional Feedback Practices at the School and the Volunteer Teacher (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 10.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-2\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher to understand the learning goals, classroom context, and student assets (e.g., cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests and learning needs; jointly select with the volunteer teacher one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, including evidence to be collected; and plan for the observation?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate and the volunteer teacher do not discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs. Selection of the CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected, including student work, is made by the candidate without input from the volunteer teacher or element(s) are not selected. OR No plan is established for the coaching and/or observation process. OR In the commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate and volunteer teacher broadly discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs, including student work. Candidate minimally involves the volunteer teacher in the selection of CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected during the observation. It is not clear what aspects of instruction need to be the focus of the observation, and a plan is not specified and/or agreed to by the volunteer teacher OR candidate creates plan for observation without input from the volunteer teacher. In the commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis* of their ability to listen to and/or talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly engages the volunteer teacher in a detailed and focused pre-observation discussion about the classroom context, student assets and learning needs, and content- specific learning goals of the lesson. Candidate and the volunteer teacher jointly select the CSTP element(s) and together determine evidence to be collected during the observation, including student work. Candidate and volunteer teacher create a plan for the observation, clearly identifying the focal aspects of instruction. In the commentary, candidate provides an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies to engage in a two-way discussion with the volunteer teacher to draw out their specific areas of need in relation to learning goals, classroom context, student assets, CSTP element(s), and observation plans.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies, including re-directing as appropriate for the conversation, to deepen discussion regarding equitable opportunities for all students in the class to learn.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Note: *Analysis: What the candidate does, why they do it, and the impact their action had Sources of Evidence: • Part B: Written Narrative: Classroom Context, Lesson, and Observation (up to 4 pages) • Part C: Volunteer Teacher’s Lesson Plan, Including Student Work Product Description • Part D: 2 Video Clips of the Pre-Observation Meeting (up to 6 minutes each) • Part E: Commentary of the Pre-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 15.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-3\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate recognize, document, and analyze the volunteer teacher’s teaching practice related to the selected CSTP element(s) and learning goals of the lesson?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate provides observational evidence that is unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals of the observed lesson. OR Candidate’s observation notes are too vague to provide CSTP- related guidance. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of the volunteer teacher’s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate provides evidence of the observed lesson that is mostly unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals. Candidate documents evidence that may be CSTP related, but evidence is vague and/or too general and may not be useful to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of the volunteer teacher’s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate accurately recognizes and documents observational evidence of essential teaching practices for the selected CSTP element(s) and content-specific student learning goals. Candidate’s observation notes are detailed and clearly provide CSTP-specific guidance in a meaningful and useful way to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate clearly provides a clear analysis of the volunteer teacher’s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate’s observational evidence documented either a. missed teaching opportunities that would have supported students to meet the content-specific learning goals OR b. successful CSTP-related practices that the volunteer teacher did implement that positively impacted student learning during the lesson.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate documents how the volunteer teacher addresses whole-class, small-group, and individual student learning needs during the lesson and how they adapted or did not adapt their instruction to meet student needs as aligned to the learning goals of the lesson and the selected CSTP element(s).</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) • Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting • Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) • Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 20.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-4\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to foster a learning conversation in a post- observation meeting using CSTP-focused observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student work with the volunteer teacher regarding strengths and area(s) for growth?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate provides inaccurate or irrelevant feedback to the volunteer teacher, making limited use of the observation and other collected evidence (e.g., lesson observation video, student work). OR No strengths or area(s) of growth for the volunteer teacher are discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate provides the volunteer teacher with minimal CSTP-focused evidence or feedback, with limited discussion of findings. Candidate vaguely connects observation evidence, lesson observation video, or quality of student work to the volunteer teacher’s strengths or area(s) for growth. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate fosters a thoughtful and productive collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher, citing the CSTP-specific observation evidence, the jointly viewed lesson observation video, and student work. Jointly with the volunteer teacher, candidate respectfully and supportively identifies teacher's strengths and area(s) for growth based on gathered CSTP evidence and student work. In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate makes deep connections with the volunteer teacher among the pre- observation meeting considerations, the CSTP observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student learning as evidenced in the student work produced during the lesson.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate fosters a learning conversation that increases the volunteer teacher’s capacity for self-evaluation of instruction and assessment of student learning.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) • Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting • Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) • Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 21.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-5\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><p>Essential Question: In partnership with the volunteer teacher, how does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to co- determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support based on the CSTP-related evidence during the post-observation meeting?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate solely determines the volunteer teacher’s next steps for professional growth. OR There is little to no identification of resources and/or additional coaching support for the volunteer teacher. OR No relationship to CSTP-related evidence for next steps is discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate occasionally allows the volunteer teacher to add their insights or perspective to the discussion on the teacher’s next steps for professional growth. The conversation with the volunteer teacher lacks identification of resources and additional coaching support and/or the inclusion of CSTP- related evidence. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate participates in a collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher to co- determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support, that are clearly based on the CSTP- related evidence (observation feedback, lesson observation video, and/or student work). In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate and the volunteer teacher identify professional growth with specific attention to addressing individual students’ learning needs for CSTP-related professional learning.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides specific evidenced-based resources to support the volunteer teacher’s professional growth and engages the teacher in developing detailed next steps for professional learning and growth.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) • Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting • Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) • Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 22.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-6\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate analyze their capacity to conduct a CSTP-focused coaching and observation process, based on their experience in this coaching cycle and feedback from the volunteer teacher, and refer to evidence to demonstrate their ability to facilitate and maintain a coaching partnership?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not provide an analysis of their capacity to support teacher development through CSTP-focused coaching and/or observation processes. OR Candidate does not use the volunteer teacher’s feedback regarding their coaching skills. OR Candidate does not reflect on or refer to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate minimally describes strengths and/or areas for growth in CSTP-focused coaching and observation. Candidate’s analysis provides cursory use of the volunteer teacher’s feedback. Candidate minimally reflects upon and/or vaguely refers to evidence of how they facilitated and/or maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate analyzes CSTP- focused coaching and observation strengths and clearly identifies areas for growth. Candidate’s analysis clearly draws on the volunteer teacher’s feedback and other learning gained throughout the cycle. Candidate clearly reflects upon and refers to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides an extensive analysis of the volunteer teacher’s feedback to them regarding their coaching and observation abilities in planning and conducting the coaching cycle and discusses how they would change their future approach to coaching based on that feedback.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how evidence-based practices or research related to coaching skills support teacher development and/or adult learning.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 24.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-7\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, reflect on their role as an instructional leader acting as an instructional coach and reflect on the potential impact of coaching?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not demonstrate an awareness of the connection between equitable leadership and instructional coaching. OR Candidate does not reflect upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely reflects on and describes a minimal understanding of how instructional coaching and/or equitable leadership inform a continuous improvement mindset. Candidate minimally reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, clearly reflects on their role as a school leader acting as an instructional coach. Candidate clearly reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate cites specific evidence from the cycle, drawing on the volunteer teacher’s feedback and/or other collected evidence, to support their reflection and analysis of their capacity to be an equitable leader and an instructional coach.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites evidence-based practices or research that support instructional coaching and observation as a viable and equitable strategy to support teacher growth and benefit students.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 25.</p></section></div>","search":[{"title":"Part A / I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices","url":"#cycle/c3/A1","html":"<span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tOutline how often during the year teachers are coached, observed, or provided instructional feedback and what the intended outcomes are for the school, teachers, and students. After reviewing current practices at this school, provide an analysis of the effectiveness of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain frequency, procedures, and intended outcomes for the school, teachers, and students, then evaluate effectiveness using available evidence. Distinguish evaluation, walkthroughs, and voluntary instructional coaching; they may serve different purposes and create different relationships.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"4\"><p>[Certificated teachers are required to participate in a formal yearly evaluation process that is conducted with the site administrator which involves an initial pre-observation conference, two observations, and post-observation conferences. During the initial conference, the teacher consults with the site administrator and chooses one or two California Standards for the Teaching Profession (CSTP) elements that they would like to focus on in their teaching practice, using district-provided materials that detail teaching standards. The selected CSTPs are also a focusing tool that administrators use during the two observations that are conducted during the school year, so that they can give specific instructional feedback. The formal observations are conducted during the fall semester and during the spring semester, and the post-observation conferences are conducted within 5 days of the observations. During the post-observation conferences, teachers receive instructional feedback about their employment of teaching practices during the observations, with a focus on the CSTPs that were selected in the beginning of the evaluation process. The intended outcomes of this formal evaluation process are: (1) to ensure that the school maintains a high-quality certificated teaching staff that are equipped to advance student achievement, and (2) to inform teachers of their teaching practices and facilitate their ongoing growth as educators. There are also informal observations, or walkthroughs, that are conducted a few times a year with site administration, and there does not appear to be any pre- or post-observation processes associated with these types of walkthroughs. However, the existing evaluation coaching cycles and informal observation practices have not appeared to make an impact on student achievement as measured by the 3rd, 4th, and 5th grade SBAC ELA and Math data from the last few years. For example, the ELA proficiency for grades 3-5 has steadily declined; from 2023 to 2025, the percentage of students who met proficiency declined from 34% to 18%. The Math proficiency for grades 3-5 declined from 2023 to 2025, from 24% of 3rd, 4th, and 5th graders meeting proficiency to 17% of 3rd, 4th, and 5th graders meeting proficiency. Furthermore, this is the first year that instructional coaching is launching at the district, and this elementary school is one of four school sites that has received a full-time instructional coach for this current school year. For coaching cycles to be successful, the instructional coach and the teacher must develop a trusting and collaborative working relationship. Teachers can self-select into coaching cycles that consist of collaborative meetings in which the teacher and the coach establish a collaborative focus based on student strengths and needs. The cycle begins with the teacher and coach jointly setting goals with the CSTPs as a guide, co-planning for instruction with resources, and jointly selecting what types of student data to collect for later analysis. The teacher and instructional coach also decide whether the lesson will be taught solely by the teacher and the instructional coach will observe, or if the instructional coach will model and the teacher observes, or if they will co-teach the lesson together and note their observations during the post-observation meeting. After the lesson and observation, the teacher and instructional coach will meet to analyze student data and observation data, reflect and adjust, celebrate impact, and plan next steps. Given that the instructional coaching role and its processes are early in implementation at this school site, a data analysis later next year with SBAC ELA and Math student achievement data and various sources of additional quantitative and qualitative data will be necessary to use when measuring the effectiveness of the instructional coaching implementation. The formal evaluation process with the site administrator and the instructional coaching cycle with the instructional coach share practices in the pre-observation, observation, and post-observation activities that address principles of adult learning theory. Aguilar (2024) noted several principles of adult learning theory, which emphasize the importance of trusting relationships, building upon adult’s previous learning experiences and their agency in learning, and creating opportunities for adults to apply their knowledge and skills in teaching so that they can receive relevant feedback. During preobservation meetings, teachers exercise their agency in learning as they select the CSTPs and the content area that they would like to practice and receive feedback on and apply their knowledge and skills to design lessons that incorporate strategies that address their students’ specific strengths and needs. The observations are guided by the goals and observational data that are collaboratively agreed upon by the teacher and the observer. In the post-observation meetings with the site administrator, teachers receive timely feedback on their instructional practice which creates opportunities to sustain new learning and transfer it into their daily practice. In the post-observation meetings with the instructional coach, teachers engage in discussion that centers around student needs and frames the “why” of learning in relation to student needs and problem-solving, which attends to adult learning principles that acknowledge how adults have a problem-centered orientation to learning.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response gives concrete timing for formal observations and feedback, describes a newly introduced coaching role, and distinguishes teacher choice in coaching from formal evaluation. This is a substantial account of local practices.</p><p>Declining schoolwide proficiency does not establish that observation or evaluation caused the decline or had no benefit. Those rates may involve changing cohorts and many influences. The new coaching model also cannot be judged by results collected before its implementation. Teacher participation, changes in practice, and student work would provide more direct evidence.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate established evaluation practices from the new coaching model and identify their timelines.</li><li>Describe what the achievement trend establishes and what it cannot establish about coaching effectiveness.</li><li>Use available teacher feedback, observation records, and changes in instruction to evaluate the process more directly.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The detailed account of practices, goals, and limitations supports 3. A stronger explanation of how teacher-specific practices build trust is needed before the adult-learning discussion can support a higher level.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.2 / Explaining processes and documents","url":"#cycle/c3/A2","html":"<span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Explaining processes and documents</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the processes, forms, and/or documents that are used to guide pre-observation meetings, collect observation data, and focus post-observation meetings (e.g., teacher response forms, observation tools, student work products, next steps for professional growth). If the school does not conduct teacher observations and uses a different approach to provide instructional feedback to teachers, provide a detailed description of the process(es) and forms used.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how documents are used before, during, and after observation. Identify what each tool records, how it connects to the CSTP focus, and how teachers use the resulting evidence. Naming a form is not a description of the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"6\"><p>[During the pre-observation meeting for the formal yearly evaluation process, the teacher and the administrator complete a district-provided digital form that includes the selected CSTP element/s, the procedures of the observation, and the context of the observation. The site administrator uses the district-provided digital form to collect and record observation data, which is also included in the post-observation conference to discuss the observation and provide instructional feedback.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies a district digital form that follows the observation cycle and includes the selected CSTP elements and context. That gives the process a coherent structure.</p><p>It says little about the form's evidence fields, teacher self-assessment, student work, or the way post-observation decisions are documented. It also does not clearly explain whether informal coaching uses the same tools as formal evaluation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe the relevant fields and who completes or reviews them at each stage.</li><li>Explain how observational evidence is separated from interpretation or evaluation.</li><li>Show how the tool connects CSTP evidence, student learning, feedback, and next professional steps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> This brief account supports only part of the combined 3. Describe how the documents actually focus evidence and learning; naming the digital form is not enough to strengthen the analysis.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.3 / Teacher input and ownership","url":"#cycle/c3/A3","html":"<span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.3 / Teacher input and ownership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tExplain the level of input teachers have on the focus or purpose of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which decisions teachers influence, how they exercise that choice, and any limits on it. Distinguish selecting from options offered by an administrator from genuinely shaping the purpose, evidence, and goals of coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"8\"><p>[During the formal evaluation process, teachers select one or two California Standards for the </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"9\"><p>Teaching Profession (CSTP) elements that they would like to focus on so that they can receive focused instructional feedback after the observations. After selecting the CSTPs, teachers choose a content area to design and implement a lesson in and select the student work products that will be collected during the observation. For coaching cycles with the instructional coach, teachers collaborate with the coach and jointly set the goals for the cycle, co-plan instruction, and select the focus of the instructional feedback with the CSTPs as a tool to guide this selection.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response identifies meaningful choices: CSTP elements, content area, student products, and jointly established goals. These details help explain how teachers participate.</p><p>For the Level 4 extension, explain how these opportunities respond to teachers' different needs and build trust with administrators. Describing a choice mechanism is not the same as showing its effect on the professional relationship.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a concrete example of teacher input changing the observation or feedback focus.</li><li>Explain how confidentiality, role clarity, and teacher choice operate in the local process.</li><li>Connect these practices to trust and teacher-specific needs using available evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> Teacher choice is clear, but its relationship to unique needs and trust remains underdeveloped. That is the main distinction between the combined Level 3 estimate and a confident 4.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle","url":"#cycle/c3/A4","html":"<span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tExplain the implications for this cycle based on the analysis of the current role of coaching practices (at the school) and the background and experience of the volunteer teacher.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you will retain, adapt, or clarify because of the school's practices and this teacher's experience. The task asks for implications, not merely a statement that the teacher already knows the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"11\"><p>[This coaching cycle shares similar pre-observation, observation, and post-observation components to previous coaching cycles that the volunteer teacher has participated in. Since the volunteer teacher also has experience with identifying CSTP elements that will guide the focus of the observations and instructional feedback processes, they will continue to engage with the selection and application of these elements during this coaching cycle.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response recognizes that the volunteer has experience with the three-stage process and CSTP selection. That is relevant background.</p><p>It does not explain what this familiarity changes in the candidate's approach. A teacher who knows the formal process may still need assurance about a non-evaluative partnership or more individualized support with student-work analysis.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which familiar routines can be used and which features of this cycle need clarification.</li><li>Connect a known teacher experience or need to a specific coaching decision.</li><li>Explain how you will check that the teacher understands and agrees to the purpose and evidence collection.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> This response is thin on implications; the fuller plan in II.2 helps support the combined 3. Explain what will change because of this teacher's history and context.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development","url":"#cycle/c3/A5","html":"<span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tHow do the CSTPs guide or play a role in teacher growth and development at the school? (If the school uses another set of standards, provide a crosswalk to the CSTPs for the element[s] you have identified.)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the standards guide ongoing growth: identifying a need, focusing evidence, interpreting practice, and deciding next steps. If a different local framework is used, provide the required crosswalk for the selected elements.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"13\"><p>[The CSTPs play a role in teacher growth and development because teachers select CSTP elements during the formal yearly evaluation process with the site administrator and during informal coaching cycles with the instructional coach.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The answer correctly states that CSTP elements are selected for evaluation and coaching. It is too brief to show how they guide development beyond that selection.</p><p>A standard becomes useful when it helps a teacher and coach decide what to notice and how to interpret it. The later focus on routines and access to mathematical learning can illustrate that function, but should not be substituted for an account of the school's actual practice.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain how the local process uses an element to focus observation evidence and feedback.</li><li>Describe how the same focus informs a professional learning decision.</li><li>Provide a crosswalk only if a different framework is actually used; do not invent one.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> This answer identifies CSTP use but gives little analysis. The combined 3 relies on the more detailed procedures and coaching plan elsewhere in Part A.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher","url":"#cycle/c3/A6","html":"<span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tProvide information about the volunteer teacher’s:</p><p>a.\tprofessional experience as a teacher (including years of service, school site[s], grades and content taught, languages)</p><p>b.\tcurrent assignment (grade level[s], content area, course titles)</p><p>c.\tpast experiences with coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p><p>d.\tknowledge and use of the CSTP to guide their growth and development as a teacher at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four subparts: professional experience and languages, current assignment, previous coaching experiences, and knowledge and use of the CSTPs. Explain the background accurately enough to support the individualized plan in the next question.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Professional experience</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"17\"><p>[The volunteer teacher has completed 2 years of service as a full-time certificated teacher, and this year will be her 3rd year serving as a classroom teacher at this current TK-5 elementary school. Her first year of certificated teaching was in another district in the XXX area, where she taught a 4th and 5th grade combination class at an elementary school. Her second year of certificated teaching was completed at the current elementary school that she teaches at, and she taught 5th grade last year. The volunteer teacher is fluent in the English language, and she also has speaking and listening fluency in the Cantonese language.] </p></div><h4>Current assignment</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"19\"><p>[The volunteer teacher is currently teaching a 5th grade multiple subject class.] </p></div><h4>Previous coaching</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"21\"><p>[The volunteer teacher has had previous experiences with coaching and instructional feedback in previous teaching positions. During her student teaching, she participated in a mentoring group with 3 other student teachers and a mentoring experienced educator, where they met weekly to plan lessons and conduct pre-observation planning. The student teachers in the group took turns observing each other with the mentor and conducted post-observation meetings to debrief and receive instructional feedback. The volunteer teacher also participated in two years of the beginning teacher induction program; the first year of induction was completed in X and the second year of induction was completed in this current school district. The volunteer teacher participated in a district science coaching program with her grade level team; the coaching experience consisted of pre-observation and lesson planning meetings, observations, and postobservation reflections.] </p></div><h4>Knowledge and use of the CSTPs</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"23\"><p>[The volunteer teacher has applied the CSTPs to guide her growth and development during her student teaching experiences and during the first two years that she taught as a certificated teacher, both at the previous elementary school site in X and in the current school site she is teaching at. The volunteer teacher will also continue to apply the CSTPs to guide her growth and professional development as a teacher this year during the formal yearly evaluation process with the site administrator.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response describes the teacher's early career, fifth-grade assignment, Cantonese speaking and listening fluency, induction, peer observation, and science coaching. These details provide a useful foundation.</p><p>The chronology is slightly confusing: being in a third year of teaching is not necessarily a third year at the same site when the first year was elsewhere. Experience with a coaching program also does not tell the reader which aspects the teacher found useful or difficult.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Clarify total teaching experience separately from years at the current school.</li><li>Include the teacher's account of what prior coaching helped with and what remained a need.</li><li>Use the background to inform the next answer rather than assuming experience eliminates the need for orientation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The teacher background supports the individualized-plan requirement within the overall 3. Clarify the chronology and show how reported experiences change the plan.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle","url":"#cycle/c3/A7","html":"<span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how the information you gathered about the school context and the volunteer teacher’s experiences informs your plans for conducting a coaching cycle, including how you will:</p><p>a.\torient the volunteer teacher to participate in your coaching process</p><p>b.\tparticipate in a pre-observation meeting</p><p>c.\tprepare for the observation and collection of evidence (including student work)</p><p>d.\tjointly conduct (with the volunteer teacher) the post-observation meeting</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the school and teacher information shapes four connected steps: orientation, pre-observation meeting, observation and evidence collection, and the joint post-observation meeting. State the purpose of each step and the decisions you will make together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Orientation</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"26\"><p>[I will give an overview of the coaching process during our initial conversation about her participation in this coaching cycle, where we will co-determine a date and time for our preobservation meeting. During this initial meeting, I will review the three major components and activities of the coaching cycle: (1) Pre-observation meeting; (2) Observation; and (3) Postobservation. Since my volunteer teacher has had a few years of experience with coaching cycles and has participated in past coaching processes, I will ask her facilitative and clarifying questions to connect her past experiences with coaching to the upcoming coaching cycle that we will participate in together. I will also make sure that the volunteer teacher understands the time commitments and video logistics associated with each component; for example, I will make sure that the volunteer teacher understands and agrees to the length of the pre-observation meeting (which will be about 30 minutes) and to the videos that we will record of the preobservation meeting.] </p></div><h4>Pre-observation meeting</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"28\"><p>[During the pre-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher and I will meet during a time that works best for us to discuss the instructional components to the lesson that I will be observing and to identify students’ assets and needs. Moreover, we will jointly select one or two CSTP elements and student work products that will be the focus for the data collected during the observation and this data will be shared during the post-observation meeting. I will use facilitative and clarifying prompts during our conversation to guide us towards these topics. To summarize our conversation, I will review the CSTP elements and student work products that we jointly selected to gather evidence during the observation. I will also review the coaching and observation process that involves: (1) obtaining the necessary permissions to video record the lesson observation with the volunteer teacher and her students, (2) confirming the length of the lesson observation, (3) confirming a date and time to meet for the post-observation conference and the details on how to prepare for the conference in which I will share a secure link in a shared digital folder for the volunteer teacher to access the lesson observation video and view it before our conference, and (4) detailing the components of the post-observation conference, which will include the volunteer teacher and I watching the video of the lesson observation together and having a conversation about the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment of their instruction and assessment implementation during the lesson, effectiveness of their teaching practices during the lesson, jointly determining next steps, resources, and professional development for the volunteer teacher to engage in. I will also obtain feedback from the volunteer teacher about what was helpful or not helpful during the coaching process to help me determine my own strengths and areas of growth.] </p></div><h4>Observation and evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"30\"><p>[To prepare for the observation and collection of evidence during the observation, the volunteer teacher and I will confirm the date, time, and duration of the observation. We will review the observation processes, which include conducting and video recording the classroom observation of the volunteer teacher’s lesson with her 5th grade students. We will ensure that all the necessary protocols and permissions are in place for those who will be in the video recording. I will develop an observation tool to collect data during the lesson.] </p></div><h4>Post-observation meeting</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"32\"><p>[During the post-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher and I will watch the video recording of the lesson and engage in a thoughtful and productive conversation to jointly identify teacher strengths and needs based on CSTP-specific observation evidence, the video, and student work. During the video, we will analyze the effectiveness of the teaching practices employed by the volunteer teacher during the lesson. We will also jointly analyze student work and other data related to the lesson to co-determine next steps for the volunteer teacher’s professional growth. I will use facilitative, clarifying, and reflective questions to guide the collaborative conversation and to jointly identify instructional strengths, areas of growth, resources, and additional coaching support based on the CSTP-related observation data and feedback, student work, and lesson video. Furthermore, I will ask the volunteer teacher to share feedback on my coaching skills by asking her what was helpful or not helpful during the coaching and observation cycle; this feedback will help me reflect on my own strengths and areas for growth regarding my coaching.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The plan is detailed about logistics, permissions, CSTP selection, video access, student work, questioning, and teacher feedback. It keeps the post-observation discussion collaborative.</p><p>Much of the detail would fit any teacher. Strengthen the link to this teacher's experience and anticipated needs, and specify how the observation tool will capture learning rather than only visible behavior. Plan to observe instruction as it occurs, collect focused notes, and record the observation for later joint review; those are distinct activities in the handbook's sequence.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each major coaching choice to the teacher's background or a stated need.</li><li>Jointly define what evidence will address both the CSTP focus and lesson learning goals, including student work.</li><li>Confirm live observation, duration, recording permissions, secure handling, and shared review of the evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The four-stage plan is detailed enough to support 3. Stronger teacher-specific connections and an explanation of trust-building would help establish the Level 4 extension.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.3 / Anticipating challenges","url":"#cycle/c3/A8","html":"<span id=\"a-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Anticipating challenges</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any challenges you may face in conducting a coaching cycle with the volunteer teacher and provide strategies for addressing these challenges.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible challenges for this teacher and context, explain why each may occur, and give a practical response. Tie the strategy to the challenge rather than listing generic good coaching practices.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"34\"><p>[During the pre-observation part of the coaching cycle, I may encounter challenges with establishing clear, specific goals for the observation. To address this challenge, I will use plan facilitative and clarifying questions to focus our collaborative conversation on identifying: (1) specific, measurable learning goals, strengths and assets of students, and evidence/student work samples (2) CSTPs that will guide the collection of observational data and student work products. During the observation, I may face challenges with collecting specific observational data related to the CSTPs and student work samples. To address this challenge, I will prepare an observational tool that helps me collect evidence that is related and focused on the one or two CSTP elements that the volunteer teacher and I agreed upon. In the post-observation meeting, it may be challenging to foster a learning conversation that includes multiple pieces of evidence. To mitigate this challenge, I will plan facilitative questions that helps me foster a collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher, and I will also prepare notes that cite CSTP-specific observation evidence, the observation video, and student work that highlights teacher strengths and areas for growth.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response anticipates vague goals, unfocused evidence collection, and difficulty discussing multiple sources. Preparing questions and a focused observation tool are sensible responses.</p><p>The plan needs a clearer link to the actual teacher and lesson. It should also explain what the coach will do if evidence complicates the selected focus or if the teacher interprets the same evidence differently.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Ground each anticipated challenge in the school context or teacher's reported experience.</li><li>Specify an observable goal and the evidence fields that will keep the observation focused.</li><li>Plan how to invite disagreement and revise interpretations jointly without taking ownership away from the teacher.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.1: 3 / 5.</strong> The planned responses to focused challenges support the combined 3. Their value would be clearer if linked to this teacher's actual needs rather than a general coaching process.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / 1 / Classroom context and learning goals","url":"#cycle/c3/B","html":"<span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / 1 / Classroom context and learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tClassroom context, including:</p><p>a.\ttopic of lesson, learning and instructional goals, students’ prior knowledge, and what was taught in the prior lesson</p><p>b.\tclassroom setting (face-to-face, online, or hybrid) and organization for learning (whole class, small groups, individual practice, hybrid)</p><p>c.\tdescription of students served, including their assets and learning needs (e.g., grade level, demographics, student assets [cultural and/or linguistic] and interests, learning supports and adaptations)</p><p>d.\tmaterials and/or resources used during the lesson by the teacher or students to enhance or extend learning</p><p>e.\tother relevant classroom context information (e.g., time of day for the lesson, time of year, support staff that are in the room during the lesson, materials used, safety issues, bell schedule, parent support)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all five components: the lesson and prior learning; the setting and organization; students' assets and learning needs; materials; and other relevant context. Explain information that affects instruction and observation, not just demographic totals.</p><p>Distinguish class enrollment from attendance in the observed lesson and distinguish student engagement from achievement of the mathematical goal.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>What each form of evidence contributes</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it establishes</th><th scope=\"col\">Its limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Lesson plan</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Intended learning, instruction, supports, and student work.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">It does not establish what happened during instruction.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and lesson recording</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Specific teaching actions and student responses.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Events require interpretation; participation alone does not establish learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">What particular learners demonstrated on the task.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">A few examples do not establish a whole-class pattern or the cause of a result.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Meeting clips and commentary</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">The coaching interaction and an explanation of leadership decisions.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Commentary cannot establish an interaction absent from the submitted evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Lesson and prior learning</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"43\"><p>[Lesson 11: Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1, is a lesson in the 5th grade Math Unit 2: Fractions as Quotients and Fraction Multiplication from the district-adopted math curriculum. The purpose of the lesson is for students to find the area of a rectangle when one of the side lengths is a fraction greater than 1. The lesson’s instructional goals are: (1) Students describe (orally and in writing) how different equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a given rectangle with fractional sides greater than 1 and (2) Explain (orally) strategies used to determine the area of a rectangle with fractional side lengths and represent the area with a multiplication expression. Before this lesson, students practiced with visual models to multiply fractions by whole numbers and found the area of rectangles when one of the side lengths was a fraction less than 1. They also had practice using these visual models to explain (orally and in writing) their reasoning. For example, they used diagrams of rectangles to explain why ⅔ x 4 = (2x4) x ⅓. Using area diagrams, students apply this prior knowledge, reasoning, and strategies to this lesson to find the area of a rectangle with a fractional side greater than 1. These lessons address the following California mathematics standards in the Numbers and Operations </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"44\"><p>Fractions domains.] </p></div><h4>Setting</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"46\"><p>[This whole-group lesson was taught in a self-contained classroom for in-person learning. There were a total of 17 students present in the classroom, and students were sitting at their desks. The desks were arranged in pairs, with students facing the digital display that showed digital contents of the lesson and the items displayed under the document camera.] </p></div><h4>Students and learning needs</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"48\"><p>[The volunteer teacher’s fifth-grade class consists of 22 total students, 13 males and 9 females this year. Regarding student assets, the volunteer teacher described how her students are very friendly and they enjoy participating in class and in school events. 7 students in the classroom have individualized learning plans (IEPs) and receive additional push-in and pull-out support from specialized staff. 14 students are identified as multilingual, or English learners. According to the volunteer teacher, the students enjoy participating in class activities and discussions. All </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"53\"><p>22 students come from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds: 19 Hispanic/Latine students, 1 multiracial student, 1 Pacific Islander student, and 1 Filipino student.] </p></div><h4>Materials</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"55\"><p>[During the lesson, the teacher used a large-screen television (digital display) connected to the computer and to the document camera to present digital learning materials for all parts of the lesson.] </p></div><h4>Other relevant context</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"57\"><p>[The lesson was taught in the morning, beginning at 9:30am and concluding at 10:35am, in the beginning of October during the fall semester. There were 2 paraprofessional staff members in the room who were supporting 3 students with their Individualized Education Plan accommodations and supports. Each student had their own consumable math workbook that accompanied the district-approved math curriculum and had individual access to all material components of the math lesson. There were no safety issues, no major disruptions, and no major changes to the school’s bell schedule that day (it was a minimum day to accommodate parent-teacher conferences).] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response clearly identifies the fractional-area lesson, oral and written goals, prior work with fractions, classroom arrangement, resources, and adult support. It distinguishes 22 enrolled students from 17 present.</p><p>The description of assets is largely that students are friendly and enjoy participation. It needs more specific evidence of the knowledge, languages, strategies, or experiences the lesson can draw on. The overlap among the 14 multilingual or English learner students and seven students with IEPs is not explained; those categories should not be added together.</p><p>Later commentary focuses heavily on routines. The context should help the reader understand how those routines support mathematical explanation for these particular learners, not merely a quiet room.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify specific assets and learning needs relevant to explaining fractional area, using teacher or student evidence.</li><li>Explain the planned language supports, adaptations, and adult roles without inferring needs from demographic categories.</li><li>Maintain the distinction between 22 enrolled, 17 present, and the number of work samples actually analyzed.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> This context supports the provisional 4 when combined with the reported pre-observation dialogue. The narrative alone cannot demonstrate two-way coaching; more specific assets and supports would deepen the conversation.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / Teacher lesson plan and student-work description","url":"#cycle/c3/C","html":"<span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Teacher lesson plan and student-work description</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit a copy of the volunteer teacher’s lesson plan, including the student work product description.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 11; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Provide the volunteer teacher's actual plan and a description of the student work that will show learning. Explain how the work relates to the lesson goals. An adopted curriculum plan establishes intended instruction, not proof of what occurred.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"61\"><p>Volunteer Teacher Lesson Plan: Unit 2 Lesson 11-Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1 The volunteer teacher’s lesson plan is from the adopted math curriculum, and the volunteer teacher utilized the attached lesson plan, Unit 2, Lesson 11: Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1, to teach the lesson plan during their lesson observation. The lesson plan is represented in the images on pages 2-9 of this document. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"63\"><p>Description of Student Work Sample: The student work sample is the Cool Down from Unit 2 </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"64\"><p>Lesson 11: Fractional Side Lengths Greater Than 1, from the adopted 5th grade math curriculum. This particular cool down addresses 5th grade California Common Core standard 5.NF.B.4.b, and is a formative assessment given at the end of a math lesson. The student work sample Find the Area consists of two items: (1) “Write a multiplication expression to represent the area of the shaded region” and (2) “What is the area of the shaded region?” The cool down assessment is also shown on page 9 of this document. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"66\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-66-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 1</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"67\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-67-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"68\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-68-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 3</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"69\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-69-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 4</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"70\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-70-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 5</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"71\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-71-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 6</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"72\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-72-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 7</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"73\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-73-0.jpg\" alt=\"Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Teacher lesson plan and work-product description, source image 8</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The example includes the adopted fractional-side-length lesson and identifies a two-item cool down asking for a multiplication expression and area. The materials make the intended content visible.</p><p>The oral explanation goal is not fully represented by a written answer alone. The pre-observation discussion should establish what evidence of reasoning will be collected, how the teacher's actual adaptations will be documented, and how the work relates to the chosen CSTP focus.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the actual lesson adaptations agreed with the teacher and distinguish them from the published plan.</li><li>Specify what successful mathematical reasoning would look or sound like.</li><li>Connect the planned work and observation evidence to both the learning goals and the selected CSTP element.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The plan supports the provisional 4 across Parts B-E, but it is not separately scored. The clips must demonstrate how the teacher and coach jointly used it to decide the focus and evidence.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / Pre-observation meeting video clips","url":"#cycle/c3/D","html":"<span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / Pre-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Select and submit 2 video clips, each up to 6 minutes, that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Teacher Needs, Lesson Plan Review, and CSTP Selection (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates: • Your coaching, including asking prompting and clarifying questions, that leads to the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment of instructional needs and challenges based on the lesson plan they intend to teach and the student work product that will be the outcome of the lesson; student assets and needs; and classroom context</p><p>Supporting the teacher to then identify one or two elements of one of the six CSTP domains for which they would like observation data collected to guide the post-observation meeting • Video Clip 2: Observation Process and CSTP Data (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates:  Your review of the coaching and observation process with the teacher • Your review of the CSTP–element-focused data you will collect during the observation to share and discuss in the post-observation meeting, in addition to the student work products and other relevant data shared by the teacher</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 12 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit two clips, up to six minutes each, showing the required pre-observation discussion. The first concerns teacher needs, the lesson, and CSTP selection; the second concerns the observation process and evidence to collect. Joint decisions must be visible in the actual exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><p class=\"scope-note\">The source commentary refers to video clips, but those recordings were not available for this review. No example video or invented transcript is substituted.</p></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The written example supplies a detailed account of questions, replies, and selected evidence, but the recordings were not available for this review. The account alone cannot establish that both clips contain every required component or that the interaction was genuinely two-way.</p><p>Use the commentary critique below to examine reasoning, while reserving judgment about demonstrated practice until the clips can be watched.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify the two clips against the distinct functions and six-minute limits in the current handbook.</li><li>Ensure the teacher's input and the jointly agreed focus and evidence are visible and audible.</li><li>Check every commentary timestamp against the submitted clips and keep the interaction authentic.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: Not independently assessable without the video.</strong> The written commentary is consistent with a provisional 4 elsewhere in this review. The actual dialogue, clip alignment, and joint decisions remain unverified without the recordings.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 1 / Teacher self-assessment","url":"#cycle/c3/E1","html":"<span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 1 / Teacher self-assessment</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Self-Assessment: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher determined their instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element(s).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the teacher identified instructional needs and challenges and how your listening and questioning helped connect them to the CSTP focus. Analyze the teacher's thinking and the effect of your coaching, not only the questions you planned to ask.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"79\"><p>[To collaboratively determine the volunteer teacher’s instructional needs and challenges based on a CSTP element, I wanted to begin with facilitative questions that would help the volunteer teacher identify the instructional goals of the lesson. For example, from minute 0:00 to minute 0:42 in video clip 1, I ask facilitative questions such as “What are the main learning goals of the lesson?” and use paraphrasing language such as, “So it sounds like there are speaking and writing domains in your learning goals?”, that aimed to facilitate a 2-way conversation in which the volunteer teacher identified the learning goals. When the volunteer teacher identified how the math learning goals included speaking, listening, and writing instructional goals, I recognized how these various goals would relate to upcoming topics in our collaborative conversation, such as identifying students’ instructional needs and challenges that also related to the CSTP element that would be the focus of our data collection. To get to these topics, I wanted to ask facilitative questions that would encourage the volunteer teacher to identify her students’ assets and needs and also identify aspects of the classroom context. For example, from minute 3:39 to minute 4:20 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher identifies a particular student cultural asset when she says, “They love participating…” and also identifies students’ instructional needs concerning student engagement when she says, “I think that something that needs support in this classroom would be for them to stay engaged with the material because it is quite challenging---fractions is not super easy.” Here, I recognized that supporting student engagement would connect with the CSTP element that we collaboratively identified later in the conversation, as this is related to the pedagogical strategies that a teacher may implement to increase student engagement and support students with their learning when the content of the lesson may be challenging. This means that as a coach, I was able to facilitate the conversation towards co-determining a CSTP element to focus on because I knew that I had to practice active and engaged listening early in the conversation to identify the specific aspects of the learning goals and instructional needs and challenges that would later connect to a collaboration process with the volunteer teacher to create a plan for the CSTP-element-focused observation.  Looking back at this moment in our pre-observation conversation, I see how I was able to make these connections because I used facilitative questions to draw out specific areas of need in regards to learning goals, student assets and needs, and the learning context, and I listened carefully to the volunteer teacher as she shared the math learning goals, students’ assets and needs, and details about the classroom context.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"80\"><p>I also recognized how the classroom context would be related to determining instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element that we identified for focused data collection during the observation, so I used facilitative questions to invite the teacher to share more about the classroom context. For example, from minute 4:21 to minute 4:28 in video clip 1, I ask the volunteer teacher to describe the learning environment and her students’ classroom culture, and at minute 4:30 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher identifies a positive behavioral goals that she is supporting the class with for the school year, such as “raising their hands” and “listening to one person at a time.” By identifying these instructional needs and challenges during this part of the conversation, we established a rationale for the CSTP element that we collaboratively identified later in the meeting. At minute 4:38 in video clip 1, I use facilitative questions to invite the volunteer teacher to choose a CSTP element that she is interested in receiving feedback on. Later in the conversation, I use facilitative questions and paraphrasing language to confirm the CSTP-element-focused data that would be collected during the lesson, such as in minute 5:04 in video clip 1, I ask, “Which element or elements align with areas that you’re interested in getting feedback on during this lesson?” and at minute 5:12 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “Since I am a newer teacher I do want to keep working on classroom management and I think 2.6 here makes sense because I was talking about wanting to support positive behavior, such as making sure that one person is speaking at a time.” Here, the volunteer teacher identified CSTP element 2.6 which related to the previous topics in our pre-observation meeting that identified instructional needs and challenges. Looking back, I see how my coaching decisions to use facilitative questions, prompts, and paraphrasing language invited the volunteer teacher to identify which CSTP element would relate to implementing strategies that supported all of her students with positive behaviors so that they could access the learning goals and outcomes.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage reports questions about goals, student needs, classroom culture, and the teacher's interest in routines. It explains how active listening helped the coach connect those topics to CSTP selection.</p><p>The evidence would be easier to follow with fewer repeated statements of intention and more attention to what the teacher's response changed. There is also an internal clip-reference inconsistency: the assets discussion is attributed to clip 2 here but to clip 1 in the lesson-review passage. The actual recordings must resolve it.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct clip references by checking the recordings, rather than inferring the intended timestamp.</li><li>Identify the teacher's specific self-assessed need and show how it influenced the jointly selected focus.</li><li>Explain the impact of a facilitative question using the teacher's resulting reasoning, not merely agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported questions draw out specific needs and support a provisional 4. Resolve the clip-reference inconsistency and use the actual teacher response to demonstrate impact.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 2 / Reviewing the lesson together","url":"#cycle/c3/E2","html":"<span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 2 / Reviewing the lesson together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tLesson Plan Review: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher reviewed the lesson plan. Think about:</p><p>a.\tstudent work product,</p><p>b.\tclassroom context, and</p><p>c.\tstudents’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Connect the student work, classroom context, and student assets and needs to the lesson review. Show how your questions helped the teacher clarify what students should learn and how the planned evidence will reveal it.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Student work and success evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"83\"><p>[To determine the student work product or the success criteria that would provide evidence that the students met the learning and instructional goals of the lesson, it was important to first collaboratively identify the main learning goals. At minute 0:00-0:10 in video clip 1, I ask, “What are the main learning goals for your lesson and what do you hope students will be able to do at the end?” At minute 0:10 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responded and detailed two learning goals: “There will be two learning goals. The first one will be describing orally and in writing how different equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a given rectangle with fractional side lengths greater than 1. And the second one is explain orally strategies used to determine the area of a rectangle with fraction side lengths and represent the area with a multiplication expression.” At minute 0:37 in video clip 1, I clarify, “So it sounds like there are speaking and writing domains in your learning goals?” At minute 0:42 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes.” At minute 0:48 in video clip 1, I ask, “How do these particular goals in this lesson (it says lesson 11, right), how do they connect with what students have already learned going into this lesson?” At minute 0:58 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responds, “So in previous lessons, students have multiplied fractions by whole numbers and they have found the area of rectangles with one of the side lengths when the fraction was less than 1.” At minute 1:12 in video clip 1, I ask, “How will you know if the students met the learning goals by the end of the lesson? What are some student products we’re hoping that would show that they met the learning goal?” At minute 1:23 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “I hope to see that they are talking with each other using certain sentence frames to explain orally, and also for them to be able to write the multiplication expression to describe certain diagrams of rectangles.” I recognized that the volunteer teacher’s response was connected to the main learning goals, but I wanted to use facilitative questioning to help the volunteer teacher identify specific student work products that would show how they met the writing instructional goals of the lesson. This means that at this moment in our conversation, I was listening actively for the volunteer teacher to share work products that met the speaking and writing domains in the learning goals that she stated earlier and I was also actively listening for another form of formative assessment that aligned with the learning goals and the lesson plan that she shared. Looking back at this moment in the conversation, I wanted to use facilitative questions or specific examples that were evidence-based student work products. At minute 1:43 in video clip 1, I ask, “Are there any exit tickets or cool downs or anything like that, that are going to show that they met the outcomes?” At minute 1:52 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “Absolutely!” and at minute 1:53 in video clip 1, I say, “Tell me about that.” At minute 1:55 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “In this lesson, there is a cool down which is known as an exit ticket and they will be asked to write a multiplication expression to represent the area of a shaded region, and to tell us what the area is.”] </p></div><h4>Classroom context</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"85\"><p>[I wanted to invite the classroom teacher to describe the learning context so that we could determine components of the learning environment that would connect to our discussion about the CSTP-element-focused data. At minute 4:21 in video clip 1, I ask, “How would you describe the classroom culture or the learning environment for your students this year?” At minute 4:30 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responds, “This year we’re working on raising their hands when we have a comment or listening to one person at a time and I think we’re still working on that.” Looking back at this moment in our conversation, I recognized how her description of the classroom context identified positive behaviors that her students were working on, which demonstrated a specific area of need that we could later connect to our collaboration on a </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"86\"><p>CSTP element that aligned with this need.] </p></div><h4>Student assets and needs</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"88\"><p>[I wanted to invite the classroom teacher to share information about the students’ cultural and linguistic assets and their learning needs, so that we could relate this important contextual information to our selection of the CSTP-element-focused data. At minute 3:39 in video clip 1, I ask, “In terms of your students, what strengths do they bring to the classroom culturally and linguistically and are there any students who might need significant scaffolding or supports?” At minute 3:53 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher responds, “My class is, has a lot of great ideas. They love participating, so that’s one of their cultural assets.” At minute 4:05 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher identifies potential student supports and says, “I think that something that needs support in this classroom would be for them to stay engaged with the material because it is quite challenging---fractions is not super easy.” Looking back at this moment in our conversation, I recognized how her identification of a specific student need connected to our collaborative selection of a CSTP element that was focused on creating and maintaining effective environments for student learning, especially when students engaged with challenging academic concepts.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The cool-down discussion is concrete: the coach asks about success evidence and the teacher identifies the multiplication expression and area task. This connects the plan to an inspectable work product.</p><p>The assets discussion remains at the level of students enjoying participation, while the main need is described as staying engaged with challenging fractions. Enjoying participation can be a strength, but it does not by itself identify a specific cultural or linguistic asset. The conversation needs to explore how students' knowledge and language resources can support mathematical explanation.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the success criteria for the work and the evidence needed for the oral as well as written goals.</li><li>Draw out specific relevant student assets and supports instead of treating demographic labels or enthusiasm as sufficient.</li><li>Show how a contextual detail changed the lesson discussion or observation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported questioning about goals and work supports a provisional 4 across Part E. The shallow asset discussion limits a stronger claim that the conversation deepened equitable opportunities for all learners.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus","url":"#cycle/c3/E3","html":"<span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tCSTP Selection: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher selected one or two CSTP elements based on the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment and students’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the selected element fits the teacher's self-assessment and students' needs, and how the selection was genuinely joint. The current handbook specifies one or two elements within the same CSTP domain.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"90\"><p>[While we examined the detailed district guide that described the CSTPs, I used facilitative questions to invite the volunteer teacher to select one or two CSTP elements that she was interested in focusing on, based on her self-assessment and students’ assets and learning needs. At minute 4:38 in video clip 1, I say, “Now we’re going to move into selecting one or two CSTP, or California Standards for the Teaching Profession elements that match the areas of teaching that you’re most interested in exploring or getting feedback on.” At minute 5:00 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher and I are looking at a physical copy of the district’s detailed CSTP guide that outlines all of the CSTPs, and I say, “So here are the CSTPs and the standards. Which element or elements align with areas that you’re interested in getting feedback on during this lesson?” At minute 5:12 in video clip 1, while looking at the district’s detailed CSTP descriptions and pointing to the specific CSTP, the volunteer teacher says, “Since I am a newer teacher I do want to keep working on classroom management and I think 2.6 here makes sense because I was talking about wanting to support positive behavior, such as making sure that one person is speaking at a time.” Looking back at this moment in our conversation, I recognize how my decision to invite the volunteer teacher to select a CSTP that she was interested in getting feedback on connected with my previous decisions to facilitate the conversation in a way that started with eliciting the volunteer teacher’s thinking about student learning goals, student assets and needs, and classroom context. These previous decisions to begin the preobservation meeting with these topics led to our collaboration to jointly select a CSTP element that attended to student learning needs and the instructional strategies that the volunteer teacher identified as the focal aspects of collecting evidence and feedback during the observation.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher selects the routines and positive-behavior focus and explains a personal growth interest. The coach connects that choice to earlier discussion rather than simply assigning a standard.</p><p>The analysis should go one step further: how will routines make mathematical learning more accessible, and what evidence will distinguish access from compliance? Hand-raising and turn-taking can support discussion but do not establish understanding of fractional area.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link the selected element to the specific lesson goals and student needs discussed earlier.</li><li>Identify observable teaching practices and student responses that will provide relevant evidence.</li><li>Preserve the teacher's rationale and agency while jointly testing whether the focus is sufficiently precise.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The teacher's reported selection and rationale support a provisional 4 alongside the other conversation evidence. Deeper inquiry into how routines support different learners would be needed for 5.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 4 / Agreeing on observation evidence","url":"#cycle/c3/E4","html":"<span id=\"e-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 4 / Agreeing on observation evidence</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tCoaching and Observation Process: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher collaboratively reviewed the coaching and observation process, and the CSTP–element-focused data to be collected during the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the jointly agreed process, duration, focus, and evidence collection. Include teacher practices, student responses, and student work. Analyze how the conversation produced shared understanding rather than presenting logistics as a checklist.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"92\"><p>[To collaboratively review the coaching and observation process, I wanted to confirm with the volunteer teacher how long the observation would be and confirm the CSTP-element focused data to be collected during the lesson. From minute 0:00 to minute 0:18 in video clip 2, I say, “Ok, just to wrap up this pre-observation meeting, I just want to close with an overview of the coaching cycle that we’re going to go through and make sure that we understand like how long the observation’s gonna be, right? So how long is this lesson?” At minute 0:19 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher checks her lesson plan and says, “It will be an hour and 10 minutes…closer to an hour.” At minute 0:27 in video clip 2, I nod my head as I write down notes and say, “An hour? Ok, that sounds good!” At this point in the conversation, I wanted to confirm the main points of our collaborative pre-observation meeting to make sure that I wasn’t missing any details or information that we would need as we scheduled the logistics of the observation. At minute 0:31 in video clip 2, I say, “In this pre-observation meeting we talked about all of the instructional components to the lesson [volunteer teacher nods in agreement], we talked about your students and their learning goals and their assets and their needs [volunteer teacher nods and says, “Mmmhmmm” in agreement], we collaborated for the focus of the data collected [volunteer teacher nods head in agreement]…and did we say it was going to be CSTP 2.6?” When I mention the specific CSTP 2.6 at minute 0:48 in video clip 2 and ask a clarifying question, I am pointing at the district CSTP guide that is between the volunteer teacher and I, and as she looks at the CSTP that I point to in the district guide, the volunteer teacher says, “Yes! Correct.” To further clarify the CSTP that we collaboratively chose, at minute 0:53 in video clip 2, I read the CSTP 2.6 aloud as it is written in the district guide: “Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn [the volunteer teacher nods head in agreement].” At this point in the conversation, I wanted to confirm the observation data that would provide evidence for CSTP 2.6 and wanted to paraphrase what the volunteer teacher had shared with me in the preobservation conference. At minute 1:02 in video clip 2, I ask, “And the observation data that would show this is…students…you emphasized them being able to show you different kinds of positive behavior for listening?” Immediately after I ask the volunteer teacher this question, at minute 1:18 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher nods in agreement and smiles. Looking back at this point, I recognize that I wanted to use facilitative questions to elicit the volunteer teacher’s response and confirmation of the specific behaviors that would serve as evidence or data related to the CSTP element. At minute 1:18 in video clip 2, I ask, “Remind me again…what those behaviors, listening behaviors, would be?” At minute 1:23 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “Right, some listening behaviors like raising their hand, being able to clarify other people’s thoughts or add on instead of repeating, and you know, even if it’s repeating, saying that, oh, I’m repeating from this certain individual...” [At this point, I nod my head in agreement and say, “Yeah!” and continue to listen to the volunteer teacher as she is expressing her ideas about the data that we should collect during the observation that relates to CSTP 2.6]. At minute 1:38 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “And equity sticks, I think, could also help with that, to make sure that everyone has a voice.” At this point, I wanted to affirm and use paraphrase what I had heard to let the volunteer know that I was actively listening to her response, and at minute 1:44 in video clip 2, I say, “Alright! So I’ll also be…not only looking out for what the students are doing and how they’re showing these positive behaviors, but also the strategies that you’re using as a teacher [the volunteer teacher says, “Yup! Mmhmm” and nods her head in agreement] to elicit [the volunteer teacher says, “Yes!” and smiles] those positive behaviors and maintaining that effective learning environment for all student learning.” At minute 2:00 in video clip 2, I am looking down at the district’s CSTP guide and citing CSTP 2.6, and the volunteer teacher is also looking at the CSTP 2.6 as I cite it during the conversation. At minute 2:04 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher confirms our review of the CSTP-element focused data that we collaboratively generated by saying, “Yeah!” and at minute 2:05 in video clip 2, I confirm by saying, “Ok sounds good.” To continue to review the coaching and observation process, at minute 2:07 in video clip 2 I say, “After the observation we’re going to review the video [the volunteer teacher says, “Ok”] during our post-observation conference as well as the student work products [the volunteer teacher says “Ok”] and other relevant data that matched the CSTP element and we’ll analyze it together.” To confirm, at minute 2:27 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “Ok, sounds good!” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"93\"><p>I wanted to use facilitative questions to have the volunteer teacher identify data to collect during the lesson that would highlight the CSTP 2.6 element. At minute 5:30 in video clip 1, I ask, “Which types of observation evidence would best showcase this element that we’re selecting here?” and I point to CSTP 2.6 in the district CSTP guide. At minute 5:40 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “I think…noticing they’re raising their hand and not speaking while others are speaking. Maybe having them repeat what each other says, to work on listening skills.” Here, the volunteer teacher identified observable behavioral indicators of positive behavior, as detailed in the district description of CSTP 2.6.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response gives detailed dialogue about lesson length, listening behaviors, participation routines, teacher strategies, and joint review of video and student work. The teacher contributes examples rather than only receiving a plan.</p><p>Some questions invite confirmation of the coach's proposed interpretation. The plan would be stronger if it identified how evidence will be recorded and how students' reasoning and access will be distinguished from visible on-task behavior. Long transcript-like passages need analysis of the consequential decisions, not repeated descriptions of nodding.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the agreed evidence categories and how the observation tool will capture them.</li><li>Identify a teacher contribution that changed or refined the plan.</li><li>Confirm the live observation and subsequent joint video/work review, and explain how the plan addresses the mathematical goals.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.2: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported two-way clarification of data and process supports a provisional 4. Confirm it in the clips and distinguish eliciting the teacher's thinking from obtaining agreement to the coach's interpretation.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / CSTP-focused observation notes","url":"#cycle/c3/F","html":"<span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / CSTP-focused observation notes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Collect specific evidence during the live observation and connect it to the agreed CSTP element and lesson goals. Separate observed actions and student responses from your interpretation or proposed alternative. Useful notes allow the teacher to examine a claim, not merely accept your judgment.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>From observation to a coaching question</summary><p>Keep three moves distinct: describe an event, offer a cautious interpretation tied to the agreed focus, and invite the teacher to examine it. A question should allow a genuine alternative interpretation.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Observed event</th><th scope=\"col\">Cautious interpretation</th><th scope=\"col\">Question for joint examination</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Observed event\">Illustrative only: a student reports finishing, but the collected response states an answer without explaining the reasoning.</td><td data-label=\"Cautious interpretation\">Completion did not establish understanding in this instance. The record does not establish the student's effort or the teacher's intention.</td><td data-label=\"Question for joint examination\">What does this response show about understanding, and what further evidence would help us decide?</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching demonstration, not an event from the CTC example or a substitute for observation notes. Connect your actual evidence to the CSTP element and lesson goal selected with the teacher.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"137\"><table class=\"source-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"1\"><p>CSTP: CSTP 2 Creating and Maintaining Effective Environments for Student Learning </p><p>Element: 2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn </p><p>Teacher’s Goal or Focus Question (from Pre-Observation): Classroom Management </p><p>Make sure that one person is speaking at a time </p><p>Wanting to support positive behavior </p><p>Students are raising their hands and not speaking when others are speaking </p><p>Having them repeat what each other says (Clarifying and repeating) </p><p>Listening skills (look for these student behaviors) </p><p>Instructional Goal 1: Students describe (orally and in writing) how different equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a given rectangle with fractional sides greater than 1. </p><p>Instructional Goal 2: Students explain (orally) strategies used to determine the area of a rectangle with fractional side lengths and represent the area with a multiplication expression. </p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"138\"><p>Collection of Data/Evidence </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"139\"><p>Teacher opens lesson by displaying the student learning goal (Let’s find the area of more rectangles) on the digital display. Invites students to participate by asking, “Can someone read what we’re doing today?” Multiple students raise their hands to volunteer. When a student begins reading the lesson goal and needs assistance, teacher starts to read the learning goal with the student and asks the student if they have their glasses and if they can see what is displayed. Reminds students that the learning goal is also in their individual workbooks that contain all of the student materials needed for the lesson. When multiple students begin talking at once, teacher reminds students, “One voice at a time.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"141\"><p>Missed opportunity: To ensure clarity of student learning goals in the beginning of the lesson, teacher can provide a connection between prior learning goals and the current lesson’s learning goals. Teacher can use question prompts to elicit student responses that can connect to prior knowledge and practice with concepts connected to the lesson goals. Teacher can also provide success criteria in the beginning of the lesson that connect with current lesson’s learning goals, so students will know how they will be demonstrating their understanding and learning by the end of the lesson. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"143\"><p>Teacher shares that they are moving onto the warm-up now and displays warm-up materials on the digital display. Teacher shares, “Let me explain what we’re doing. I see some people already trying and it’s ok but let me just tell us what we’re doing.”  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"144\"><p>While previewing the true/false warm-up activity with the students, asks a clarifying question, “What does true mean?” and reminds students to raise their hands to offer a response. Teacher reviews the three parts of the activity, invites students to try the activity, and sets a timer while saying, “I’m going to give us a minute and see what we get. I’m going to walk around and make sure you are doing it.” Teacher circulates the room, confers with students, and shares reminders to ensure that the learning environment is quiet during this time. Some students ask, “Are you going to pick lucky ducks?” Towards the end of the minute, teacher confirms with students that she is “picking lucky ducks” to share out (“Lucky Ducks” is a digital student randomizer used often in the classroom to encourage student engagement and participation). [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"146\"><p>Teacher uses digital student randomizer during review of warm-up math activities, and 4 different students are chosen to participate and share their reasoning. As students are sharing their thinking, other students in the class appear to listen to the student who is sharing by turning their heads towards that student. Teacher also supports by scripting what the student is sharing on the whiteboard. The teacher monitors student engagement and lets a student know that they are next to share. When a student in the back of the room appears to be disengaged and starts to make noise, the teacher addresses the student and lets the student know that she will expect the student to repeat what the other student is sharing out: “We’re working on our listening skills.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] When the student shares that they can’t hear what the student in the front of the class is saying, teacher asks the student if they could speak a little louder so the students in the back of the room could hear them and encourages the student to speak with confidence. When the next student is chosen to participate by the digital student randomizer, teacher encourages student to also explain their reasoning to support why they chose true or false and offers a sentence starter: “This statement is false because…”. As students share their reasoning, other students in the class appear to quietly listen and some students look over at the students who are taking turns speaking. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"148\"><p>Teacher shares that they are moving on to Activity 1 and reminds students of the learning goal for that lesson. Teacher displays materials for Activity 1 on the digital display and reminds students of yesterday’s exit ticket that connects with this current lesson: </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"149\"><p>“Remember yesterday’s cool down, we did quite well! Can we keep it up? There’s another cool down today!” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher displays the diagram for the activity, holds up the student workbook displaying the corresponding material, and reminds students that the same diagram is on page 55 in their individual workbooks. Teacher walks around the classroom, asks students to point to the diagram, and shares that she is looking for students to point to diagram to show that they are engaged in this part of the activity. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Asks students, “What are we doing with this problem?” as they indicate to the first math prompt posed on the corresponding page. A student volunteers to read the prompt aloud, and as the student is reading the prompt, the class is sitting quietly and looking at the prompt in the workbooks.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"151\"><p>Teacher continues Activity 1 and asks students questions to facilitate dialogue about the diagram. One student raises their hand (this student has already volunteered earlier during the lesson). After initiating 10 seconds of wait time, the teacher uses a student randomizer paper tool to choose a student to participate. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] As each student participates and shares thinking when responding to teacher’s facilitative and clarifying questions, other students in the class appear to listen to responses. One student raises their hand and offers an explanation for the diagram. As student is sharing and teacher is using facilitative and clarifying questions, the students appear to listen to conversation and attend to the diagram parts that teacher is emphasizing during the conversation. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher notices another student raise their hand and invites student to share their thinking. Student shares their thinking and teacher confirms by indicating to the diagram. Teacher provides clear directions for expectations for students to continue the rest of the activity, reminds students about the purpose and learning goals of the activity, and makes connections between today’s activity and yesterday’s lesson goals. Teacher states the time length that students have to work on the activity and sets timer: “I’m going to give us 5 minutes, how does that sound, to work on this.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher asks clarifying question about the class procedure to use if students need help: “If you really don’t know, what do you do?” Students reply: “Ask 3 people!” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] After the timer starts, students immediately start working on activity, individually and in pairs. Teacher monitors and encourages students to be specific when asking peers for help: “Ask specifically—what are you looking for?” Teacher encourages students to read the directions again to help them ask specific questions for help from their peers. Teacher walks around the room, listening to student conversations and occasionally stops to have 1:1 conversations with students. Students asking peers, “Can you help me?” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"153\"><p>Teacher gives an attention signal to clarify expectations for the activity. Teacher moves to the back of the room and says, “Flat tire!” Students say, “Ssssssssss” and turn their attention towards the teacher. Teachers asks students a clarifying question about the directions for the activity. A few students raise their hands to offer clarifying answers to the question. Teacher encourages students to use additional activity resources in their workbooks to help them with the math activity. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Students raise their hands to ask the teacher clarifying questions, and teacher immediately acknowledges and responds. Students continue to work on the activity, individually and in pairs. Teacher circulates around the room and supports students 1:1 during the activity. Teacher announces, “I see someone with an expression already!” Teacher continues to observe, and walk around room, encouraging students and checking in with students. When a student begins distractive behaviors, teacher immediately walks over, confers with student, and encourages them to talk to their partner; teacher encourages the partner student, “Why don’t you try explaining to him…” and indicates the math activity. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"155\"><p>Teacher gives a signal to class about the next transition after this activity. Teacher says, “Thirty more seconds and then we’re going to share what we thought of.” Teacher encourages students to write down their thinking. When timer signal indicates the end of the designated time for the activity, the teacher says, “Flat tire!” and the students immediately respond by saying , “Ssssss” and turn their attention to the teacher. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher acknowledges that the math activity can feel challenging, but asks if she can choose a student to share their thinking with the class. A few students raise their hands. Teacher notices a student volunteering (who has not participated yet during the lesson) and asks her to come up to the digital display to share her thinking. As student walks up to the front of the room, teacher encourages class to support their peer as she comes up to share her thinking. Teacher says, “If you guys are great supporters of [student name], you should be listening.” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher shares that she is going to use the digital student randomizer to choose the next student to participate and share their thinking about the math activity. While student is sharing math thinking on the digital display, students in the class are listening and teacher is standing near a group of students on the side of the room as she facilitates discussion. Teacher encourages students to be supportive of student sharing by counting with her as she marks the diagram and explains her thinking. The class immediately begins to participate and count with student who is sharing. Some students ask clarifying questions during the discussion. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"157\"><p>27:00 Teacher provides guidance on how students can explain their math thinking in writing. Teacher writes a sentence frame on the board, “This expression matches the diagram because __________.” A few students in class write the sentence frame in their workbooks, and teacher shares, “I see [student name 1] writing it down, and I see [student name 2] writing it down. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"158\"><p>28:00 Students work on the activity independently and in pairs, and some students walk up to the teacher to confer. When a student says, “This is so hard,” teacher encourages students to look at the diagram, shares that one of their peers has solved it, and encourages students: “I see a lot of you writing it down. Someone has already solved it. So if one of you can do it, all of you can do it.” As students continue to work on the activity, teacher monitors and says to a student, “I see you’ve got it!” and encourages the student to talk to another student and share their thinking. “Talk to each other.” Teacher confers with a student pair and then one student walks up to teacher and confers. Some students walk across the room to talk to other students; some of these conversations do not appear to be focused on the math activity. Teacher says, “Some of us are trying really hard, and some of us are not trying to learn this because we’re off topic.”  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"160\"><p>36:00 Teacher signals a transition from this part of the activity. Teacher says, “Alright, everyone back in their seats in 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2…” Students quickly move about the room to find their seats, and one student says, “C’mon, sit down!” Teacher says, “I’m going to take off a letter because people are talking.” 2 students are in the front of the room and one student in class says, “C’mon, stop talking!” Students are annotating on the digital diagram, and most of class is quiet. It doesn’t appear that all students are attending to the digital display, and teacher says, “I’m sorry, some of us are not listening. How do I know? Your attention is not on the screen, your attention is outside for some of you, you don’t have learning bodies…better! [Student name] turn around, please. The screen is that way.” Students at the front continue to explain math thinking with digital diagram, and teacher asks facilitative and clarifying questions. Teacher encourages student to show their thinking on the screen. Invites another student to join student who is sharing in the front and teacher thanks student for coming up and sharing.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"162\"><p>41:00 When another student who is sitting in the back gets up to sharpen pencil and stands at the sharpener for more than 20 seconds sharpening pencil, teacher reminds student to ask for another pencil. Student asks for a pencil. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] As student in the front continues to share their math thinking, teacher walks over to student at the sharpener and quietly confers with them. Student quietly stands with teacher, teacher hands student a pencil, and the student goes back to their desk. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"164\"><p>42:00 Student continuing to share their thinking at the front of the room, and helps student refer to digital diagram to show their thinking. Teacher asks class if they have any questions about the student’s thinking. When a student asks a question in class and their voice volume is low, the teacher asks the student to speak a little louder because it’s hard to listen. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] When the students ask for the student who is sharing to explain it again, the teacher immediately responds and asks the student to share again and “speak a little bit louder, please.” The student who is sharing begins to explain their thinking again, and students in class are looking at the screen as the student annotates on the diagram. After the student explains, the teacher asks the student to label the diagram: “Can you label the 14? Just count 14 to show us what you mean.” The teacher confirms the counting and labeling, and points out how the width and length matches the expression, and thanks the student for sharing. [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"166\"><p>43:30 Teacher signals a transition to the next activity in the lesson. “Solve for A or C. Find the area.” The teacher modified the activity to create entry points for students into the activity after determining the need for more support based on student work in the previous part of the lesson. The teacher circulates the classroom and monitors while students work on the task. Some students immediately bring materials to work in established groups. [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"168\"><p>44:50 Teacher gives time signal to indicate how much time students have left to complete the task: “I’m going to give us 1 more minute to solve for A or C.” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"169\"><p>46:30 Teacher gives signal that task time is over and guides students to transition. Teacher says, “Ok, back at your seats in 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.” [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"171\"><p>47:00 Teacher guides students to transition into sharing math thinking around the task. Teacher asks, “Did anyone get to solve these expressions and would like to show us? [Student name], come up.” Teacher invites a student who has not shared yet to come up and show their thinking on the whiteboard. Student begins writing on the board to share math thinking. Teacher monitors class as the student is writing on board, and then says, “Sorry [student name] someone is being disrespectful right now, so [another student name] I’m going to ask you to repeat [student name’s] thinking.”  [CSTP2.6 Employing norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]   </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"172\"><p>48:00 Teacher steps in to support student who is explaining their thinking: As student is sharing their thinking in front of the class, teacher displays the student’s workbook and diagram under the document camera to help the student explain their thinking with the diagram visual. [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] Teacher asks, “Is it one twothirds or how many are there?” Student says, “4.” Teacher helps student annotate the diagram to help explain their thinking.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"173\"><p>48:40 Teacher checks in with student who was asked to explain their thinking after the previous student. Teacher asks student to explain how the previous student got the area of the diagram. Student begins to explain how the other student explained their thinking. Teacher thanks the student for explaining.  [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.]  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"175\"><p>Missed opportunity: To further clarify how the student determined the side lengths, teacher could ask facilitative questions that clarify how the student determined which numbers to multiply to get the area of the rectangle. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"177\"><p>49:20 Teacher invites another student to explain their math thinking in the activity. The teacher maintains student engagement and says, “I’m going to randomly pick someone to share what you heard from [student name].” The teacher facilitates and uses detailed descriptions when the student is explaining their thinking, to make the math thinking visible. The teacher chooses a student to paraphrase what the student did to solve the area, and asks, “What did [student name] do?” [CSTP2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn.] The student says that they didn’t hear the student, so the teacher asks the student to explain again and speak a little louder so everyone can hear. The student explains again, and the other student paraphrases what they said.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"179\"><p>51:00 Teacher transitions students to the last activity of the lesson (the cool down, or exit ticket). Teacher says, “I want you to take out your packet now.” Student begins looking for the packet, and then teacher says, “Everyone take it out in a minute!” and puts a minute on the timer. Teacher announces, “Page 79!” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"181\"><p>Missed opportunity: Teacher can state the expectation for students to have all materials ready at the beginning of the lesson, and practice this expectation to make it a routine. This can reduce the amount of time students spend looking for materials and increase time on task. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"183\"><p>53:00 Teacher displays the cool down/exit ticket task on a digital display and explains directions to complete the task.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"185\"><p>Missed opportunity: Teacher can ask students facilitative questions such as, “What do you notice?” to prompt students to identify what the task is asking them to do. This can promote student engagement and ownership of task.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"186\"><p>Missed opportunity to check for understanding and circle back to the lesson goals: Teacher could ask students what they learned how to do and practice during the lesson, which strategies they used to identify the area, and transfer this to the formative assessment task (the exit ticket) and the success criteria for students to show their understanding on this task. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"188\"><p>53:38 Teacher highlights how this task connects to a previous task. Teacher says, “It’s similar to yesterday’s–and then you’re going to solve for it, find the area.” Teacher gives directions on what students can do after they finish (read quietly).  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"190\"><p>Missed opportunity: Teacher can have these tasks (maybe a choice menu for additional tasks to do when finished) posted in the room to make it a routine. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"192\"><p>Students work on exit tickets for the last 10 minutes of class. </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The notes document routines, teacher language, participation, transitions, and specific missed opportunities. They connect classroom management with time and access to the mathematical task and include concrete examples that can anchor feedback.</p><p>Labels such as missed opportunity are interpretations. The underlying event must remain clear, followed by an explanation of how a different action might support learning. Some successful behavior routines are described without evidence that students understood the mathematics; that distinction should be retained.</p><p>This record supports a stronger analysis than a generic observation checklist. To extend it further, examine how the teacher responded to whole-class, small-group, and individual needs, including adaptations that did not produce the intended access.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Keep time, observed teacher action, student response, and interpretation distinguishable.</li><li>Link each proposed missed opportunity to the lesson learning goal, not just a preference for a different routine.</li><li>Compare responses across different learners and connect the relevant observations to actual work samples.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: 4 / 5, provisional.</strong> The concrete notes and identified missed opportunities support a provisional 4 with the commentary. The record is less systematic about adaptations across whole-class, small-group, and individual learning needs, as required for 5.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part G / Student work from the observed lesson","url":"#cycle/c3/G","html":"<span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Student work from the observed lesson</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use actual work produced during the lesson and discussed with the teacher. Identify the task, the success criteria, what the work demonstrates, and what remains uncertain. A correct answer, an incorrect answer, and visible reasoning can each provide different information.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"97\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-97-0.jpg\" alt=\"Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Student work sample 1: marked 2 out of 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"98\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-98-0.jpg\" alt=\"Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Student work sample 2: marked 1 out of 2</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"99\"><figure class=\"source-figure\"><button type=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge example document\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 3: Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2\"><img src=\"assets/reviews/cycle3/block-99-0.jpg\" alt=\"Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Student work sample 3: marked 0 out of 2</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>Three photographed work samples receive marks of 2/2, 1/2, and 0/2. They provide a useful contrast: the middle sample has an accepted area answer but an incorrect expression, while the last has errors in both. The marks and written reasoning should be examined separately; a total score does not explain the misconception.</p><p>The sample set should be identified clearly: why these three examples were discussed and whether they are typical or selected cases. The teacher's later statement that only two students earned full credit does not mean that every other student learned nothing; item-level reasoning matters. The three photographs alone do not establish the distribution of results for the class.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use anonymous, stable sample labels that also appear in the commentary and observation discussion.</li><li>Apply clear criteria to identify the demonstrated strategy, correct reasoning, and specific misconception.</li><li>State the number of works examined and avoid generalizing a few selected examples to the entire class.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: 4 / 5, provisional. Rubric 3.4: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The work makes learning inspectable, but the score depends on how it is analyzed with observation and coaching evidence. Three samples alone cannot establish the quality of the entire conversation or the class result.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part H / Post-observation meeting video clips","url":"#cycle/c3/H","html":"<span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Post-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the video of the post-observation meeting where you and the volunteer teacher are seen and heard, select 1 to 3 video clips. Your 1 to 3 video clips may total up to 15 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 15 minutes of video recording submitted).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 17; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit one to three clips, totaling up to fifteen minutes, from the joint post-observation meeting. The evidence should show examination of the lesson video and student work, discussion of practice, shared next steps, and teacher feedback on your coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><p class=\"scope-note\">The source commentary refers to video clips, but those recordings were not available for this review. No example video or invented transcript is substituted.</p></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The narrative reports a substantive discussion, but the actual recordings were not reviewed. It cannot independently establish how much the teacher led the interpretation, whether the referenced work and lesson video were jointly examined, or whether all cited moments appear within the submitted limits.</p><p>The provisional scores below judge the written account against the criteria without treating it as a substitute for the demonstration.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual clips against the current handbook's content and duration requirements.</li><li>Verify the evidence of joint viewing, teacher reasoning, shared next steps, and feedback.</li><li>Confirm the commentary's precise clip locations and do not manufacture dialogue to fill a gap.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: Not independently assessable without the video. Rubric 3.4: Not independently assessable without the video. Rubric 3.5: Not independently assessable without the video.</strong> The written account permits provisional judgments about the described reasoning, but not an independent score for unseen clips. Review the recordings before treating the estimates as confirmed demonstrations.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 1 / Jointly assessing student learning","url":"#cycle/c3/I1","html":"<span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Jointly assessing student learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Assessment of Student Learning:</p><p>Describe how you supported the volunteer teacher in assessing student learning during the CSTP-focused lesson.</p><p>Describe how you shared the evidence of CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher.</p><p>Describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-analyzed the video of the lesson, student work, and/or other data (if collected) from the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three components: how you supported the teacher's assessment of learning, shared CSTP-focused evidence, and jointly analyzed the lesson video and student work. Explain how your coaching helped the teacher interpret evidence, not simply that you looked at it together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Supporting assessment of learning</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"106\"><p>[During the lesson, the volunteer teacher assessed student learning by monitoring student engagement behaviors to ensure that they were participating while the class practiced strategies to meet the math learning goals. The volunteer teacher explained how it was important for her to see that students were using the strategy of counting the side lengths of the rectangles correctly so that they could also write the correct math expression to solve for the area of the rectangle, and employ these strategies after the lesson when they completed their exit tickets, or the formative assessment after the lesson. For example, at minute 1:16 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “I was counting with them to ensure that they were all counting the side lengths, not just the presenter, and that everyone else was also engaged and wanting to count their lengths and in order to write their expressions.” At minute 1:30 in video clip 1, I ask a clarifying question to connect the volunteer teacher’s teaching decision of practicing this strategy to the formative assessment: “That was part of their exit ticket, for them to show that they met the learning goal and be able to count those.” At minutes 1:36 and 1:38 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes.” I extend the discussion of this strategy of counting the side lengths of the rectangle at minute 1:38 in video clip 1 when I ask, “How did that instructional strategy that you employed there, that you used…How did you see that show up in the student work after the lesson?” Referring to the formative assessments completed by students after the lesson, at minute 1:50 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “In their cooldowns, right? Here it seems like there were 2 students, one being the one who asked a question, which is that she couldn’t hear the presenter, so she ended up doing really well, both the expression, writing the expression and finding the area.” At minute 2:09 in video clip 1, I confirm, “Which were the learning goals of the lesson,”] and the volunteer teacher agrees. At minute 2:11 in video clip 1, I continue to jointly look at the students’ formative assessments (exit tickets/cool down) with the volunteer teacher, and the volunteer teacher says, “Yes, there are two students who got 100% because of the expressions that they had written. So, they somehow found the area.” At minute 2:31 in video clip 1, I ask, “Were they able to use that strategy that you were showing in the video?” At minute 2:34 in video clip 1, while looking through multiple student work samples, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yeah! Honestly, a lot, most of them counted 11…. counting the lengths.” Here, the volunteer teacher was referring to student work samples that showed students correctly counting 11 fractional pieces that represented one side length of the given rectangle, which also showed evidence of students who employed the strategy taught by the volunteer teacher in the lesson video.]  </p></div><h4>Sharing observation evidence</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"108\"><p>[During our post-observation meeting, as the volunteer teacher and I watched the video of the lesson, I paused the video at certain moments to engage the volunteer teacher in a conversation about CSTP-focused data that we viewed in the video, and I also shared evidence from my notes. The CSTP-focused data related to CSTP 2.6: Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn, from the district’s CSTP guide that we used to jointly choose the CSTP to focus on. For example, at minute 0:00-0:03 in video clip 1, I begin by asking the volunteer teacher to “explain what’s happening in this part of the lesson here”. At minute 0:03 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “So I have a student up on the at the board showing her work, and there was another student in the audience asking a question, and she I couldn't hear her from the back, so I asked her to speak a little louder. And then, you know, the student in the back actually couldn't hear what the presenter was saying so I asked her to also speak a little louder. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"109\"><p>So, I'm trying to have them practice this routine of speaking louder in the classroom.” At minute 0:41, I said, “In my notes, I also noted that that you were employing a support to ensure engagement and positive behavior that went along with the CSTP.” Another example of how I shared CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher occurred at minute 5:03 in video clip 1 when I said, “I see you also, I made in my notes, you’re circulating around the room, watching each student group do the activity. And I noted that that is also evidence of the CSTP in ensuring that there's a positive behavior so that all of them could attend to the task and their learning task.” I also shared notes that corresponded to moments in the lesson video in which the volunteer teacher employed strategies such as routines, procedures, norms and supports that were related to CSTP-focused data. For example, while discussing an instructional strategy (counting the lengths of the rectangle) employed by the volunteer teacher to ensure student engagement and employ supports for students to practice math strategies that would help them access the lesson’s learning goal, at minute 2:48 in video clip 1, I said, “I noticed you enlarged the screen for them to be able to count along with you and other students who were presenting. And I noted that in my notes as well.”] </p></div><h4>Joint analysis</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"111\"><p>[The volunteer teacher and I watched the lesson video together, frequently pausing the video to discuss CSTP-related evidence and to examine students’ formative assessments (exit tickets/cool downs). For example, while examining student work, at minute 3:02 in video clip 1, I said, “Yeah, I see students notating the parts and circling them in their student work, in their exit tickets,” and the volunteer teacher agrees, and at minute 3:11 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher says, “Yeah. And I do see like, dots of them counting,” and at minute 3:14 in video clip 1, I agree and say, “Oh yeah, using that strategy that was shown during the lesson,” as I point to the lesson video on the computer screen. At the points that we paused the video to discuss what we were observing in the video, I posed facilitative questions to invite the volunteer teacher to share what they were observing in the video and their thinking about instructional decisions that they made during the lesson, For example, at minute 3:26 in video clip 1, I said, “Here in the lesson, right, I'm noticing that you displayed the next part of the activity, and there were four items…” and at minute 3:34 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes, with the expressions,” and at minute 3:37 in video clip 1, I share, “I noticed a modification. Can you tell me about how you changed that part of activity to support students?”  The volunteer teacher shared her rationale for her decision making at minute 3:46 in video clip 1: “Originally, they were asked to choose the expressions that matches the situation of the area, but I gave them the expressions instead of having them deduce, because I felt like that would have been quite challenging for certain individuals, most of them.” I responded immediately at minute 4:12 in video clip 1, I asked, “Was time a factor, too, in terms of the lesson?”, and at minute 4:17 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher confirmed, “Definitely,” and I asked her to tell me more about that. At minute 4:22 in video clip 1, the volunteer teacher shared, “I think we just finished the first activity in 44- or 44-minute mark right here, yeah, and math is normally about an hour or so.” I share an observation about this part of the lesson video at minute 4:33 in video clip 1 I said, “so you adjusted, knowing that they would need to also have time to complete an exit ticket?” and the volunteer teacher immediately confirmed at minute 4:39 in video clip 1, “Yes, yeah.” I continue clarifying at minute 4:40 in video clip 1 and said, “so making that adjustment gave them more support in at least being able to access the activity and attempt it for more practice before the exit ticket,” and while I shared this, the volunteer teacher confirmed this observation at minutes 4:47, 4:49, and 5:51 in video clip 1 by saying, “Yes, yep, yep.”] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The passage connects notes about instructional supports with the lesson video and marks on exit tickets. The teacher explains why she adjusted an activity and the coach invites further reasoning. This is relevant evidence of a learning conversation.</p><p>The account sometimes moves too quickly from engagement to learning. Dots or counting marks can show use of a strategy without proving that the expression or area is correct. Giving students expressions rather than asking them to derive them changes the demand of the task; the joint analysis should examine what learning can still be inferred.</p><p>The strongest next step is to trace a learning goal from the pre-observation discussion through observed instruction to a specific feature of student work, then explain how the teacher's interpretation changed.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the work sample, criterion, and mathematical inference supporting each claim of learning.</li><li>Examine how the in-the-moment task modification affected what the work can demonstrate.</li><li>Connect the teacher's interpretation with the prior goal and the relevant observation evidence, and explain your coaching contribution.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.3: 4 / 5, provisional. Rubric 3.4: 3 / 5, provisional.</strong> The reported use of video, notes, and work supports the combined provisional judgments. The strongest remaining need is a deeper connection between prior learning goals, the adapted task, and what the student work actually demonstrates.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps","url":"#cycle/c3/I2","html":"<span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tEffectiveness of Teaching Practices Including Next Steps for Volunteer Teacher Growth:</p><p>Describe how you facilitated a joint discussion of the effectiveness of the volunteer teacher’s teaching practices to support student learning, identifying any missed opportunities.</p><p>After discussing the volunteer teacher’s effective teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-determined possible next steps of the volunteer teacher’s professional growth in the identified CSTP element(s) (what you will do and what the volunteer teacher will do).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze the joint discussion of successful practices and missed opportunities, then explain the next professional steps you co-determined. Include what the teacher will do, what you will do, the resources or support, and the evidence behind the plan.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Making joint next steps concrete</summary><p>A useful next step connects an identified learning need to a teacher action, your coaching support, appropriate resources, and evidence to revisit. Explain how both adults shaped the decision rather than presenting advice as an agreement.</p><p>Distinguish the next lesson from the observed lesson: a proposed check for understanding has not happened yet. Identify what the teacher will try, what you will help prepare or examine, and how the subsequent discussion could determine whether the change addressed the need.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>Effectiveness and missed opportunities</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"114\"><p>[While watching the video of the lesson together, the volunteer teacher and I discussed the CSTP-focused data and observed teaching practices that supported student learning. I used facilitative prompts and questions to engage the volunteer teacher in reflections on her teaching practices that supported student engagement, positive behaviors, and student learning towards the learning goals of the lesson. For example, to give feedback on effective practices that I observed during the lesson, as we watched certain parts of the video lesson, I referred to my notes to confirm the video evidence of effective teaching practices. For example, at minute 1:37 in video 2, I referred to my observation notes as we were watching the video lesson and said, “Here, I noted that you were connecting the previous learning and activities to the current task, and that's a signal that also supports them to access the learning goals is to connect the current learning to the previous learning.” At minutes 1:44 and 1:54 in video 2, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes.” I also used facilitative prompts and questions to guide the volunteer teacher to identify missed opportunities to support student learning. For example, at minute 0:00 in video clip 2, I ask, “what are you noticing about this part of the lesson, when they're transitioning?” and at minute 0:08 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “That it's taking a while and that it is not a routine…someone asking, ‘Take what out?’, not knowing that it is the end of a lesson and we have our cool down…that signal…”. I immediately acknowledge the volunteer teacher’s observation, and at minute 0:30 in video clip 2, I ask, “Could this become a routine and be taught as a routine?” At minute 0:31 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher says, “Yes.” I immediately followed up with a facilitative question to invite the volunteer teacher to reflect on strategies that would help the students employ more positive behaviors at a recurring part of the math lesson in which they have their materials ready to engage in the formative assessment or cool down/exit ticket part of the math lessons. At minute 0:33 in video clip 2 I ask, “How would you set it up and practice this with the students for next time?” and at minute 0:40 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher reflects, “I'm thinking that maybe there should be just like that signal, like after we finish the synthesis, I say, ‘Okay, we're done with the synthesis’ (that word synthesis) and we're doing our cool down, then they take it out. Or I could have them take it out like as a morning routine in the morning and have it ready on their desk.” To affirm the volunteer’s teacher reflection and add on, at minute 1:05 in video clip 2 I say, “Like already, have the page up somewhere visible, and that could eventually become a routine, and be part of the environment that ensures positive behavior for all.” The volunteer teacher confirms and agrees at minute 1:18 in video clip 2, they share, “Yeah. And I've turned those cool downs into packets already, so that they could have it easily accessible. Organization is something that I’m trying to also…”. Immediately I confirm hearing when the volunteer teacher shares a possible next step for professional growth and at minute 1:29 in video clip 2, I repeat, “Organization”, and nod my head, and then at minute 1:31 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yeah, because I see a few people like, where's my packet?” and I immediately affirm, “Oh, okay!” to let the volunteer teacher know that I understood the video evidence that she cited to inform her next steps. There were also moments while reviewing student work that initiated conversation about missed opportunities and next steps. For example, at minute 2:06 in video 2, as the volunteer teacher is examining student work, she said, “He was so close. He counted four wholes here, but three. So, because of that, he solved the area wrong. So he just wrote the wrong expression, but he knew how to solve for the area.” I immediately respond to the volunteer teacher’s observation about the student’s work, and at minute 2:23 in video 2 I ask, “Is there a point in the lesson that maybe you feel like you could have scaffolded that part to support students using that strategy?” The volunteer teacher responds at minute 2:33 in video 2 the volunteer teacher reflects, “I think it's also a routine, having them get into a routine of double checking their work. Like, even during the year, during certain tests, I'm like, double check you guys. It's something that I'm still trying to make it…I'm trying to normalize that it's okay to go back and double check your answers. Okay, because they just want to get it done. And they're like, I'm done, okay, and move on, type of thing. So that's something I would like to reinforce my classroom.” At minute 3:07 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher continues, “There’s something else I notice,” and motions to the lesson video on the computer. I immediately respond with a facilitative question to initiate further reflection from the volunteer teacher, and at minute 3:09 in video clip 2, I ask,” Yeah. What else did you notice?” At minute 3:11 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher said, “The routine of cool down itself as well. They're not super quiet, even though I keep saying it's an independent work, it's telling me what you know, not what someone else knows. But it doesn't seem to be the most quiet that it could be.” I immediately identify the volunteer teacher’s reflection as an indicator of a next step to increase the effectiveness of her teaching practices towards ensuring that all students in her class are employing positive behaviors for learning during the independent work task of the cool down, or formative assessment task, at the end of the math lessons.] </p></div><h4>Joint next steps</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"116\"><p>[After the volunteer teacher and I discussed the teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, I used facilitative prompts, questions, and clarifying language to help us co-determine possible next steps for growth in the identified CSTP element (2.6 Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn). For example, after the volunteer teacher shared her observation that all students in the lesson video were not employing expected behaviors during the cool down, at minute 3:33 in video 2, I said, “That would be a next step, too! So, I'm hearing you say that your next steps would be working on certain parts of this math lesson of this new math curriculum to eventually become routines.” At minute 3:50 in video clip 2, the volunteer teacher confirms, “Yes, yes!” I continue facilitating, and at minute 3:53 in video clip 2, I ask, “In order to make these things routine…so what kinds of things would be your next steps to do?” At minute 3:59 in video 2, the volunteer teacher offers her reflections on next steps to address this instructional need, and said, “Maybe really talking about the expectations of what each part of a lesson looks like. For example, warm up, expectations, activity, expectations, to cool down expectations, and then what to do after a cool down.” To further encourage reflection on next steps, at minute 4:25 in video 2, I asked, “Is there anything in the classroom environment that you could add to support with making those routines?” The volunteer teacher immediately responded at minute 4:32 in video 2, and said, “Yeah, I use a timer very often in my classroom. Yeah, like you have one minute to think quietly to yourself.” After I shared that I also observed this and noted the volunteer teacher’s use of the timer, the volunteer shared that it was helpful. At minute 4:47 in video 2, I affirm and encourage the volunteer teacher’s employment of this instructional strategy, and said, “You were able to get through all of the parts too. Yeah, you know, from the warm up to the activities to the very end to wrap it up, to see what they're thinking and what they walked away with.” The volunteer teacher confirmed her rationale for employing the use of the timer, and at minute 5:01 in video clip 2, she said, “Yes, yes. That's very important to me that even if I like skip one activity, I still need to get to the cool down to see where they're at.” As the volunteer teacher and I moved towards the latter parts of our postobservation meeting, I wanted to facilitate a discussion to summarize and clarify the volunteer teacher’s next steps for professional growth related to the CSTP element 2.6 (Employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, and supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn), as well as clarify how I could support these next steps. At minute 0:00 in video clip 3, I said, “For next steps in just continuing to grow and develop towards employing classroom routines, procedures and norms, the CSTP element, it says employing classroom routines, procedures, norms, supports for positive behavior to ensure a climate in which all students can learn from our conversation, I heard you mention making certain things routines. Can you just summarize that for me and tell me what kinds of things you want to work on making routines?” At minute 0:34 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher says, “Yeah, from warm up expectations, to activity expectations, to synthesis, to cool down expectations and what to do after the cool down is something I want to work on building in my class.” At this moment in the conversation, I wanted to refer to an earlier moment in our discussion when the volunteer teacher mentioned wanting to work on student organization to support positive behaviors and to ensure a climate in which all students can learn, related to the CSTP element. At minute 0:53 in video clip 3, I said, “I also heard you mention wanting to work on supporting students organization, yeah, tell me more about that.” The volunteer teacher responded at minute 1:03 in video clip 3, and said, “It’s about getting their notebooks, math notebooks, out at the beginning of a lesson, and their math workbooks. There are math workbooks for every unit, and we are in our second unit now, and there are about six to eight workbooks, because six to eight units, so I want them to be able to get out the right workbook, and the rest should go home and stay with them, because it would be too many workbooks in their desk if they keep it all for the year. And their cool down packets should stay in their folders, but some of them are not in their folders, so then they lose it.” At this point, I asked a facilitative question to elicit strategies from the volunteer teacher, and at minute 1:46 in video 3 I ask, “Yeah. How would you support those students that are still losing those things?” The volunteer teacher immediately responded with a possible strategy and next steps, and at minute 1:51 in video 3, she said, “I keep it possibly, like I have this student who has two right here,” and she indicated to the student work that had duplicates because that student had lost their packet. I wanted to point out that her possible strategy could work by mentioning what I noticed, and at minute 1:57 in video 3, I said, “I noticed that there’s just a few, right, to support…that could be a next step for sure, to help students with organization.” Later in the conversation, as I asked for feedback, I also offered my next steps to ensure my support with my volunteer teacher’s professional growth, and at minute 2:51 in video clip 3, I said, “I am going to share all these notes with you too, so you can see all the details of where the CSTP elements were and everything.”] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher notices transition difficulties and identifies possible routines; the conversation also examines a student's counting error and organization needs. These examples are more useful than broad advice about classroom management.</p><p>The next-step discussion largely identifies intentions: clarify expectations, use routines, organize materials, and share notes. It does not fully specify the resources, coaching support, or follow-up needed to enact and examine those changes. The later request for differentiation resources reinforces that gap.</p><p>The mathematical error also deserves careful analysis. Reminding students to double-check may help, but it does not by itself address a misconception about side lengths or area. Connect the professional learning plan to the diagnosed need rather than treating all errors as inattention.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish a behavioral or organizational barrier from a mathematical misconception using the work.</li><li>Specify a jointly chosen practice, resource, and coaching action, with responsibilities and a review date.</li><li>Identify how the plan addresses particular students' learning needs and what new evidence will show whether it helps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.4: 3 / 5, provisional. Rubric 3.5: 2 / 5, provisional.</strong> The distinction matters: the learning conversation supports a provisional 3 on Rubric 3.4, while the missing specificity of resources and follow-up support leaves Rubric 3.5 at a provisional 2.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 3 / Learning from teacher feedback","url":"#cycle/c3/I3","html":"<span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Learning from teacher feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tVolunteer Teacher Feedback on Candidate’s Coaching Practices:</p><p>Describe the feedback the volunteer teacher provided regarding your coaching skills during the coaching and observation cycle, including what they found helpful and/or not helpful. Explain how this feedback informs your understanding of your strengths and areas for growth as an instructional coach.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Report what the teacher found helpful and what was missing or unhelpful, then explain what that feedback reveals about your coaching strengths and growth. The current question explicitly asks for your interpretation, not just a transcript of the feedback exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><h4>What was helpful</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"119\"><p>[To elicit feedback from the volunteer teacher about my coaching skills, at minute 2:08 in video clip 3, I said, “Lastly, I want to get some feedback from you as my volunteer teacher about the coaching process. I want to know what was helpful and what wasn't helpful.” At minute 2:21 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher said, “Well, first off, so thank you so much for helping and being here in my classroom. Because, you know, I always say it takes a village to make sure to help this class, but I think what was really helpful is for you to be in the classroom to observe and see what's going on in the class, so that you can give feedback and as to how I can improve, or what I can do to help these the students.”] </p></div><h4>What was missing</h4><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"121\"><p>[At minute 3:03 in video clip 3, I asked, “What was something that maybe during the coaching process, you maybe would have wanted that was missing from this observation cycle?” At first, the volunteer teacher took some time to think about the question and wasn’t sure how to respond, and at minute 3:18 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher said, “Maybe we could…” As she hesitated, at minute 3:23 in video clip 3, I offered, “Like resources, or additional professional development…” and immediately at minute 3:25 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher agreed and said, “Oh yeah!” Immediately, at minute 3:27 in video clip 3, I ask, “In what areas do you think?” At minute 3:28 in video clip 3 the volunteer teacher immediately responded, “Classroom management, especially with this class, or how to differentiate the curriculum even more.” I immediately affirmed the volunteer teacher’s request, and at minute 3:38 in video 3, the volunteer teacher said, “Looking at this cool down now, yeah, what do I how do I move on from this? Only two students got 100% but this is data, and then what do we do with that data?” At minute 3:53 in video clip 3, I affirmed the volunteer teacher’s possible next steps and said, “Yeah, definitely, yeah. So using data to inform your next steps of instruction, yeah? Is something, yeah, I would love to help with that.” At minute 4:09 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher confirmed, “It’s always nice to have someone come in and see it in a new perspective.”]  </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The teacher values having another person observe and offer perspective, then asks for resources on classroom management, differentiation, and using assessment data. These comments give the coach a substantive direction for growth.</p><p>The passages report the exchange but leave much of its interpretation to Part J. Here, briefly explain the strength that is supported and the unmet need that calls for change. The coach suggests resources while the teacher is hesitating; analyze how that prompt may have shaped the feedback rather than assuming it was entirely unprompted.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish the teacher's specific feedback from the options suggested by the coach.</li><li>Explain what the comments establish about a coaching strength and an unmet support need.</li><li>State how future coaching will address that need, reserving the fuller reflection for Part J.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.6: 4 / 5.</strong> The combined Part J reflection supports a 4 for Rubric 3.6. This passage supplies the feedback, but needs its own brief interpretation to fully answer current Part I question 3; it is not a separately scored answer.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth","url":"#cycle/c3/J1","html":"<span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tBased on your experience in this coaching cycle, and the feedback you elicited from the volunteer teacher, reflect on your instructional coaching abilities during the post-observation meeting in Step 3. Refer to the volunteer teacher’s feedback/input and evaluate your strengths and areas for growth in coaching and observing a teacher’s practice and providing instructional feedback in relation to the agreed-upon CSTP element(s). Explain why you think you should work on them.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Evaluate your coaching using the teacher's feedback and the cycle evidence. Explain why each strength or growth area matters for the agreed CSTP focus, and how your future approach will change. A hypothetical question is a proposed improvement, not something you actually asked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"126\"><p>[During the post-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher shared how the coaching cycle was helpful and shared what she thought would have been helpful to include in the coaching cycle. The volunteer teacher shared that the observation process was helpful because I was present in her class for the observation, as she shared in video clip 3 at minute 2:08 when she said, “Thank you so much for helping and being here in my classroom…I think what was really helpful is for you to be in the classroom to observe and see what’s going on in the class, so that you can give feedback and as to how I can improve, or what I can do to help the students.” Looking back, I would have asked a follow-up question after she shared that, such as, “What specific feedback was especially helpful to you during this particular coaching cycle, in regard to the CSTP we chose and the instructional goals of the lesson?” Another follow-up question I could have asked at that moment was, “What feedback was particularly helpful that you might incorporate into your teaching practice next?” In future coaching cycles, I want to make sure that I ask follow-up, facilitative questions that clarify the specific coaching moves that were especially helpful to the volunteer teacher. I think that it is important for me to work on this because I believe that it will help me to build a stronger coaching relationship with my volunteer teachers during future coaching cycles, and better understand what coaching moves and feedback were specifically helpful.  </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"127\"><p>To ask for feedback on what was not helpful during the coaching cycle, in video clip 3 at minute 3:03 I asked the volunteer teacher, “What was something that maybe during the coaching process, you maybe would have wanted that was missing from this observation cycle?” At minute 3:28 in video clip 3, the volunteer teacher shared that she would have liked additional resources on classroom management, or curriculum differentiation, when she said, “Classroom management, especially with this class, or how to differentiate the curriculum even more.” At that moment, I affirmed the volunteer teacher’s request, and made the decision to give 10 seconds of wait time as I noticed the volunteer teacher looking over the student’s work, and then in video clip 3 at minute 3:38 the volunteer teacher continued, “Looking at this cool down now, yeah, what do I how do I move on from this? Only two students got 100% but this is data, and then what do we do with that data?” I immediately acknowledged the volunteer teacher and asked a clarifying question in video clip 3 at minute 3:53: “So using data to inform your next steps of instruction? I would love to help with that.” Looking back, I would have incorporated more time during the pre-observation meeting to carefully look over the lesson plan and facilitate a more detailed conversation with the volunteer teacher about differentiating instruction. I might have asked questions such as, “Are there any students who need significant scaffolding or extra challenges/extensions? How can we plan for this?” Through a collaborative process, the volunteer teacher and I could identify specific moments in the math lesson to scaffold learning for the identified students, plan specific question prompts to ask during the activities, or prepare resources or supplemental activities that would help students strengthen their conceptual understanding related to the math learning objectives. </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"128\"><p>In the post-observation meeting, I used active listening strategies such as paraphrasing, affirmations, and requests for clarification to communicate with the volunteer teacher. For example, in the post-observation video clip 1 at minute 1:16, the volunteer teacher shared how it was important for her to engage her students in practicing the strategy to count the side lengths during the lesson, I asked a clarifying question to help connect the volunteer teacher’s teaching strategy to the students’ formative assessment. After I asked the clarifying question with this purpose, the volunteer teacher turned her attention to connecting this teaching decision to examine student work, as seen in post-observation video clip 1 at minute 1:50, when she looked for evidence of the students applying this strategy in their cool downs/exit tickets, or the formative assessments of their learning. This demonstrated my coaching decisions to use active listening strategies (to ask relevant clarifying questions) was a strength during the coaching cycle because I was able to reflect what the volunteer teacher shared with me and help her make connections between her teaching decisions and the evidence in the students’ work towards meeting the instructional goals of the lesson. Using active listening strategies like paraphrasing, affirmations, and request for clarification while communicating with volunteer teachers during coaching cycles is a strength that I would like to continue to employ and to develop because these strategies also create space and time for volunteer teachers to reflect on their thinking, decision making, and pedagogy. I want to continue to work on asking follow-up questions during coaching conversations so that I can obtain specific details related to a volunteer teacher’s goals for further development and growth of their teaching practice, and also gain specific feedback on which parts of the coaching cycle were helpful and not helpful so that I can continue to develop and grow in my effectiveness as an instructional leader.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response examines helpful observation, missed follow-up questions, the request for resources, and the need to plan differentiation more deliberately. It identifies concrete changes to future pre- and post-observation conversations.</p><p>The distinction between reported actions and retrospective alternatives is mostly clear and worth preserving. Stronger analysis would address the limited resource follow-through and the effect of leading prompts, then define how the coach will know whether the proposed changes improve teacher ownership and instructional decision-making.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each growth area to a specific teacher comment or observable coaching moment.</li><li>Specify the resource or follow-up support that will accompany better questioning.</li><li>Define how feedback from a future cycle will show whether the revised approach is more useful.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.6: 4 / 5.</strong> The extensive examination of helpful and missing support, with concrete changes to future meetings, supports 4. Research or evidence-based practices would need to explain teacher or adult learning to establish 5.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership","url":"#cycle/c3/J2","html":"<span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tReflect on and refer to evidence from Steps 1–3 to demonstrate how you were able to facilitate and maintain a two-way conversation throughout the coaching, observation, and instructional feedback cycle. How were you able to encourage teacher voice and ownership of their strengths and areas for growth based on evidence of practice in relation to the CSTP element(s), the video of instruction, and student work product(s)?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use evidence across the cycle to explain how the teacher retained voice and ownership in identifying strengths, needs, and next steps. Asking questions is not sufficient by itself: show how the teacher's thinking influenced the work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"130\"><p>[After investigating the current coaching, observation, and instructional feedback practices at my current school site in Step 1, it was evident that there were few opportunities during the year for teachers to participate in regular instructional feedback cycles. As I engaged in a coaching cycle with my participating teacher during Steps 1-3, it was important for me to clearly understand the components and purposes for each meeting during the coaching cycle so that I could anticipate how to ask facilitative questions to encourage the volunteer teacher to authentically share their thoughts and reflections. During the pre-observation conference, I used facilitative prompts and questions to encourage collaborative conversation to draw out instructional needs and challenges based on a CSTP element that would become the focus of data collection and analysis during and after the observation. I also asked facilitative questions during the preobservation meeting to help the volunteer teacher identify specific student learning goals for the lesson, describe the classroom context, and identify student assets and needs. For example, in video clip 1 at minute 3:39 during the pre-observation conference, I asked the volunteer teacher to describe her students’ cultural and linguistic strengths and to identify challenges that students may have during the lesson, and after sharing her students’ strengths (“they love participating”), she shared that her students may need support with staying engaged with the lesson because the instructional focus on fractions is a challenging math concept for her students. As a coach and facilitator, at this moment I recognized how the volunteer teacher’s identification of this prominent student need would later connect to a later part of our conversation where we would jointly identify a CSTP element that she would want feedback on. To encourage my volunteer teacher’s agency in attending to the anticipated challenges and supports that she identified here, I kept this information in mind as I facilitated a collaborative selection of the CSTP element as seen in video clip 1 at minute 5:00 during the pre-observation meeting when I said, “So here are the CSTPs and the standards. Which element or elements align with areas that you’re interested in getting feedback on during this lesson?” At minute 5:12 in video clip 1 of the preobservation meeting, the volunteer teacher said, “Since I am a newer teacher, I do want to keep working on classroom management and I think 2.6 here makes sense because I was talking about wanting to support positive behavior, such as making sure that one person is speaking at a time.” In this moment of the pre-observation meeting, I recognized how her previous description of the classroom context, student assets and needs, and the instructional goals connected to the selection of the CSTP element that I invited her to select. In the preobservation meeting, I also invited the volunteer teacher to identify the student work products that we would collect to provide evidence of students growth towards the learning goals, as seen in video clip 1 at minute 5:30 when I asked, “Which types of observation evidence would best showcase this element that we’re selecting here?” and the volunteer teacher identified observable behavioral data. I also used facilitative questions to draw out specific student work products, such as the cool down or exit ticket as seen in video clip 1 at minute 1:43 when I asked, “Are there any exit tickets or cool downs or anything like that, that are going to show that they met the outcomes?” and the volunteer teacher replied, “In this lesson, there is a cool down which is known as an exit ticket and they will be asked to write a multiplication expression to represent the area of a shaded region, and to tell us what the area is.” </p></div><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"131\"><p>Later during the post-observation meeting, I was able to encourage the volunteer teacher to authentically voice their strengths and areas of growth by employing active listening strategies such as paraphrasing, affirmations, and requests for clarification, and asking facilitative questions. For example, in video clip 2 during the post-observation meeting, the volunteer teacher shared how she observed students who needed support with employing positive behaviors during the cool down/exit ticket, and at minute 3:33 in video clip 2 I said, ““That would be a next step, too! So, I'm hearing you say that your next steps would be working on certain parts of this math lesson of this new math curriculum to eventually become routines!” and the volunteer teacher immediately confirmed. To elicit teacher agency and reflection, at minute 3:53 in video clip 2 during the post-observation meeting, I asked, ““In order to make these things routine…so what kinds of things would be your next steps to do?” After I asked this question, the volunteer teacher shared her next steps and ideas to employ to practice the expected positive behaviors during the exit tickets with her students, such as having discussions about the expectations during this time, supporting students with organizing their materials for learning, and helping students distinguish specific expectations during each of the major moments of the math lessons in their new math curriculum. I also encouraged teacher agency and voice during the post-observation meeting when I asked clarifying questions to encourage the volunteer teacher to share how her teaching of math strategies connected to how the students employed those strategies in the formative assessments, or cool downs/exit tickets, after the lesson. For example, at minute 1:30 in video clip 1 during the post-observation meeting, I asked, ““That was part of their exit ticket, for them to show that they met the learning goal and be able to count those?” and the volunteer teacher confirmed, “yes,” and as we continued watching the lesson video, at minute 1:38 in video clip 1 I asked, “How did that instructional strategy that you employed there, that you used…How did you see that show up in the student work after the lesson?” This question prompted the volunteer teacher to look through the student work samples and at minute 2:34 in video clip 1, she shared, ““Yeah! Honestly, a lot, most of them counted 11…. counting the lengths.” This meant that she was able to connect her decisions to teach specific math strategies to how her students employed those strategies in their own work products.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response cites teacher selection of the CSTP focus, questions about student evidence, and teacher-generated routines. It explains why listening and follow-up mattered.</p><p>The analysis would be more balanced if it examined an exchange where the coach's wording narrowed the answer or supplied the conclusion. Repeated yes responses may signal agreement, but they are weaker evidence of ownership than an explanation, disagreement, or alternative initiated by the teacher.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a teacher-originated interpretation that changed your plan or understanding.</li><li>Analyze one moment where a leading prompt or rapid summary may have limited the conversation.</li><li>Explain how the partnership supported the teacher's capacity to interpret evidence independently.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.6: 4 / 5.</strong> This partnership evidence contributes to the combined 4 with question 1. It would be stronger if it examined moments that limited teacher ownership as carefully as the successful exchanges.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 3 / Equity and continuous improvement","url":"#cycle/c3/J3","html":"<span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Equity and continuous improvement</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tInformed by a continuous improvement mindset and focus on equitable leadership, reflect on your role (or future role) as an equity-driven school leader. Discuss the potential impact of supporting teacher growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what this cycle taught you about equitable instructional leadership and the potential of coaching to support teacher growth. Connect your leadership stance to actual teacher feedback, classroom evidence, and student access. Potential impact should not be written as a guaranteed outcome.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><ul><li>Reflect on your role as an instructional coach through continuous improvement and equitable leadership.</li><li>Explain the potential impact of coaching, observation, or feedback on teacher growth.</li><li>For Level 4, support the reflection with specific evidence from this cycle; Level 5 adds relevant evidence-based practices or research.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>CTC example</h3><div class=\"ctc-response\"><div class=\"original\" data-block=\"133\"><p>[As an equity-driven school leader, I am committed to supporting the growth and well-being of the stakeholders in our school community so that we can meet the needs of all our students every day. Supporting teacher’s growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback is effective because it is ongoing and differentiated sustained learning that directly improves the academic and socioemotional learning outcomes for our students. By employing transformational instructional coaching that is grounded in equity, teachers can be supported in developing their instructional skill set, expand their capacity to reflect on their instructional decision making, and develop their effectiveness and resilience as teachers (Aguilar, 2024). Collaborative coaching is focused on aligning student, school, and teacher goals and employs a facilitative process that encourages teacher agency, reflection, and actions based on CSTP observational evidence and on student work that is collected and analyzed during coaching cycles. Cultivating teacher agency is an important component to the instructional feedback cycle, as evident in the ways that we invite our teachers to share what they would like to focus on and co-construct goals during the process (Aguilar, 2024). As an instructional leader, I understand how impactful coaching cycles are on the growth of our teachers to collaboratively participate in making decisions on their own learning, to understand the whys of the learning, and to meet their personal and professional needs. By committing to regular collaborative coaching cycles, school leaders engage in active listening, facilitating, observing, and analyzing in partnership with teachers, and build collaborative and trusting coaching relationships with teachers that positively impact the learning and growth of all students.] </p></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Critique</h3><p>The response clearly values teacher agency, sustained learning, reflection, and trusting partnerships. It uses a research reference to explain why coaching can support growth.</p><p>It does not sufficiently use this cycle's evidence to analyze equity. The teacher's request for differentiation support and the limited success on the cool down offer a concrete opportunity: whose needs remained unmet, what did the coaching reveal, and what would the leader do next? General claims that coaching directly improves outcomes overstate what this one cycle establishes.</p><h4>Specific improvements</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific teacher comment and student-work finding to explain an equity issue revealed by the cycle.</li><li>Describe how your leadership response would improve access while preserving teacher agency.</li><li>Distinguish the potential benefits of coaching from outcomes demonstrated in this evidence set.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Likely score and why</h4><p><strong>Rubric 3.7: 3 / 5.</strong> The clear leadership stance and potential benefits support 3. Specific evidence from this coaching cycle is needed for 4; a general research citation cannot replace it.</p></section></section>"}],"anchors":["top","preparation","foundation","A1","a-q1","part-a","A2","a-q2","A3","a-q3","A4","a-q4","A5","a-q5","A6","a-q6","A7","a-q7","A8","a-q8","B","b-q1","part-b","C","c-q1","part-c","D","d-q1","part-d","E1","e-q1","part-e","E2","e-q2","E3","e-q3","E4","e-q4","F","f-q1","part-f","G","g-q1","part-g","H","h-q1","part-h","I1","i-q1","part-i","I2","i-q2","I3","i-q3","J1","j-q1","part-j","J2","j-q2","J3","j-q3","alignment","limits","source-record","scores","final-check","rubric-map","rubric-3-1","rubric-3-2","rubric-3-3","rubric-3-4","rubric-3-5","rubric-3-6","rubric-3-7"],"aliases":{"part-a":"A1","a-q1":"A1","a-q2":"A2","a-q3":"A3","a-q4":"A4","a-q5":"A5","a-q6":"A6","a-q7":"A7","a-q8":"A8","part-b":"B","b-q1":"B","part-c":"C","c-q1":"C","part-d":"D","d-q1":"D","part-e":"E1","e-q1":"E1","e-q2":"E2","e-q3":"E3","e-q4":"E4","part-f":"F","f-q1":"F","part-g":"G","g-q1":"G","part-h":"H","h-q1":"H","part-i":"I1","i-q1":"I1","i-q2":"I2","i-q3":"I3","part-j":"J1","j-q1":"J1","j-q2":"J2","j-q3":"J3","foundation":"preparation","rubric-map":"scores","final-check":"scores"}}};
